window.GATE_BANK=window.GATE_BANK||{};window.GATE_BANK["programming-data-structures"]=[{"id": "DS-U1-Q01", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "data-types-operators", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Compiled with a C99-or-later compiler on a machine where <code class=\"mm\">int</code> is 32 bits, what does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(&quot;%d %d\\n&quot;, 7 / 2 * 2, -7 / 2);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["6 -3", "7 -3", "6 -4", "7 -4"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In C99 and later, integer division truncates towards zero, so <code class=\"mm\">-7 / 2</code> is <code class=\"mm\">-3</code>, not <code class=\"mm\">-4</code>. Multiplication and division have equal precedence and associate left to right.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">7 / 2</code> is integer division: both operands are <code class=\"mm\">int</code>, so the result is <code class=\"mm\">3</code>.</li><li>Then <code class=\"mm\">3 * 2</code> gives <code class=\"mm\">6</code> — the multiply happens after the division because of left-to-right associativity.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">-7 / 2</code> discards the fractional part towards zero: the exact quotient is <code class=\"mm\">-3.5</code>, truncated to <code class=\"mm\">-3</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">6 -3</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Python and many pocket calculators use floor division, where <code class=\"mm\">-7 / 2</code> is <code class=\"mm\">-4</code>. C truncates towards zero, so the second value is <code class=\"mm\">-3</code>. The first trap is reading <code class=\"mm\">7 / 2 * 2</code> as <code class=\"mm\">7 / (2 * 2)</code> = 1 or as floating point 7.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q02", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "data-types-operators", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">On an implementation where <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(int)</code> is 4, what is the value of <code class=\"mm\">sizeof('A')</code> in C?</p>", "options": ["1", "2", "4", "It depends on the character encoding used by the compiler"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In C a character constant such as <code class=\"mm\">'A'</code> has type <code class=\"mm\">int</code>, not <code class=\"mm\">char</code>. Its size is therefore the size of an <code class=\"mm\">int</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The literal <code class=\"mm\">'A'</code> is a character constant whose type is <code class=\"mm\">int</code> (C11 §6.4.4.4).</li><li>No conversion to <code class=\"mm\">char</code> takes place inside <code class=\"mm\">sizeof</code>, because the operand of <code class=\"mm\">sizeof</code> is not evaluated or converted to a narrower type.</li><li>Hence <code class=\"mm\">sizeof('A') == sizeof(int) == 4</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">4</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> This is the classic C-versus-C++ confusion: in C++ <code class=\"mm\">'A'</code> has type <code class=\"mm\">char</code> and the answer would be 1. The character encoding never changes the size of an <code class=\"mm\">int</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q03", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "data-types-operators", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a = 6, b = 3;\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, a ^ b | a &amp; b);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["7", "5", "2", "1"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Bitwise AND binds tighter than XOR, which binds tighter than OR, so the expression groups as <code class=\"mm\">(a ^ b) | (a & b)</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">a &amp; b</code> with <code class=\"mm\">a = 6 (110₂)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">b = 3 (011₂)</code> gives <code class=\"mm\">010₂ = 2</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">a ^ b</code> gives <code class=\"mm\">101₂ = 5</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">5 | 2</code> gives <code class=\"mm\">101₂ | 010₂ = 111₂ = 7</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">7</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reading the expression left to right and computing <code class=\"mm\">(6 ^ 3) | 6</code> first, or assuming all three bitwise operators share precedence. The precedence order is <code class=\"mm\">&amp;</code> above <code class=\"mm\">^</code> above <code class=\"mm\">|</code>. Option 5 comes from evaluating only <code class=\"mm\">a ^ b</code> and stopping.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q04", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "data-types-operators", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print, assuming <code class=\"mm\">CHAR_BIT == 8</code>?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    unsigned char c = 250;\n    c = c + 10;\n    printf(&quot;%u\\n&quot;, c);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["260", "4", "0", "10"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Unsigned arithmetic is performed modulo <code class=\"mm\">2ⁿ</code> for an <code class=\"mm\">n</code>-bit type. Assignment to an unsigned type reduces the value modulo <code class=\"mm\">2ⁿ</code>; it never traps and never wraps into a negative number.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>In <code class=\"mm\">c + 10</code> the <code class=\"mm\">unsigned char</code> is promoted to <code class=\"mm\">int</code>, so the sum is the ordinary integer <code class=\"mm\">260</code>.</li><li>The assignment back to <code class=\"mm\">unsigned char</code> converts the value modulo <code class=\"mm\">2⁸ = 256</code>: <code class=\"mm\">260 mod 256 = 4</code>.</li><li>So <code class=\"mm\">c</code> holds 4 and <code class=\"mm\">%u</code> prints 4.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">4</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 260 ignores the conversion on assignment; answering 0 assumes the value wraps to a signed negative and is then read as unsigned. Neither happens — the reduction is modulo 256 and the result is 4.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q05", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "data-types-operators", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about the standard C integer types are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["sizeof(short) &lt;= sizeof(int) on every conforming implementation", "sizeof(char) is 1 on every conforming implementation", "Signed integer overflow is defined to wrap around, exactly as it does for unsigned types", "Plain char is signed on every conforming implementation"], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The C standard fixes minimum ranges and a rank order for the integer types, but leaves the size of most of them to the implementation. It also leaves signed overflow undefined and the signedness of plain <code class=\"mm\">char</code> implementation-defined.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: the standard requires <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(short) &lt;= sizeof(int) &lt;= sizeof(long)</code>, so a <code class=\"mm\">short</code> can never be larger than an <code class=\"mm\">int</code>.</li><li>(B) is true: <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(char) == 1</code> by definition — a byte in C is exactly one <code class=\"mm\">char</code>, whatever its bit width.</li><li>(C) is false: unsigned arithmetic is defined modulo <code class=\"mm\">2ⁿ</code>, but signed overflow is undefined behaviour. An optimiser may assume it never happens.</li><li>(D) is false: whether plain <code class=\"mm\">char</code> behaves as <code class=\"mm\">signed char</code> or <code class=\"mm\">unsigned char</code> is implementation-defined; only <code class=\"mm\">signed char</code> and <code class=\"mm\">unsigned char</code> are distinct types with fixed signedness.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Options (A) and (B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> The tempting error is (C): students see unsigned wrap-around and assume signed types do the same. They do not — signed overflow is undefined, and a program that relies on it can behave differently at <code class=\"mm\">-O0</code> and <code class=\"mm\">-O2</code>. Option (D) is the other classic: <code class=\"mm\">char</code> is not guaranteed signed.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q06", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "data-types-operators", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int x, y = 2;\n    x = (y += 3, y * 2);\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, x);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["5", "10", "8", "2"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The comma operator evaluates its left operand, discards that value, then evaluates the right operand and yields its value. The parentheses make the whole comma expression the right-hand side of the assignment to <code class=\"mm\">x</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The left operand <code class=\"mm\">y += 3</code> runs first: <code class=\"mm\">y</code> becomes <code class=\"mm\">2 + 3 = 5</code>; its value 5 is discarded.</li><li>The right operand <code class=\"mm\">y * 2</code> is then evaluated with the updated <code class=\"mm\">y</code>: <code class=\"mm\">5 * 2 = 10</code>.</li><li>The comma expression yields 10, which is assigned to <code class=\"mm\">x</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">10</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Two traps. First, forgetting that the left operand has already changed <code class=\"mm\">y</code>, so the second operand uses 5 rather than 2 — that gives the distractor 4 or 5. Second, reading the comma as a declaration separator; inside an expression it is the comma operator. Note also that <code class=\"mm\">int x, y = 2;</code> initialises only <code class=\"mm\">y</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q07", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "data-types-operators", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    unsigned char c = 0xF0;\n    int r = c &lt;&lt; 4;\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, r);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "3840", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a shift expression the operands are promoted to <code class=\"mm\">int</code> first (the usual arithmetic conversions), so the left shift happens on a 32-bit value, not on an 8-bit one. The result of <code class=\"mm\">c &lt;&lt; 4</code> is therefore not reduced modulo 256.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">c</code> holds <code class=\"mm\">0xF0 = 240</code>.</li><li>Integer promotion converts <code class=\"mm\">c</code> to the <code class=\"mm\">int</code> value 240 before the shift.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">240 &lt;&lt; 4</code> = <code class=\"mm\">240 × 16 = 3840</code>, which is <code class=\"mm\">0xF00</code> and still fits in a 32-bit <code class=\"mm\">int</code>.</li><li>The assignment to <code class=\"mm\">r</code> is a plain <code class=\"mm\">int</code> assignment, so no truncation occurs and <code class=\"mm\">%d</code> prints 3840.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">3840</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> The classic error is to keep the value 8 bits wide and answer 0: <code class=\"mm\">0xF0 &lt;&lt; 4</code> truncated to 8 bits is <code class=\"mm\">0x00</code>. That would be true only if the shift were performed in <code class=\"mm\">unsigned char</code> arithmetic, which C does not do. Remember that shifts do not truncate to the left operand's type.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q08", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "control-structures", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print? (It compiles; the compiler only warns.)</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int x = 0;\n    if (x = 0) printf(&quot;A\\n&quot;);\n    else       printf(&quot;B\\n&quot;);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["A", "B", "Nothing is printed", "It does not compile"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The condition of an <code class=\"mm\">if</code> is any scalar expression; here it is an assignment expression, whose value is the value stored. Storing 0 makes the condition false.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">x = 0</code> assigns 0 to <code class=\"mm\">x</code> and yields the value 0.</li><li>A zero value is false, so the <code class=\"mm\">else</code> branch runs.</li><li>The program prints <code class=\"mm\">B</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">B</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reading <code class=\"mm\">x = 0</code> as the comparison <code class=\"mm\">x == 0</code>. That comparison would be true and print <code class=\"mm\">A</code>. This is why good compilers warn about an assignment used as a condition. With <code class=\"mm\">x = 1</code> the program would print <code class=\"mm\">A</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q09", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "control-structures", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int i, j;\n    for (i = 0, j = 10; i &lt; j; i++, j--)\n        ;\n    printf(&quot;%d %d\\n&quot;, i, j);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["4 6", "5 5", "6 4", "10 0"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A <code class=\"mm\">for</code> header has three parts separated by semicolons: initialisation, condition and update. The update here is a single comma expression, so both <code class=\"mm\">i++</code> and <code class=\"mm\">j--</code> run at the end of every iteration.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Start: <code class=\"mm\">i = 0, j = 10</code>; condition <code class=\"mm\">0 &lt; 10</code> true.</li><li>After each iteration both counters move: <code class=\"mm\">(1, 9), (2, 8), (3, 7), (4, 6)</code>.</li><li>At <code class=\"mm\">(5, 5)</code> the condition <code class=\"mm\">5 &lt; 5</code> is false, so the loop stops with <code class=\"mm\">i = 5</code> and <code class=\"mm\">j = 5</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">5 5</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Stopping at <code class=\"mm\">4 6</code> — that is the state at the start of the last successful iteration, not the state after the failing test. The update clause always runs before the condition is re-tested, so the counters meet at 5.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q10", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "control-structures", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int c = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++)\n        for (int j = i; j &lt; 5; j++)\n            c++;\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, c);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "15", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The inner loop starts at <code class=\"mm\">j = i</code>, so it runs <code class=\"mm\">5 - i</code> times for each value of <code class=\"mm\">i</code>. The total is the sum of an arithmetic series.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>For <code class=\"mm\">i = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4</code> the inner loop runs 5, 4, 3, 2, 1 times.</li><li>Total: <code class=\"mm\">5 + 4 + 3 + 2 + 1 = 15</code>.</li><li>Equivalently <code class=\"mm\">n(n + 1)/2</code> with <code class=\"mm\">n = 5</code>, i.e. the number of pairs <code class=\"mm\">(i, j)</code> with <code class=\"mm\">0 ≤ i ≤ j &lt; 5</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">15</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming both loops always run 5 times and answering 25, or counting only the strictly upper-triangular pairs (10) by starting the inner loop at <code class=\"mm\">j = i + 1</code>. The condition is <code class=\"mm\">j &lt; 5</code> with <code class=\"mm\">j</code> starting at <code class=\"mm\">i</code>, so the diagonal is included.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q11", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "control-structures", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int x = 2;\n    switch (x) {\n        case 1: printf(&quot;one&quot;);\n        case 2: printf(&quot;two&quot;);\n        case 3: printf(&quot;three&quot;); break;\n        default: printf(&quot;other&quot;);\n    }\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["two", "twothree", "onetwothree", "twoother"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A <code class=\"mm\">switch</code> transfers control to the matching <code class=\"mm\">case</code> label and then simply falls through the following statements until a <code class=\"mm\">break</code> or the end of the block. Labels do not act as implicit exits.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The controlling value is 2, so control jumps to <code class=\"mm\">case 2:</code>.</li><li>The statement <code class=\"mm\">printf(\"two\")</code> runs.</li><li>There is no <code class=\"mm\">break</code> after it, so execution continues into <code class=\"mm\">case 3:</code> and prints <code class=\"mm\">three</code>.</li><li>The <code class=\"mm\">break</code> after <code class=\"mm\">case 3:</code> ends the switch, so the <code class=\"mm\">default</code> is never reached.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">twothree</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming each <code class=\"mm\">case</code> is exclusive, as in an if-else chain, and answering <code class=\"mm\">two</code>. Fall-through is the default in C; a <code class=\"mm\">break</code> is required at the end of every case you want to end. Note also that the <code class=\"mm\">default</code> is skipped here only because a <code class=\"mm\">break</code> intervened.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q12", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "control-structures", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">On a typical 32-bit implementation, which of these loops TERMINATE?</p>", "options": ["for (unsigned int i = 10; i &gt;= 0; i--) { }", "int i = 0; while (i &lt; 5) i += 2;", "int i = 0; do { i++; } while (i &lt; 0);", "for (int i = 0; i != 10; i += 3) { }"], "answer_display": "Options (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A loop terminates when its condition eventually becomes false. For unsigned counters and for equality tests with a stride that does not divide the gap, the condition can be permanently true.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) An <code class=\"mm\">unsigned int</code> is never negative, so <code class=\"mm\">i &gt;= 0</code> is always true. When <code class=\"mm\">i</code> is 0 the decrement wraps to <code class=\"mm\">4294967295</code> and the loop never ends.</li><li>(B) <code class=\"mm\">i</code> takes 0, 2, 4 and then 6, at which point <code class=\"mm\">6 &lt; 5</code> is false. It terminates.</li><li>(C) The body runs once (<code class=\"mm\">i</code> becomes 1) and then <code class=\"mm\">1 &lt; 0</code> is false. A <code class=\"mm\">do-while</code> always executes its body at least once. It terminates.</li><li>(D) <code class=\"mm\">i</code> takes 0, 3, 6, 9, 12, … and never equals 10, so the test is never false. It does not terminate.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Options (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Option (A) is the trap: the loop looks exactly like a signed countdown that ends at 0, but on an unsigned type the final decrement wraps instead of going negative. Option (D) shows the second trap — <code class=\"mm\">!=</code> is safe only when the stride divides the target.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q13", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "control-structures", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int s = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++) {\n        switch (i) {\n            case 0: continue;\n            case 1: s += 1;\n            case 2: s += 2; break;\n            case 3: s += 4;\n            default: s += 8;\n        }\n        s += 100;\n    }\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, s);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["425", "325", "417", "405"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Inside a <code class=\"mm\">switch</code> that is inside a loop, <code class=\"mm\">continue</code> belongs to the loop: it abandons the rest of the switch and the rest of the loop body, and jumps to the loop's update step. Fall-through still applies between case labels.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>i = 0: <code class=\"mm\">case 0</code> executes <code class=\"mm\">continue</code>, so neither the switch nor the trailing <code class=\"mm\">s += 100</code> runs. <code class=\"mm\">s = 0</code>.</li><li>i = 1: <code class=\"mm\">case 1</code> adds 1, falls into <code class=\"mm\">case 2</code> and adds 2, then <code class=\"mm\">break</code> exits the switch. <code class=\"mm\">s += 100</code> runs: <code class=\"mm\">s = 3 + 100 = 103</code>.</li><li>i = 2: <code class=\"mm\">case 2</code> adds 2 and breaks, then <code class=\"mm\">s += 100</code>: <code class=\"mm\">s = 105 + 100 = 205</code>.</li><li>i = 3: <code class=\"mm\">case 3</code> adds 4 and falls through into <code class=\"mm\">default</code>, adding 8: <code class=\"mm\">s = 209 + 8 = 217</code>; then <code class=\"mm\">s += 100</code> gives 317.</li><li>i = 4: <code class=\"mm\">default</code> adds 8: <code class=\"mm\">s = 325</code>; then <code class=\"mm\">s += 100</code> gives 425.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">425</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Two mistakes produce the distractors. Treating <code class=\"mm\">continue</code> as <code class=\"mm\">break</code> — that is, letting the loop carry on to <code class=\"mm\">s += 100</code> for <code class=\"mm\">i = 0</code> — gives 325. Forgetting the fall-through from <code class=\"mm\">case 1</code> into <code class=\"mm\">case 2</code>, or from <code class=\"mm\">case 3</code> into <code class=\"mm\">default</code>, gives 417 or 405. Note that in C, unlike Java, <code class=\"mm\">continue</code> inside a switch acts on the enclosing loop.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q14", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "functions-and-parameter-passing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nvoid swap(int a, int b) {\n    int t = a;\n    a = b;\n    b = t;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int x = 1, y = 2;\n    swap(x, y);\n    printf(&quot;%d %d\\n&quot;, x, y);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["1 2", "2 1", "2 2", "1 1"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> C has only call by value. The parameters <code class=\"mm\">a</code> and <code class=\"mm\">b</code> are fresh objects inside <code class=\"mm\">swap</code>, initialised with copies of the arguments, so swapping them changes nothing in the caller.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The call copies <code class=\"mm\">x</code> into <code class=\"mm\">a</code> and <code class=\"mm\">y</code> into <code class=\"mm\">b</code>.</li><li>The body exchanges the copies: <code class=\"mm\">a = 2</code>, <code class=\"mm\">b = 1</code>.</li><li>On return those copies are destroyed; <code class=\"mm\">x</code> and <code class=\"mm\">y</code> are untouched.</li><li>The program prints <code class=\"mm\">1 2</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">1 2</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Expecting the swap to work, as it would in a language with call by reference. To make it work in C you must pass addresses — <code class=\"mm\">void swap(int *a, int *b)</code> called as <code class=\"mm\">swap(&amp;x, &amp;y)</code> — and dereference inside.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q15", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "functions-and-parameter-passing", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint sq(int x) { return x * x; }\n\nint main(void) {\n    int s = 0;\n    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 4; i++)\n        s += sq(i);\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, s);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "30", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The function is called once per iteration with the current value of <code class=\"mm\">i</code>, and its return value is added to the accumulator.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Calls: <code class=\"mm\">sq(1) = 1</code>, <code class=\"mm\">sq(2) = 4</code>, <code class=\"mm\">sq(3) = 9</code>, <code class=\"mm\">sq(4) = 16</code>.</li><li>Sum: <code class=\"mm\">1 + 4 + 9 + 16 = 30</code>.</li><li>Closed form: <code class=\"mm\">n(n + 1)(2n + 1)/6 = 4·5·9/6 = 30</code> for <code class=\"mm\">n = 4</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">30</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Summing 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10 by reading the call as <code class=\"mm\">sq(i) = i</code>, or stopping the loop at <code class=\"mm\">i &lt; 4</code> and losing the 16. The upper bound is <code class=\"mm\">i &lt;= 4</code>, so four calls are made.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q16", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "functions-and-parameter-passing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint f(void) {\n    static int c = 0;\n    c++;\n    return c;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    f();\n    f();\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, f());\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["1", "2", "3", "0"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A <code class=\"mm\">static</code> local variable is created once, before the program starts, and lives for the whole execution. It keeps its value between calls; only its name is scoped to the function.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The initialiser <code class=\"mm\">c = 0</code> is applied once, at program start — not on every call.</li><li>First call: <code class=\"mm\">c</code> becomes 1, returns 1. Second call: becomes 2, returns 2.</li><li>The third call (the one inside <code class=\"mm\">printf</code>) makes <code class=\"mm\">c = 3</code> and returns 3, which is printed.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">3</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming the local is re-initialised on every call and answering 1. That would be true for an automatic local such as <code class=\"mm\">int c = 0;</code>. <code class=\"mm\">static</code> changes storage duration, not scope: <code class=\"mm\">c</code> is still invisible outside <code class=\"mm\">f</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q17", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "functions-and-parameter-passing", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">An array declared as <code class=\"mm\">int a[10]</code> is passed to a function declared as <code class=\"mm\">void g(int a[])</code> and called as <code class=\"mm\">g(a)</code>. Which statements are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["Inside g the parameter is a pointer, so sizeof a gives the size of a pointer, not 40", "The call copies all ten elements into the activation record of g", "An assignment to a[i] inside g is visible to the caller", "The number of elements cannot be recovered from the parameter alone"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a parameter list, an array declarator is adjusted to a pointer type: <code class=\"mm\">int a[]</code> and <code class=\"mm\">int a[10]</code> both mean <code class=\"mm\">int *a</code>. What is passed is the address of the first element, not a copy of the array.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: after adjustment the parameter has type <code class=\"mm\">int *</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">sizeof a</code> is the pointer size (4 or 8 bytes), never 40.</li><li>(B) is false: C never copies an array on a call. Only the pointer value is copied.</li><li>(C) is true: <code class=\"mm\">a[i]</code> designates the caller's element through the pointer, so writes are visible after return.</li><li>(D) is true: the pointer carries no length, which is why such functions take a separate count parameter.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Options (A), (C) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> The natural but wrong reading is (B) — \"arrays are passed by value like everything else\". The value that is copied is the address, so element changes propagate while reassigning the pointer itself does not. And because the length is lost, <code class=\"mm\">sizeof</code> inside the callee is the classic way to get a wrong byte count.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q18", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "functions-and-parameter-passing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nvoid inc(int *p)  { *p = *p + 1; }\nvoid bump(int p)  { p = p + 1; }\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a = 5;\n    inc(&amp;a);\n    bump(a);\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, a);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["5", "6", "7", "8"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Only the callee that receives an address can change the caller's object. <code class=\"mm\">inc</code> dereferences its pointer and writes through it; <code class=\"mm\">bump</code> increments its own local copy.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">inc(&amp;a)</code> passes the address of <code class=\"mm\">a</code>; <code class=\"mm\">*p = *p + 1</code> stores 6 in <code class=\"mm\">a</code> itself.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">bump(a)</code> passes the value 6 into a new local <code class=\"mm\">p</code>; <code class=\"mm\">p = p + 1</code> makes that local 7 and it is discarded on return.</li><li>So <code class=\"mm\">a</code> is 6 and the program prints 6.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">6</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Adding both increments and answering 7, on the assumption that <code class=\"mm\">bump</code> also changes the caller. A value parameter is a copy; only writes through a pointer reach the caller. A related trap is writing <code class=\"mm\">void inc(int p)</code> and expecting the caller to change.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q19", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "functions-and-parameter-passing", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint f(int n) {\n    static int s = 0;\n    s = s + n;\n    return s;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int i, t = 0;\n    for (i = 1; i &lt;= 4; i++)\n        t += f(i);\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, t);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "20", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A <code class=\"mm\">static</code> local keeps its value across calls, so <code class=\"mm\">f</code> returns a running total rather than just <code class=\"mm\">n</code>. The calls here are sequential, so the order of evaluation is fully determined.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Call <code class=\"mm\">f(1)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">s = 0 + 1 = 1</code>, returns 1. <code class=\"mm\">t = 1</code>.</li><li>Call <code class=\"mm\">f(2)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">s = 1 + 2 = 3</code>, returns 3. <code class=\"mm\">t = 4</code>.</li><li>Call <code class=\"mm\">f(3)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">s = 3 + 3 = 6</code>, returns 6. <code class=\"mm\">t = 10</code>.</li><li>Call <code class=\"mm\">f(4)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">s = 6 + 4 = 10</code>, returns 10. <code class=\"mm\">t = 20</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">20</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Two errors. Ignoring <code class=\"mm\">static</code> and computing <code class=\"mm\">1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10</code> gives the distractor 10. Summing the returned values but forgetting that <code class=\"mm\">s</code> also accumulates them (answering <code class=\"mm\">10 + 10 = 20</code> by a wrong route) is harmless here, but the reliable method is to track <code class=\"mm\">s</code> call by call. Note the initialiser runs only once for the whole program.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q20", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "recursion-stack-execution", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint f(int n) {\n    if (n == 0) return 0;\n    return n + f(n - 1);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, f(4));\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["4", "10", "24", "6"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The recursive call is inside an addition, so each frame must wait for the value returned by the frame below it before it can finish. The results are added on the way back up the stack.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Calls descend: <code class=\"mm\">f(4) → f(3) → f(2) → f(1) → f(0)</code>.</li><li>The base case returns 0. Unwinding: <code class=\"mm\">f(1) = 1 + 0 = 1</code>, <code class=\"mm\">f(2) = 2 + 1 = 3</code>, <code class=\"mm\">f(3) = 3 + 3 = 6</code>, <code class=\"mm\">f(4) = 4 + 6 = 10</code>.</li><li>This is the sum <code class=\"mm\">1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">10</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 24 treats the function as a factorial (<code class=\"mm\">4! = 24</code>); the multiplication is nowhere in the code. Answering 4 reads the return as just <code class=\"mm\">n</code>. The addition of the returned value is what makes this a sum.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q21", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "recursion-stack-execution", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The program below prints a sequence of digits. Which statements about that output are TRUE?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nvoid f(int n) {\n    if (n &gt; 0) {\n        printf(&quot;%d&quot;, n % 2);\n        f(n / 2);\n    }\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    f(13);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["The digits are printed in the order 1, 0, 1, 1", "The first digit printed is the least significant bit of 13", "The program prints the binary numeral of 13 in the usual most-significant-first order", "The last digit printed is the most significant bit of 13"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each frame prints the least significant remaining bit and only then recurses on the quotient, so the bits emerge least significant first. Code placed before a recursive call runs on the way down the stack.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">f(13)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">13 mod 2 = 1</code>, print 1, recurse on <code class=\"mm\">13 / 2 = 6</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">f(6)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">6 mod 2 = 0</code>, print 0, recurse on 3. <code class=\"mm\">f(3)</code>: print 1, recurse on 1. <code class=\"mm\">f(1)</code>: print 1, recurse on 0, which stops.</li><li>Digits printed: 1, 0, 1, 1. The first is the low-order bit of 13; the last, printed by the deepest frame that prints anything, is the high-order bit. 13 is <code class=\"mm\">1101₂</code>, so the output is that numeral reversed.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Options (A), (B) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Option (C) is the trap: students assume a bit-printing recursion prints the numeral in reading order. Moving the <code class=\"mm\">printf</code> after the recursive call would do exactly that and produce <code class=\"mm\">1101</code>. Option (D) is easy to get wrong by assuming the last frame is <code class=\"mm\">f(13)</code> — it is the deepest one.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q22", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "recursion-stack-execution", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nvoid f(int n) {\n    if (n == 0) return;\n    f(n - 1);\n    printf(&quot;%d&quot;, n);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    f(3);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["321", "123", "0123", "3"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The <code class=\"mm\">printf</code> is placed after the recursive call, so it runs while the stack unwinds — from the deepest frame back to the shallowest.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Calls descend <code class=\"mm\">f(3) → f(2) → f(1) → f(0)</code>; <code class=\"mm\">f(0)</code> returns immediately.</li><li>Unwinding, <code class=\"mm\">f(1)</code> prints 1, then <code class=\"mm\">f(2)</code> prints 2, then <code class=\"mm\">f(3)</code> prints 3.</li><li>The output is <code class=\"mm\">123</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">123</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering <code class=\"mm\">321</code> assumes the print happens before the recursion. The distinction is worth memorising: code before the recursive call runs on the way down (most significant first), code after it runs on the way up (least significant first). Nothing prints 0 because the base case returns before printing.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q23", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "recursion-stack-execution", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which statements about recursion and the run-time stack in C are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["Any recursive function can be rewritten iteratively with an explicit stack that mimics the activation records", "A tail-recursive call can reuse the caller's activation record when the compiler performs tail-call optimisation", "The C standard guarantees that recursion depth is limited only by available memory", "Each active call has its own copy of the parameters and of the automatic local variables"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A call in C uses an activation record (frame) on the run-time stack, holding the return address, the saved state, the parameters and the automatic locals. Recursion is therefore just a sequence of frames, and it can always be simulated explicitly.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: a recursive call does nothing that a manually maintained stack of the same frames cannot reproduce.</li><li>(B) is true: when the call is the last operation of the caller, the callee can reuse the frame; this is a permitted optimisation, and <code class=\"mm\">-foptimize-sibling-calls</code> enables it in GCC.</li><li>(C) is false: the standard sets no minimum depth and no guarantee. The stack is finite and typically a few megabytes, so deep recursion overflows it — a crash, not an out-of-memory error.</li><li>(D) is true: that per-call copy is exactly why a value parameter cannot be changed in the caller and why automatic locals do not interfere across frames.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Options (A), (B) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Option (C) is the trap: students confuse \"the stack grows as needed\" with a standard guarantee. In practice the depth limit is small (tens of thousands of frames at most) and exceeding it is undefined behaviour. A second trap is assuming (B) happens automatically in C — it is an optimisation, not a language requirement.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q24", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "recursion-stack-execution", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint f(int n) {\n    return (n &lt; 2) ? n : f(n - 1) + f(n - 2);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, f(6));\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "8", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> This is the Fibonacci recurrence with base values <code class=\"mm\">f(0) = 0</code> and <code class=\"mm\">f(1) = 1</code>. Each value is the sum of the two before it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Compute upwards: <code class=\"mm\">f(2) = 1 + 0 = 1</code>, <code class=\"mm\">f(3) = 1 + 1 = 2</code>, <code class=\"mm\">f(4) = 2 + 1 = 3</code>, <code class=\"mm\">f(5) = 3 + 2 = 5</code>, <code class=\"mm\">f(6) = 5 + 3 = 8</code>.</li><li>The sequence is 0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8 for <code class=\"mm\">n = 0…6</code>.</li><li>So the program prints 8.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">8</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Shifting the index by one and answering 5 (the value of <code class=\"mm\">f(5)</code>) or 13 (the value of <code class=\"mm\">f(7)</code>). Note also that this naive recursion recomputes the same subproblems repeatedly — <code class=\"mm\">f(6)</code> needs 25 calls — which is why the exponential cost of the naive version matters.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q25", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "recursion-stack-execution", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint f(int n, int *c) {\n    (*c)++;\n    if (n &lt;= 1) return 1;\n    return f(n - 1, c) + f(n - 2, c);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int c = 0;\n    f(5, &amp;c);\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, c);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["9", "15", "25", "8"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The counter is incremented once per call, in every frame, through a pointer shared by all of them. The answer is the total number of calls made by the recursion tree, not the value returned.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Let <code class=\"mm\">C(n)</code> be the number of calls made by <code class=\"mm\">f(n)</code>. One call happens at the top, plus the calls made by its two children.</li><li>Base cases: <code class=\"mm\">C(0) = C(1) = 1</code>. For <code class=\"mm\">n ≥ 2</code>: <code class=\"mm\">C(n) = 1 + C(n − 1) + C(n − 2)</code>.</li><li>Compute: <code class=\"mm\">C(2) = 1 + 1 + 1 = 3</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C(3) = 1 + 3 + 1 = 5</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C(4) = 1 + 5 + 3 = 9</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C(5) = 1 + 9 + 5 = 15</code>.</li><li>So <code class=\"mm\">c</code> ends at 15.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">15</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 8, the value <code class=\"mm\">f</code> returns, instead of the count. Answering 9 is <code class=\"mm\">C(4)</code>, the count one level down. The recurrence <code class=\"mm\">C(n) = C(n−1) + C(n−2) + 1</code> is worth remembering: for <code class=\"mm\">n = 5</code> it gives 15, and in general it grows like the Fibonacci numbers — the reason naive recursion here is exponential.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q26", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "data-types-operators", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">On an implementation where <code class=\"mm\">int</code> is 4 bytes and <code class=\"mm\">float</code> is 4 bytes, what is the value of <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(1.0f + 1)</code>?</p>", "options": ["1", "4", "8", "It cannot be determined without knowing the value of 1.0f + 1."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The type of an arithmetic expression is fixed by the usual arithmetic conversions, and <code class=\"mm\">sizeof</code> reports the size of that type, not of a computed value.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">1.0f</code> has type <code class=\"mm\">float</code> and <code class=\"mm\">1</code> has type <code class=\"mm\">int</code>.</li><li>The usual arithmetic conversions convert the <code class=\"mm\">int</code> operand to <code class=\"mm\">float</code>, so the sum has type <code class=\"mm\">float</code>.</li><li>There is no <code class=\"mm\">double</code> in the expression: a <code class=\"mm\">float</code> stays <code class=\"mm\">float</code> unless a <code class=\"mm\">double</code> operand forces promotion.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">sizeof(float)</code> is 4 on this implementation, so the value is 4.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that any floating-point expression is evaluated in <code class=\"mm\">double</code> and answering 8. Only an unsuffixed literal such as <code class=\"mm\">1.0</code> is a <code class=\"mm\">double</code>; <code class=\"mm\">1.0f</code> is not. A second trap is thinking <code class=\"mm\">sizeof</code> evaluates the operand — it does not, which is why the value of the sum is irrelevant.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q27", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "data-types-operators", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a = 017, b = 0x1F;\n    printf(&quot;%d %d\\n&quot;, a, b);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["15 31", "17 31", "15 1F", "017 31"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The base of an integer literal is set by its prefix: a leading <code class=\"mm\">0</code> means octal, <code class=\"mm\">0x</code> means hexadecimal, and no prefix means decimal.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">017</code> is octal: 1·8 + 7 = 15.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">0x1F</code> is hexadecimal: 1·16 + 15 = 31.</li><li>Both initialisers store the integer values 15 and 31; the base affects only how the source text is read.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">%d</code> prints them in decimal, so the output is <code class=\"mm\">15 31</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading <code class=\"mm\">017</code> as the decimal seventeen. The second trap is expecting <code class=\"mm\">%d</code> to echo the hexadecimal digits — to see <code class=\"mm\">1f</code> you must print with <code class=\"mm\">%x</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q28", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "data-types-operators", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">On a machine where <code class=\"mm\">int</code> is 32 bits, what does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int i = -1;\n    unsigned int u = 1;\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, i &lt; u);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["1", "0", "-1", "The behaviour is undefined"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> When an <code class=\"mm\">int</code> meets an <code class=\"mm\">unsigned int</code> of the same rank, the usual arithmetic conversions turn the <code class=\"mm\">int</code> into <code class=\"mm\">unsigned int</code> before the comparison.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The conversion of a negative value to unsigned is defined as arithmetic modulo 2³², so <code class=\"mm\">-1</code> becomes 4294967295u.</li><li>The comparison is therefore <code class=\"mm\">4294967295u &lt; 1u</code>, which is false.</li><li>A relational operator yields <code class=\"mm\">int</code> 0 or 1, so the value passed to <code class=\"mm\">%d</code> is 0.</li><li>Hence the program prints 0.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reasoning in signed arithmetic, where −1 &lt; 1 is true, and answering 1. This is the mirror image of the classic <code class=\"mm\">-1 &lt; sizeof(x)</code> trap: an unsigned operand in a mixed expression silently reinterprets the signed value.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q29", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "data-types-operators", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int x = 1;\n    x &lt;&lt;= 3;\n    x |= 1;\n    x ^= 5;\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, x);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["8", "9", "12", "13"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Compound assignment operators apply one bitwise operation at a time, in the order written, and each one assigns the result back to the variable.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">x = 1</code>, i.e. binary 0001.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">x &lt;&lt;= 3</code> shifts left by three: 1000, so <code class=\"mm\">x = 8</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">x |= 1</code> sets the low bit: 1001, so <code class=\"mm\">x = 9</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">x ^= 5</code> is 1001 XOR 0101 = 1100, so <code class=\"mm\">x = 12</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using <code class=\"mm\">|</code> and <code class=\"mm\">^</code> in the wrong order, or replacing the XOR by an OR, which gives 13. Bit 3 survives the OR and is cleared by the XOR because 5 has that bit set.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q30", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "data-types-operators", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about C integer and character constants are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["The integer literal 10 has type int.", "The hexadecimal literal 0x1F has the decimal value 31.", "An integer literal that begins with 0 is octal, so 011 equals 9.", "A character constant such as 'a' has type char."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Literal type is determined by the literal's form and by the value it must hold; character constants are the one constant form whose type surprises most candidates.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) An unsuffixed decimal literal whose value fits in <code class=\"mm\">int</code> has type <code class=\"mm\">int</code>. TRUE.</li><li>(B) <code class=\"mm\">0x1F</code> = 1·16 + 15 = 31. TRUE.</li><li>(C) A leading zero means octal, and 011 octal = 1·8 + 1 = 9. TRUE.</li><li>(D) In C a character constant has type <code class=\"mm\">int</code>, not <code class=\"mm\">char</code> — which is why <code class=\"mm\">sizeof('a')</code> equals <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(int)</code>. FALSE.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (D) is the standard error, imported from C++, where character constants do have type <code class=\"mm\">char</code>. In C the value of <code class=\"mm\">'a'</code> is an <code class=\"mm\">int</code> holding the execution character set code.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q31", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "data-types-operators", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, (5 &gt; 3) + (2 == 2) + (1 != 1));\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Relational and equality operators yield the <code class=\"mm\">int</code> values 1 and 0, so their results can be added like any other integers.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">5 &gt; 3</code> is true, giving 1.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">2 == 2</code> is true, giving 1.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">1 != 1</code> is false, giving 0.</li><li>The sum is 1 + 1 + 0 = 2.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing comparison results cannot be used in arithmetic, or assuming they are of type <code class=\"mm\">_Bool</code> and therefore print differently. In C the result of a comparison is a plain <code class=\"mm\">int</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q32", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "data-types-operators", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">On a machine where <code class=\"mm\">unsigned int</code> is 32 bits, what does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    unsigned int a = 3000000000u;\n    printf(&quot;%u\\n&quot;, a * 2u);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "1705032704", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Unsigned arithmetic is performed modulo 2ⁿ, so a result that overflows wraps around rather than invoking undefined behaviour.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The exact product is 3000000000 × 2 = 6000000000.</li><li>The modulus for 32-bit <code class=\"mm\">unsigned int</code> is 2³² = 4294967296.</li><li>6000000000 − 4294967296 = 1705032704, which is still below the modulus, so one subtraction suffices.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">%u</code> prints that wrapped value.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1705032704</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 6000000000, or assuming the wrap-around is undefined behaviour. Wrapping is guaranteed for unsigned types only; the same computation on a signed <code class=\"mm\">int</code> would indeed be undefined.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q33", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "control-structures", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int i = 0, s = 0;\n    while (i &lt; 6) {\n        i++;\n        if (i % 3 == 0) continue;\n        s += i;\n    }\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, s);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["9", "10", "12", "15"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">continue</code> abandons the rest of the loop body and re-tests the condition; it does not skip the update that already happened.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">i</code> is incremented before the divisibility test, so no iteration is lost.</li><li>Values 1 and 2 are added: <code class=\"mm\">s = 3</code>.</li><li>3 is a multiple of 3, so <code class=\"mm\">continue</code> skips the addition.</li><li>4 and 5 are added: <code class=\"mm\">s = 12</code>; 6 is skipped; the loop then ends.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Putting the increment after the <code class=\"mm\">continue</code> would have made this an infinite loop, so the increment must come first. A second trap is adding 3 and 6 as well, which gives 21, or omitting 5, which gives 10.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q34", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "control-structures", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int i, j, c = 0;\n    for (i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++)\n        for (j = 0; j &lt; 3; j++) {\n            if (j == i) break;\n            c++;\n        }\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, c);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["2", "3", "6", "9"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">break</code> leaves only the innermost enclosing loop or switch, and the outer loop then continues with its next iteration.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>For <code class=\"mm\">i = 0</code> the very first inner test <code class=\"mm\">j == i</code> breaks out, adding nothing.</li><li>For <code class=\"mm\">i = 1</code>: <code class=\"mm\">j = 0</code> counts, then <code class=\"mm\">j = 1</code> breaks — one addition.</li><li>For <code class=\"mm\">i = 2</code>: <code class=\"mm\">j = 0</code> and <code class=\"mm\">j = 1</code> count, then <code class=\"mm\">j = 2</code> breaks — two additions.</li><li>Total 0 + 1 + 2 = 3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming <code class=\"mm\">break</code> escapes both loops, which would give 0, or assuming it only skips one iteration, which would give 6. Note that the inner loop is re-entered with <code class=\"mm\">j</code> reset to 0 each time.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q35", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "control-structures", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int x = 3, r = 0;\n    switch (x) {\n        default: r += 1;\n        case 1:  r += 2; break;\n        case 3:  r += 4;\n    }\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, r);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["1", "3", "4", "7"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A <code class=\"mm\">switch</code> jumps to the matching case label only; <code class=\"mm\">default</code> is selected solely when no case matches, and execution then falls through the labels below the entry point.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">x</code> is 3, so control jumps to <code class=\"mm\">case 3</code>; the <code class=\"mm\">default</code> label above it is skipped.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">r += 4</code> makes <code class=\"mm\">r = 4</code>.</li><li>There is no statement after <code class=\"mm\">case 3</code>, so the switch ends; the fall-through into <code class=\"mm\">case 1</code> never happens because labels are not re-entered backwards.</li><li>The program prints 4.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Two traps. First, believing <code class=\"mm\">default</code> runs before the matching case because it is written first. Second, expecting the fall-through to wrap around to <code class=\"mm\">case 1</code>, which would give 7. Fall-through is always downward.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q36", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "control-structures", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about C loop and switch control are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["The body of a do-while loop executes at least once, even if its condition is false on entry.", "In a for statement all three expressions must be present.", "A break inside a switch terminates only that switch, not an enclosing loop.", "A continue inside a while loop transfers control to the loop condition test."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each loop form fixes where control resumes on <code class=\"mm\">continue</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">break</code> always binds to the nearest enclosing loop or switch.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) A <code class=\"mm\">do-while</code> tests its condition after the body, so the body runs once even when the condition is false. TRUE.</li><li>(B) All three parts may be omitted — <code class=\"mm\">for (;;)</code> is a legal infinite loop, and <code class=\"mm\">for (i = 0; i &lt; n;)</code> is legal too. FALSE.</li><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">break</code> leaves the nearest enclosing loop or switch; inside a switch it does not touch an outer loop. TRUE.</li><li>(D) In a <code class=\"mm\">while</code> loop <code class=\"mm\">continue</code> jumps to the condition test (in a <code class=\"mm\">for</code> loop it jumps to the increment expression instead). TRUE.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (B) is the usual error, and it hides a real hazard: with a <code class=\"mm\">continue</code> inside a <code class=\"mm\">for</code> loop the increment still runs, but inside the equivalent <code class=\"mm\">while</code> loop it does not, which can turn the loop infinite.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q37", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "control-structures", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n = 10, c = 0;\n    do {\n        n -= 3;\n        c++;\n    } while (n &gt; 0);\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, c);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A <code class=\"mm\">do-while</code> checks its condition after each iteration, so the body always runs at least once and the loop stops only when the test fails.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>After iteration 1, <code class=\"mm\">n = 7</code> and <code class=\"mm\">c = 1</code>.</li><li>After iteration 2, <code class=\"mm\">n = 4</code> and <code class=\"mm\">c = 2</code>.</li><li>After iteration 3, <code class=\"mm\">n = 1</code> and <code class=\"mm\">c = 3</code>; 1 &gt; 0, so the loop continues.</li><li>After iteration 4, <code class=\"mm\">n = -2</code> and <code class=\"mm\">c = 4</code>; the test fails and the loop ends.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Stopping at 3 because <code class=\"mm\">n</code> reached 1 and 'looks finished', or running a fifth iteration. The test happens after the subtraction, so the negative value is reached but not iterated again.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q38", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "control-structures", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int c = 0;\n    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 10; i++)\n        for (int j = i; j &lt;= 10; j += 2)\n            c++;\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, c);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "30", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a nested loop whose inner index starts at the outer index and advances with a fixed stride, the inner trip count depends on the parity of <code class=\"mm\">i</code>, not on a single fixed number.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The inner loop visits <code class=\"mm\">j = i, i+2, i+4, …</code> up to 10.</li><li>For odd <code class=\"mm\">i</code> (1, 3, 5, 7, 9) the trip counts are 5, 4, 3, 2, 1 — odd values reach 9 at most.</li><li>For even <code class=\"mm\">i</code> (2, 4, 6, 8, 10) the trip counts are 5, 4, 3, 2, 1 — even values reach 10.</li><li>Sum: (5+4+3+2+1) + (5+4+3+2+1) = 15 + 15 = 30.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 30</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Multiplying 10 by an average of 5 and answering 50, or assuming every outer iteration runs the inner loop five times. The stride of 2 makes the count fall by one every second value of <code class=\"mm\">i</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q39", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "functions-and-parameter-passing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nvoid f(int x) {\n    x = x * 2;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a[3] = {1, 2, 3};\n    f(a[1]);\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, a[1]);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["1", "2", "4", "6"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> C passes every argument by value: the parameter is a fresh object initialised with a copy of the argument, so assigning to it cannot touch the caller.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">a[1]</code> is an <code class=\"mm\">int</code> lvalue holding 2; its value is copied into the parameter <code class=\"mm\">x</code>.</li><li>Inside <code class=\"mm\">f</code>, <code class=\"mm\">x</code> becomes 4, but that is the local copy.</li><li>When <code class=\"mm\">f</code> returns, the copy is destroyed and <code class=\"mm\">a[1]</code> still holds 2.</li><li>The program prints 2.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming an array element is passed by reference because an array name decays to a pointer elsewhere. Only the array name decays; a single element is an ordinary value.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q40", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "functions-and-parameter-passing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint f(int n) {\n    static int c = 0;\n    c += n;\n    return c;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a = f(2);\n    int b = f(3);\n    printf(&quot;%d %d\\n&quot;, a, b);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["2 3", "2 5", "5 5", "0 0"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A <code class=\"mm\">static</code> local has static storage duration: it is created once, initialised once, and keeps its value between calls.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The initialiser <code class=\"mm\">static int c = 0;</code> runs once, before <code class=\"mm\">main</code> begins.</li><li>First call: <code class=\"mm\">c = 0 + 2 = 2</code>, returned and stored in <code class=\"mm\">a</code>.</li><li>Second call: <code class=\"mm\">c = 2 + 3 = 5</code>, returned and stored in <code class=\"mm\">b</code>.</li><li>The two statements are sequenced, so the output is <code class=\"mm\">2 5</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering <code class=\"mm\">2 3</code> by assuming the static variable is re-initialised per call. The related hazard is writing the two calls inside one <code class=\"mm\">printf</code>: the order in which arguments are evaluated is unspecified, so <code class=\"mm\">f(2)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">f(3)</code> could run in either order.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q41", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "functions-and-parameter-passing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nvoid f(int *p) {\n    int q = 100;\n    p = &amp;q;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int x = 5;\n    f(&amp;x);\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, x);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["5", "100", "0", "The behaviour is undefined"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A pointer parameter is itself passed by value: the function receives a copy of the address, so re-pointing that copy cannot redirect the caller's pointer or modify the caller's variable.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">f(&amp;x)</code> copies the address of <code class=\"mm\">x</code> into the parameter <code class=\"mm\">p</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">p = &amp;q</code> overwrites that local copy with the address of the local <code class=\"mm\">q</code>; nothing is written through the pointer.</li><li>No dereference assignment ever happens, so <code class=\"mm\">x</code> keeps the value 5.</li><li>The program prints 5.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a pointer parameter is 'passed by reference' and that <code class=\"mm\">*p = ...</code> was performed. To change the caller's variable you must assign through the pointer (<code class=\"mm\">*p = 100</code>), not re-point it. This is also why returning <code class=\"mm\">p</code> would be wrong: <code class=\"mm\">q</code> dies when <code class=\"mm\">f</code> returns.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q42", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "functions-and-parameter-passing", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about C function declarations and definitions are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["A prototype such as int f(int, double); lets the compiler check the number and types of the arguments at each call.", "A C function can return an array by value.", "A function defined as void g(void) can be called as g().", "A declaration int h(); is a prototype that specifies that h takes no arguments."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A prototype carries parameter type information, which is exactly what separates a prototype from an old-style declaration with an empty parameter list.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) A prototype declares the parameter types, so the compiler can convert arguments and diagnose mismatches. TRUE.</li><li>(B) An array type cannot be returned by value; return a pointer to its first element, or wrap the array in a struct. FALSE.</li><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">void g(void)</code> declares no parameters, and <code class=\"mm\">g()</code> is the correct call. TRUE.</li><li>(D) In C, empty parentheses in a declaration mean the parameters are unspecified, not that there are none — it is not a prototype. FALSE.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (D) is the classic confusion, and it has a real consequence: because <code class=\"mm\">int h();</code> suppresses argument checking, a wrong-argument call may compile silently. Use <code class=\"mm\">(void)</code> to mean 'no arguments'.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q43", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "functions-and-parameter-passing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint g(int a, int b) { return a &gt; b ? a : b; }\nint h(int a, int b, int c) { return g(g(a, b), c); }\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, h(3, 9, 5));\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["3", "9", "5", "15"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A function call used as an argument is evaluated first, and its returned value becomes the argument — here <code class=\"mm\">g</code> is composed with itself to take a maximum of three values.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The inner call <code class=\"mm\">g(3, 9)</code> returns 9, since 3 &gt; 9 is false and the conditional yields <code class=\"mm\">b</code>.</li><li>The outer call becomes <code class=\"mm\">g(9, 5)</code>, which returns 9.</li><li>So <code class=\"mm\">h(3, 9, 5)</code> returns 9 and the program prints 9.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the conditional backwards and taking the minimum, which gives 3. Remember <code class=\"mm\">a &gt; b ? a : b</code> returns <code class=\"mm\">a</code> when the test is true.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q44", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "functions-and-parameter-passing", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about swapping two <code class=\"mm\">int</code> variables through a function are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["A function whose parameters are declared int a, int b cannot change the caller's variables.", "With void swap(int *a, int *b) { int t = *a; *a = *b; *b = t; }, the call swap(&x, &y) exchanges x and y.", "With void swap(int *a, int *b) { int *t = a; a = b; b = t; }, the call swap(&x, &y) exchanges x and y.", "For the call swap(&x, &y) to compile, x and y must be declared static."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Swapping through a function requires writing through pointers to the caller's objects; rearranging the pointer copies themselves has no effect on the pointees.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) Value parameters are copies, so the caller's variables are untouched. TRUE.</li><li>(B) The body dereferences: <code class=\"mm\">t</code> saves the value of <code class=\"mm\">x</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">*a = *b</code> and <code class=\"mm\">*b = t</code> write both pointees. TRUE.</li><li>(C) This version copies the two pointers and swaps the copies; no dereference assignment happens, so <code class=\"mm\">x</code> and <code class=\"mm\">y</code> are unchanged. FALSE.</li><li>(D) Any object with an address can have it taken — <code class=\"mm\">static</code> is irrelevant to passing a pointer. FALSE.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (C) is the trap: it is symmetric with the correct version but swaps the wrong level of indirection. Students who remember 'swapping needs pointers' often write the pointer-swapping variant by mistake.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q45", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "recursion-stack-execution", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint c = 0;\n\nint f(int n) {\n    c++;\n    if (n == 0) return 0;\n    return f(n - 1);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    f(4);\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, c);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["4", "5", "6", "1"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The call count of a linear recursion is the number of distinct arguments, and the base-case call is a call like any other.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">f</code> is invoked with 4, then 3, 2, 1 and finally 0.</li><li>Each invocation increments the global <code class=\"mm\">c</code> before testing the base case.</li><li>The call with <code class=\"mm\">n = 0</code> also increments <code class=\"mm\">c</code> before returning.</li><li>So <code class=\"mm\">c = 5</code> when control returns to <code class=\"mm\">main</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 4 by counting only the non-base calls. If the increment had been placed after the base-case test, the answer would indeed be 4 — the position of the statement decides it.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q46", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "recursion-stack-execution", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nvoid f(int n) {\n    if (n == 0) return;\n    printf(&quot;%d&quot;, n % 10);\n    f(n / 10);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    f(407);\n    printf(&quot;\\n&quot;);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["407", "704", "74", "47"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Work done before the recursive call happens on the way down the stack, so the digits appear in the reverse of the order in which they are peeled off.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">f(407)</code> prints <code class=\"mm\">407 % 10 = 7</code>, then calls <code class=\"mm\">f(40)</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">f(40)</code> prints 0, then calls <code class=\"mm\">f(4)</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">f(4)</code> prints 4, then calls <code class=\"mm\">f(0)</code>, which returns immediately.</li><li>The digits appear as 7, 0, 4 — the output is <code class=\"mm\">704</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 407 by assuming the digits are printed most-significant first. To get 407 the recursive call must come before the <code class=\"mm\">printf</code>, so that printing happens while the stack unwinds.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q47", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "recursion-stack-execution", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about recursion and the run-time stack in C are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["Each active call of a function has its own copy of its automatic local variables.", "A static local variable inside a recursive function is re-initialised on every call.", "The C standard fixes a maximum recursion depth.", "A recursive function with no base case still compiles and links."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Automatic storage is per activation, static storage is per program, and the depth limit is an implementation property of the stack rather than a language rule.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) Each activation record holds fresh automatic locals, which is why one call cannot see another's <code class=\"mm\">n</code>. TRUE.</li><li>(B) A static local is initialised once, before the program starts, and shared by all activations. FALSE.</li><li>(C) No limit is specified; exceeding the available stack causes a crash, but the number is not fixed by the standard. FALSE.</li><li>(D) A missing base case is not a compile error — the function compiles, links and then fails to terminate. TRUE.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (C) is often marked true on the assumption that 'the standard must limit recursion somewhere'. It does not; that is why the same program can run on one machine and overflow on another with a smaller stack.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q48", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "recursion-stack-execution", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint f(int n) {\n    if (n == 0) return 0;\n    if (n % 2) return n + f(n - 1);\n    return f(n - 1);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, f(7));\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["9", "12", "16", "28"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The recurrence has two branches: an odd argument contributes its own value plus the result on <code class=\"mm\">n − 1</code>, an even argument contributes only the result on <code class=\"mm\">n − 1</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">f(7) = 7 + f(6)</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">f(6) = f(5)</code> because 6 is even.</li><li>So <code class=\"mm\">f(5) = 5 + f(4)</code> with <code class=\"mm\">f(4) = f(3)</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">f(3) = 3 + f(2)</code> with <code class=\"mm\">f(2) = f(1)</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">f(1) = 1 + f(0) = 1</code>, giving <code class=\"mm\">f(2) = 1</code>, <code class=\"mm\">f(3) = 4</code>, <code class=\"mm\">f(5) = 9</code>, <code class=\"mm\">f(7) = 16</code>.</li><li>In effect the sum is 7 + 5 + 3 + 1 = 16.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 28 by summing 7 + 6 + … + 1, which ignores the even branch, or 12 by dropping one term. The even case adds nothing at all, so only the odd arguments survive.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q49", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "recursion-stack-execution", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint f(int n) {\n    if (n == 0) return 0;\n    return (n % 2) + f(n / 2);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, f(100));\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["2", "3", "4", "7"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Dividing by 2 strips one binary digit per call, and <code class=\"mm\">n % 2</code> is the digit removed, so the recursion counts the 1-bits.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>100 in binary is 1100100, which has three 1-bits.</li><li>The first call adds 100 % 2 = 0 and recurses on 50.</li><li>The chain continues 100 → 50 → 25 → 12 → 6 → 3 → 1 → 0, adding the low bit at each step.</li><li>The bits added are 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1, 1 — a total of 3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 7 by counting the number of bits of 100 instead of the number of 1-bits, or 2 by miscounting the binary expansion. Writing 100 = 64 + 32 + 4 makes the three set bits visible immediately.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q50", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "recursion-stack-execution", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">During the execution of this program, what is the maximum number of activation records of <code class=\"mm\">f</code> that are alive at the same time?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint f(int n) {\n    if (n &lt;= 1) return 1;\n    return f(n / 2) + f(n / 2);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    f(16);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "5", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The number of live frames is the depth of the recursion, not the number of calls: the second recursive call starts only after the first has returned and its frame has been destroyed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The chain of nested calls is <code class=\"mm\">f(16) → f(8) → f(4) → f(2) → f(1)</code>.</li><li>Each of those five calls is still on the stack when <code class=\"mm\">f(1)</code> is entered, so the depth is 5.</li><li>The second <code class=\"mm\">f(n / 2)</code> in each frame runs after the first has returned, reusing that depth rather than adding to it.</li><li>The total number of calls is 31, but the maximum number of live frames is 5.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 5</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 31 by counting calls instead of depth, or 4 by forgetting that the base-case call <code class=\"mm\">f(1)</code> is itself a live frame. Depth is the longest root-to-leaf chain, not the size of the tree.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2-Q01", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointer-arithmetic", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Compiled with a C99 compiler on a machine where <code class=\"mm\">int</code> is 32 bits, what does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">int a[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};\nint *p = a + 2;\nprintf(&quot;%d&quot;, *(p - 1) + p[1]);</div>", "options": ["50", "60", "70", "40"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Pointer arithmetic is scaled by the size of the pointed-to type, and the subscript <code class=\"mm\">p[k]</code> is defined as <code class=\"mm\">*(p + k)</code>. Both forms index relative to <code class=\"mm\">p</code>, not to the start of the array.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">p = a + 2</code> points at <code class=\"mm\">a[2]</code>, whose value is 30.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">*(p - 1)</code> = <code class=\"mm\">*(a + 1)</code> = <code class=\"mm\">a[1]</code> = 20.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">p[1]</code> = <code class=\"mm\">*(p + 1)</code> = <code class=\"mm\">*(a + 3)</code> = <code class=\"mm\">a[3]</code> = 40.</li><li>20 + 40 = 60.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">60</code> (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading <code class=\"mm\">p[1]</code> as <code class=\"mm\">a[1]</code>. A subscript applied to a pointer is always relative to where that pointer currently points, so <code class=\"mm\">p[1]</code> is the element after <code class=\"mm\">*p</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <code class=\"mm\">p[k]</code> &#8801; <code class=\"mm\">*(p + k)</code> &#8801; <code class=\"mm\">k[p]</code>; the base of the offset is always <code class=\"mm\">p</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2-Q02", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointer-arithmetic", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">On a target where <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(int) == 4</code> and <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(struct node) == 8</code> for <code class=\"mm\">struct node { int key; char tag; };</code>, consider the fragment below. What integer does it print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">struct node t[10];\nstruct node *p = &amp;t[2];\nprintf(&quot;%ld&quot;, (char *)(p + 3) - (char *)p);</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "24", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">p + 3</code> advances by three <i>elements</i> of type <code class=\"mm\">struct node</code>, so the byte distance is 3 &#215; <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(struct node)</code>. Casting both pointers to <code class=\"mm\">char *</code> before subtracting makes the subtraction count bytes, because <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(char) == 1</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">p = &t[2]</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">p + 3</code> = <code class=\"mm\">&t[5]</code>.</li><li>The element difference is 3; the byte difference is 3 &#215; 8 = 24.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(char *)(p + 3) - (char *)p</code> = 24.</li><li>Without the casts the same expression would print 3, because pointer subtraction on typed pointers divides by the element size.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">24</code></span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 3 (the element count) or 12 (assuming a 4-byte struct from the <code class=\"mm\">int</code> member alone and forgetting the <code class=\"mm\">char</code> member and padding).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Typed pointer subtraction counts elements; cast to <code class=\"mm\">char *</code> when the byte count is what the question asks for.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2-Q03", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointer-arithmetic", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">For <code class=\"mm\">int a[10];</code> and an index <code class=\"mm\">i</code> with <code class=\"mm\">0 &#8804; i &lt; 10</code>, which of the following expressions is <b>NOT</b> equivalent to <code class=\"mm\">a[i]</code>?</p>", "options": ["*(a + i)", "*(i + a)", "i[a]", "*(a + i * sizeof(int))"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The C standard defines <code class=\"mm\">a[i]</code> as <code class=\"mm\">*(a + i)</code>. Pointer addition is commutative, so <code class=\"mm\">*(i + a)</code> and, by the same definition, <code class=\"mm\">i[a]</code> are the identical expression.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">a</code> decays to <code class=\"mm\">int *</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">a + i</code> advances <code class=\"mm\">i</code> elements.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">a + i * sizeof(int)</code> advances <code class=\"mm\">i * 4</code> elements, since the scale factor 4 is applied again inside pointer arithmetic.</li><li>For <code class=\"mm\">i = 1</code> that expression is <code class=\"mm\">a[4]</code>, not <code class=\"mm\">a[1]</code>.</li><li>Options A, B and C all evaluate to the address of the same element as <code class=\"mm\">a[i]</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">*(a + i * sizeof(int))</code> (option D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Carrying byte arithmetic from assembly or from <code class=\"mm\">char *</code> code into typed pointer arithmetic, where the compiler already multiplies by the element size.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> In C, <code class=\"mm\">pointer + integer</code> is already measured in elements.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2-Q04", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointer-arithmetic", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about pointer arithmetic in C are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["Adding 1 to a pointer of type int * advances it by sizeof(int) bytes.", "Subtracting two pointers that point into two different arrays is defined by the C standard.", "For an array a of n elements, the value a + n may be formed and compared, but must not be dereferenced.", "If p and q point into the same array, p - q has type int."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Pointer arithmetic is defined only within one array object (plus its one-past-the-end address); the scale factor is the size of the pointed-to type, and the type of a pointer difference is <code class=\"mm\">ptrdiff_t</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) TRUE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">p + 1</code> is the next object of the pointed-to type, which is <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(int)</code> bytes further on.</li><li><b>(B) FALSE.</b> Subtracting pointers that refer to different array objects is undefined behaviour; only pointers into the same array (or one past its end) may be subtracted.</li><li><b>(C) TRUE.</b> One-past-the-end is a valid pointer value that may be formed, compared and subtracted, but dereferencing it is undefined behaviour.</li><li><b>(D) FALSE.</b> The result type is <code class=\"mm\">ptrdiff_t</code>, a signed integer type that may be wider than <code class=\"mm\">int</code>; it is not <code class=\"mm\">int</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: (A) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating <code class=\"mm\">p - q</code> as an <code class=\"mm\">int</code> because it \"looks like\" an integer; and assuming any two pointers may be subtracted, which is only guaranteed inside one array.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Pointer arithmetic lives inside one array object; one past the end is addressable but not readable.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2-Q05", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointer-arithmetic", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">For a two-dimensional array declared as <code class=\"mm\">int a[3][4];</code>, what is the type of the expression <code class=\"mm\">a + 1</code>?</p>", "options": ["int *", "int **", "int (*)[4]", "int [4]"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An array name decays one level only: <code class=\"mm\">int[3][4]</code> decays to a pointer to its first element, and that element is a row of type <code class=\"mm\">int[4]</code>. Hence the decayed type is \"pointer to array of 4 int\", written <code class=\"mm\">int (*)[4]</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The element type of <code class=\"mm\">a</code> is <code class=\"mm\">int[4]</code>.</li><li>So <code class=\"mm\">a</code> decays to <code class=\"mm\">int (*)[4]</code> and <code class=\"mm\">a + 1</code> has that same type.</li><li>Adding 1 moves by one whole row: with 4-byte <code class=\"mm\">int</code>, <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(*(a + 1))</code> evaluates to 16, which confirms the pointed-to type is <code class=\"mm\">int[4]</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">int (*)[4]</code> (option C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the decay goes all the way to <code class=\"mm\">int **</code>. That only happens for an array of pointers, such as <code class=\"mm\">int *a[3]</code>, never for a true two-dimensional array.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> An n-dimensional array decays to a pointer to an (n&#8722;1)-dimensional array, and only one level is lost.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2-Q06", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointer-arithmetic", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">int a[4] = {1, 2, 3, 4};\nint *p = a;\nint **pp = &amp;p;\n*pp += 2;\n*(*pp + 1) += 10;\n(*pp)--;\nprintf(&quot;%d %d %d&quot;, *p, a[2], a[3]);</div>", "options": ["1 3 14", "2 3 14", "2 14 14", "3 3 14"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">pp</code> is a pointer to the pointer variable <code class=\"mm\">p</code>. Writing <code class=\"mm\">*pp</code> designates the object <code class=\"mm\">p</code> itself, so <code class=\"mm\">*pp += 2</code> moves the pointer, whereas <code class=\"mm\">**pp += 2</code> would have changed <code class=\"mm\">a[0]</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">*pp += 2</code> sets <code class=\"mm\">p = a + 2</code>; the array is untouched.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">*pp + 1</code> is <code class=\"mm\">p + 1</code> = <code class=\"mm\">&a[3]</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">*(*pp + 1) += 10</code> makes <code class=\"mm\">a[3] = 4 + 10 = 14</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(*pp)--</code> decrements the pointer: <code class=\"mm\">p = a + 1</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">*p = a[1] = 2</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">a[2]</code> was never written, so it is still 3. Output: <code class=\"mm\">2 3 14</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">2 3 14</code> (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading <code class=\"mm\">*pp += 2</code> as an increment of the pointed-to value. The left operand of <code class=\"mm\">+=</code> here is the pointer object <code class=\"mm\">p</code> reached through <code class=\"mm\">pp</code>, not the element it addresses.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Count the stars: one star reaches the pointer, two stars reach the element.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2-Q07", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointer-arithmetic", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What is printed by the following fragment?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">int a[6] = {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5};\nint *p = a + 5;\nwhile (p &gt; a) printf(&quot;%d&quot;, *--p);</div>", "options": ["54321", "4321", "43210", "543210"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">*--p</code> is a pre-decrement: <code class=\"mm\">p</code> is moved first and the new target is then dereferenced. <code class=\"mm\">*p--</code> would dereference first and move afterwards.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Initially <code class=\"mm\">p = a + 5</code>, pointing at 5; the test <code class=\"mm\">p > a</code> is true.</li><li>First iteration: <code class=\"mm\">p</code> becomes <code class=\"mm\">a + 4</code> and 4 is printed.</li><li>The loop repeats for 3, 2, 1 and 0.</li><li>When <code class=\"mm\">p</code> reaches <code class=\"mm\">a</code> the test fails, so 5 is never printed and the last character printed is 0. Output: <code class=\"mm\">43210</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">43210</code> (option C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the starting element is printed, which is what <code class=\"mm\">*p--</code> would do, giving <code class=\"mm\">54321</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The loop bound <code class=\"mm\">p > a</code> guards against reading <code class=\"mm\">a[-1]</code> when the decrement form is used.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2-Q08", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointers-to-functions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following declarations declares <code class=\"mm\">f</code> as a pointer to a function that takes two <code class=\"mm\">int</code> arguments and returns <code class=\"mm\">int</code>?</p>", "options": ["int (*f)(int, int);", "int *f(int, int);", "int *(*f)(int, int);", "int (*f[2])(int, int);"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The postfix function-call operator binds tighter than the prefix <code class=\"mm\">*</code>. Parentheses around <code class=\"mm\">*f</code> are therefore what make <code class=\"mm\">f</code> a pointer; without them <code class=\"mm\">f</code> is a function.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A)</b> <code class=\"mm\">int (*f)(int, int)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">f</code> is a pointer to a function taking two <code class=\"mm\">int</code> and returning <code class=\"mm\">int</code>. This is the required type.</li><li><b>(B)</b> <code class=\"mm\">int *f(int, int)</code>: a function taking two <code class=\"mm\">int</code> and returning <code class=\"mm\">int *</code>.</li><li><b>(C)</b> <code class=\"mm\">int *(*f)(int, int)</code>: a pointer to a function returning <code class=\"mm\">int *</code>.</li><li><b>(D)</b> <code class=\"mm\">int (*f[2])(int, int)</code>: an array of two function pointers.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">int (*f)(int, int);</code> (option A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Dropping the parentheses, which silently turns a pointer into a function declaration returning a pointer.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Read declarations inside-out, starting from the identifier: <code class=\"mm\">(*f)</code> first, then the parameter list, then the return type.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2-Q09", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointers-to-functions", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about pointers to functions in C are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["A function name used in an expression, other than as the operand of sizeof or of unary &amp;, converts to a pointer to that function.", "Two function pointers may be compared with == and !=, but the relational operators &lt; and &gt; are not defined for them.", "A function pointer must be dereferenced with * before a call: (*fp)() is valid but fp() is a constraint violation.", "A function pointer can be converted to void * and back without loss on every conforming implementation."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Function designators decay to pointers to the function; a function pointer supports assignment, equality comparison and a call, but the standard does not place function pointers in the same representation class as object pointers.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) TRUE.</b> Apart from <code class=\"mm\">sizeof</code> and unary <code class=\"mm\">&amp;</code>, a function designator converts to \"pointer to function returning ...\" &#8212; this is why <code class=\"mm\">g(f)</code> passes the address of <code class=\"mm\">f</code>.</li><li><b>(B) TRUE.</b> Equality and inequality are defined for pointers to functions; ordering comparisons of function pointers are undefined behaviour.</li><li><b>(C) FALSE.</b> The <code class=\"mm\">&amp;</code> and <code class=\"mm\">*</code> operators are optional around a function pointer, so <code class=\"mm\">fp()</code>, <code class=\"mm\">(*fp)()</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(**fp)()</code> all call the same function.</li><li><b>(D) FALSE.</b> Converting a function pointer to <code class=\"mm\">void *</code> is not guaranteed by the standard; some architectures keep code and data in separate address spaces.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: (A) and (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that the explicit <code class=\"mm\">*</code> is required for a call, or that <code class=\"mm\">void *</code> is a universal pointer type. Neither is true in standard C.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Function pointers compare for equality only, and call through either form of the expression.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2-Q10", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointers-to-functions", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What integer does the following program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }\nint sub(int a, int b) { return a - b; }\nint mul(int a, int b) { return a * b; }\n\nint main(void) {\n    int (*ops[3])(int, int) = {add, sub, mul};\n    int r = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) r += ops[i](7, 3);\n    printf(&quot;%d&quot;, r);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "35", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An array of function pointers is indexed and then called exactly like an array of objects: <code class=\"mm\">ops[i]</code> is a function pointer and <code class=\"mm\">ops[i](7, 3)</code> is a call through it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">ops[0] = add</code> &#8594; 7 + 3 = 10.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">ops[1] = sub</code> &#8594; 7 &#8722; 3 = 4.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">ops[2] = mul</code> &#8594; 7 &#215; 3 = 21.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">r = 10 + 4 + 21 = 35</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">35</code></span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Writing <code class=\"mm\">ops[i](7, 3)</code> as <code class=\"mm\">*ops[i](7, 3)</code> or assuming the subscript selects the argument rather than the callee.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A dispatch table turns a chain of <code class=\"mm\">switch</code> cases into one indexed call, which is the standard way to implement callbacks.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2-Q11", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointers-to-functions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">typedef int (*Op)(int, int);\nint add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }\nint sub(int a, int b) { return a - b; }\nOp table[2] = {add, sub};\n\nint apply(Op f, int a, int b) { return f(a, b); }\n\nint main(void) {\n    int r = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 2; i++)\n        r += apply(table[i], table[i](10, 4), 2);\n    printf(&quot;%d&quot;, r);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["20", "18", "22", "16"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">apply</code> receives a function pointer and calls it on the two values it is given. Its first argument is evaluated by a call through the same table entry, so the table entry is used twice per iteration.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">i = 0</code>: <code class=\"mm\">table[0](10, 4) = add(10, 4) = 14</code>. Then <code class=\"mm\">apply(add, 14, 2) = 14 + 2 = 16</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">i = 1</code>: <code class=\"mm\">table[1](10, 4) = sub(10, 4) = 6</code>. Then <code class=\"mm\">apply(sub, 6, 2) = 6 &#8722; 2 = 4</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">r = 16 + 4 = 20</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">20</code> (option A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Applying the second operand 2 as a third argument, or using <code class=\"mm\">table[i](10, 4)</code> as the second argument of the same call, which reverses the nesting and gives 18.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A <code class=\"mm\">typedef</code> for a function pointer type makes higher-order signatures readable and keeps argument order obvious.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2-Q12", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointers-to-functions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A function <code class=\"mm\">int f(int);</code> and a function <code class=\"mm\">void g(int (*fn)(int));</code> are both in scope. Which of the following calls correctly passes <code class=\"mm\">f</code> to <code class=\"mm\">g</code>?</p>", "options": ["g(f());", "g(&amp;f());", "g(*f());", "g(f);"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A function designator converts to a pointer to the function, so the bare name <code class=\"mm\">f</code> already has the required type <code class=\"mm\">int (*)(int)</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">f()</code> <i>calls</i> <code class=\"mm\">f</code> and yields an <code class=\"mm\">int</code>, which is not a function pointer; the argument type is wrong.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">&amp;f()</code> takes the address of the <code class=\"mm\">int</code> returned by the call. That value is not an lvalue, so the <code class=\"mm\">&amp;</code> operator is not applicable.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">*f()</code> dereferences that <code class=\"mm\">int</code>, which is also invalid.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">g(f)</code> passes the converted function pointer. Writing <code class=\"mm\">g(&amp;f)</code> would be equally correct.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">g(f);</code> (option D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding a call&#8217;s parentheses out of habit, which turns the function pointer into the function&#8217;s return value.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Name the function without parentheses to take its address; add parentheses only to call it.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2-Q13", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointers-to-functions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following declarations makes <code class=\"mm\">F</code> an alias for a pointer to a function that takes one <code class=\"mm\">int</code> and returns a <code class=\"mm\">char</code>?</p>", "options": ["typedef char *F(int);", "typedef char **F(int);", "typedef char (*F[2])(int);", "typedef char (*F)(int);"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a <code class=\"mm\">typedef</code> the declarator is read the same way as in an object declaration: the parenthesised <code class=\"mm\">(*F)</code> is what makes <code class=\"mm\">F</code> a pointer, and the return type is whatever precedes the declarator.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A)</b> <code class=\"mm\">typedef char *F(int)</code> names a <i>function type</i> taking <code class=\"mm\">int</code> and returning <code class=\"mm\">char *</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">F</code> is not a pointer.</li><li><b>(B)</b> same shape, returning <code class=\"mm\">char **</code>.</li><li><b>(C)</b> <code class=\"mm\">F</code> is an array of two pointers to functions returning <code class=\"mm\">char</code>.</li><li><b>(D)</b> <code class=\"mm\">typedef char (*F)(int)</code> names the type \"pointer to function taking <code class=\"mm\">int</code> returning <code class=\"mm\">char</code>\", so <code class=\"mm\">F t = some_function;</code> is a valid initialisation.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">typedef char (*F)(int);</code> (option D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading <code class=\"mm\">char *F(int)</code> as \"pointer to function\". In a typedef the <code class=\"mm\">*</code> belongs to the return type unless it is parenthesised with the name.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The <code class=\"mm\">typedef</code> name sits exactly where a variable name would; write the declaration for a variable first, then replace the name.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2-Q14", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "dynamic-memory-allocation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about the C dynamic allocation functions is TRUE?</p>", "options": ["malloc zero-fills the block it returns, while calloc leaves the bytes indeterminate.", "calloc takes two arguments, multiplies them, and zero-fills the block; malloc takes one size in bytes and leaves the contents indeterminate.", "realloc never moves the block, so the returned pointer always equals the pointer passed in.", "free(p) sets the pointer variable p itself to NULL."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">malloc(n)</code> requests <code class=\"mm\">n</code> bytes with indeterminate contents; <code class=\"mm\">calloc(n, m)</code> requests <code class=\"mm\">n * m</code> bytes and zero-fills them; <code class=\"mm\">realloc</code> may move the block; <code class=\"mm\">free</code> takes the pointer by value.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) FALSE.</b> The behaviours are the other way round: <code class=\"mm\">malloc</code> does not initialise, <code class=\"mm\">calloc</code> does.</li><li><b>(B) TRUE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">calloc(nmemb, size)</code> computes <code class=\"mm\">nmemb * size</code> and zeroes the result.</li><li><b>(C) FALSE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">realloc</code> commonly moves the block when the new size does not fit in place, which is why its return value must be used.</li><li><b>(D) FALSE.</b> The argument is passed by value, so the caller's variable keeps its old value; that stale value is a dangling pointer.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting <code class=\"mm\">malloc</code> to hand back zeroed memory because it often does on a freshly started process.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Use <code class=\"mm\">calloc</code> when zeroes are needed; never assume <code class=\"mm\">malloc</code> gives them.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2-Q15", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "dynamic-memory-allocation", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">On a target where <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(struct rec) == 16</code> for <code class=\"mm\">struct rec { int id; double w; };</code>, a program executes the statement below. How many bytes does the call request in total?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">struct rec *r = calloc(6, sizeof(struct rec));</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "96", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">calloc(nmemb, size)</code> allocates space for an array of <code class=\"mm\">nmemb</code> objects, each of <code class=\"mm\">size</code> bytes, and zero-fills the whole region.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The object size is given as 16 bytes.</li><li>The element count is 6.</li><li>Total = 6 &#215; 16 = 96 bytes.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">calloc</code> returns a pointer to 96 zero bytes, or <code class=\"mm\">NULL</code> on failure.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">96</code></span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Passing the element size as the count, as in <code class=\"mm\">calloc(sizeof(struct rec), 6)</code>. The product is the same, but the intent and the type of each argument are reversed, which hides bugs when the values differ.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <code class=\"mm\">calloc</code> multiplies its two arguments; the byte count is never written out by hand.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2-Q16", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "dynamic-memory-allocation", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about dynamic storage management in C are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["Calling free(p) where p was not returned by an allocation function is undefined behaviour.", "After free(p) the value of p is indeterminate and using it is undefined behaviour.", "In C99, realloc(p, 0) is guaranteed to free the block and return NULL.", "malloc(0) may return either NULL or a unique pointer that must not be dereferenced but may be passed to free."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The allocation functions own the heap blocks; a pointer that has been freed or never allocated carries no defined behaviour when used, and the zero-size cases are left implementation-defined by the standard.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) TRUE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">free</code> requires an argument that is a null pointer or a pointer returned by <code class=\"mm\">malloc</code>, <code class=\"mm\">calloc</code> or <code class=\"mm\">realloc</code>; anything else is undefined behaviour.</li><li><b>(B) TRUE.</b> The pointer object still exists, but its value is indeterminate after the free, so any use is undefined behaviour &#8212; this is the dangling-pointer case.</li><li><b>(C) FALSE.</b> C99 makes <code class=\"mm\">realloc(p, 0)</code> implementation-defined: the call either returns <code class=\"mm\">NULL</code> without freeing, or behaves as <code class=\"mm\">realloc(p, 1)</code>. It is not guaranteed to free, which is why <code class=\"mm\">free(p); p = NULL;</code> is preferred.</li><li><b>(D) TRUE.</b> For a zero size the call may return <code class=\"mm\">NULL</code> or a unique pointer to no object; if a pointer is returned it must be freed and must not be dereferenced.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that <code class=\"mm\">realloc(p, 0)</code> is a portable way to free memory. It is not; call <code class=\"mm\">free</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Free exactly what was allocated, exactly once, and set the handle to <code class=\"mm\">NULL</code> afterwards.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2-Q17", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "dynamic-memory-allocation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A buffer allocated with <code class=\"mm\">malloc</code> is grown by the statement below. If the reallocation fails, what is the defect?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">p = realloc(p, 100 * sizeof *p);</div>", "options": ["realloc returns NULL and p is overwritten, so the original block is leaked and its address is lost.", "There is none: realloc leaves p unchanged when it fails, so the old block is still reachable.", "It is a constraint violation that a conforming compiler must diagnose.", "It leaks memory even when the reallocation succeeds."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">realloc</code> may move the block, so it returns the new address. On failure it returns <code class=\"mm\">NULL</code> and leaves the original block allocated and unchanged. Assigning the result straight back to <code class=\"mm\">p</code> therefore destroys the only handle to that block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Success: the new address is returned and the old block is released. Assigning to <code class=\"mm\">p</code> is then correct.</li><li>Failure: <code class=\"mm\">NULL</code> is returned, and the old block is still allocated.</li><li>Writing <code class=\"mm\">p = realloc(p, n)</code> stores <code class=\"mm\">NULL</code> into <code class=\"mm\">p</code>, so the old address is lost and the block can never be freed &#8212; a leak.</li><li>The safe pattern keeps the result in a temporary: <code class=\"mm\">q = realloc(p, n); if (q) p = q;</code></li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that a failed <code class=\"mm\">realloc</code> frees the old block. It does not; the caller still owns it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Never overwrite the only pointer to a block with a value that may be <code class=\"mm\">NULL</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2-Q18", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "dynamic-memory-allocation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Consider the function below. Which statement correctly describes the pointer it returns?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">int *make(void) {\n    int x = 42;\n    return &amp;x;\n}</div>", "options": ["It is valid: the lifetime of x extends to the end of the program because the pointer escapes the function.", "It is a compile-time error to return the address of an automatic variable, so the program cannot be built.", "It is undefined behaviour: the pointer refers to an object whose lifetime ended when make returned.", "It is valid, but the value read through the pointer is merely unspecified."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">x</code> has automatic storage duration: its lifetime begins on entry to the block and ends when the block terminates. A pointer to it after that point is dangling.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The object <code class=\"mm\">x</code> ceases to exist when <code class=\"mm\">make</code> returns.</li><li>The returned address therefore refers to no object; the C standard makes any use of it undefined behaviour.</li><li>Compilers usually diagnose it (<code class=\"mm\">-Wreturn-local-addr</code>), but it is not a constraint violation, so a conforming implementation may accept the program.</li><li>Undefined behaviour is stronger than \"unspecified value\": the program has no defined meaning at all, and the value may even appear to be 42 on some builds.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Calling the result \"unspecified\" or \"garbage but harmless\". Reading through a dangling pointer is undefined behaviour, not merely an unpredictable value.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Return a pointer to a <code class=\"mm\">static</code> object, to a caller-supplied buffer, or to a heap block &#8212; never to an automatic local.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2-Q19", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "dynamic-memory-allocation", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">How many bytes are leaked when the following function returns?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">void f(void) {\n    char *p = malloc(20);\n    char *q = malloc(30);\n    p = q;\n    free(p);\n}</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "20", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A block is reachable only through the pointers that hold its address. Overwriting the last such pointer loses the block permanently, and that is what \"leaked\" means.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">malloc(20)</code> returns block A and stores its address in <code class=\"mm\">p</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">malloc(30)</code> returns block B and stores its address in <code class=\"mm\">q</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">p = q</code> overwrites the only handle to block A. Block A is still allocated but no longer reachable.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">free(p)</code> frees block B, which is correct. Block A is never freed, so 20 bytes leak.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">20</code></span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 30, the size of the block that was freed, or 50, the total requested. A leak is measured by the block that became unreachable.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Free in the reverse order of allocation, and null a handle as soon as the block behind it is released.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2-Q20", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "macro-expansion-scope-lifetime", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does the following program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#define SQR(x) x*x\n#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\nint main(void) {\n    printf(&quot;%d&quot;, SQR(2 + 3));\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["11", "25", "13", "10"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The preprocessor performs textual substitution. <code class=\"mm\">SQR(x) x*x</code> replaces the parameter by the argument tokens exactly as written, with no parentheses added.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">SQR(2 + 3)</code> expands to <code class=\"mm\">2 + 3*2 + 3</code>.</li><li>Multiplication binds tighter than addition, so the expression is 2 + 6 + 3.</li><li>The value is 11, and it is printed as <code class=\"mm\">11</code>.</li><li>Defining <code class=\"mm\">#define SQR(x) ((x)*(x))</code> would give <code class=\"mm\">((2+3)*(2+3))</code> = 25.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">11</code> (option A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating the macro as a function call and answering 25. A macro has no parameter objects; it has tokens.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Parenthesise every parameter and the whole body: <code class=\"mm\">#define SQR(x) ((x)*(x))</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2-Q21", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "macro-expansion-scope-lifetime", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about the C preprocessor are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["A macro name is replaced by the preprocessor before compilation, so the arguments of a function-like macro are not type-checked.", "#define MAX(a,b) ((a)&gt;(b)?(a):(b)) evaluates each of its arguments exactly once.", "A macro may be redefined without error provided the new definition is identical to the previous one.", "#undef removes a macro definition, after which the identifier may be redefined."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Macro expansion happens in translation phase 4, before any type information exists, and it is pure token substitution.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) TRUE.</b> The preprocessor knows nothing about types; the expanded text is type-checked only afterwards, as ordinary code.</li><li><b>(B) FALSE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">MAX(i++, j)</code> expands to <code class=\"mm\">((i++)>(j)?(i++):(j))</code>, so the chosen argument is evaluated twice. This is the classic reason to prefer an inline function.</li><li><b>(C) TRUE.</b> An identical redefinition is permitted; a different one must be diagnosed.</li><li><b>(D) TRUE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">#undef</code> ends the definition, and the name may then be defined again, possibly with a different body.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a macro behaves like a function with arguments evaluated once, which silently breaks expressions with side effects.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Never pass an expression with a side effect to a macro parameter.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2-Q22", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "macro-expansion-scope-lifetime", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\nvoid f(void) { static int n; n++; printf(&quot;%d &quot;, n); }\nint main(void) { f(); f(); f(); return 0; }</div>", "options": ["1 1 1", "1 2 3", "0 1 2", "3 3 3"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">static</code> on a block-scope variable changes its storage duration, not its scope. The object has static storage duration: it is created and zero-initialised once, before the program starts, and lives for the whole run.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">n</code> is zero-initialised a single time.</li><li>First call: <code class=\"mm\">n</code> becomes 1, prints <code class=\"mm\">1</code>.</li><li>Second call: the same object is incremented to 2.</li><li>Third call: 3. Output <code class=\"mm\">1 2 3 </code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">1 2 3</code> (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting <code class=\"mm\">0 1 2</code> by treating the initialiser as running on every call, or <code class=\"mm\">1 1 1</code> by forgetting that <code class=\"mm\">static</code> gives the object a lifetime beyond the call.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <code class=\"mm\">static</code> at block scope = one object, one initialisation, program lifetime, still invisible outside the block.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2-Q23", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "macro-expansion-scope-lifetime", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What integer does the following program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#define SQ(x) x * x\n#define CUBE(x) SQ(x) * x\n#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\nint main(void) {\n    printf(&quot;%d&quot;, CUBE(1 + 2));\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "7", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Macro arguments are substituted as raw token sequences, and the result is then rescanned for further macros. With no parentheses anywhere, the surrounding operators re-associate the whole expression.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">CUBE(1 + 2)</code> &#8594; <code class=\"mm\">SQ(1 + 2) * 1 + 2</code>.</li><li>The rescan expands <code class=\"mm\">SQ(1 + 2)</code> &#8594; <code class=\"mm\">1 + 2 * 1 + 2</code>.</li><li>The full expression is <code class=\"mm\">1 + 2 * 1 + 2 * 1 + 2</code>.</li><li>Multiplication first: 1 + 2 + 2 + 2 = 7.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">7</code></span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 27, the mathematical cube of 3, or 9 by expanding only the inner macro. Both assume the macros were parenthesised, and neither was.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Write <code class=\"mm\">#define SQ(x) ((x)*(x))</code> and <code class=\"mm\">#define CUBE(x) (SQ(x)*(x))</code>; a macro is safe only when every argument and the whole body are parenthesised.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2-Q24", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "macro-expansion-scope-lifetime", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\nint x = 10;\nint main(void) {\n    int x = 20;\n    {\n        extern int x;\n        printf(&quot;%d &quot;, x);\n    }\n    printf(&quot;%d&quot;, x);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["10 20", "20 20", "10 10", "20 10"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An <code class=\"mm\">extern</code> declaration at block scope declares an identifier with external linkage; it does not introduce a new object, it names the one already defined at file scope.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>At file scope <code class=\"mm\">x</code> is defined and initialised to 10, with external linkage.</li><li>In <code class=\"mm\">main</code>, the automatic <code class=\"mm\">int x = 20;</code> hides the file-scope object inside the body of <code class=\"mm\">main</code>.</li><li>Inside the inner block, <code class=\"mm\">extern int x;</code> declares <code class=\"mm\">x</code> again with external linkage, so the name refers to the file-scope object: 10 is printed.</li><li>After the block ends, the inner declaration goes out of scope and the automatic <code class=\"mm\">x</code> = 20 is visible again. Output: <code class=\"mm\">10 20</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">10 20</code> (option A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting <code class=\"mm\">20 20</code>, on the assumption that <code class=\"mm\">extern</code> simply re-declares the nearest visible <code class=\"mm\">x</code>. Linkage, not visibility, decides which object is named.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <code class=\"mm\">extern</code> inside a block reaches outside the function; it is the one declaration that ignores shadowing.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2-Q25", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "macro-expansion-scope-lifetime", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about scope, linkage and lifetime in C are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["A block-scope variable declared static keeps its value between calls and is initialised only once.", "An object declared const has static storage duration by default.", "A file-scope variable declared static has internal linkage and is not visible from another translation unit.", "The lifetime of an automatic variable ends when its block terminates, and the storage it used may then be reused."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> C separates three ideas: scope (where a name is visible), linkage (whether another translation unit can refer to it) and storage duration (how long the object exists). <code class=\"mm\">static</code> and <code class=\"mm\">const</code> touch different ones.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) TRUE.</b> Block scope plus static storage duration: one object, initialised once, value preserved across calls.</li><li><b>(B) FALSE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">const</code> qualifies the type, not the storage duration. A block-scope <code class=\"mm\">const</code> object has automatic storage duration unless it is also declared <code class=\"mm\">static</code>.</li><li><b>(C) TRUE.</b> At file scope, <code class=\"mm\">static</code> gives internal linkage, so the name is private to that translation unit.</li><li><b>(D) TRUE.</b> Automatic storage duration ends at the closing brace; the memory may be reused, which is why a pointer to it becomes dangling.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Conflating <code class=\"mm\">const</code> with <code class=\"mm\">static</code>. <code class=\"mm\">const</code> says the object will not be modified; it says nothing about when the object exists.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Ask three separate questions of any declaration: where is the name visible, who can link to it, and how long does the object live.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2B-Q01", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointer-arithmetic", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">For an array declared as <code class=\"mm\">int a[10];</code>, what is the type of the expression <code class=\"mm\">&amp;a</code>?</p>", "options": ["int *", "int **", "int (*)[10]", "int [10]"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Unary <code class=\"mm\">&amp;</code> applies to the array object itself, not to the pointer it decays to, so the result points to a whole array type.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>In almost every expression the name <code class=\"mm\">a</code> decays to <code class=\"mm\">int *</code>, pointing at <code class=\"mm\">a[0]</code>.</li><li>That decay is suppressed when the name is the operand of <code class=\"mm\">sizeof</code>, of unary <code class=\"mm\">&amp;</code>, or the initialiser of a character array. Here it is the operand of <code class=\"mm\">&amp;</code>.</li><li>So <code class=\"mm\">&amp;a</code> is a pointer to the object of type <code class=\"mm\">int[10]</code>: its type is <code class=\"mm\">int (*)[10]</code>.</li><li>The two types are visible in the arithmetic: <code class=\"mm\">&amp;a + 1</code> jumps 40 bytes (the whole array), while <code class=\"mm\">a + 1</code> jumps 4 bytes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">int (*)[10]</code> (option C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Concluding that <code class=\"mm\">&amp;a</code> is <code class=\"mm\">int *</code> because <code class=\"mm\">a</code> behaves like one. The two pointers hold the same address but have different types, so they scale differently and are not interchangeable.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2B-Q02", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointer-arithmetic", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">int a[5] = {2, 4, 6, 8, 10};\nint *p = a;\np += 2;\nprintf(&quot;%d %d&quot;, *p, p[-1]);</div>", "options": ["6 4", "6 2", "4 6", "8 6"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A subscript on a pointer is relative to where the pointer currently points: <code class=\"mm\">p[k]</code> means <code class=\"mm\">*(p + k)</code>, and a negative index is legal as long as the resulting address is inside the same array.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">p = a</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">p += 2</code> moves it two elements on, so <code class=\"mm\">p</code> points at <code class=\"mm\">a[2]</code>, whose value is 6.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">*p</code> is therefore 6.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">p[-1]</code> is <code class=\"mm\">*(p - 1)</code>, one element back from <code class=\"mm\">a[2]</code>: that is <code class=\"mm\">a[1]</code>, whose value is 4.</li><li>The output is <code class=\"mm\">6 4</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">6 4</code> (option A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reading <code class=\"mm\">p[-1]</code> as <code class=\"mm\">a[-1]</code> and calling it out of bounds. The index is measured from <code class=\"mm\">p</code>, not from the start of the array, so it lands on a perfectly valid element.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2B-Q03", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointer-arithmetic", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">On a machine where <code class=\"mm\">int</code> is 4 bytes, what integer does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">int a[4][3];\nprintf(&quot;%d&quot;, (int)((char *)(a + 1) - (char *)a));</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "12", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Pointer arithmetic is scaled by the size of the pointed-to type. Here <code class=\"mm\">a</code> has type <code class=\"mm\">int[4][3]</code> and decays to a pointer to its first row, of type <code class=\"mm\">int (*)[3]</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>One step of <code class=\"mm\">a + 1</code> advances by <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(int[3])</code>, the size of a whole row.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">sizeof(int[3]) = 3 &times; 4 = 12</code> bytes.</li><li>Casting both addresses to <code class=\"mm\">char *</code> makes the subtraction a plain byte count, which is 12.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 12</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reporting 4, the size of one element, or 3, the number of elements in a row. A row pointer steps by the size of the row in bytes, not by the size of an <code class=\"mm\">int</code> and not by the element count.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2B-Q04", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointer-arithmetic", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">int a[3] = {7, 8, 9};\nint *p = a + 1;\nint **q = &amp;p;\n(*q)[1] += 10;\nprintf(&quot;%d %d&quot;, a[1], a[2]);</div>", "options": ["8 9", "18 9", "18 19", "8 19"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">q</code> points at the pointer variable <code class=\"mm\">p</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">*q</code> is <code class=\"mm\">p</code> itself and the subscript applies to <code class=\"mm\">p</code>, not to <code class=\"mm\">a</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">p = a + 1</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">p</code> points at <code class=\"mm\">a[1]</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">*q</code> is <code class=\"mm\">p</code>; therefore <code class=\"mm\">(*q)[1]</code> is <code class=\"mm\">p[1]</code>, which is <code class=\"mm\">*(p + 1)</code> = <code class=\"mm\">a[2]</code> = 9.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">9 + 10 = 19</code> is stored into <code class=\"mm\">a[2]</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">a[1]</code> was never touched and stays 8, so the output is <code class=\"mm\">8 19</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">8 19</code> (option D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Treating <code class=\"mm\">(*q)[1]</code> as <code class=\"mm\">a[1]</code>. The dereference yields the pointer <code class=\"mm\">p</code>, so index 1 means one element past <code class=\"mm\">p</code>'s target, which is <code class=\"mm\">a[2]</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2B-Q05", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointer-arithmetic", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">For <code class=\"mm\">int a[4][5];</code> and <code class=\"mm\">int (*r)[5] = a;</code>, on a machine where <code class=\"mm\">int</code> is 4 bytes, which of the following statements are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["r + 1 advances the address by 20 bytes.", "r[2][3] denotes the same element as a[2][3] .", "sizeof(*r) equals 20.", "r[0] + 5 may be dereferenced to read a[1][0] ."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">r</code> is a pointer to a row, so every step and every dereference is scaled by the size of one row, and each row is an array object in its own right.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) TRUE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">r + 1</code> advances by <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(int[5]) = 20</code> bytes.</li><li><b>(B) TRUE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">r[2]</code> is <code class=\"mm\">*(r + 2)</code>, the row <code class=\"mm\">a[2]</code>; subscripting it again gives <code class=\"mm\">a[2][3]</code>.</li><li><b>(C) TRUE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">*r</code> has type <code class=\"mm\">int[5]</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">sizeof</code> does not decay it, so <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(*r) = 20</code>.</li><li><b>(D) FALSE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">r[0]</code> is the row <code class=\"mm\">a[0]</code>, an array of 5 ints. <code class=\"mm\">r[0] + 5</code> is exactly its one-past-the-end address; forming it is allowed, but dereferencing it is undefined behaviour, because pointer arithmetic is bounded by the array object it started in.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming that because the rows sit contiguously, <code class=\"mm\">r[0] + 5</code> must reach <code class=\"mm\">a[1][0]</code>. The standard tracks the array object, not the address, so the dereference is invalid even though the byte is physically there.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2B-Q06", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointer-arithmetic", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">On a machine where pointers are 8 bytes and <code class=\"mm\">int</code> is 4 bytes, what does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">int a[3][4] = {{0}};\nint *b[3];\nint (*c)[4] = a;\nprintf(&quot;%d %d&quot;, (int)sizeof(b), (int)sizeof(*c));</div>", "options": ["24 16", "24 48", "48 16", "12 16"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Postfix <code class=\"mm\">[]</code> binds tighter than <code class=\"mm\">*</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">int *b[3]</code> is an array of three <code class=\"mm\">int</code> pointers, whereas <code class=\"mm\">int (*c)[4]</code> is one pointer to an array of four ints.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">b</code> is an array of 3 pointers; <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(b) = 3 &times; 8 = 24</code> bytes.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">c</code> points to a row of four ints, so <code class=\"mm\">*c</code> has type <code class=\"mm\">int[4]</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">sizeof(*c) = 4 &times; 4 = 16</code> bytes. Only <code class=\"mm\">c</code> itself is a pointer, and it is not what <code class=\"mm\">sizeof</code> was applied to.</li><li>The output is <code class=\"mm\">24 16</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">24 16</code> (option A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reading <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(b)</code> as the size of three ints (12) or <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(*c)</code> as the size of a pointer (8). An array of pointers stores pointers, and dereferencing a row pointer yields a row, not an <code class=\"mm\">int</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2B-Q07", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointer-arithmetic", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What integer does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">int a[7] = {3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2};\nint *p = a, *q = a + 6;\nint c = 0;\nwhile (p &lt; q) { if (*p++ &lt; *q--) c++; }\nprintf(&quot;%d&quot;, c);</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Post-increment in a condition reads the current element and then advances the pointer, and both increments happen on every iteration whether or not the test succeeds.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Iteration 1: <code class=\"mm\">p</code> reads 3, <code class=\"mm\">q</code> reads 2. 3 &lt; 2 is false, <code class=\"mm\">c</code> stays 0; both pointers advance.</li><li>Iteration 2: <code class=\"mm\">p</code> reads 1, <code class=\"mm\">q</code> reads 9. 1 &lt; 9 is true, so <code class=\"mm\">c</code> becomes 1.</li><li>Iteration 3: <code class=\"mm\">p</code> reads 4, <code class=\"mm\">q</code> reads 5. 4 &lt; 5 is true, so <code class=\"mm\">c</code> becomes 2.</li><li>Now both pointers point at <code class=\"mm\">a[3]</code>, the test <code class=\"mm\">p &lt; q</code> fails, and the loop ends with <code class=\"mm\">c = 2</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting the third comparison, or assuming the loop runs for all seven elements. The two pointers close in from both ends and stop when they meet, so only three comparisons are made.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2B-Q08", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointers-to-functions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following declarations declares <code class=\"mm\">a</code> as an array of three pointers to functions that each take one <code class=\"mm\">int</code> and return <code class=\"mm\">void</code>?</p>", "options": ["void *a[3](int);", "void (*a[3])(int);", "void (*a)(int)[3];", "void *(*a[3])(int);"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A declarator is read from the identifier outwards, with postfix <code class=\"mm\">[]</code> and <code class=\"mm\">()</code> binding tighter than prefix <code class=\"mm\">*</code> unless parentheses override the order.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>In <code class=\"mm\">void (*a[3])(int)</code>, the parentheses first bind <code class=\"mm\">*</code> to <code class=\"mm\">a</code>, but inside them the postfix <code class=\"mm\">[3]</code> still applies first: <code class=\"mm\">a</code> is an array of 3.</li><li>Each element is then <code class=\"mm\">(*&hellip;)</code>, a pointer, and the trailing <code class=\"mm\">(int)</code> says it points to a function taking an <code class=\"mm\">int</code>.</li><li>The leading <code class=\"mm\">void</code> is that function's return type.</li><li>Option (A) is an array of functions returning <code class=\"mm\">void *</code>, (C) is a syntax error, and (D) is an array of pointers to functions returning <code class=\"mm\">void *</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">void (*a[3])(int);</code> (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Dropping the parentheses and writing <code class=\"mm\">void *a[3](int)</code>, which declares an array of functions, not of pointers to functions. Without the parentheses the <code class=\"mm\">*</code> attaches to the return type instead of to <code class=\"mm\">a</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2B-Q09", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointers-to-functions", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What integer does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">int inc(int x) { return x + 1; }\nint dbl(int x) { return x * 2; }\n\nint main(void) {\n    int (*t[2])(int) = {inc, dbl};\n    int v = 3, s = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 2; i++) { v = t[i](v); s += v; }\n    printf(&quot;%d&quot;, s);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "12", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An array of function pointers lets a loop apply a chain of different functions to a running value; the result of one call is the argument of the next.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">v = 3</code>, <code class=\"mm\">s = 0</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">i = 0</code>: <code class=\"mm\">t[0]</code> is <code class=\"mm\">inc</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">v = 4</code> and <code class=\"mm\">s = 0 + 4 = 4</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">i = 1</code>: <code class=\"mm\">t[1]</code> is <code class=\"mm\">dbl</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">v = 8</code> and <code class=\"mm\">s = 4 + 8 = 12</code>.</li><li>The loop ends and 12 is printed.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 12</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Applying both functions to the original 3 and summing 4 + 6 = 10. Each call receives the value produced by the previous one, so the chain is 3 &rarr; 4 &rarr; 8.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2B-Q10", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointers-to-functions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }\nint mul(int a, int b) { return a * b; }\nint apply(int (*f)(int, int), int a, int b) { return f(a, b); }\n\nint main(void) {\n    int (*p)(int, int) = &amp;mul;\n    printf(&quot;%d&quot;, apply(p, 2 + 3, 4));\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["9", "14", "20", "24"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A function pointer is an ordinary value: it can be stored, passed as an argument and called through, and unary <code class=\"mm\">&amp;</code> on a function name is optional.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">p = &amp;mul</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">&amp;mul</code> is the same value as <code class=\"mm\">mul</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">2 + 3</code> is evaluated before the call, giving the argument 5.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">apply(p, 5, 4)</code> calls <code class=\"mm\">p(5, 4)</code>, which is <code class=\"mm\">mul(5, 4) = 20</code>.</li><li>The output is 20.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 20 (option C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 14 by using <code class=\"mm\">add</code>, or 24 by reading the call as <code class=\"mm\">mul(2 + 3 * 4)</code>. The arguments are two separate values, 5 and 4, and the function called is the one the pointer holds.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2B-Q11", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointers-to-functions", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about pointers to functions in C are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["int (*f)(int); declares f as a pointer to a function taking one int and returning int .", "A function designator, except as the operand of sizeof or of unary & , is converted to a pointer to that function.", "Two function pointers may be compared with < to decide which function has the lower address.", "A function pointer may be stored in a void * and converted back without loss on every conforming implementation."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A function designator decays to a pointer to the function, and only the equality operators are defined on those pointers.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) TRUE.</b> That is exactly what the declarator says: <code class=\"mm\">f</code> is a pointer, and the function it points to takes an <code class=\"mm\">int</code> and returns an <code class=\"mm\">int</code>.</li><li><b>(B) TRUE.</b> This is the conversion rule for function designators, which is why <code class=\"mm\">qsort(v, n, sz, cmp)</code> needs no <code class=\"mm\">&amp;</code>.</li><li><b>(C) FALSE.</b> Only <code class=\"mm\">==</code> and <code class=\"mm\">!=</code> are defined between function pointers; relational comparison has no defined meaning.</li><li><b>(D) FALSE.</b> Conversion to <code class=\"mm\">void *</code> is guaranteed only for pointers to objects. Storing a function pointer in a <code class=\"mm\">void *</code> is a common extension, not a portable guarantee.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A) and (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming all pointers are interchangeable. Object pointers and function pointers live in different conversion rules, and ordering them is not part of the language at all.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2B-Q12", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointers-to-functions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }\nint mul(int a, int b) { return a * b; }\ntypedef int (*Op)(int, int);\n\nOp pick(char c) { return c == '+' ? add : mul; }\n\nint main(void) {\n    Op a = pick('+'), b = pick('*');\n    printf(&quot;%d&quot;, a(b(2, 3), 4));\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["20", "14", "24", "10"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A function can return a function pointer, and a call written as <code class=\"mm\">a(b(2, 3), 4)</code> evaluates the inner call first because it is an argument of the outer one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">pick('+')</code> returns <code class=\"mm\">add</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">a</code> is <code class=\"mm\">add</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">pick('*')</code> returns <code class=\"mm\">mul</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">b</code> is <code class=\"mm\">mul</code>.</li><li>The inner call <code class=\"mm\">b(2, 3)</code> is <code class=\"mm\">mul(2, 3) = 6</code>.</li><li>The outer call <code class=\"mm\">a(6, 4)</code> is <code class=\"mm\">add(6, 4) = 10</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 10 (option D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 20 by applying <code class=\"mm\">mul</code> last, or 14 by applying <code class=\"mm\">add</code> twice. The innermost call is the argument of the outer one, so <code class=\"mm\">mul</code> runs first.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2B-Q13", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointers-to-functions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">int f(int x) { return x + 1; }\nint g(int x) { return x * 2; }\n\nint main(void) {\n    int (*p)(int) = f, (*q)(int) = g, (*r)(int) = p;\n    p = q;\n    q = r;\n    printf(&quot;%d %d&quot;, p(3), q(3));\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["4 6", "6 4", "6 6", "4 4"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A function pointer is a variable holding an address; assigning one pointer to another copies the address, it does not link the two variables.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Initially <code class=\"mm\">p = f</code>, <code class=\"mm\">q = g</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">r = p</code> copies the value <code class=\"mm\">f</code> into <code class=\"mm\">r</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">p = q</code> copies <code class=\"mm\">g</code> into <code class=\"mm\">p</code>. Now <code class=\"mm\">p</code> is <code class=\"mm\">g</code>; <code class=\"mm\">q</code> is still <code class=\"mm\">g</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">q = r</code> copies <code class=\"mm\">f</code> into <code class=\"mm\">q</code>. Now <code class=\"mm\">q</code> is <code class=\"mm\">f</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">p(3) = g(3) = 6</code> and <code class=\"mm\">q(3) = f(3) = 4</code>, so the output is <code class=\"mm\">6 4</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">6 4</code> (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Treating <code class=\"mm\">r = p</code> as a reference, so that changing <code class=\"mm\">p</code> would change <code class=\"mm\">r</code>. It is a value copy taken at the moment of assignment, which is why <code class=\"mm\">r</code> still holds <code class=\"mm\">f</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2B-Q14", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "dynamic-memory-allocation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">On a target where <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(double) == 8</code>, what does the call <code class=\"mm\">calloc(5, sizeof(double))</code> request?</p>", "options": ["40 bytes, with every bit set to zero.", "40 bytes, with indeterminate contents.", "8 bytes, with every bit set to zero.", "13 bytes, with every bit set to zero."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">calloc(n, size)</code> allocates space for an array of <code class=\"mm\">n</code> objects of <code class=\"mm\">size</code> bytes each and initialises all of it to zero bits.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The two arguments are multiplied: <code class=\"mm\">5 &times; 8 = 40</code> bytes.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">calloc</code> then sets every bit of those 40 bytes to 0, which is why it is preferred when a zero-initialised array is wanted.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">malloc</code> would request the same 40 bytes but leave the contents indeterminate.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 40 bytes, zero-filled (option A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Swapping the two functions and choosing \"indeterminate\". Also note that zero bits is not the same as a floating-point 0.0 on every implementation, though for IEEE-754 it happens to coincide.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2B-Q15", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "dynamic-memory-allocation", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">On a target where <code class=\"mm\">int</code> is 4 bytes, a program executes the statement below. How many bytes does the call request in total?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">struct pt { int x, y; };\nstruct pt *a = calloc(12, sizeof(struct pt));</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "96", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">calloc</code> multiplies its two arguments, so the byte count is the element count times the element size.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">struct pt</code> holds two <code class=\"mm\">int</code> members, so <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(struct pt) = 8</code> bytes; no padding is added because both members are 4-byte aligned and the total is already a multiple of 4.</li><li>The call requests <code class=\"mm\">12 &times; 8 = 96</code> bytes, all zero-filled.</li><li>The result is a pointer to the first of 12 <code class=\"mm\">struct pt</code> objects.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 96</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Adding the arguments (12 + 8 = 20) or using the size of a member (12 &times; 4 = 48). The second argument is the size of the whole element, and the two arguments multiply.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2B-Q16", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "dynamic-memory-allocation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Consider <code class=\"mm\">int *q = realloc(p, 8 * sizeof *p);</code>. If the call succeeds by moving the block to a new address, which statement is correct?</p>", "options": ["realloc returns NULL on failure and frees the original block.", "p and q always refer to the same block.", "The old block has been released by realloc , so p must not be used again.", "realloc always returns the same address it was given."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">realloc</code> may extend the block in place or allocate a new one and copy; on success the old block is no longer owned by the program.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>On success, <code class=\"mm\">realloc</code> returns a pointer to the block of the new size and releases the old one if it had to move.</li><li>So after a moving reallocation, <code class=\"mm\">p</code> is a dangling pointer: any use of it is undefined behaviour, and <code class=\"mm\">q</code> is the only valid handle.</li><li>On failure, <code class=\"mm\">realloc</code> returns <code class=\"mm\">NULL</code> and leaves the original block allocated and untouched — which is why the safe idiom stores the result in a temporary before overwriting <code class=\"mm\">p</code>.</li><li>Options (A), (B) and (D) each contradict one of those rules.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Believing that a failed <code class=\"mm\">realloc</code> destroys the original block, or that a successful one keeps the same address. Both beliefs cause leaks and use-after-free bugs respectively.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2B-Q17", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "dynamic-memory-allocation", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about dynamic storage management in C are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["free(NULL) has no effect and is well defined.", "Freeing the same block twice is undefined behaviour.", "If realloc(p, n) fails, the original block is still allocated and must still be freed.", "malloc returns storage that is guaranteed to be filled with zero bytes."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The allocation functions have narrow, precisely stated guarantees: only <code class=\"mm\">calloc</code> zero-fills, only a live block may be freed, and a failed <code class=\"mm\">realloc</code> changes nothing.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) TRUE.</b> The standard says that if the argument is a null pointer, no action occurs. It is therefore safe and idiomatic.</li><li><b>(B) TRUE.</b> A second <code class=\"mm\">free</code> on the same block is a double free, which is undefined behaviour.</li><li><b>(C) TRUE.</b> Failure means the old block was left as it was, so the program still owns it and must free it.</li><li><b>(D) FALSE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">malloc</code> leaves the contents indeterminate; only <code class=\"mm\">calloc</code> guarantees zero bits.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming a failed <code class=\"mm\">realloc</code> has already freed the old block, and so skipping the <code class=\"mm\">free</code> and leaking it. Failure is a no-op on the existing allocation.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2B-Q18", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "dynamic-memory-allocation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Consider this fragment. Which statement about <code class=\"mm\">q</code> after the <code class=\"mm\">free</code> is correct?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">int *p = malloc(3 * sizeof *p);\nfor (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) p[i] = i + 1;\nint *q = p + 1;\nfree(p);</div>", "options": ["q still points to valid storage, because only p was freed.", "free(p) also resets q to NULL .", "Reading *q still prints 2, because the value has not been overwritten yet.", "q is a dangling pointer; reading *q is undefined behaviour."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">free</code> releases the whole allocated block, and every pointer into that block becomes invalid at once, not just the pointer that was passed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">p</code> owns a block of 3 ints; <code class=\"mm\">q = p + 1</code> points at the second element, inside the same block.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">free(p)</code> ends the lifetime of the entire block.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">q</code> is not modified by <code class=\"mm\">free</code>, so it still holds the old address, but that address no longer denotes a live object: it is a dangling pointer.</li><li>Reading or writing <code class=\"mm\">*q</code> is therefore undefined behaviour, even though the bytes may still hold 2.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Thinking that only the exact pointer passed to <code class=\"mm\">free</code> becomes invalid, or that the value surviving in memory makes the read legal. A pointer's validity depends on the object's lifetime, not on what the bytes happen to contain.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2B-Q19", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "dynamic-memory-allocation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">On a machine where pointers are 8 bytes and <code class=\"mm\">int</code> is 4 bytes, how many allocation calls does this fragment make, and how many bytes does it request in total?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">int **m = malloc(3 * sizeof *m);\nfor (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) m[i] = malloc(4 * sizeof **m);</div>", "options": ["1 call, 72 bytes", "4 calls, 72 bytes", "4 calls, 60 bytes", "3 calls, 48 bytes"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A dynamically allocated two-dimensional array is built from one allocation for the row-pointer array plus one allocation per row; the pointers live in the outer block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Call 1: <code class=\"mm\">3 &times; sizeof(int *) = 3 &times; 8 = 24</code> bytes for the array of row pointers.</li><li>Calls 2&ndash;4: three calls of <code class=\"mm\">4 &times; sizeof(int) = 16</code> bytes each, that is 48 bytes of row storage.</li><li>Total: 4 calls and <code class=\"mm\">24 + 48 = 72</code> bytes.</li><li>The rows need not be contiguous with each other or with the pointer array, which is exactly why the two-level layout costs one allocation per row.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4 calls, 72 bytes (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting only the three row allocations (3 calls), or pricing the pointers at 4 bytes each, which would give 60 bytes. A row pointer is a pointer, so it costs 8 bytes here, not the 4 bytes of an <code class=\"mm\">int</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2B-Q20", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "macro-expansion-scope-lifetime", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#define DOUBLE(x) (x) + (x)\nprintf(&quot;%d&quot;, 3 * DOUBLE(4));</div>", "options": ["24", "16", "12", "8"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A function-like macro is a textual substitution, so the expansion is spliced into the surrounding expression and the operators around it take part in the evaluation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">DOUBLE(4)</code> expands to <code class=\"mm\">(4) + (4)</code>.</li><li>The whole expression becomes <code class=\"mm\">3 * (4) + (4)</code>.</li><li>Multiplication binds tighter than addition, so it is <code class=\"mm\">12 + 4 = 16</code>.</li><li>Had the macro been written <code class=\"mm\">#define DOUBLE(x) ((x) + (x))</code> the answer would be 24, because the whole expansion would be one primary expression.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 16 (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Treating the macro as a function and answering 24. Parenthesising each parameter is not enough; the entire body needs its own pair of parentheses.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2B-Q21", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "macro-expansion-scope-lifetime", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about scope, linkage and lifetime in C are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["A block-scope static object is initialised once, before the first entry to its block, and keeps its value between calls.", "An extern declaration at block scope allocates storage for the object it names.", "Two functions may each declare a local variable of the same name; each declaration introduces a distinct object.", "A file-scope object declared static has internal linkage, so other translation units cannot refer to it by that name."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Scope is where a name is visible, linkage is whether other translation units can see it, and lifetime is when the object exists. The storage class specifiers move these three properties independently.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) TRUE.</b> A block-scope <code class=\"mm\">static</code> object has static storage duration: it is initialised once and survives until the program ends, so a counter inside a function keeps counting.</li><li><b>(B) FALSE.</b> An <code class=\"mm\">extern</code> declaration is a declaration, not a definition; it tells the compiler the object exists elsewhere and allocates nothing.</li><li><b>(C) TRUE.</b> Block scope is per block: two locals with the same spelling in different functions are unrelated objects, and neither can see the other.</li><li><b>(D) TRUE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">static</code> at file scope gives internal linkage, so the name is private to that translation unit.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reading <code class=\"mm\">extern</code> as \"make it external, so allocate it here\". It is the opposite: the keyword asserts that the storage is defined somewhere else.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2B-Q22", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "macro-expansion-scope-lifetime", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Why is a multi-statement macro conventionally wrapped in <code class=\"mm\">do { &hellip; } while (0)</code>?</p>", "options": ["It makes the expansion a single statement, so a trailing semicolon and an unbraced if / else parse correctly.", "It makes the macro return a value to the caller.", "It guarantees that the body executes exactly once even inside a loop.", "It forces each macro argument to be evaluated exactly once."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A macro expansion is a token sequence inserted where the name appears, and it must behave like a single statement when it is used as one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>If the body were bare statements in braces, <code class=\"mm\">if (c) MACRO; else &hellip;</code> would expand to <code class=\"mm\">if (c) { &hellip; } ; else &hellip;</code>, and the stray semicolon would detach the <code class=\"mm\">else</code> — a compile error.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">do { &hellip; } while (0)</code> plus the semicolon the caller writes forms exactly one statement, so it nests correctly under any control structure.</li><li>The loop runs exactly once because the condition is the constant 0, which the compiler removes entirely.</li><li>It does not affect how many times arguments are evaluated: a parameter that appears twice in the body is still evaluated twice.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Believing the wrapper protects against multiple evaluation of arguments. Only a real function, or a macro that mentions each parameter exactly once, does that.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2B-Q23", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "macro-expansion-scope-lifetime", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What integer does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#define A 3\n#define B A + 2\nprintf(&quot;%d&quot;, B * 2);</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "7", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An object-like macro is replaced by its token sequence exactly as written. If the definition is not parenthesised, the expansion inherits the precedence of the operators around it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">B</code> is replaced by <code class=\"mm\">A + 2</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">A</code> in turn by <code class=\"mm\">3</code>.</li><li>The expression becomes <code class=\"mm\">3 + 2 * 2</code>.</li><li>Multiplication is evaluated first: <code class=\"mm\">3 + 4 = 7</code>.</li><li>With <code class=\"mm\">#define B (A + 2)</code> the result would be <code class=\"mm\">5 * 2 = 10</code>, which is what most readers expect.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 7</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Evaluating <code class=\"mm\">B</code> as the value 5 first and answering 10. Macro expansion is substitution, not evaluation, so the surrounding <code class=\"mm\">* 2</code> reaches only the last term.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2B-Q24", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "macro-expansion-scope-lifetime", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What is the result of compiling and running this program?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#define SIZE 5\nint main(void) {\n    int SIZE = 3;\n    printf(&quot;%d&quot;, SIZE);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["3", "5", "A compile-time error: the macro replaces the identifier, so the declaration reads int 5 = 3; .", "Undefined behaviour, because the macro and the variable conflict at run time."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The preprocessor runs before the compiler parses the program and has no notion of scope, so a macro name is replaced wherever it appears as a token, including in declarations.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">SIZE</code> is defined as <code class=\"mm\">5</code>, so every occurrence of that token is replaced by <code class=\"mm\">5</code>.</li><li>The declaration <code class=\"mm\">int SIZE = 3;</code> therefore becomes <code class=\"mm\">int 5 = 3;</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">5</code> is not a valid identifier, so the compiler reports a syntax error and no program is produced.</li><li>A local declaration cannot shadow a macro: shadowing is a compile-time scope rule, and the substitution has already happened by then.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: a compile-time error (option C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Expecting the local variable to win and printing 3. Macros are not variables and cannot be shadowed; the convention of upper-case macro names exists precisely to avoid this collision.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2B-Q25", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "macro-expansion-scope-lifetime", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about the C preprocessor are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["#define performs textual substitution before compilation, so the preprocessor has no knowledge of C types.", "A macro definition may span several lines provided every line but the last ends with a backslash.", "#ifdef … #endif can remove code from the translation unit entirely, so the removed text need not even be valid C.", "sizeof applied to an expression containing a macro is evaluated by the preprocessor."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The preprocessor is a separate translation phase that works on tokens and lines; it knows nothing about types, values or sizes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) TRUE.</b> It substitutes tokens, which is why a macro has no argument type checking and why <code class=\"mm\">#define MAX(a,b)</code> can be passed anything at all.</li><li><b>(B) TRUE.</b> A backslash immediately before the newline splices the physical lines, so a long definition can be written over several lines.</li><li><b>(C) TRUE.</b> Conditional compilation happens before parsing, so the excluded text is never compiled and may be incomplete or ungrammatical.</li><li><b>(D) FALSE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">sizeof</code> is an operator of the compiled language. The preprocessor expands the macro text and hands it on; the compiler then evaluates the size.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Confusing <code class=\"mm\">#if</code> with a run-time condition, or expecting the preprocessor to compute sizes. Anything that needs a type, a value or a size belongs to the compiler, not the preprocessor.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q01", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "arrays-multidimensional", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "An array is declared as int A[5][8]; and stored in row-major order starting at byte address 1000. On a machine where int occupies 4 bytes, what is the byte address of A[3][2]?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">1056</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">1104</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">1096</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">1108</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In row-major order the rows are laid out one after another, so the element <code class=\"mm\">A[i][j]</code> of an <code class=\"mm\">m &times; n</code> array sits at element offset <code class=\"mm\">i &times; n + j</code> from the base, and the byte offset is that number times the element size.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Here <code class=\"mm\">m = 5</code> and <code class=\"mm\">n = 8</code>, so a row holds 8 integers.</li><li>The offset in elements is <code class=\"mm\">3 &times; 8 + 2 = 26</code>.</li><li>At 4 bytes each, that is <code class=\"mm\">26 &times; 4 = 104</code> bytes.</li><li>Address = <code class=\"mm\">1000 + 104 = 1104</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">1104 (option B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Using the row count (5) instead of the column count (8) gives <code class=\"mm\">3 &times; 5 + 2 = 17</code> elements, or 68 bytes, and 1068 &mdash; a number that looks plausible but corresponds to a 5-column layout.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q02", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "arrays-multidimensional", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "The array int A[3][4]; is stored in column-major order starting at byte address 2000, and int occupies 4 bytes. What is the address of A[2][1]?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">2036</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">2012</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">2020</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">2044</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In column-major order the columns are stored one after another, so for an <code class=\"mm\">m &times; n</code> array the element offset is <code class=\"mm\">j &times; m + i</code> &mdash; the roles of the two indices swap relative to row-major order.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">m = 3</code> rows, so each column holds 3 integers.</li><li>Offset in elements = <code class=\"mm\">1 &times; 3 + 2 = 5</code>.</li><li>Byte offset = <code class=\"mm\">5 &times; 4 = 20</code>.</li><li>Address = <code class=\"mm\">2000 + 20 = 2020</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">2020 (option C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Applying the row-major formula <code class=\"mm\">2 &times; 4 + 1 = 9</code> gives 2036, which is offered as option (A). The layout convention has to be read from the question, not assumed.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q03", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "arrays-multidimensional", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "int A[5][6]; is stored in row-major order and int occupies 4 bytes. How many bytes apart are the starting addresses of A[2][0] and A[0][5]?", "options": [], "answer_display": "28", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two elements of the same array differ in address by the difference of their element offsets, multiplied by the element size. The order of the two addresses does not matter, only the gap.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Offset of <code class=\"mm\">A[2][0]</code> = <code class=\"mm\">2 &times; 6 + 0 = 12</code> elements.</li><li>Offset of <code class=\"mm\">A[0][5]</code> = <code class=\"mm\">0 &times; 6 + 5 = 5</code> elements.</li><li>Difference = <code class=\"mm\">12 &minus; 5 = 7</code> elements.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">7 &times; 4 = 28</code> bytes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">28</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Subtracting the row and column indices separately, or using the number of rows (5) as the stride, gives 4 or 20. Only the flat element offset is meaningful.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q04", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "arrays-multidimensional", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "The lower triangle, including the diagonal, of a 10 × 10 matrix is stored row by row in a one-dimensional array T beginning at index 0: row 0 first, then row 1, and so on. What is the index in T of the element A[7][3]?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">32</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">28</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">34</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">31</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Row <code class=\"mm\">i</code> of the lower triangle holds <code class=\"mm\">i + 1</code> entries, so the first element of row <code class=\"mm\">i</code> sits at index <code class=\"mm\">1 + 2 + &hellip; + i = i(i + 1)/2</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Rows 0 to 6 together hold <code class=\"mm\">1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 + 6 + 7 = 28</code> entries, occupying indices 0 to 27.</li><li>Row 7 therefore begins at index 28.</li><li>Within row 7 the entry with column index 3 is the fourth element, at offset 3.</li><li>Index = <code class=\"mm\">28 + 3 = 31</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">31 (option D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Using <code class=\"mm\">7 &times; 8 / 2 = 28</code> and stopping there treats the row start as the answer, giving 28 &mdash; option (B). The column offset inside the row still has to be added.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q05", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "arrays-multidimensional", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "The array int A[2][3][4]; begins at byte address 0 and is stored in row-major order with int occupying 4 bytes. What is the byte address of A[1][2][3]?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">92</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">88</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">96</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">80</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> For a three-dimensional array the row-major offset is built from the inside out: <code class=\"mm\">((i &times; d&#8322; + j) &times; d&#8323; + k)</code>, where <code class=\"mm\">d&#8322;</code> and <code class=\"mm\">d&#8323;</code> are the second and third dimensions.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">d&#8322; = 3</code> and <code class=\"mm\">d&#8323; = 4</code>.</li><li>Inner index: <code class=\"mm\">1 &times; 3 + 2 = 5</code> &mdash; the plane number.</li><li>Element offset: <code class=\"mm\">5 &times; 4 + 3 = 23</code>.</li><li>Byte address: <code class=\"mm\">23 &times; 4 = 92</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">92 (option A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Flattening the indices as <code class=\"mm\">1 + 2 + 3 = 6</code>, or as <code class=\"mm\">1 &times; 3 &times; 4 + 2 &times; 4 + 3 = 23</code> &mdash; the second is right, the first gives 24 and 96, which is option (C).</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q06", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "arrays-multidimensional", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "For int A[3][4]; compiled as C, which of the following statements are TRUE?", "options": ["In an expression such as <code class=\"mm\">A + 1</code>, the name <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is converted to a pointer of type <code class=\"mm\">int (*)[4]</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">A[i][j]</code> denotes the same object as <code class=\"mm\">*(*(A + i) + j)</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">A</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A[0]</code> have the same type after the usual array-to-pointer conversion.", "<code class=\"mm\">sizeof(A[0])</code> is equal to <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(int)</code>."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A two-dimensional array is an array of rows. The array name decays to a pointer to its first element, and its first element is a whole row, not a single <code class=\"mm\">int</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) TRUE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is an <code class=\"mm\">int[3][4]</code>, so its first element is an <code class=\"mm\">int[4]</code> and the decayed type is <code class=\"mm\">int (*)[4]</code>. Stepping <code class=\"mm\">A + 1</code> therefore moves by a whole row.</li><li><b>(B) TRUE.</b> That is the definition of subscripting: <code class=\"mm\">A[i]</code> is <code class=\"mm\">*(A + i)</code>, a row, and a second subscript picks an element out of it.</li><li><b>(C) FALSE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">A</code> decays to <code class=\"mm\">int (*)[4]</code> while <code class=\"mm\">A[0]</code> decays to <code class=\"mm\">int *</code>. They hold the same address but have different types.</li><li><b>(D) FALSE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">A[0]</code> is an <code class=\"mm\">int[4]</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(A[0])</code> is 16 when an <code class=\"mm\">int</code> is 4 bytes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">options (A) and (B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming that because <code class=\"mm\">A</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A[0]</code> print the same address they must have the same type. Equal addresses, different pointer types, different step sizes.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q07", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "arrays-multidimensional", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "For int A[2][3][4]; on a machine where int occupies 4 bytes, which of the following statements are TRUE?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">A[1]</code> has type <code class=\"mm\">int [3][4]</code>, and in an expression it decays to <code class=\"mm\">int (*)[4]</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">A[0][1]</code> decays to <code class=\"mm\">int (*)[4]</code> in an expression.", "<code class=\"mm\">A + 1</code> differs from <code class=\"mm\">A</code> by 48 bytes.", "<code class=\"mm\">sizeof(A[0])</code> is 8, the size of a pointer."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each subscript peels one dimension off the type: <code class=\"mm\">int[2][3][4]</code> &rarr; <code class=\"mm\">int[3][4]</code> &rarr; <code class=\"mm\">int[4]</code> &rarr; <code class=\"mm\">int</code>. The pointer produced by decay always points at one element of the peeled type.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) TRUE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">A[1]</code> is a plane of type <code class=\"mm\">int[3][4]</code>; its first element is an <code class=\"mm\">int[4]</code>, so the decayed pointer is <code class=\"mm\">int (*)[4]</code>.</li><li><b>(B) FALSE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">A[0][1]</code> is already an <code class=\"mm\">int[4]</code>, so it decays to <code class=\"mm\">int *</code>, not to a pointer to an array.</li><li><b>(C) TRUE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">A</code> decays to <code class=\"mm\">int (*)[3][4]</code>, whose element is one plane: <code class=\"mm\">3 &times; 4 &times; 4 = 48</code> bytes.</li><li><b>(D) FALSE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">A[0]</code> is an <code class=\"mm\">int[3][4]</code> object, and <code class=\"mm\">sizeof</code> does not decay it: <code class=\"mm\">3 &times; 4 &times; 4 = 48</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">options (A) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Applying the decay rule inside <code class=\"mm\">sizeof</code>. <code class=\"mm\">sizeof</code> is one of the three contexts that suppress decay, so it always reports the whole array object.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q08", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "singly-doubly-circular-linked-lists", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A singly linked list is maintained with a pointer to its first node only, and there is no tail pointer. Which of the following operations runs in Θ(1) time in the worst case?", "options": ["Inserting a new node at the end of the list.", "Deleting the last node of the list.", "Inserting a new node at the front of the list.", "Searching for a node that holds a given value."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A singly linked list gives constant-time access only to what a pointer already points at. Reaching any other position requires walking the <code class=\"mm\">next</code> chain.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A)</b> To append, the last node must be found, which costs &Theta;(n).</li><li><b>(B)</b> Deleting the last node needs its predecessor, so the walk is &Theta;(n). A tail pointer alone does not help: it points at the node to remove, not at the one before it.</li><li><b>(C)</b> Prepending rewrites the new node's <code class=\"mm\">next</code> and the head pointer. Two assignments, &Theta;(1).</li><li><b>(D)</b> An unsorted list must be scanned, which is &Theta;(n).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">option (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Believing that a tail pointer makes deletion at the tail &Theta;(1). It makes insertion at the tail &Theta;(1) but leaves deletion at &Theta;(n) unless the list is doubly linked or the node before the tail is tracked.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q09", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "singly-doubly-circular-linked-lists", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A circular singly linked list contains 9 nodes. Starting at any node and following the next pointer repeatedly, how many times must next be followed before the traversal first returns to the starting node?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">9</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">8</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">10</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">1</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a circular list no <code class=\"mm\">next</code> pointer is null; the last node points back to the first, so the chain is one cycle through every node.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Every node has exactly one outgoing <code class=\"mm\">next</code>, so the cycle has exactly 9 distinct nodes.</li><li>Leaving the start node and stepping 9 times visits the other 8 nodes and lands back on the start.</li><li>A count of 8 would return to the start only in a list that had a null terminator and 9 nodes, which is not circular.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">9 (option A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 8 by analogy with a linear list of 9 nodes, where following <code class=\"mm\">next</code> 8 times reaches the last node. In a circular list the ninth step closes the loop.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q10", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "singly-doubly-circular-linked-lists", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "The loop below reverses a singly linked list in place. For a list of 6 nodes, how many times is the assignment q->next = p; executed?\nnode *p = NULL, *q = head, *r;\nwhile (q != NULL) {\n    r = q->next;\n    q->next = p;\n    p = q;\n    q = r;\n}", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">5</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">7</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">6</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">12</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The loop body runs once per node: each iteration redirects exactly one node's <code class=\"mm\">next</code> backwards. Reversal therefore performs one pointer reassignment per node.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Iteration 1: <code class=\"mm\">q</code> is the first node; its <code class=\"mm\">next</code> becomes <code class=\"mm\">NULL</code>.</li><li>Iterations 2 to 6: each redirects one further node to its predecessor.</li><li>After the sixth iteration <code class=\"mm\">q</code> is <code class=\"mm\">NULL</code> and the loop test fails, so there is no seventh execution.</li><li>Total: 6 reassignments, and 6 is also the number of <code class=\"mm\">next</code> fields in the list.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">6 (option C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 5 by analogy with the number of \"gaps\" between 6 nodes. Every node's <code class=\"mm\">next</code> is rewritten, including the first, which becomes <code class=\"mm\">NULL</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q11", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "singly-doubly-circular-linked-lists", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A singly linked list contains the nodes 1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5 → 6 → 7, and the last node points back to node 4. A slow pointer advances one node per step and a fast pointer advances two nodes per step; both start at node 1. After how many steps do they first meet, and at which node?", "options": ["3 steps, at node 4", "4 steps, at node 5", "5 steps, at node 6", "They never meet"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Once the fast pointer is inside the cycle it gains exactly one node per step on the slow pointer, so the two must meet. The meeting point is found by simulating the two walks on the actual cycle.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Step 0: slow at 1, fast at 1.</li><li>Step 1: slow 1&rarr;2, fast 1&rarr;3.</li><li>Step 2: slow 2&rarr;3, fast 3&rarr;5.</li><li>Step 3: slow 3&rarr;4, fast 5&rarr;7.</li><li>Step 4: slow 4&rarr;5, fast 7&rarr;4&rarr;5. Both are now at node 5.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">4 steps, at node 5 (option B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting the steps of the slow pointer only up to the entry node (3 steps) and declaring a meeting at node 4. The pointers meet after the slow pointer has entered the cycle, not at its entry.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q12", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "singly-doubly-circular-linked-lists", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about linked lists are TRUE?", "options": ["Reversing a singly linked list in place takes &Theta;(n) time and &Theta;(1) extra space.", "In a doubly linked list, deleting a node given only a pointer to that node takes &Theta;(1) time.", "A circular doubly linked list with n nodes has 2n link fields.", "With both a head and a tail pointer, deleting the last node of a singly linked list takes &Theta;(1) time."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The cost of a linked-list operation is decided by which nodes it must reach, and whether the structure stores a pointer to them.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) TRUE.</b> The three-pointer loop touches each node once and uses a fixed number of variables, so time is &Theta;(n) and space is &Theta;(1).</li><li><b>(B) TRUE.</b> The node's own <code class=\"mm\">prev</code> and <code class=\"mm\">next</code> give both neighbours directly; four assignments unlink it.</li><li><b>(C) TRUE.</b> Each of the n nodes carries a <code class=\"mm\">prev</code> and a <code class=\"mm\">next</code>, so there are 2n links, and in a circular list none of them is null.</li><li><b>(D) FALSE.</b> The tail pointer identifies the node to delete but not its predecessor, so the list must still be walked to find the node before the tail: &Theta;(n).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">options (A), (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Concluding that a tail pointer upgrades every tail operation to constant time. It only helps insertion, because insertion needs the old tail, whereas deletion needs the node before it.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q13", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "singly-doubly-circular-linked-lists", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Seven people numbered 1 to 7 stand in a circle and are stored in a circular singly linked list in that order. Counting begins at person 1 and proceeds 1, 2, 1, 2, …; every second person is eliminated, and counting restarts at the next surviving person. What is the number of the last survivor?", "options": [], "answer_display": "7", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> This is the Josephus problem with <code class=\"mm\">k = 2</code> on a circular list. Because elimination always skips one surviving node and removes the next, the list shrinks by one node per round and the circle closes up behind the removed node.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Circle 1 2 3 4 5 6 7, counting from 1: 1 is counted first, 2 is second and is eliminated. The circle is now 1 3 4 5 6 7 and counting resumes at 3.</li><li>From 3: 3 counted first, 4 second, so 4 is eliminated. Circle 1 3 5 6 7, resume at 5.</li><li>From 5: 5 first, 6 second, so 6 is eliminated. Circle 1 3 5 7, resume at 7.</li><li>From 7: 7 first, 1 second, so 1 is eliminated. Circle 3 5 7, resume at 3.</li><li>From 3: 3 first, 5 second, so 5 is eliminated. Circle 3 7, resume at 7.</li><li>From 7: 7 first, 3 second, so 3 is eliminated. Only 7 remains.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">7</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Restarting the count at the node after the eliminated one instead of the node after the one that was just counted. In this variant the survivor after two eliminations is <code class=\"mm\">7</code>, whereas the more common \"eliminate the first, restart at the next\" convention gives <code class=\"mm\">4</code> for seven people.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q14", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "stacks-applications-infix-postfix", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "What is the postfix form of the infix expression A + B * C?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">A B + C *</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">A B C + *</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">A B C * +</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">A + B C *</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Postfix notation places each operator immediately after its two operands, so the higher-precedence operation is completed first and no parentheses are needed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Multiplication binds tighter than addition, so the expression groups as <code class=\"mm\">A + (B * C)</code>.</li><li>Convert the inner product: <code class=\"mm\">B C *</code>.</li><li>The product is now the right operand of <code class=\"mm\">+</code>: <code class=\"mm\">A (B C *) +</code>.</li><li>Removing the brackets gives <code class=\"mm\">A B C * +</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">A B C * + (option C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reading the expression strictly left to right gives <code class=\"mm\">A B + C *</code>, which computes <code class=\"mm\">(A + B) * C</code> &mdash; a different value.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q15", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "stacks-applications-infix-postfix", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "The postfix expression 6 2 / 1 + 3 * is evaluated with a stack. What is the result?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">9</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">15</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">3</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">12</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Postfix evaluation pushes operands and, on each operator, pops the right operand first and then the left one, applies the operator, and pushes the result.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Push 6, push 2. Read <code class=\"mm\">/</code>: pop 2 (right) and 6 (left), compute <code class=\"mm\">6 / 2 = 3</code>, push 3. Stack: [3].</li><li>Push 1. Read <code class=\"mm\">+</code>: pop 1 and 3, compute <code class=\"mm\">3 + 1 = 4</code>, push 4. Stack: [4].</li><li>Push 3. Read <code class=\"mm\">*</code>: pop 3 and 4, compute <code class=\"mm\">4 &times; 3 = 12</code>. Stack: [12].</li><li>The expression is exhausted, so the single remaining value is the answer.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">12 (option D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Popping in the wrong order on the division and computing <code class=\"mm\">2 / 6</code>. The right operand is on top of the stack, so it must be popped first.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q16", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "stacks-applications-infix-postfix", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Evaluate the postfix expression 5 3 + 8 2 - *. Give the integer result.", "options": [], "answer_display": "48", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each binary operator consumes the two most recent values on the stack, so the operands of an operator are the two subexpressions immediately to its left.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Push 5, push 3. Read <code class=\"mm\">+</code>: <code class=\"mm\">5 + 3 = 8</code>. Stack: [8].</li><li>Push 8, push 2. Read <code class=\"mm\">-</code>: <code class=\"mm\">8 &minus; 2 = 6</code>. Stack: [8, 6].</li><li>Read <code class=\"mm\">*</code>: <code class=\"mm\">8 &times; 6 = 48</code>. Stack: [48].</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">48</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Subtracting in the order the numbers appear, <code class=\"mm\">2 &minus; 8 = &minus;6</code>, which yields <code class=\"mm\">&minus;48</code>. Subtraction is not commutative and the top of the stack is the right operand.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q17", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "stacks-applications-infix-postfix", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "What is the postfix form of the infix expression (A + B) * (C - D) + E?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">A B + C D - * E +</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">A B + C D * - E +</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">A B + C D - E + *</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">A B C + D - * E +</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Converting with a stack: operands are emitted at once; an operator is emitted only after every operator of higher or equal precedence above it has been popped; a closing bracket pops up to its matching opening bracket.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">(A + B)</code> becomes <code class=\"mm\">A B +</code>; the bracket disappears once it closes.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(C - D)</code> becomes <code class=\"mm\">C D -</code>.</li><li>The <code class=\"mm\">*</code> between the two brackets combines them: <code class=\"mm\">A B + C D - *</code>.</li><li>The final <code class=\"mm\">+ E</code> has lower precedence, so it is appended last: <code class=\"mm\">A B + C D - * E +</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">A B + C D - * E + (option A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Option (C) moves the <code class=\"mm\">*</code> past the <code class=\"mm\">E +</code>, which would compute <code class=\"mm\">(A + B) * ((C - D) + E)</code> instead of adding <code class=\"mm\">E</code> to the product.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q18", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "stacks-applications-infix-postfix", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about stacks and their applications are TRUE?", "options": ["In an array-based stack, a push is rejected when the stack already holds as many elements as the array can store, and that test is made before the write.", "While converting an infix expression to postfix, the operator stack can never hold more than two operators.", "A closing bracket that arrives when the bracket stack is empty proves the expression is not balanced.", "Two stacks stored in one array of size n, growing towards each other from the two ends, can hold at most n &minus; 1 items in total."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A stack is a last-in-first-out store with a single access point, so every implementation has to guard its boundary conditions: overflow at the top of the capacity and underflow at the bottom.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) TRUE.</b> The capacity test must precede the write; writing first would overrun the array.</li><li><b>(B) FALSE.</b> Nesting depth, not operator count, bounds the stack: <code class=\"mm\">A + (B + (C + (D + E)))</code> leaves four operators pending at once.</li><li><b>(C) TRUE.</b> A closing bracket with nothing to match is exactly the unbalanced case the stack detects.</li><li><b>(D) FALSE.</b> When the two top indices become adjacent the array is full, so all n cells can be used.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">options (A) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Thinking a shared array must waste one cell, as a circular queue does. The two stacks meet, and they can meet at the last free cell, so nothing is wasted.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q19", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "stacks-applications-infix-postfix", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "The integers 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 are pushed onto a stack in that order, each exactly once, and may be popped at any time. Which of the following output sequences cannot be produced?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">2 3 1 5 4</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">3 1 2 5 4</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">4 5 3 2 1</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">5 4 3 2 1</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Whatever is pushed last must be popped first. So if a larger number is still sitting on the stack when a smaller one is popped, the larger one must come out before any number that was pushed before it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A)</b> Push 1, 2; pop 2. Push 3; pop 3. Pop 1. Push 4, 5; pop 5, pop 4. Gives 2 3 1 5 4.</li><li><b>(B)</b> To print 3 first, push 1, 2, 3 and pop 3. The next output must be 1, but 2 sits on top of 1 and cannot be passed. Printing 1 would require popping 2 first, which contradicts the sequence. Impossible.</li><li><b>(C)</b> Push 1, 2, 3, 4; pop 4. Push 5; pop 5. Pop 3, 2, 1. Gives 4 5 3 2 1.</li><li><b>(D)</b> Push all five, then pop all five.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">3 1 2 5 4 (option B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Checking only that the first element is reachable. The obstruction in (B) appears at the second element, where the stack top blocks access to the value required next.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q20", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "queues-circular-queues-deques", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A circular queue uses an array of size 8 with indices 0 to 7. Both front and rear start at 0, the queue is empty exactly when front == rear, and every enqueue executes rear = (rear + 1) % 8. After 5 enqueues and no dequeue, what is the value of rear?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">5</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">4</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">6</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">0</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a circular queue the indices wrap modulo the array size, and <code class=\"mm\">rear</code> always designates the next cell to be written, not the last one written.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Start: <code class=\"mm\">rear = 0</code>.</li><li>Five enqueues increment it five times: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.</li><li>No value reaches 8, so the modulo never wraps.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">rear = 5</code>, and the five stored elements occupy cells 0 to 4.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">5 (option A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reporting 4 by treating <code class=\"mm\">rear</code> as the index of the last element stored. Here it is the next free cell, which is why <code class=\"mm\">front == rear</code> can mean empty.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q21", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "queues-circular-queues-deques", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Using the same circular queue (array size 8, front = rear = 0, rear = (rear + 1) % 8 on enqueue, front = (front + 1) % 8 on dequeue): after 5 enqueues, then 3 dequeues, then 4 enqueues, what is the value of rear?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">0</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">2</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">5</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">1</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Dequeues move only <code class=\"mm\">front</code>; <code class=\"mm\">rear</code> is advanced by enqueues alone, modulo the array size. Tracking the two indices separately is what keeps the circular buffer correct.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>After 5 enqueues: <code class=\"mm\">front = 0</code>, <code class=\"mm\">rear = 5</code>, five elements in cells 0 to 4.</li><li>After 3 dequeues: <code class=\"mm\">front = 3</code>, <code class=\"mm\">rear = 5</code>, two elements in cells 3 and 4.</li><li>Four enqueues advance <code class=\"mm\">rear</code>: 5 &rarr; 6 &rarr; 7 &rarr; 0 &rarr; 1, wrapping once at the end of the array.</li><li>Final state: <code class=\"mm\">front = 3</code>, <code class=\"mm\">rear = 1</code>, holding 6 elements &mdash; within the capacity of 7.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">1 (option D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 0 by assuming the queue restarts from index 0 after the dequeue, or 5 by forgetting that the last enqueue wraps. The modulo applies to every single increment.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q22", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "queues-circular-queues-deques", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A circular queue is implemented in an array of 10 slots, and one slot is deliberately left unused so that front == rear can stand for the empty state. What is the largest number of elements the queue can hold?", "options": [], "answer_display": "9", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With only the two indices, the states \"empty\" and \"full\" would both show <code class=\"mm\">front == rear</code>. Sacrificing one slot keeps the two conditions distinguishable without a counter.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Empty is <code class=\"mm\">front == rear</code>.</li><li>Full is declared one step short: <code class=\"mm\">(rear + 1) % 10 == front</code>.</li><li>So <code class=\"mm\">rear</code> may never catch up with <code class=\"mm\">front</code>, and at most 9 of the 10 cells can be occupied.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">9</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 10 by counting the array cells. Allowing all 10 makes full indistinguishable from empty, and the queue would report an empty buffer while holding 10 elements.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q23", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "queues-circular-queues-deques", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A queue is built from two stacks S1 and S2. Enqueue pushes onto S1. Dequeue pops from S2; if S2 is empty, every element of S1 is first popped and pushed onto S2, and then one element is popped from S2. Starting from empty stacks, the operations enqueue(1), enqueue(2), enqueue(3), dequeue, enqueue(4), dequeue, dequeue, dequeue are performed. Counting pushes onto S1 and onto S2 together, how many push operations are executed in total?", "options": [], "answer_display": "8", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each element is pushed onto <code class=\"mm\">S1</code> once when it arrives, and is pushed onto <code class=\"mm\">S2</code> at most once, when the transfer is triggered. So the total is bounded by 2 per element, and the exact count follows the transfer points.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>enqueue(1), enqueue(2), enqueue(3): three pushes onto <code class=\"mm\">S1</code>. Running total 3. <code class=\"mm\">S1</code> holds [1, 2, 3] bottom to top; <code class=\"mm\">S2</code> empty.</li><li>dequeue: <code class=\"mm\">S2</code> is empty, so three elements move from <code class=\"mm\">S1</code> to <code class=\"mm\">S2</code> &mdash; three more pushes. Total 6. <code class=\"mm\">S2</code> holds [3, 2, 1] with 1 on top; pop returns 1.</li><li>enqueue(4): one push onto <code class=\"mm\">S1</code>. Total 7.</li><li>dequeue: <code class=\"mm\">S2</code> is not empty, so no transfer; pop returns 2. Total stays 7.</li><li>dequeue: pop returns 3. Total stays 7.</li><li>dequeue: <code class=\"mm\">S2</code> is now empty, so the single element of <code class=\"mm\">S1</code> moves across &mdash; one push. Total 8. Pop returns 4.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">8</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Charging a transfer on every dequeue, which would give 6 pushes to <code class=\"mm\">S2</code> instead of 4. The transfer happens only when <code class=\"mm\">S2</code> is empty, which is why the amortised cost of a dequeue stays constant.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q24", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "queues-circular-queues-deques", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about queues and deques are TRUE?", "options": ["In a circular queue of array size n that leaves one slot unused, the number of stored elements is <code class=\"mm\">(rear &minus; front + n) % n</code>.", "A deque can provide both stack behaviour and queue behaviour without any additional structure.", "A circular queue that does not leave a slot unused can still distinguish a full queue from an empty one without a counter or a flag.", "A queue implemented as a singly linked list with head and tail pointers supports enqueue and dequeue in &Theta;(1) time."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The number of elements in a circular buffer is the forward distance from <code class=\"mm\">front</code> to <code class=\"mm\">rear</code>, taken modulo the array size; and a deque exposes both ends, which subsumes both restricted containers.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) TRUE.</b> The modulo keeps the count right when <code class=\"mm\">rear</code> has wrapped past <code class=\"mm\">front</code>. For <code class=\"mm\">front = 3</code>, <code class=\"mm\">rear = 1</code>, <code class=\"mm\">n = 8</code> it gives <code class=\"mm\">(1 &minus; 3 + 8) % 8 = 6</code>.</li><li><b>(B) TRUE.</b> Push and pop at the same end behave as a stack; push at one end and pop at the other behave as a queue.</li><li><b>(C) FALSE.</b> Without a spare slot, a full buffer and an empty one both satisfy <code class=\"mm\">front == rear</code>, so a counter or flag is required.</li><li><b>(D) TRUE.</b> Enqueue links the new node after the tail; dequeue unlinks the head. Both touch a constant number of pointers.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">options (A), (B) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Believing a deque is only a queue with extra features. Restricting the operations turns it into either a stack or a queue, which is why deques are used as the general building block.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q25", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "queues-circular-queues-deques", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "The integers 1 to 6 are placed in a queue in that order. The following is repeated until the queue is empty: dequeue an element and print it, then dequeue the next element and enqueue it at the rear. What is the fourth number printed?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">4</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">2</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">6</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">8</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each round removes two elements from the front and puts the second one back at the rear, so the queue loses exactly one element per round while the survivors rotate.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Round 1: print 1, move 2 to the rear. Queue: 3 4 5 6 2.</li><li>Round 2: print 3, move 4 to the rear. Queue: 5 6 2 4.</li><li>Round 3: print 5, move 6 to the rear. Queue: 2 4 6.</li><li>Round 4: print 2, move 4 to the rear. The fourth number printed is 2.</li><li>For completeness the run ends with print 6 (move 4), then print 4: the order is 1 3 5 2 6 4.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">2 (option B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Printing the odd positions 1, 3, 5 and then assuming the evens follow as 2, 4, 6 in order. The moved element goes behind the untouched ones, so 2 is printed before 6 and 4.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q26", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "arrays-multidimensional", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "An array is declared as int A[1..10][1..8]; — both subscripts are 1-based — and is stored in row-major order starting at byte address 100. Each int occupies 2 bytes. What is the byte address of A[5][3] ?", "options": ["152", "160", "168", "176"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C)</span></p><h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In row-major order the row index moves in strides of one whole row, so the flat element offset of <code class=\"mm\">A[i][j]</code> is <code class=\"mm\">(i &minus; lo<sub>1</sub>) &times; n + (j &minus; lo<sub>2</sub>)</code>, where <code class=\"mm\">lo<sub>1</sub></code> and <code class=\"mm\">lo<sub>2</sub></code> are the lower bounds and <code class=\"mm\">n</code> is the number of columns. The lower bound must be subtracted; the count of columns, not the count of rows, is the stride.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The row stride is the number of columns, 8, not the number of rows, 10.</li><li>Element offset = <code class=\"mm\">(5 &minus; 1) &times; 8 + (3 &minus; 1) = 32 + 2 = 34</code>.</li><li>Byte offset = <code class=\"mm\">34 &times; 2 = 68</code>.</li><li>Address = <code class=\"mm\">100 + 68 = 168</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Using the subscripts directly, <code class=\"mm\">5 &times; 8 + 3 = 43</code>, which silently treats the array as 0-based; or using 10 as the stride because 10 is the first bound written.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Write the offset formula with the lower bounds before touching numbers, then multiply by the element size once.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q27", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "arrays-multidimensional", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "int A[4][5]; is stored in column-major order from byte address 500, and int occupies 4 bytes. What is the byte address of A[2][3] ?", "options": ["532", "548", "556", "564"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C)</span></p><h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Column-major order stores one whole column before the next, so the column index moves in strides of the number of rows: element offset = <code class=\"mm\">j &times; m + i</code> for an <code class=\"mm\">m &times; n</code> array. Row-major would use <code class=\"mm\">i &times; n + j</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The stride here is the number of rows, 4.</li><li>Element offset = <code class=\"mm\">3 &times; 4 + 2 = 14</code>.</li><li>Byte offset = <code class=\"mm\">14 &times; 4 = 56</code>.</li><li>Address = <code class=\"mm\">500 + 56 = 556</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Applying the row-major formula <code class=\"mm\">2 &times; 5 + 3 = 13</code>, which gives 552 — the wrong stride is the whole error.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Fix the layout first, then the stride: rows in the stride means column-major, columns in the stride means row-major.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q28", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "arrays-multidimensional", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "On a 64-bit machine where int occupies 4 bytes and a pointer occupies 8 bytes, the declaration int A[4][5]; is compiled. Which one of the following is TRUE?", "options": ["sizeof(A) is 20", "sizeof(A[2]) is 20", "sizeof(&A[0]) is 20", "sizeof(A[0][0]) is 8"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B)</span></p><h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is an array of 4 arrays of 5 <code class=\"mm\">int</code>; <code class=\"mm\">A[2]</code> is one of those inner arrays, of type <code class=\"mm\">int[5]</code>; <code class=\"mm\">&amp;A[0]</code> is a pointer of type <code class=\"mm\">int (*)[5]</code>. The <code class=\"mm\">sizeof</code> operator does not let an array decay to a pointer, so it reports the whole object, while a pointer is reported by pointer width.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">sizeof(A) = 4 &times; 5 &times; 4 = 80</code> bytes, so (A) is false.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">sizeof(A[2]) = 5 &times; 4 = 20</code> bytes, so (B) is true.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">sizeof(&amp;A[0])</code> is the size of a pointer, 8 bytes, so (C) is false.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">sizeof(A[0][0])</code> is the size of one <code class=\"mm\">int</code>, 4 bytes, so (D) is false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Believing that <code class=\"mm\">A[2]</code> has already decayed to a pointer inside <code class=\"mm\">sizeof</code>. Decay happens when the array is used as a value in an expression, not as the operand of <code class=\"mm\">sizeof</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Read the type of the operand first: <code class=\"mm\">int[5]</code> is 20 bytes, <code class=\"mm\">int *</code> is 8.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q29", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "arrays-multidimensional", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A 4 × 5 array of 4-byte elements begins at the same base address in two separate programs; one stores it in row-major order and the other in column-major order. Apart from A[0][0] , which element occupies the same byte offset from the base in both layouts?", "options": ["A[1][4]", "A[2][2]", "A[3][4]", "A[4][3]"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C)</span></p><h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Row-major offset is <code class=\"mm\">i &times; n + j</code> and column-major offset is <code class=\"mm\">j &times; m + i</code>. Equality of the two offsets is an equation in the indices, and it must be solved inside the legal index ranges, which are <code class=\"mm\">0 &le; i &le; m &minus; 1</code> and <code class=\"mm\">0 &le; j &le; n &minus; 1</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>With <code class=\"mm\">m = 4</code> and <code class=\"mm\">n = 5</code>, set <code class=\"mm\">5i + j = 4j + i</code>.</li><li>Rearrange: <code class=\"mm\">4i = 3j</code>.</li><li>The solutions with <code class=\"mm\">i &le; 3</code> and <code class=\"mm\">j &le; 4</code> are <code class=\"mm\">(0, 0)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(3, 4)</code>.</li><li>Excluding the base element, the answer is <code class=\"mm\">A[3][4]</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Choosing <code class=\"mm\">A[4][3]</code> because it looks like the mirror of the right answer. The first index is a row and runs only to 3, so <code class=\"mm\">A[4][3]</code> does not exist; it would be the answer only if the array were 5 &times; 4.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> When an index equation has a symmetric-looking solution, check it against the declared bounds before believing it.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q30", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "singly-doubly-circular-linked-lists", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "In a doubly linked list, p points to a node that is neither the first nor the last. That node must be deleted, and p is the only pointer available — there is no head pointer and no size field. How many link fields must be rewritten?", "options": ["1", "2", "3", "4"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B)</span></p><h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A doubly linked node is held from both sides, so removing it requires telling both neighbours about each other: <code class=\"mm\">p-&gt;prev-&gt;next = p-&gt;next</code> and <code class=\"mm\">p-&gt;next-&gt;prev = p-&gt;prev</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Set <code class=\"mm\">p-&gt;prev-&gt;next = p-&gt;next</code> — the predecessor skips over <code class=\"mm\">p</code>.</li><li>Set <code class=\"mm\">p-&gt;next-&gt;prev = p-&gt;prev</code> — the successor skips back over <code class=\"mm\">p</code>.</li><li>Two fields are rewritten; the fields inside <code class=\"mm\">p</code> become unreachable and need no repair before the node is freed.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 1 by analogy with a singly linked list, where deleting a node with only a pointer to its predecessor costs one field. Here <code class=\"mm\">p</code> itself is the handle, and both sides must be fixed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Count the neighbours a node is referenced from, not the number of pointers you hold.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q31", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "singly-doubly-circular-linked-lists", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A singly linked list is maintained with a head pointer only — there is no tail pointer and no size field. What is the worst-case time to append a new node at the end of a list of n nodes?", "options": ["Θ(1)", "Θ(log n)", "Θ(n)", "Θ(n log n)"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C)</span></p><h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Insertion into a linked list is constant time only when the predecessor is already in hand. With a head pointer alone the last node is not addressable, so it must be found by walking.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Start at the head and follow <code class=\"mm\">next</code> until the node whose <code class=\"mm\">next</code> is <code class=\"mm\">NULL</code>.</li><li>That walk touches all <code class=\"mm\">n</code> nodes.</li><li>The link assignment itself is one operation, but the search dominates: <code class=\"mm\">&Theta;(n)</code>.</li><li>Keeping a tail pointer makes the same append <code class=\"mm\">&Theta;(1)</code>, because the old last node is then directly available.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> \"Insertion in a linked list is O(1)\" is a statement about insertion at a known position, usually the head. Without a tail pointer the position is not known.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A linked list buys O(1) insertion at the ends only if you keep pointers to the ends.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q32", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "singly-doubly-circular-linked-lists", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Binary search is run on a sorted singly linked list of n nodes. Compared with the same algorithm on a sorted array, its running time is:", "options": ["Θ(log n)", "Θ(√n)", "Θ(n)", "Θ(n log n)"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C)</span></p><h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each probe of binary search needs random access to the middle of the current range. An array gives that in <code class=\"mm\">&Theta;(1)</code>; a linked list gives it only after a walk from the start of the range, which costs time proportional to the range length.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The first probe walks about <code class=\"mm\">n/2</code> nodes, the next about <code class=\"mm\">n/4</code>, and so on.</li><li>The recurrence is <code class=\"mm\">T(n) = T(n/2) + &Theta;(n)</code>, whose solution is <code class=\"mm\">&Theta;(n)</code>.</li><li>The number of <i>comparisons</i> is still <code class=\"mm\">&Theta;(log n)</code>; the traversal cost is what changes the bound.</li><li>So the list version is <code class=\"mm\">&Theta;(n)</code>, no better than one linear scan.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting only comparisons and concluding <code class=\"mm\">&Theta;(log n)</code>. The bound must cover every operation the algorithm performs, including the ones that reach a position.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A good asymptotic bound counts the cost of reaching data, not just the cost of comparing it.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q33", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "singly-doubly-circular-linked-lists", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "The function below is called with the head of a singly linked list that holds 10 nodes and contains no cycle. What does it return? int count(struct node *h) { struct node *p = h, *q = h; int c = 0; while (p != NULL && p->next != NULL) { p = p->next->next; q = q->next; c++; } return c; }", "options": ["4", "5", "6", "10"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B)</span></p><h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The loop condition protects two dereferences: <code class=\"mm\">p</code> must exist and <code class=\"mm\">p-&gt;next</code> must exist. So the loop body runs while <code class=\"mm\">p</code> is one of the first <code class=\"mm\">n &minus; 1</code> nodes, and each iteration advances <code class=\"mm\">p</code> by two nodes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Iteration 1: <code class=\"mm\">p</code> moves from node 1 to node 3, <code class=\"mm\">c = 1</code>.</li><li>Iteration 2: <code class=\"mm\">p</code> 3 &rarr; 5, <code class=\"mm\">c = 2</code>.</li><li>Iteration 3: <code class=\"mm\">p</code> 5 &rarr; 7, <code class=\"mm\">c = 3</code>.</li><li>Iteration 4: <code class=\"mm\">p</code> 7 &rarr; 9, <code class=\"mm\">c = 4</code>.</li><li>Iteration 5: node 9 still has a successor, so the body runs again and <code class=\"mm\">p</code> becomes <code class=\"mm\">NULL</code>, <code class=\"mm\">c = 5</code>. The condition now fails on <code class=\"mm\">p != NULL</code>.</li><li>The function returns 5, which is <code class=\"mm\">n/2</code> for <code class=\"mm\">n = 10</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Stopping at <code class=\"mm\">c = 4</code> by assuming the loop ends when <code class=\"mm\">p</code> reaches the last node. It ends one iteration later, when <code class=\"mm\">p</code> steps past it, because node 9 does have a successor.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Trace the guard, not the intent: <code class=\"mm\">p-&gt;next != NULL</code> allows one more jump from the last node.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q34", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "stacks-applications-infix-postfix", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which one of the following is not an application of a stack?", "options": ["Converting an infix expression to postfix", "Checking whether the parentheses in an expression are balanced", "Round-robin CPU scheduling", "Evaluating a postfix expression"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C)</span></p><h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A stack is last-in-first-out and is used wherever the most recent unfinished item must be resumed first — nested brackets, nested calls, the rightmost pending operator. Round-robin scheduling keeps processes in arrival order and resumes the oldest one, which is first-in-first-out.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Infix to postfix: the pending operators are held on a stack and the most recent one is popped first.</li><li>Bracket matching: each closing bracket must match the most recently opened one, a LIFO match.</li><li>Postfix evaluation: the two most recently seen operands are consumed by each operator.</li><li>Round-robin scheduling: processes leave in the order they arrived, which is a queue.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Treating every \"data structure\" answer as interchangeable. The test is the order of resumption: LIFO is a stack, FIFO is a queue.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Ask which item must be served first; the answer names the structure.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q35", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "stacks-applications-infix-postfix", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "The postfix expression A B C + * D - is equivalent to which one of the following infix expressions?", "options": ["A * B + C - D", "(A * (B + C)) - D", "A * (B + (C - D))", "((A * B) + C) - D"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B)</span></p><h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Postfix is read left to right; operands are pushed and an operator pops the two most recent operands, taking the <i>second</i> popped as its left operand. The parenthesisation is therefore fixed by the order of the operators, not by any convention about precedence.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Push <code class=\"mm\">A</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C</code>.</li><li>The first operator is <code class=\"mm\">+</code>: it pops <code class=\"mm\">C</code> then <code class=\"mm\">B</code>, giving the subexpression <code class=\"mm\">(B + C)</code>.</li><li>The next operator is <code class=\"mm\">*</code>: it pops <code class=\"mm\">(B + C)</code> then <code class=\"mm\">A</code>, giving <code class=\"mm\">(A * (B + C))</code>.</li><li>The last operator is <code class=\"mm\">-</code>: it pops <code class=\"mm\">D</code> then <code class=\"mm\">(A * (B + C))</code>, giving <code class=\"mm\">(A * (B + C)) - D</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reading the trailing <code class=\"mm\">D -</code> as <code class=\"mm\">D</code> subtracted from the whole, or as <code class=\"mm\">(C &minus; D)</code> — the second operand popped is the left operand, and <code class=\"mm\">D</code> is the second operand here.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> In postfix conversion the operator always takes the last two things on the stack, in the order they were pushed.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q36", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "stacks-applications-infix-postfix", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Two stacks share a single array of 20 slots, indexed 0 to 19. Stack 1 grows upward from index 0 and its top is currently at index 8; stack 2 grows downward from index 19 and its top is currently at index 10. How many more pushes can be accepted before an overflow?", "options": ["0", "1", "2", "11"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B)</span></p><h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The two stacks collide when the top of the upward stack and the top of the downward stack become adjacent. Every index strictly between the two tops is free, so the free count is <code class=\"mm\">top<sub>2</sub> &minus; top<sub>1</sub> &minus; 1</code>, and the overflow test is <code class=\"mm\">top<sub>1</sub> + 1 == top<sub>2</sub></code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Stack 1 occupies indices 0 to 8; stack 2 occupies indices 10 to 19.</li><li>Index 9 is the only slot that belongs to neither stack.</li><li>Free slots = <code class=\"mm\">10 &minus; 8 &minus; 1 = 1</code>.</li><li>One more push fills index 9 and the next one overflows.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Computing <code class=\"mm\">19 &minus; 8 = 11</code> and forgetting that the eleven indices above stack 1 are mostly occupied by stack 2. Only the gap between the two tops is available.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> In a shared array the capacity is the gap between the tops, not the distance from one top to the end of the array.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q37", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "stacks-applications-infix-postfix", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "The postfix expression 3 4 2 * 1 5 - 2 3 ^ ^ / + is evaluated with a stack. What is the maximum number of elements held on the stack at any instant?", "options": ["3", "4", "5", "6"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C)</span></p><h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> While scanning postfix, a stack element is added by every operand and removed twice and replaced once by every binary operator. The peak depth therefore depends on where the operands cluster, and must be read off a trace rather than guessed from the number of operands.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">3</code> &rarr; depth 1; <code class=\"mm\">4</code> &rarr; 2; <code class=\"mm\">2</code> &rarr; 3.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">*</code> pops two and pushes one &rarr; depth 2 (stack <code class=\"mm\">3, 8</code>).</li><li><code class=\"mm\">1</code> &rarr; 3; <code class=\"mm\">5</code> &rarr; 4; <code class=\"mm\">-</code> &rarr; 3 (stack <code class=\"mm\">3, 8, &minus;4</code>).</li><li><code class=\"mm\">2</code> &rarr; 4; <code class=\"mm\">3</code> &rarr; 5 — this is the peak.</li><li>The remaining operators only reduce the depth: <code class=\"mm\">^</code> &rarr; 4, <code class=\"mm\">^</code> &rarr; 3, <code class=\"mm\">/</code> &rarr; 2, <code class=\"mm\">+</code> &rarr; 1.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reporting 4 by forgetting that <code class=\"mm\">2</code> and <code class=\"mm\">3</code> are both pushed before the second <code class=\"mm\">^</code> is applied, or counting the final single result.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For a peak-depth question, write the depth after every token; never estimate from the number of operators.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q38", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "queues-circular-queues-deques", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following supports insertion and deletion at both ends in Θ(1) worst-case time?", "options": ["A singly linked list with a head pointer only", "An array with a single front index", "A doubly linked list with head and tail pointers", "A binary min-heap"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C)</span></p><h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A deque needs a handle on both ends, and every node on the end being changed must have a pointer to its inward neighbour. A doubly linked list supplies that from either side; a singly linked list does not, because a node has no way back.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Singly linked list with head only: removal at the tail needs a full walk, <code class=\"mm\">&Theta;(n)</code>.</li><li>Array with one front index: removing at the front forces a shift, <code class=\"mm\">&Theta;(n)</code>.</li><li>Doubly linked list with head and tail: each end operation rewrites a constant number of pointers, <code class=\"mm\">&Theta;(1)</code>.</li><li>A binary heap supports the minimum only, and its other end is not a deque end.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming a singly linked list with a tail pointer would do. A tail pointer finds the last node but cannot reach its predecessor, so tail deletion still costs a walk.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A double-ended structure needs backward links, not just end pointers.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q39", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "queues-circular-queues-deques", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A circular array of 8 slots (indices 0 to 7) implements a queue in which one slot is always left unused. front is the index of the first element, count is the number of elements, and rear is the index of the next free slot. With front = 3 and count = 5 , what is rear ?", "options": ["0", "1", "7", "8"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A)</span></p><h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a circular array every index is reduced modulo the array size. Since <code class=\"mm\">count</code> elements occupy the slots <code class=\"mm\">front, front + 1, &hellip;, front + count &minus; 1</code>, the next free slot is <code class=\"mm\">(front + count) mod 8</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">front + count = 3 + 5 = 8</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">8 mod 8 = 0</code>.</li><li>Check the occupancy: the five elements sit in slots 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, so the next free slot is 0 — the queue has wrapped exactly once.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 8, which is not an index of an array of 8 slots. The wrap is the whole point of the circular representation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Reduce modulo the size before interpreting an index, never after.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q40", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "queues-circular-queues-deques", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A deque is initially empty. The operations push_back(1) , push_back(2) , push_back(3) , push_back(4) , push_back(5) , pop_front() , pop_front() , push_front(6) , pop_back() , pop_back() are performed in that order. What remains, from front to back?", "options": ["3, 4", "6, 3", "6, 4", "4, 5"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B)</span></p><h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A deque has four distinct ends: <code class=\"mm\">push_front</code> and <code class=\"mm\">push_back</code> add at opposite ends, and <code class=\"mm\">pop_front</code> and <code class=\"mm\">pop_back</code> remove from opposite ends. Keeping the front and the back apart is the only thing the question tests.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>After the five <code class=\"mm\">push_back</code> calls: <code class=\"mm\">1, 2, 3, 4, 5</code>.</li><li>Two <code class=\"mm\">pop_front</code> calls remove 1 and 2: <code class=\"mm\">3, 4, 5</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">push_front(6)</code> adds at the front: <code class=\"mm\">6, 3, 4, 5</code>.</li><li>Two <code class=\"mm\">pop_back</code> calls remove 5 and 4: <code class=\"mm\">6, 3</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Treating <code class=\"mm\">push_front(6)</code> as an append, which leaves <code class=\"mm\">3, 4, 5, 6</code> and, after two pops at the back, <code class=\"mm\">3, 4</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Write the deque out after each operation; four ends are easy to confuse in the head.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q41", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "arrays-multidimensional", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "The lower triangle, diagonal included, of an n × n symmetric matrix is stored row by row in a one-dimensional array T with 1-based indexing, T[1] holding the element (1, 1) . Which of the following statements are TRUE?", "options": ["For n = 10 the array needs 55 locations.", "For i ≥ j the element (i, j) is stored at T[i(i − 1)/2 + j] .", "For n = 100 the array needs 5050 locations.", "This packing halves the storage requirement only when n is even."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B), (C)</span></p><h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A symmetric matrix satisfies <code class=\"mm\">A[i][j] = A[j][i]</code>, so the lower triangle carries all the information. Row <code class=\"mm\">i</code> of the triangle holds <code class=\"mm\">i</code> elements, so the total is <code class=\"mm\">1 + 2 + &hellip; + n = n(n + 1)/2</code>, and the element <code class=\"mm\">(i, j)</code> with <code class=\"mm\">i &ge; j</code> is preceded by all complete rows above it plus <code class=\"mm\">j &minus; 1</code> elements of its own row.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) TRUE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">10 &times; 11 / 2 = 55</code>.</li><li><b>(B) TRUE.</b> Rows 1 to <code class=\"mm\">i &minus; 1</code> hold <code class=\"mm\">i(i &minus; 1)/2</code> elements, and <code class=\"mm\">(i, j)</code> is the <code class=\"mm\">j</code>-th in row <code class=\"mm\">i</code>, so the 1-based position is <code class=\"mm\">i(i &minus; 1)/2 + j</code>. Check: <code class=\"mm\">(3, 2)</code> gives <code class=\"mm\">3 + 2 = 5</code>, and the row layout is <code class=\"mm\">1 | 2 3 | 4 5 6</code>, so the fifth entry is indeed <code class=\"mm\">(3, 2)</code>.</li><li><b>(C) TRUE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">100 &times; 101 / 2 = 5050</code>.</li><li><b>(D) FALSE.</b> The saving is <code class=\"mm\">n<sup>2</sup> &minus; n(n + 1)/2 = n(n &minus; 1)/2</code> entries for every <code class=\"mm\">n</code>, and the ratio tends to one half as <code class=\"mm\">n</code> grows. Parity plays no part.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Checking only the closed-form count and forgetting to verify the index formula on an actual element, or assuming the ratio is exactly one half when it is only asymptotically one half.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For a packed triangular array, verify the index formula on a small element in the middle of the triangle.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q42", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "singly-doubly-circular-linked-lists", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A circular doubly linked list of n nodes is maintained with a head pointer that is never NULL . Which of the following statements are TRUE?", "options": ["The last node's next field points to the head node.", "head->prev points to the last node.", "Walking forward from the head with next pointers returns to the head after exactly n steps.", "Inserting a new node immediately before the head requires rewriting exactly two link fields."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B), (C)</span></p><h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a circular list the ring is closed in both directions: the last node's <code class=\"mm\">next</code> is the head and the head's <code class=\"mm\">prev</code> is the last node. In a doubly linked structure every node is referenced from two sides, so an insertion or a deletion touches both neighbours plus the new node's own two fields.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) TRUE.</b> That link is what makes the list circular.</li><li><b>(B) TRUE.</b> The backward ring mirrors the forward one.</li><li><b>(C) TRUE.</b> Each step visits one new node, and after <code class=\"mm\">n</code> steps the walk has returned to the starting node.</li><li><b>(D) FALSE.</b> Four fields change: <code class=\"mm\">new-&gt;next</code>, <code class=\"mm\">new-&gt;prev</code>, <code class=\"mm\">head-&gt;prev-&gt;next</code> and <code class=\"mm\">head-&gt;prev</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting only the two writes into the new node and forgetting the two neighbours, or thinking (C) needs n + 1 steps because the walk returns to the head \"after\" the last node.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Count fields by counting references: a doubly linked insertion touches four.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q43", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "stacks-applications-infix-postfix", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A stack is implemented with a singly linked list in which every push inserts at the head and every pop removes the head. Which of the following statements are TRUE?", "options": ["push runs in Θ(1) worst-case time.", "pop runs in Θ(1) worst-case time.", "Pushing at the tail would also run in Θ(1) without a tail pointer.", "The stack can overflow only when memory is exhausted."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (D)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B), (D)</span></p><h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The head of a singly linked list is the only position that is directly addressable, which is exactly what a stack needs: the top must be reachable in constant time. A linked implementation has no fixed capacity, so the classical \"stack overflow\" of an array-backed stack becomes allocation failure.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) TRUE.</b> Inserting at the head rewrites one pointer.</li><li><b>(B) TRUE.</b> Removing the head rewrites one pointer and frees one node.</li><li><b>(C) FALSE.</b> Without a tail pointer, reaching the last node costs a walk of <code class=\"mm\">&Theta;(n)</code>.</li><li><b>(D) TRUE.</b> The size is limited by available memory, not by a declared capacity.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Carrying the array's overflow notion over to a linked stack, or assuming the tail is as cheap as the head.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A linked stack is the head of a list; that single fact answers cost, capacity and correctness together.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q44", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "queues-circular-queues-deques", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "The maximum of every window of size k in an array of n values is computed with a deque that stores indices. Before each insertion, the indices at the back whose values are less than or equal to the incoming value are removed; the index at the front is removed once it has left the window. Which of the following statements are TRUE?", "options": ["The deque holds at most k indices at any time.", "The values at the indices in the deque increase from front to back.", "Each index enters the deque once and leaves it at most once, so the total work is Θ(n) .", "The front of the deque always holds the index of the maximum of the current window."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C), (D)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C), (D)</span></p><h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The deque keeps only the indices that can still become a window maximum: a value that is smaller and older than a later value can never be the answer again, so it is discarded at the back. What survives is a decreasing sequence of values whose indices increase — a monotone deque.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) TRUE.</b> Only indices inside the current window are retained, and the window holds <code class=\"mm\">k</code> positions.</li><li><b>(B) FALSE.</b> The values <i>decrease</i> from front to back: the front is the largest. Any value greater than its predecessor would have evicted that predecessor at the back.</li><li><b>(C) TRUE.</b> Every index is pushed once, and each index is removed at most once, so the amortised cost per step is constant and the total is <code class=\"mm\">&Theta;(n)</code>.</li><li><b>(D) TRUE.</b> Discarding smaller values at the back and expired indices at the front leaves exactly the largest value of the window at the front.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reading the monotonicity in the wrong direction, or thinking the deque costs <code class=\"mm\">&Theta;(nk)</code> because the removals look like extra work.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> In an amortised structure, count how often each element can enter and leave, not how many operations one step may perform.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q45", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "queues-circular-queues-deques", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements are TRUE?", "options": ["A queue returns elements in the reverse of the order in which they were inserted.", "A deque allows insertion and deletion at both ends.", "In a circular queue, no element has to be shifted when an element is removed from the front.", "A queue built on a singly linked list with both head and tail pointers enqueues in Θ(n) time."], "answer_display": "Options (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (B), (C)</span></p><h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A queue is first-in-first-out: the element inserted earliest leaves first. A circular representation removes the shifting that a plain array would need, and a tail pointer makes the enqueue a constant-time operation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) FALSE.</b> Reversal is the behaviour of a stack, not a queue.</li><li><b>(B) TRUE.</b> That is the definition of a double-ended queue.</li><li><b>(C) TRUE.</b> The front index simply advances modulo the array size.</li><li><b>(D) FALSE.</b> With a tail pointer the insertion is <code class=\"mm\">&Theta;(1)</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Confusing FIFO with LIFO in the first statement, or assuming that any linked-list operation is linear.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Queue means FIFO; the data structure chosen to implement it should not change that.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q46", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "arrays-multidimensional", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "How many elements does the array int A[-2..5][3..7][1..4]; hold?", "options": [], "answer_display": "160", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 160</span></p><h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The number of values along one dimension is <code class=\"mm\">upper &minus; lower + 1</code>, and the total is the product of the three counts. Negative lower bounds are allowed and change only the count, not the address formula.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>First dimension: <code class=\"mm\">5 &minus; (&minus;2) + 1 = 8</code>.</li><li>Second dimension: <code class=\"mm\">7 &minus; 3 + 1 = 5</code>.</li><li>Third dimension: <code class=\"mm\">4 &minus; 1 + 1 = 4</code>.</li><li>Total = <code class=\"mm\">8 &times; 5 &times; 4 = 160</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Using the upper bounds themselves as counts, <code class=\"mm\">5 &times; 7 &times; 4 = 140</code>, or <code class=\"mm\">7 &times; 4 &times; 3 = 84</code> by dropping the negative sign.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Count per dimension with the +1, then multiply.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q47", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "singly-doubly-circular-linked-lists", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A circular singly linked list has 12 nodes, and each node stores only data and next . You are given a pointer p to one node, but not the head. What is the minimum number of next -pointer steps needed to reach the node whose next field points to p ?", "options": [], "answer_display": "11", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 11</span></p><h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a circular list every node is reachable from every other node by following <code class=\"mm\">next</code>. The predecessor of <code class=\"mm\">p</code> is the last distinct node on the way round, so it is reached after <code class=\"mm\">n &minus; 1</code> steps, and no shorter walk exists because a singly linked list cannot go backwards.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Start at <code class=\"mm\">p</code>, which is step 0.</li><li>Following <code class=\"mm\">next</code> visits the 11 other nodes in turn.</li><li>The eleventh distinct node visited has <code class=\"mm\">next == p</code>, so it is the predecessor.</li><li>Answer: 11 steps.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 12 by counting the return to <code class=\"mm\">p</code> itself, which is one step beyond the predecessor.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> On a ring of n nodes the predecessor of a node is n − 1 steps ahead, and the node itself is n steps ahead.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q48", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "stacks-applications-infix-postfix", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "The infix expression A - B + C * (D - E) + F is converted to postfix with the standard operator-stack algorithm. Counting only operators that are popped from the stack — the parenthesis that is discarded on ) is not counted — how many pops occur in total, including the flush of the stack at the end?", "options": [], "answer_display": "5", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 5</span></p><h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The algorithm pops while the operator on top of the stack has precedence greater than or equal to the incoming operator, and pops everything down to the matching <code class=\"mm\">(</code> when a <code class=\"mm\">)</code> arrives. Equal precedence pops too, because subtraction and addition are left-associative.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">-</code> is pushed; on <code class=\"mm\">+</code> the top <code class=\"mm\">-</code> has equal precedence, so it is popped (1).</li><li><code class=\"mm\">*</code> is pushed above <code class=\"mm\">+</code>; <code class=\"mm\">(</code> is pushed; <code class=\"mm\">-</code> is pushed inside the bracket.</li><li>On <code class=\"mm\">)</code> the inner <code class=\"mm\">-</code> is popped (2); the <code class=\"mm\">(</code> is then discarded and is not an operator.</li><li>On the second <code class=\"mm\">+</code>: <code class=\"mm\">*</code> is popped (3) and then <code class=\"mm\">+</code> is popped (4).</li><li>At the end the remaining <code class=\"mm\">+</code> is popped (5). Total 5.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Forgetting the flush at the end (giving 4), or forgetting that equal precedence pops, so that the first <code class=\"mm\">+</code> leaves the <code class=\"mm\">-</code> on the stack (giving 3).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Trace the stack token by token, and remember that left-associative operators pop their equals.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q49", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "stacks-applications-infix-postfix", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "The integers 1, 2, 3, 4 are pushed onto an initially empty stack in that order, each exactly once, and elements may be popped at any time, including between pushes. How many different output sequences can be produced?", "options": [], "answer_display": "14", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 14</span></p><h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The number of distinct permutations of <code class=\"mm\">1 &hellip; n</code> obtainable from a single stack with a fixed input order is the <code class=\"mm\">n</code>-th Catalan number <code class=\"mm\">C<sub>n</sub> = (1/(n+1)) &times; binom(2n, n)</code>. Each push/pop schedule corresponds to a balanced parenthesis string of length <code class=\"mm\">2n</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>For <code class=\"mm\">n = 4</code>: <code class=\"mm\">binom(8, 4) = 70</code>.</li><li>Divide by <code class=\"mm\">n + 1 = 5</code>: <code class=\"mm\">70 / 5 = 14</code>.</li><li>Sanity check by enumeration: starting from 1 and repeatedly either pushing the next integer or popping, exactly 14 distinct output orders arise; for example 4, 3, 2, 1 is possible and 4, 1, 2, 3 is not.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering <code class=\"mm\">4! = 24</code> by assuming every permutation is reachable, or <code class=\"mm\">2<sup>3</sup> = 8</code> by counting the choice points instead of the outcomes. Permutations such as <code class=\"mm\">3, 1, 2, 4</code> are impossible.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Stack-ordered permutations are counted by Catalan numbers, not by factorials.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q50", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "queues-circular-queues-deques", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A queue is built from two stacks S1 and S2 . Enqueue pushes onto S1 . Dequeue pops from S2 , and only when S2 is empty is every element of S1 popped and pushed onto S2 before the pop. Starting from empty, the operations are: enqueue 1, enqueue 2, enqueue 3, dequeue, enqueue 4, dequeue, dequeue. How many elements remain in the queue afterwards?", "options": [], "answer_display": "1", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1</span></p><h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The transfer of <code class=\"mm\">S1</code> into <code class=\"mm\">S2</code> reverses the order twice — once going in and once coming out — which is exactly the FIFO behaviour a queue needs. The elements are split between the two stacks at any moment, so the queue's size is <code class=\"mm\">|S1| + |S2|</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Enqueue 1, 2, 3: <code class=\"mm\">S1 = [1, 2, 3]</code> with 3 on top, <code class=\"mm\">S2</code> empty.</li><li>Dequeue: transfer gives <code class=\"mm\">S2 = [3, 2, 1]</code> with 1 on top, then pop returns 1. <code class=\"mm\">S2 = [3, 2]</code>.</li><li>Enqueue 4: <code class=\"mm\">S1 = [4]</code>.</li><li>Dequeue returns 2 from <code class=\"mm\">S2</code>; dequeue returns 3. <code class=\"mm\">S2</code> is now empty.</li><li>Remaining: <code class=\"mm\">S1 = [4]</code>, so 1 element.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Forgetting that enqueue goes to S1 while S2 still holds elements, which leads to counting 2, or assuming the transfer happens on every dequeue.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Track both stacks separately; the queue is their union, and the transfer happens only when S2 is empty.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-Q01", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "binary-tree-properties", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "In a binary tree the height of a node is the number of edges on the longest downward path from that node to a leaf, and the height of the tree is the height of the root. What are the minimum and the maximum number of nodes in a binary tree of height 5?", "options": ["6 and 63", "5 and 63", "6 and 32", "7 and 64"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A binary tree of height <code class=\"mm\">h</code> spans levels <code class=\"mm\">0</code> to <code class=\"mm\">h</code>, that is <code class=\"mm\">h + 1</code> levels. The fewest nodes come from a single chain, the most from a perfect tree in which every level is completely filled.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Minimum: one node per level, so <code class=\"mm\">h + 1 = 6</code> nodes.</li><li>Maximum: level <code class=\"mm\">i</code> holds at most <code class=\"mm\">2^i</code> nodes, so the total is <code class=\"mm\">2^0 + 2^1 + &hellip; + 2^5 = 2^6 &minus; 1 = 63</code>.</li><li>The pair is therefore 6 and 63.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; 6 and 63</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Using <code class=\"mm\">2^h &minus; 1 = 31</code> or <code class=\"mm\">2^h = 32</code> for the maximum, which counts levels <code class=\"mm\">1</code> to <code class=\"mm\">h</code> and drops level 0, or giving 5 as the minimum by treating the height as the number of levels.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Height counted in edges means <code class=\"mm\">h + 1</code> levels, so the maximum is always <code class=\"mm\">2^(h+1) &minus; 1</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-Q02", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "binary-tree-properties", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A complete binary tree has 15 nodes. The root is at level 0 and the height is the number of edges on the longest root-to-leaf path. What is the height of this tree?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">2</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">4</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">7</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">3</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a complete binary tree every level except possibly the last is full, and the last level is filled from the left. So the node count of a complete tree of height <code class=\"mm\">h</code> lies between <code class=\"mm\">2^h</code> and <code class=\"mm\">2^(h+1) &minus; 1</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Levels 0 to 2 can hold at most <code class=\"mm\">1 + 2 + 4 = 7</code> nodes, which is fewer than 15.</li><li>Levels 0 to 3 can hold at most <code class=\"mm\">1 + 2 + 4 + 8 = 15</code> nodes, exactly 15.</li><li>So the tree is the perfect tree with four complete levels and its height is 3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) &mdash; 3</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 4 by counting the levels <code class=\"mm\">0, 1, 2, 3</code> instead of the edges, or answering 7 by taking <code class=\"mm\">log2 15</code> rounded up as the height.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <code class=\"mm\">n</code> nodes in a complete tree give height <code class=\"mm\">&lfloor;log2 n&rfloor; = 3</code> when the count is exactly <code class=\"mm\">2^(h+1) &minus; 1</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-Q03", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "binary-tree-properties", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A full binary tree is one in which every node has either 0 or 2 children. If such a tree has 41 leaves, how many nodes does it have in total?", "options": [], "answer_display": "81", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a full (proper) binary tree the number of leaves and the number of internal nodes differ by exactly one: <code class=\"mm\">L = I + 1</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>With <code class=\"mm\">L = 41</code>, the internal nodes are <code class=\"mm\">I = L &minus; 1 = 40</code>.</li><li>Total nodes <code class=\"mm\">n = L + I = 41 + 40 = 81</code>.</li><li>Equivalently <code class=\"mm\">n = 2L &minus; 1 = 81</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 81</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reporting 82 by using <code class=\"mm\">2L</code>, or 40 by answering with the number of internal nodes instead of the total.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Full tree: <code class=\"mm\">L = I + 1</code> and <code class=\"mm\">n = 2L &minus; 1</code>. Both follow from counting edges two ways.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-Q04", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "binary-tree-properties", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A complete binary tree with 100 nodes is stored in a 1-indexed array A[1..100]: the root is A[1], and the children of the node at index i are at 2i and 2i + 1. How many of the 100 array cells hold leaves?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">50</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">49</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">51</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">99</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In the array form of a complete binary tree the internal nodes occupy the first <code class=\"mm\">&lfloor;n/2&rfloor;</code> cells, because a node has a child exactly when <code class=\"mm\">2i &le; n</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Internal nodes: <code class=\"mm\">&lfloor;100/2&rfloor; = 50</code>, occupying <code class=\"mm\">A[1]</code> to <code class=\"mm\">A[50]</code>.</li><li>Leaves: <code class=\"mm\">100 &minus; 50 = 50</code>, occupying <code class=\"mm\">A[51]</code> to <code class=\"mm\">A[100]</code>.</li><li>Check: <code class=\"mm\">A[50]</code> has children <code class=\"mm\">A[100]</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A[101]</code>, and only the first exists, so the boundary is right.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; 50</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reporting 49 by assuming a node needs both children to count as internal, or 51 by rounding the half upwards.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Leaves <code class=\"mm\">= &lceil;n/2&rceil;</code> and internal nodes <code class=\"mm\">= &lfloor;n/2&rfloor;</code> for any complete binary tree.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-Q05", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "binary-tree-properties", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "For a non-empty binary tree let n be the number of nodes, n0 the number of leaves, n1 the number of nodes with exactly one child, n2 the number with two children, and h the height in edges. Which of the following are always TRUE?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">n0 = n2 + 1</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">n = n0 + n1 + n2</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">h &ge; &lceil;log2(n + 1)&rceil; &minus; 1</code>", "Every level <code class=\"mm\">i</code> (root at level 0) holds exactly <code class=\"mm\">2^i</code> nodes."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The first two identities come from counting the nodes and then the edges of the same tree two different ways; the height bound comes from the largest tree a given height can hold.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(B) Every node falls in exactly one class, so <code class=\"mm\">n = n0 + n1 + n2</code>.</li><li>A tree with <code class=\"mm\">n</code> nodes has <code class=\"mm\">n &minus; 1</code> edges. Counting edges by parent type gives <code class=\"mm\">n1 + 2n2</code>, hence <code class=\"mm\">n &minus; 1 = n1 + 2n2</code>.</li><li>Substituting (B) into that equation gives <code class=\"mm\">n0 + n1 + n2 &minus; 1 = n1 + 2n2</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">n0 = n2 + 1</code>: (A) holds.</li><li>A tree of height <code class=\"mm\">h</code> holds at most <code class=\"mm\">2^(h+1) &minus; 1</code> nodes, so <code class=\"mm\">n &le; 2^(h+1) &minus; 1</code>, giving <code class=\"mm\">h &ge; log2(n + 1) &minus; 1</code> and, since <code class=\"mm\">h</code> is an integer, <code class=\"mm\">h &ge; &lceil;log2(n + 1)&rceil; &minus; 1</code>: (C) holds.</li><li>(D) fails for a chain: it has one node per level, not <code class=\"mm\">2^i</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B), (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Accepting (D) because it is true for a perfect tree and forgetting that a general binary tree may leave levels half empty.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <code class=\"mm\">n0 = n2 + 1</code> holds for every binary tree, not only for full ones.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-Q06", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "binary-tree-properties", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "How many structurally different binary trees with exactly 5 nodes are there, where a left child and a right child are treated as different?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">14</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">42</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">120</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">132</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Ordered binary tree shapes with <code class=\"mm\">n</code> nodes are counted by the Catalan numbers <code class=\"mm\">C_n = C(2n, n)/(n + 1)</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The first Catalan numbers are <code class=\"mm\">C_0 = 1</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C_1 = 1</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C_2 = 2</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C_3 = 5</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C_4 = 14</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C_5 = 42</code>.</li><li>With <code class=\"mm\">n = 5</code>: <code class=\"mm\">C(10, 5) = 252</code> and <code class=\"mm\">252 / 6 = 42</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; 42</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Giving 14, which is the count for 4 nodes, or 120 = <code class=\"mm\">5!</code>, which counts label orders rather than shapes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Shapes with <code class=\"mm\">n</code> nodes: Catalan <code class=\"mm\">C_n</code>; labelled trees are a different, larger count.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-Q07", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "binary-tree-properties", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Each node of a binary tree is stored in a record with three pointer fields: left, right and parent. For a tree with n ≥ 1 nodes, how many of the 3n pointer fields are NULL?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">n + 1</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">2n + 2</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">n &minus; 1</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">n + 2</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Count the NULLs field by field. Every node except the root is somebody's child, so the child pointers that are set number exactly <code class=\"mm\">n &minus; 1</code> out of <code class=\"mm\">2n</code>. The parent pointer is set for every node except the root.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Left and right fields together: <code class=\"mm\">2n</code> fields, of which <code class=\"mm\">n &minus; 1</code> point at a child (each edge is stored once), so <code class=\"mm\">2n &minus; (n &minus; 1) = n + 1</code> are NULL.</li><li>Parent fields: <code class=\"mm\">n</code> fields, of which <code class=\"mm\">n &minus; 1</code> are set, so exactly 1 (the root's) is NULL.</li><li>Total NULLs <code class=\"mm\">(n + 1) + 1 = n + 2</code>.</li><li>Check on a single node: <code class=\"mm\">n = 1</code> gives 3 NULLs, and <code class=\"mm\">1 + 2 = 3</code> agrees.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) &mdash; n + 2</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering <code class=\"mm\">n + 1</code> by quoting the familiar result for the two child fields and forgetting that the <code class=\"mm\">parent</code> field of the root is also NULL.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <code class=\"mm\">n + 1</code> NULLs is a statement about child links only; add one more per extra upward link.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-Q08", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "tree-traversals-inorder-pre-post", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A binary tree with distinct keys has preorder A B D E C F and inorder D B E A C F. What is its postorder traversal?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">D E B F C A</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">D E B C F A</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">E D B F C A</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">D B E F C A</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The first key of the preorder is the root; its position in the inorder splits the remaining keys into the left and the right subtree.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Root is <code class=\"mm\">A</code>. Inorder <code class=\"mm\">D B E | A | C F</code> gives left <code class=\"mm\">{D, B, E}</code> and right <code class=\"mm\">{C, F}</code>.</li><li>Left subtree: preorder <code class=\"mm\">B D E</code> makes <code class=\"mm\">B</code> the root; inorder <code class=\"mm\">D B E</code> puts <code class=\"mm\">D</code> on the left and <code class=\"mm\">E</code> on the right.</li><li>Right subtree: preorder <code class=\"mm\">C F</code> makes <code class=\"mm\">C</code> the root; inorder <code class=\"mm\">C F</code> puts <code class=\"mm\">F</code> on the right, so <code class=\"mm\">C</code> has no left child.</li><li>Postorder = left, right, root: <code class=\"mm\">D E B</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">F C</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">A</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; D E B F C A</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Option (B) reverses the last two keys by placing <code class=\"mm\">C</code> before <code class=\"mm\">F</code>, which happens if the right subtree is read as a left chain.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Rebuild the shape from preorder plus inorder first, then read off the postorder; never try to transform the strings directly.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-Q09", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "tree-traversals-inorder-pre-post", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A binary tree with 7 nodes has level-order traversal A B C D E F G and inorder traversal D B E A F C G. What is its preorder traversal?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">A B C D E F G</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">A D B E C F G</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">A B D E C F G</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">A B D E F C G</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Level order fixes the parent-child relation level by level, and the inorder then fixes which child sits on which side.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Level order starts with <code class=\"mm\">A</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is the root, and the next level is <code class=\"mm\">B</code> and <code class=\"mm\">C</code>, its two children.</li><li>Next level <code class=\"mm\">D E F G</code>: <code class=\"mm\">D, E</code> are the children of <code class=\"mm\">B</code> and <code class=\"mm\">F, G</code> the children of <code class=\"mm\">C</code>.</li><li>Inorder <code class=\"mm\">D B E | A | F C G</code> confirms the sides: <code class=\"mm\">B</code> has <code class=\"mm\">D</code> left and <code class=\"mm\">E</code> right; <code class=\"mm\">C</code> has <code class=\"mm\">F</code> left and <code class=\"mm\">G</code> right.</li><li>Preorder = root, left, right: <code class=\"mm\">A B D E C F G</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; A B D E C F G</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Option (A) simply repeats the level order, and option (D) drops <code class=\"mm\">C</code> behind <code class=\"mm\">E</code> by treating the right subtree as part of the left one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Level order plus inorder is enough; the level order gives the shape, the inorder the orientation.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-Q10", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "tree-traversals-inorder-pre-post", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A binary tree with the distinct keys A, B, C has preorder A B C and postorder C B A. Left and right children are distinguished. How many different binary trees satisfy both traversals together?", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Preorder and postorder together do not determine a binary tree whenever some node has a single child: the two traversals say which node is whose child, but not on which side.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Preorder <code class=\"mm\">A B C</code> forces <code class=\"mm\">A</code> to be the root, <code class=\"mm\">B</code> a child of <code class=\"mm\">A</code> and <code class=\"mm\">C</code> a child of <code class=\"mm\">B</code>.</li><li>Any such chain has postorder <code class=\"mm\">C B A</code> whatever the sides, because each node is visited after its only child.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">B</code> may be a left or a right child of <code class=\"mm\">A</code>: 2 choices. <code class=\"mm\">C</code> may be a left or a right child of <code class=\"mm\">B</code>: 2 choices.</li><li>Total <code class=\"mm\">2 &times; 2 = 4</code> trees: <code class=\"mm\">A&rarr;B&rarr;C</code> as left-left, left-right, right-left and right-right chains.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 1, assuming two traversals always pin the tree down; that is true only when no node has exactly one child.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The pair (preorder, postorder) is unique exactly when every internal node has two children.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-Q11", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "tree-traversals-inorder-pre-post", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A binary search tree holds distinct integer keys. Which traversal lists the keys in increasing order?", "options": ["preorder", "postorder", "level order", "inorder"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a BST every key in the left subtree is smaller than the node and every key in the right subtree is larger. Inorder visits left subtree, then node, then right subtree.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Inorder emits the whole left subtree first, and by induction it is ascending and every key in it is below the node.</li><li>Then the node, then the right subtree, which is ascending and entirely above the node.</li><li>The concatenation is therefore ascending. Preorder and postorder visit the node before or between the subtrees, so they interleave large and small keys.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) &mdash; inorder</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Choosing preorder because it starts at the root and looks ordered; the root of a BST is only the middle key, not the smallest.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Inorder of a BST is sorted, which is why \"is this tree a BST?\" is answered by one inorder scan.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-Q12", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "tree-traversals-inorder-pre-post", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "For a binary tree whose keys are all distinct, which of these pairs of traversals determine the tree uniquely?", "options": ["preorder and inorder", "postorder and inorder", "preorder and postorder", "level order and inorder"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A reconstruction needs one traversal that identifies the root of each subtree and one that says how the remaining keys split into left and right.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Inorder always supplies the split, because the root's position separates left keys from right keys.</li><li>Preorder gives the root first, postorder gives it last, and level order gives it first within each subtree. Each of them therefore pairs with inorder: (A), (B) and (D) are unique.</li><li>(C) fails: with preorder <code class=\"mm\">A B C</code> and postorder <code class=\"mm\">C B A</code> both the left chain and the right chain fit, so the side of a single child is unknown.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B), (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Believing preorder plus postorder is enough because together they name every node in two orders; they still leave single-child nodes unoriented.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Any traversal that names the root, plus inorder, is sufficient; two root-naming traversals without inorder are not.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-Q13", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "tree-traversals-inorder-pre-post", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A binary tree with 9 nodes has preorder A B D G H C E F I and inorder G D H B A E C I F. How many nodes of this tree have exactly one child?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">0</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">1</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">2</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">3</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Rebuild the tree recursively: the preorder supplies each subtree root, and the inorder says how many keys fall to its left and to its right.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Root <code class=\"mm\">A</code>; inorder <code class=\"mm\">G D H B | A | E C I F</code> gives 4 keys left and 4 right.</li><li>Left: preorder <code class=\"mm\">B D G H</code> makes <code class=\"mm\">B</code> the root; inorder <code class=\"mm\">G D H B</code> puts all three keys on its left, so <code class=\"mm\">B</code> has no right child.</li><li>Inside that left part, preorder <code class=\"mm\">D G H</code> makes <code class=\"mm\">D</code> the root with <code class=\"mm\">G</code> left and <code class=\"mm\">H</code> right.</li><li>Right: preorder <code class=\"mm\">C E F I</code> makes <code class=\"mm\">C</code> the root; inorder <code class=\"mm\">E C I F</code> gives <code class=\"mm\">E</code> left and <code class=\"mm\">I F</code> right, so <code class=\"mm\">C</code> has two children.</li><li>Preorder <code class=\"mm\">F I</code> with inorder <code class=\"mm\">I F</code> makes <code class=\"mm\">F</code> the root with <code class=\"mm\">I</code> on its left only.</li><li>Single-child nodes: <code class=\"mm\">B</code> (left only) and <code class=\"mm\">F</code> (left only). All others have 0 or 2.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; 2</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting the leaves <code class=\"mm\">G, H, E, I</code> as single-child nodes, or stopping after the first level and answering 0.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Draw the tree first; every later question about it (child counts, height, mirror, diameter) then becomes a lookup.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-Q14", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "threaded-binary-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "In an inorder-threaded binary tree, the right pointer of a node that has no right child is replaced by a thread. What does that thread point to?", "options": ["its parent", "its inorder successor", "its inorder predecessor", "the root of the tree"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Threading reuses the NULL pointers of a binary tree to store the inorder neighbours of a node, so that inorder traversal needs no stack.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A node with no right child is the last node visited in its own subtree, so the next node of the inorder walk is its ancestor on the first turn to the right.</li><li>That ancestor is the inorder successor, so the right thread points there.</li><li>Symmetrically, a NULL left pointer becomes a thread to the inorder predecessor.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; its inorder successor</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Saying the parent: for a node that is a left child the successor is the parent, but for a right child it is some higher ancestor, so \"parent\" is not general.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Right thread = successor, left thread = predecessor; that is what makes \"find next in inorder\" an O(1) step.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-Q15", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "threaded-binary-trees", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A binary tree with 20 nodes is stored as a fully threaded binary tree, so every NULL left or right pointer is replaced by a thread. How many thread pointers does the tree contain?", "options": [], "answer_display": "21", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A binary tree with <code class=\"mm\">n</code> nodes has exactly <code class=\"mm\">n + 1</code> NULL child pointers, because it has <code class=\"mm\">2n</code> child fields and <code class=\"mm\">n &minus; 1</code> of them hold edges.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Child fields: <code class=\"mm\">2 &times; 20 = 40</code>.</li><li>Fields holding a real child: <code class=\"mm\">20 &minus; 1 = 19</code> edges.</li><li>NULL fields: <code class=\"mm\">40 &minus; 19 = 21</code>. Full threading turns every one of them into a thread, so there are 21 threads.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 21</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 20, one thread per node; the count is per NULL pointer, and a leaf contributes two.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Threads <code class=\"mm\">= n + 1</code> for a fully threaded tree of <code class=\"mm\">n</code> nodes, the same count as the NULL pointers it replaces.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-Q16", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "threaded-binary-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Compared with an ordinary binary tree, what is the main cost of threading?", "options": ["Each node needs an extra child pointer.", "Insertion and deletion must update the threads of neighbouring nodes.", "Inorder traversal needs an auxiliary stack.", "The tree can no longer be searched in time proportional to its height."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Threading trades update work for traversal work: it stores inorder neighbours in the unused pointers, which removes the stack but makes structural updates more delicate.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Traversal gets cheaper: inorder runs in <code class=\"mm\">O(n)</code> time and <code class=\"mm\">O(1)</code> extra space, so (C) is false.</li><li>No pointer is added; a NULL child pointer is reused, possibly with one tag bit, so (A) is false.</li><li>Searching still follows one path of length at most the height, so (D) is false.</li><li>When a node is inserted or deleted, the threads that pointed to the old neighbour must be redirected, and that repair work is what threading costs: (B) is true.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming threading needs extra storage per node; the whole point is to reuse the NULL pointers that already exist.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Threading pays off when the tree is built once and traversed many times.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-Q17", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "threaded-binary-trees", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about threaded binary trees are TRUE?", "options": ["In a fully threaded binary tree a NULL left pointer is replaced by a thread to the inorder predecessor.", "Threading reduces the number of nodes that must be stored.", "Threads must be distinguishable from ordinary child pointers, typically by a one-bit tag per pointer.", "An inorder traversal of a fully threaded tree needs <code class=\"mm\">O(1)</code> extra space."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A thread is a pointer to an inorder neighbour stored in a child field that would otherwise be NULL; a tag bit tells the two uses of the same field apart.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) true: the left thread of a node is its inorder predecessor, the right thread its successor.</li><li>(B) false: threading changes no node count. It adds no node and removes none; only the meaning of some pointer fields changes.</li><li>(C) true: without a tag the traversal cannot tell whether a field holds a child or a thread.</li><li>(D) true: the successor is reached by following one thread, so no stack and no recursion is needed.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C), (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Accepting (B) because threading \"saves space\": it saves the <code class=\"mm\">O(h)</code> stack of a recursive traversal, not any node.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Threading is a space-for-update trade inside the existing nodes.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-Q18", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "threaded-binary-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A fully threaded binary tree uses a dummy header node. Its left pointer points to the root, its right pointer points to itself, and it acts as the inorder predecessor of the leftmost node and the inorder successor of the rightmost node. For a tree with n ≥ 1 real nodes, how many thread pointers point at the header?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">0</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">1</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">2</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">n</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With a header the structure has <code class=\"mm\">n + 1</code> nodes and therefore <code class=\"mm\">n + 2</code> threads. Every thread points at an inorder neighbour, and the header is the neighbour of exactly two nodes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The leftmost node has no left child, so its left thread points to its inorder predecessor, which is the header.</li><li>The rightmost node has no right child, so its right thread points to its inorder successor, which is again the header.</li><li>Every other thread points at a real node, so exactly 2 threads reach the header.</li><li>Check the count: <code class=\"mm\">n + 2</code> threads in all, of which 2 go to the header and <code class=\"mm\">n</code> to real nodes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; 2</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 1 by thinking only the rightmost node needs the header, or <code class=\"mm\">n</code> by counting one header pointer per node.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The header closes the inorder walk into a cycle, so the traversal can stop on returning to it instead of testing for NULL.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-Q19", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "threaded-binary-trees", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A binary tree has root 10. Its left child is 5 with children 2 and 7. Its right child is 15 with left child 12 and right child 20, and 12 has a left child 11 and no right child. The tree is converted to a fully threaded binary tree. How many nodes have their right pointer replaced by a thread?", "options": [], "answer_display": "5", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A right pointer becomes a thread exactly for those nodes that have no right child. The left pointers are a separate question.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>List the nodes with no right child: 2 (leaf), 7 (leaf), 12 (only a left child), 11 (leaf) and 20 (leaf).</li><li>Nodes that do have a right child: 10 (child 15), 5 (child 7) and 15 (child 20). They keep a real right pointer.</li><li>So 5 nodes carry a right thread.</li><li>For contrast, nodes with no left child are 2, 7, 11 and 20, that is 4, so counting both kinds of thread would wrongly give 9.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 5</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 4 by counting leaves only: node 12 is not a leaf, but it still has no right child and therefore still carries a right thread.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Threads are per missing child pointer, not per leaf.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-Q20", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "expression-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "The operators +, −, * have their usual precedence and all are left associative. What is the postfix form of a + b * c − d?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">a b + c * d &minus;</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">a b c * + d &minus;</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">a b c + * d &minus;</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">a b c * d + &minus;</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Postfix is the postorder traversal of the expression tree: both operands of an operator appear before the operator itself, and the tree already encodes precedence and associativity.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">*</code> binds tighter than <code class=\"mm\">+</code> and <code class=\"mm\">&minus;</code>, so the expression parses as <code class=\"mm\">(a + (b * c)) &minus; d</code>.</li><li>Innermost <code class=\"mm\">b * c</code> becomes <code class=\"mm\">b c *</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">a + (b c *)</code> becomes <code class=\"mm\">a b c * +</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(a b c * +) &minus; d</code> becomes <code class=\"mm\">a b c * + d &minus;</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; a b c * + d &minus;</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Option (A) is <code class=\"mm\">(a + b) * c &minus; d</code>, which ignores precedence, and option (C) is <code class=\"mm\">a * (b + c) &minus; d</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Convert by tree, not by eye: postfix of a node is postfix(left) postfix(right) operator.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-Q21", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "expression-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which traversal of an expression tree produces the prefix form of the expression?", "options": ["inorder", "postorder", "preorder", "level order"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In an expression tree each internal node is an operator and each leaf an operand, so the three depth-first orders give the three notations.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Preorder visits the operator before its operands, which is exactly prefix notation.</li><li>Postorder visits the operator after both operands, which is postfix notation.</li><li>Inorder visits left, operator, right, which reproduces infix &mdash; without the parentheses that the tree itself encodes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; preorder</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Choosing inorder and expecting the original bracketed string; inorder alone is ambiguous for <code class=\"mm\">a + b * c</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(a + b) * c</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> preorder = prefix, postorder = postfix, inorder = infix without brackets.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-Q22", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "expression-trees", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Evaluate the postfix expression 5 3 + 8 2 − * 4 /. Every intermediate value is exact and the division is exact. Give the value.", "options": [], "answer_display": "12", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Postfix is evaluated with one stack: push every operand, and on an operator pop the right operand first and then the left.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Push 5, push 3; on <code class=\"mm\">+</code>: pop 3 and 5, push <code class=\"mm\">5 + 3 = 8</code>. Stack: 8.</li><li>Push 8, push 2; on <code class=\"mm\">&minus;</code>: pop 2 and 8, push <code class=\"mm\">8 &minus; 2 = 6</code>. Stack: 8, 6.</li><li>On <code class=\"mm\">*</code>: pop 6 and 8, push <code class=\"mm\">8 &times; 6 = 48</code>. Stack: 48.</li><li>Push 4; on <code class=\"mm\">/</code>: pop 4 and 48, push <code class=\"mm\">48 / 4 = 12</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 12</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Popping in the wrong order at the subtraction, computing <code class=\"mm\">2 &minus; 8 = &minus;6</code> and finishing with <code class=\"mm\">&minus;12</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The first pop is the right operand; for <code class=\"mm\">&minus;</code> and <code class=\"mm\">/</code> the order changes the answer.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-Q23", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "expression-trees", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Consider an expression tree in which every internal node is a binary operator and every leaf is an operand. Which of the following are TRUE?", "options": ["Its inorder traversal reproduces the original infix string with all its parentheses.", "The number of leaves is one more than the number of internal nodes.", "Evaluating the postfix form of the expression needs a stack.", "The tree is a full binary tree."], "answer_display": "Options (B), (C), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Such a tree is full by construction &mdash; every internal node has exactly two children &mdash; and the leaf/internal-node identity follows from that.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(D) true: every internal node is a binary operator with two operands as children.</li><li>(B) true: in a full binary tree <code class=\"mm\">L = I + 1</code>. With 9 operators there are 10 operands, and the token count <code class=\"mm\">2I + 1</code> agrees.</li><li>(C) true: postfix evaluation pushes operands and pops on operators, so it needs a stack of depth up to the tree height.</li><li>(A) false: inorder gives the infix order but no parentheses, so <code class=\"mm\">(a + b) * c</code> and <code class=\"mm\">a + b * c</code> produce the same inorder string <code class=\"mm\">a + b * c</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (B), (C), (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Believing the tree \"remembers\" the parentheses in its inorder; the shape carries them, the inorder string does not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Expression trees are full binary trees, so leaves <code class=\"mm\">= operators + 1</code> and tokens <code class=\"mm\">= 2 &times; operators + 1</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-Q24", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "expression-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "The expression a + b * c − d parses, with the usual precedence and left associativity, as (a + (b * c)) − d. In its expression tree, how many nodes lie in the left subtree of the root?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">5</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">3</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">4</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">6</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The root of the expression tree is the last operator applied, and its subtrees are the two operands of that operator.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The outermost operator is <code class=\"mm\">&minus;</code>, so it is the root.</li><li>Its left operand is the subexpression <code class=\"mm\">a + (b * c)</code>, whose tree is <code class=\"mm\">+</code> with children <code class=\"mm\">a</code> and <code class=\"mm\">*</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">*</code> has children <code class=\"mm\">b</code> and <code class=\"mm\">c</code>.</li><li>That is 5 nodes: <code class=\"mm\">+</code>, <code class=\"mm\">a</code>, <code class=\"mm\">*</code>, <code class=\"mm\">b</code>, <code class=\"mm\">c</code>.</li><li>Its right operand is the single leaf <code class=\"mm\">d</code>, so the whole tree has 7 nodes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; 5</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 3 by counting only the operators and the leaf <code class=\"mm\">a</code>, or 4 by forgetting the <code class=\"mm\">*</code> node.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Count every operator and every operand; a subtree with <code class=\"mm\">k</code> operators holds <code class=\"mm\">2k + 1</code> nodes.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-Q25", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "expression-trees", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "An expression tree has 9 internal nodes, all of them binary operators, and 10 leaves. Which of the following are TRUE?", "options": ["Its postfix form has 19 tokens.", "Every internal node has exactly two children.", "Its prefix form has 18 tokens.", "It has exactly one more leaf than internal node."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Prefix, infix and postfix forms of the same expression tree contain the same tokens, merely in different orders: <code class=\"mm\">I</code> operators plus <code class=\"mm\">L</code> operands.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Token count <code class=\"mm\">= I + L = 9 + 10 = 19</code>, so (A) is true and (C), which claims 18, is false.</li><li>Every internal node is a binary operator, so it has two operands as children: (B) is true.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">L = I + 1</code> because <code class=\"mm\">10 = 9 + 1</code>, the standard full-tree identity: (D) is true.</li><li>Equivalently, a full tree with <code class=\"mm\">I</code> internal nodes has <code class=\"mm\">2I + 1</code> nodes in all, here 19, which matches the token count.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B), (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Believing prefix is shorter than postfix because it \"starts with the operator\"; the token multiset is identical, so both have <code class=\"mm\">2I + 1</code> tokens.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Any expression tree gives <code class=\"mm\">2I + 1</code> tokens in each of the three notations.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S2-Q01", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "binary-tree-properties", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A binary tree has 20 nodes. Exactly 6 of them have two children and exactly 4 have exactly one child. How many leaves does the tree have?</p>", "options": ["10", "9", "11", "8"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Every node of a binary tree has 0, 1 or 2 children, so the three classes partition the nodes: <code class=\"mm\">n = n0 + n1 + n2</code>, where <code class=\"mm\">n0</code> counts leaves.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The two known classes hold <code class=\"mm\">4 + 6 = 10</code> nodes.</li><li>So <code class=\"mm\">n0 = 20 &minus; 10 = 10</code>.</li><li>Check: <code class=\"mm\">n0 = n2 + 1 = 7</code> is <i>not</i> required here, because that identity only holds when <code class=\"mm\">n1 = 0</code>; the tree does have one-child nodes, and the partition identity already fixes <code class=\"mm\">n0</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 10 leaves</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reaching for <code class=\"mm\">n0 = n2 + 1</code> and answering 7. That identity is true for every binary tree, but it relates <code class=\"mm\">n0</code> to <code class=\"mm\">n2</code> only; when <code class=\"mm\">n1 &ne; 0</code> you must also use <code class=\"mm\">n = n0 + n1 + n2</code>, and here the partition alone is enough.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Start every counting question by writing <code class=\"mm\">n = n0 + n1 + n2</code>; add <code class=\"mm\">n0 = n2 + 1</code> only as a second equation.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S2-Q02", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "binary-tree-properties", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A binary tree with 30 nodes is stored in records that carry two child pointers, <code class=\"mm\">left</code> and <code class=\"mm\">right</code>. How many of the 60 pointer fields hold NULL?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "31", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A tree on <code class=\"mm\">n</code> nodes has exactly <code class=\"mm\">n &minus; 1</code> edges, and each edge is stored in exactly one child pointer, so exactly <code class=\"mm\">n &minus; 1</code> of the <code class=\"mm\">2n</code> child pointers are non-NULL.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Non-NULL child pointers = <code class=\"mm\">30 &minus; 1 = 29</code>, one per non-root node.</li><li>NULL child pointers = <code class=\"mm\">60 &minus; 29 = 31</code>, which is <code class=\"mm\">n + 1</code>.</li><li>Equivalently, each node except the root consumes one pointer, so <code class=\"mm\">2n &minus; (n &minus; 1) = n + 1</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 31 NULL fields</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 30, one NULL per node. The count is per pointer field, not per node, and it is one more than the number of nodes, not one less.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Memorise the pair: a binary tree with <code class=\"mm\">n</code> nodes has <code class=\"mm\">n + 1</code> NULL pointers and <code class=\"mm\">n &minus; 1</code> edges.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S2-Q03", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "binary-tree-properties", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">In a strictly binary tree every node has either 0 or 2 children. Such a tree has 21 nodes with two children. How many nodes does the tree have in total?</p>", "options": ["42", "44", "43", "41"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A strictly binary tree is a full binary tree, so <code class=\"mm\">n0 = n2 + 1</code> and the total is <code class=\"mm\">n = 2n2 + 1</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Leaves: <code class=\"mm\">n0 = 21 + 1 = 22</code>.</li><li>Total: <code class=\"mm\">21 + 22 = 43</code>, that is <code class=\"mm\">2 &times; 21 + 1 = 43</code>.</li><li>Sanity check with edges: 43 nodes have 42 edges, and each internal node contributes exactly 2 of them.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 43 nodes</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 42 from <code class=\"mm\">2 &times; 21</code>, which forgets that the leaves are counted on top of the internal nodes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For a full binary tree: <code class=\"mm\">n = 2n2 + 1 = 2n0 &minus; 1</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S2-Q04", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "binary-tree-properties", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A binary tree has 100 nodes, exactly 30 of which are leaves. How many of its nodes have exactly one child?</p>", "options": ["40", "41", "39", "42"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Three equations link the node classes: <code class=\"mm\">n = n0 + n1 + n2</code>, <code class=\"mm\">n0 = n2 + 1</code>, and the edge count <code class=\"mm\">n &minus; 1 = n1 + 2n2</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>From <code class=\"mm\">n0 = 30</code>: <code class=\"mm\">n2 = 30 &minus; 1 = 29</code>.</li><li>Then <code class=\"mm\">n1 = 100 &minus; 30 &minus; 29 = 41</code>.</li><li>Check against the edge count: <code class=\"mm\">n1 + 2n2 = 41 + 58 = 99 = n &minus; 1</code>. Consistent.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 41 nodes with exactly one child</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Setting <code class=\"mm\">n2 = n0</code> and getting 40. The correct relation is <code class=\"mm\">n2 = n0 &minus; 1</code>, which shifts the answer by one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> With <code class=\"mm\">n0</code> and <code class=\"mm\">n</code> given, the answer is <code class=\"mm\">n &minus; 2n0 + 1</code>; verify it against <code class=\"mm\">n &minus; 1 = n1 + 2n2</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S2-Q05", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "binary-tree-properties", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A complete binary tree has 1000 nodes. The root sits at level 0, and nodes are numbered level by level from left to right. How many nodes lie on the deepest level?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "489", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A complete binary tree fills levels <code class=\"mm\">0</code> to <code class=\"mm\">h &minus; 1</code> completely and packs level <code class=\"mm\">h</code> from the left, so the first <code class=\"mm\">h</code> levels hold <code class=\"mm\">2^h &minus; 1</code> nodes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Find the deepest level: <code class=\"mm\">2^9 &minus; 1 = 511 &le; 1000 < 1023 = 2^10 &minus; 1</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">h = 9</code>.</li><li>Levels 0 to 8 hold 511 nodes, all of them full.</li><li>Deepest level: <code class=\"mm\">1000 &minus; 511 = 489</code> nodes, out of a possible 512.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 489 nodes on level 9</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Subtracting 512 instead of 511, which double-counts the root and gives 488. The fully filled prefix is <code class=\"mm\">2^h &minus; 1</code>, not <code class=\"mm\">2^h</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For a complete tree with <code class=\"mm\">n</code> nodes, the last level holds <code class=\"mm\">n &minus; (2^h &minus; 1)</code> nodes where <code class=\"mm\">h = &lfloor;log&#8322; n&rfloor;</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S2-Q06", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "binary-tree-properties", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">For a binary tree with <code class=\"mm\">n &ge; 1</code> nodes and height <code class=\"mm\">h</code> measured in edges, which of the following are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["The number of leaves is always exactly one more than the number of nodes with two children.", "A complete binary tree with <code class=\"mm\">n</code> nodes has height <code class=\"mm\">&lfloor;log&#8322; n&rfloor;</code>.", "Every full binary tree is also a complete binary tree.", "Every binary tree with at least two nodes has a node with exactly one child."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two different structural facts are being tested: the leaf/internal identity, which holds for <i>every</i> binary tree, and the relationship between completeness, fullness and height.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>A is TRUE.</b> Counting edges from both ends gives <code class=\"mm\">n &minus; 1 = n1 + 2n2</code> and <code class=\"mm\">n = n0 + n1 + n2</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">n0 = n2 + 1</code> for every binary tree, full or not.</li><li><b>B is TRUE.</b> Levels 0 to <code class=\"mm\">h &minus; 1</code> are full, so <code class=\"mm\">2^h &minus; 1 < n &le; 2^(h+1) &minus; 1</code>, hence <code class=\"mm\">h = &lfloor;log&#8322; n&rfloor;</code>. For <code class=\"mm\">n = 1000</code> this gives 9.</li><li><b>C is FALSE.</b> A full tree only requires every node to have 0 or 2 children. The tree with root <code class=\"mm\">1</code>, left child <code class=\"mm\">2</code> and right child <code class=\"mm\">3</code>, where 2 has children 4 and 5 and 3 has none, is full but not complete, because level 2 is not packed from the left.</li><li><b>D is FALSE.</b> A perfect tree of 3 or 7 nodes has <code class=\"mm\">n1 = 0</code>, so it has no one-child node at all.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Treating full and complete as synonyms. Full constrains child counts, complete constrains level filling; neither implies the other.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Keep the three families apart: full (0 or 2 children), complete (all levels but the last full, last packed left), perfect (all levels full).</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S2-Q07", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "binary-tree-properties", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">How many structurally different binary trees have exactly 6 nodes and height exactly 5, where the height is the number of edges on the longest path from the root to a leaf and left and right children are distinguished?</p>", "options": ["64", "16", "12", "32"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Height 5 with only 6 nodes forces the shape: the longest root-to-leaf path uses all 5 edges, so every one of the 6 nodes lies on that single path and each node has exactly one child except the last.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A tree of 6 nodes has height at most 5, and height 5 is reached only by a chain.</li><li>A chain of 6 nodes has 5 edges, and each edge independently goes to a left child or to a right child.</li><li>So the count is <code class=\"mm\">2^5 = 32</code>. They are all distinct because the sequence of left/right choices differs.</li><li>Cross-check: the number of 6-node binary trees of height at most 5 is the Catalan number <code class=\"mm\">C_6 = 132</code>, and only the chains are subtracted from it, which is consistent with 32 &lt; 132.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 32 trees</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering <code class=\"mm\">2^6 = 64</code> by counting one choice per node. The choices are per edge, and a chain of 6 nodes has 5 edges.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A chain on <code class=\"mm\">n</code> nodes has <code class=\"mm\">2^(n&minus;1)</code> distinct orientations; this is the standard witness that preorder plus postorder does not determine a binary tree.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S2-Q08", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "tree-traversals-inorder-pre-post", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A binary tree with distinct keys has inorder <code class=\"mm\">A B C D E F</code> and postorder <code class=\"mm\">B A D F E C</code>. What is its preorder traversal?</p>", "options": ["C A B E D F", "C A B E F D", "C B A E D F", "A B C D E F"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Inorder fixes which keys fall left and right of a root; postorder names the root last. Together they rebuild the tree, and the preorder is then read off the rebuilt tree.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Postorder ends in <code class=\"mm\">C</code>, so the root is <code class=\"mm\">C</code>. Inorder splits as <code class=\"mm\">A B</code> | <code class=\"mm\">C</code> | <code class=\"mm\">D E F</code>.</li><li>Left subtree: inorder <code class=\"mm\">A B</code>, postorder <code class=\"mm\">B A</code>, so the root is <code class=\"mm\">A</code> with <code class=\"mm\">B</code> as its right child.</li><li>Right subtree: inorder <code class=\"mm\">D E F</code>, postorder <code class=\"mm\">D F E</code>, so the root is <code class=\"mm\">E</code> with left child <code class=\"mm\">D</code> and right child <code class=\"mm\">F</code>.</li><li>Preorder (root, left, right): <code class=\"mm\">C</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">A B</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">E D F</code> &rarr; <code class=\"mm\">C A B E D F</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: C A B E D F</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reading postorder left to right and assuming the first symbol is a root. The root is always the <i>last</i> symbol of postorder.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Rebuild first, then read the requested traversal; never guess the traversal order from the two given strings.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S2-Q09", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "tree-traversals-inorder-pre-post", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A binary tree with distinct keys has preorder <code class=\"mm\">1 2 4 8 9 5 3 6 7</code> and inorder <code class=\"mm\">8 4 9 2 5 1 6 3 7</code>. What is its postorder traversal?</p>", "options": ["8 9 4 5 2 7 6 3 1", "9 8 4 5 2 6 7 3 1", "8 9 4 5 2 6 7 3 1", "8 9 5 4 2 6 7 3 1"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Preorder names the root first, inorder splits the remaining keys into the left and right subtrees. Rebuilding bottom-up and printing left, right, root gives the postorder.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Root <code class=\"mm\">1</code>; inorder splits as <code class=\"mm\">8 4 9 2 5</code> | <code class=\"mm\">1</code> | <code class=\"mm\">6 3 7</code>.</li><li>Left subtree, preorder <code class=\"mm\">2 4 8 9 5</code>, inorder <code class=\"mm\">8 4 9 2 5</code>: root <code class=\"mm\">2</code>, left <code class=\"mm\">4(8, 9)</code>, right <code class=\"mm\">5</code>.</li><li>Right subtree, preorder <code class=\"mm\">3 6 7</code>, inorder <code class=\"mm\">6 3 7</code>: root <code class=\"mm\">3</code>, left <code class=\"mm\">6</code>, right <code class=\"mm\">7</code>.</li><li>Postorder: <code class=\"mm\">8 9 4</code>, <code class=\"mm\">5</code>, <code class=\"mm\">2</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">6</code>, <code class=\"mm\">7</code>, <code class=\"mm\">3</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">1</code> &rarr; <code class=\"mm\">8 9 4 5 2 6 7 3 1</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 8 9 4 5 2 6 7 3 1</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Swapping the left and right children of the root, which yields the option ending <code class=\"mm\">... 2 7 6 3 1</code>. Preorder and inorder both carry the left-right distinction, so the split must be done exactly.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The left subtree of the root is exactly the keys before the root in inorder, and their preorder is exactly the matching prefix of preorder.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S2-Q10", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "tree-traversals-inorder-pre-post", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A binary tree with distinct keys has preorder <code class=\"mm\">A B C D E F G H</code> and inorder <code class=\"mm\">C B D A F E H G</code>. How many leaves does the tree have?</p>", "options": ["3", "4", "5", "2"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Rebuild the tree from the two traversals, then count nodes with no children. A leaf is exactly a node that has neither a left nor a right child.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Root <code class=\"mm\">A</code>; inorder splits as <code class=\"mm\">C B D</code> | <code class=\"mm\">A</code> | <code class=\"mm\">F E H G</code>.</li><li>Left subtree (preorder <code class=\"mm\">B C D</code>, inorder <code class=\"mm\">C B D</code>): root <code class=\"mm\">B</code> with children <code class=\"mm\">C</code> and <code class=\"mm\">D</code>. Both are leaves.</li><li>Right subtree (preorder <code class=\"mm\">E F G H</code>, inorder <code class=\"mm\">F E H G</code>): root <code class=\"mm\">E</code>, left child <code class=\"mm\">F</code>, right subtree with preorder <code class=\"mm\">G H</code> and inorder <code class=\"mm\">H G</code> gives root <code class=\"mm\">G</code> with left child <code class=\"mm\">H</code> and no right child.</li><li>Leaves: <code class=\"mm\">C</code>, <code class=\"mm\">D</code>, <code class=\"mm\">F</code>, <code class=\"mm\">H</code> &rarr; 4 leaves.</li><li>Check with the identity: internal nodes with two children are <code class=\"mm\">A</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B</code>, <code class=\"mm\">E</code> (and <code class=\"mm\">G</code> has one child), so <code class=\"mm\">n2 = 3</code> and <code class=\"mm\">n0 = n2 + 1 = 4</code>. Consistent.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4 leaves</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting only the deepest nodes, which misses <code class=\"mm\">F</code> and <code class=\"mm\">C</code>; or forgetting that <code class=\"mm\">G</code> has a single child, so it is not a leaf.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Leaves sit at several depths. Rebuild the shape first and then read off the leaves, or use <code class=\"mm\">n0 = n2 + 1</code> once the two-child nodes are known.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S2-Q11", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "tree-traversals-inorder-pre-post", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements are TRUE for binary trees whose keys are all distinct?</p>", "options": ["If the preorder and the postorder traversals of a full binary tree are both known, the tree is uniquely determined.", "The preorder traversal of a binary search tree lists its keys in increasing order.", "Two different binary trees can have the same preorder traversal and the same postorder traversal.", "The postorder traversal of a binary tree is always the reverse of its preorder traversal."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A traversal pair determines a tree only when it fixes every left/right decision. The obstruction is a node with exactly one child, which the pair cannot tell apart from its mirror.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>A is TRUE.</b> In a full tree every node has 0 or 2 children, so the size of each subtree is read directly from postorder, and the split is unambiguous at every level.</li><li><b>B is FALSE.</b> Preorder visits the root first, so for the BST <code class=\"mm\">2(1, 3)</code> it gives <code class=\"mm\">2 1 3</code>. Only the <i>inorder</i> traversal of a BST is sorted.</li><li><b>C is TRUE.</b> Take the chain <code class=\"mm\">P &rarr; Q &rarr; R</code> with all children on the left and the chain with all children on the right. Both give preorder <code class=\"mm\">P Q R</code> and postorder <code class=\"mm\">R Q P</code>.</li><li><b>D is FALSE.</b> For <code class=\"mm\">2(1, 3)</code>, preorder is <code class=\"mm\">2 1 3</code> and postorder is <code class=\"mm\">1 3 2</code>, whose reverse is <code class=\"mm\">2 3 1</code>, not <code class=\"mm\">2 1 3</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming that any two traversals always pin a binary tree down. Preorder with inorder, or postorder with inorder, always does; preorder with postorder does not, unless fullness removes the one-child ambiguity.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The pair that always works is {preorder or postorder} together with inorder. Level order plus inorder also works when all keys are distinct.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S2-Q12", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "tree-traversals-inorder-pre-post", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A binary tree with distinct keys has preorder <code class=\"mm\">P Q R S</code> and postorder <code class=\"mm\">S R Q P</code>. How many different binary trees have exactly these two traversals?</p>", "options": ["4", "6", "16", "8"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Preorder visits a root before its descendants and postorder after them. When the two orders are exact reversals, every node must be an ancestor of the next, so the tree is a chain and only the left/right orientation of each edge is free.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>In preorder the next key after <code class=\"mm\">P</code> is <code class=\"mm\">Q</code>; in postorder the key before <code class=\"mm\">P</code> is <code class=\"mm\">Q</code> as well, so <code class=\"mm\">Q</code> is the only child of <code class=\"mm\">P</code>. The same argument repeats for <code class=\"mm\">R</code> and <code class=\"mm\">S</code>.</li><li>So the shape is the chain <code class=\"mm\">P &rarr; Q &rarr; R &rarr; S</code>, with 3 edges.</li><li>Each edge is independently a left edge or a right edge, and the orientation never changes preorder or postorder.</li><li>Count: <code class=\"mm\">2^3 = 8</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 8 trees</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 1, on the belief that preorder plus postorder always determine the tree. They determine it only when no node has a single child; here every node except the last has one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Preorder and postorder together determine a tree uniquely if and only if the tree is full; otherwise the free choices are exactly the one-child nodes, giving <code class=\"mm\">2^k</code> trees for <code class=\"mm\">k</code> such nodes.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S2-Q13", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "tree-traversals-inorder-pre-post", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A binary tree with distinct keys has preorder <code class=\"mm\">A B C D E F G</code> and postorder <code class=\"mm\">C B E G F D A</code>. Which of the following is its inorder traversal?</p>", "options": ["C B A E D F G", "B C A E D G F", "C B A E D G F", "C B A D E G F"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With only preorder and postorder, the subtree sizes must be read from postorder: the postorder of a subtree is a contiguous block ending at that subtree's root, and its length is the size of the subtree.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Root <code class=\"mm\">A</code>. In preorder the next key is <code class=\"mm\">B</code>, the root of the left subtree. In postorder <code class=\"mm\">B</code> sits at index 1, so the left subtree has 2 nodes and its postorder is <code class=\"mm\">C B</code> &rarr; <code class=\"mm\">B</code> with left child <code class=\"mm\">C</code>.</li><li>The right subtree is the rest: postorder <code class=\"mm\">E G F D</code>, preorder <code class=\"mm\">D E F G</code>, so its root is <code class=\"mm\">D</code>.</li><li>Inside it, preorder next is <code class=\"mm\">E</code>, at index 0 of the postorder <code class=\"mm\">E G F</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">E</code> has no children.</li><li>The remaining right subtree has postorder <code class=\"mm\">G F</code> and preorder <code class=\"mm\">F G</code>: root <code class=\"mm\">F</code> with left child <code class=\"mm\">G</code>.</li><li>Inorder (left, root, right): <code class=\"mm\">C B</code>, <code class=\"mm\">A</code>, <code class=\"mm\">E</code>, <code class=\"mm\">D</code>, <code class=\"mm\">G F</code> &rarr; <code class=\"mm\">C B A E D G F</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: C B A E D G F</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Placing <code class=\"mm\">G</code> as the right child of <code class=\"mm\">F</code>. The split comes from postorder, where <code class=\"mm\">G</code> precedes <code class=\"mm\">F</code>, which makes it the left child. Guessing from the letters' order in preorder produces option (A).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For preorder + postorder, always measure a subtree by the position of its root in the postorder block; never assume balanced children.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S2-Q14", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "threaded-binary-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">In an inorder-threaded binary tree, the left pointer of a node that has no left child is replaced by a thread. Which node does that thread point to?</p>", "options": ["The node's inorder successor", "The node's inorder predecessor", "The node's parent", "The leftmost leaf of the whole tree"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Threads store the traversal step that the missing pointer would have wasted. A missing left pointer means the node has nothing to its left, so the traversal reaches it straight after its predecessor.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>In inorder, a node is visited after its entire left subtree. If the left subtree is empty, the visit follows the predecessor immediately.</li><li>So the left thread points to the inorder predecessor, and symmetrically the right thread of a node with no right child points to its inorder successor.</li><li>For the leftmost node the predecessor does not exist, which is why a fully threaded tree adds a dummy header node as a sentinel.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Its inorder predecessor</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Mixing up the two directions and saying the left thread points to the successor. The right thread carries the successor; the left thread carries the predecessor.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Left thread = predecessor, right thread = successor, both in inorder. The header node stands in when the predecessor or successor does not exist.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S2-Q15", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "threaded-binary-trees", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A fully threaded binary tree is one in which every NULL child pointer has been replaced by a thread. Which of the following are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["Such a tree with <code class=\"mm\">n</code> nodes contains exactly <code class=\"mm\">n + 1</code> threads.", "A binary tree with 13 nodes has 14 NULL child pointers before threading.", "Every thread points to the inorder predecessor or the inorder successor of the node that owns it.", "The number of threads in such a tree equals the number of its leaves."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Threading reuses exactly the NULL pointers, so the thread count is the NULL-pointer count, <code class=\"mm\">n + 1</code>, and each thread stores one inorder neighbour.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>A is TRUE.</b> A binary tree with <code class=\"mm\">n</code> nodes has <code class=\"mm\">2n</code> child pointers, of which <code class=\"mm\">n &minus; 1</code> are real, leaving <code class=\"mm\">n + 1</code> NULL pointers and therefore <code class=\"mm\">n + 1</code> threads.</li><li><b>B is TRUE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">13 + 1 = 14</code>.</li><li><b>C is TRUE.</b> That is the definition of an inorder thread: the field that would have held a missing child now holds the neighbour in the inorder sequence.</li><li><b>D is FALSE.</b> Threads are counted per missing child pointer, not per leaf. The perfect tree with 7 nodes has 4 leaves but 8 threads.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Equating threads with leaves. A leaf contributes two threads only if it is the only node at its position; in general a node with one child contributes one thread, so the total is <code class=\"mm\">n + 1</code> regardless of the leaf count.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Threads = NULL pointers = <code class=\"mm\">n + 1</code>. Leaves = <code class=\"mm\">n2 + 1</code>. These agree only in special shapes.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S2-Q16", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "threaded-binary-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Each node of a threaded binary tree stores two pointer fields, with a one-bit tag beside each of them. Why are the tags necessary?</p>", "options": ["A thread and a real child pointer occupy the same field, and nothing else distinguishes them.", "A thread may point to a node that has already been freed.", "Threading doubles the number of nodes in the tree.", "The tags record the depth of each node in the tree."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Threading trades a NULL pointer for a traversal link, so the same field must be able to mean two different things. A one-bit tag resolves which meaning is in force.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Without a tag, the traversal cannot tell whether a non-NULL pointer leads to a child (descend) or to an ancestor (follow the thread).</li><li>One bit per pointer is enough: <code class=\"mm\">0</code> for a real child, <code class=\"mm\">1</code> for a thread.</li><li>That is why a threaded node costs two extra bits, and why the memory saving comes from dropping the stack, not from dropping a field.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: The first option</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Believing threading stores the extra information in a new pointer field. It does not: the field is reused, and only a flag is added.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Threading is a tagged union of child and thread. Any implementation must carry the tag or lose the ability to traverse.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S2-Q17", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "threaded-binary-trees", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about inorder-threaded binary trees are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["The inorder successor of a node that has a right child is found in <code class=\"mm\">O(1)</code> time.", "Threading needs one extra bit per pointer field to mark a thread.", "In a preorder-threaded tree the preorder successor of a leaf is reached in <code class=\"mm\">O(1)</code> time.", "Threading changes the number of nodes in the tree."], "answer_display": "Options (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A thread is a stored successor. It helps only where the successor would otherwise require a descent, so the cost of finding a successor depends on whether the node has a right subtree.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>A is FALSE.</b> If the node has a right child, its inorder successor is the leftmost node of that right subtree, reached by descending a spine of length up to <code class=\"mm\">h</code>. That is <code class=\"mm\">O(h)</code>, not <code class=\"mm\">O(1)</code>.</li><li><b>B is TRUE.</b> One bit per pointer field separates a thread from a real child.</li><li><b>C is TRUE.</b> For a leaf the preorder successor is stored directly as a thread, so it is a single pointer step.</li><li><b>D is FALSE.</b> Threading rewrites NULL pointers and adds tags; the node set is unchanged.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming threads make every successor step constant time. Only the case where the needed subtree is missing is constant; a real subtree still has to be entered.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Threads remove stack use, not descent. A threaded traversal is <code class=\"mm\">O(n)</code> overall but individual successor steps may cost <code class=\"mm\">O(h)</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S2-Q18", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "threaded-binary-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A fully inorder-threaded binary tree with <code class=\"mm\">n</code> nodes is traversed inorder by following threads and descending left spines, without any stack. What is the total time of this traversal, and why?</p>", "options": ["&Theta;(n log n), because every thread step is preceded by a descent.", "&Theta;(n&sup2;), because finding a successor may require a descent to a leaf each time.", "&Theta;(h), because the traversal only ever walks the left spine.", "&Theta;(n), because across the whole traversal each edge of the tree is descended at most once."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Individual successor steps can cost <code class=\"mm\">O(h)</code>, but an amortised argument bounds the whole traversal: the descents only ever move downward along edges that are then never re-descended.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Each of the <code class=\"mm\">n</code> nodes is printed exactly once, which is already <code class=\"mm\">&Omega;(n)</code>.</li><li>Every left-spine descent moves down a tree edge, and once a subtree has been traversed the traversal never descends into it again. A tree has <code class=\"mm\">n &minus; 1</code> edges, so all descents together cost <code class=\"mm\">O(n)</code>.</li><li>Thread steps cost <code class=\"mm\">O(1)</code> each and there are <code class=\"mm\">O(n)</code> of them.</li><li>Total <code class=\"mm\">&Theta;(n)</code>, with <code class=\"mm\">O(1)</code> amortised per node and <code class=\"mm\">O(1)</code> extra space.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: &Theta;(n)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Multiplying the worst-case <code class=\"mm\">O(h)</code> successor cost by <code class=\"mm\">n</code> and concluding <code class=\"mm\">O(nh)</code>. That over-counts: the expensive descents happen along disjoint parts of the tree, not at every step.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Amortised analysis is the point of threading: worst-case <code class=\"mm\">O(h)</code> per successor, <code class=\"mm\">O(n)</code> in total, <code class=\"mm\">O(1)</code> space.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S2-Q19", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "threaded-binary-trees", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A binary tree holds 8 at the root, 3 as its left child and 10 as its right child. Node 3 has children 1 and 6, and node 6 has children 4 and 7. Node 10 has only a right child 14, and node 14 has only a left child 13. Every NULL child pointer is replaced by a thread. How many of the threads replace a missing right child?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "5", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A thread that replaces a missing right child is exactly a node with no right child, so the question counts nodes whose right pointer is NULL.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>List the nodes: 8, 3, 1, 6, 4, 7, 10, 14, 13 &mdash; nine nodes.</li><li>Nodes that do have a right child: 3 (right child 6), 6 (right child 7), 8 (right child 10), 10 (right child 14). That is four nodes.</li><li>So nine minus four, that is 5 nodes, have no right child: 1, 4, 7, 14 and 13.</li><li>Check the total: the tree has <code class=\"mm\">9 + 1 = 10</code> NULL pointers, 5 missing left children (1, 4, 7, 10, 13) and 5 missing right children.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 5 right threads</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting leaves, which gives 4. Node 14 is not a leaf, because it has a left child, but it has no right child, so it still owns a right thread.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Count missing pointers per node, not leaves: a node with one child contributes exactly one thread.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S2-Q20", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "expression-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The operators +, &minus;, * and / have their usual precedence, with * and / binding tighter than + and &minus;, and all four associate to the left. What is the postfix form of <code class=\"mm\">(a + b) * (c &minus; d) / e</code> ?</p>", "options": ["a b + c d * &minus; e /", "a b + c d &minus; * e /", "a b c d + &minus; * e /", "a b + c d &minus; e / *"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Postfix is the postorder traversal of the expression tree. Parenthesisation fixes the tree, so convert the parse tree, not the string, and division takes the whole product as its left operand.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">a + b</code> becomes <code class=\"mm\">a b +</code> and <code class=\"mm\">c &minus; d</code> becomes <code class=\"mm\">c d &minus;</code>.</li><li>Their product is <code class=\"mm\">(a b +) (c d &minus;) *</code>.</li><li>The division by <code class=\"mm\">e</code> applies to that product: <code class=\"mm\">a b + c d &minus; * e /</code>.</li><li>Check the token count: 5 operators and 6 operands, so 11 tokens, which the answer has.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: a b + c d &minus; * e /</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Writing <code class=\"mm\">a b + c d &minus; e / *</code> by reading left to right. The division is the last operation performed, so it must be the last operator emitted.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Postfix of an expression is the postorder of its tree; the last operator printed is always the root, that is, the outermost operation.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S2-Q21", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "expression-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What is the infix form of the postfix expression <code class=\"mm\">a b c * + d &minus;</code>, read with the usual precedence and left associativity?</p>", "options": ["a + b * c &minus; d", "a + (b * c &minus; d)", "(a + b) * c &minus; d", "a * b + c &minus; d"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Convert the postfix string to an expression tree with a stack, then read the tree inorder, inserting parentheses only where precedence would otherwise change the meaning.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Push a, b, c. On *: pop c and b, push <code class=\"mm\">(b * c)</code>.</li><li>On +: pop <code class=\"mm\">(b * c)</code> and a, push <code class=\"mm\">(a + (b * c))</code>.</li><li>Push d. On &minus;: pop d and the sum, push <code class=\"mm\">((a + (b * c)) &minus; d)</code>.</li><li>Remove the redundant parentheses: <code class=\"mm\">a + b * c &minus; d</code>. The * binds tightest, then the + and the &minus; in left-to-right order.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: a + b * c &minus; d</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Keeping the parentheses as <code class=\"mm\">a + (b * c &minus; d)</code>, which is a different tree: there the subtraction is inside the right operand of the plus.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Parentheses may be dropped exactly when the tree's structure is already implied by precedence and associativity.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S2-Q22", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "expression-trees", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Evaluate the postfix expression <code class=\"mm\">6 2 3 * &minus; 4 5 * +</code>. Every value is an exact integer.</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "20", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Postfix evaluation uses a stack of values: push operands, and on an operator pop the right operand first and then the left operand.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Push 6, 2, 3. On *: pop 3 (right) and 2 (left) &rarr; 6. Stack: 6, 6.</li><li>On &minus;: pop 6 (right) and 6 (left) &rarr; <code class=\"mm\">6 &minus; 6 = 0</code>. Stack: 0.</li><li>Push 4, 5. On *: <code class=\"mm\">4 &times; 5 = 20</code>. Stack: 0, 20.</li><li>On +: <code class=\"mm\">0 + 20 = 20</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 20</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Popping the operands in the wrong order at the &minus;, giving <code class=\"mm\">2 &minus; 3 = &minus;1</code> or <code class=\"mm\">6 &minus; 6</code> written as <code class=\"mm\">6 &minus; 0</code>. For non-commutative operators the first popped value is the right operand.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> In postfix evaluation, always pop right first, then left. Only + and * hide a mistake; &minus; and / expose it.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S2-Q23", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "expression-trees", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">In an expression tree every internal node is a binary operator and every leaf is an operand. Which of the following are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["A valid postfix expression with <code class=\"mm\">k</code> operators has exactly <code class=\"mm\">k + 1</code> operands.", "The expression tree of a fully parenthesised binary expression with <code class=\"mm\">k</code> operators has <code class=\"mm\">2k + 1</code> nodes.", "An inorder traversal of an expression tree may fail to reproduce the original expression unless parentheses are added.", "The expression tree of <code class=\"mm\">a + b + c + d</code>, parsed left associatively, has height 2 in edges."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An expression tree is a full binary tree: every internal node has exactly two children. That single fact fixes the node counts, and the inorder traversal is the one that loses structure.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>A is TRUE.</b> Each operator consumes two operands and returns one value, so a postfix string with <code class=\"mm\">k</code> operators nets <code class=\"mm\">k + 1</code> operands. The stack is never empty and ends with one value.</li><li><b>B is TRUE.</b> With <code class=\"mm\">k</code> internal nodes and <code class=\"mm\">k + 1</code> leaves, the tree has <code class=\"mm\">2k + 1</code> nodes, which is <code class=\"mm\">2n0 &minus; 1</code> for a full tree.</li><li><b>C is TRUE.</b> Inorder gives <code class=\"mm\">a + b * c</code> for the tree of <code class=\"mm\">a + (b * c)</code> and of <code class=\"mm\">(a + b) * c</code> alike; only parentheses restore the distinction.</li><li><b>D is FALSE.</b> Left associativity gives <code class=\"mm\">((a + b) + c) + d</code>, a left spine of 3 edges, so the height is 3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering that inorder reproduces the expression. It reproduces the token sequence but not the grouping, which is exactly why infix needs parentheses and postfix does not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> An expression tree is full, so <code class=\"mm\">operators = operands &minus; 1</code> and <code class=\"mm\">nodes = 2 &times; operators + 1</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S2-Q24", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "expression-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">How many nodes does the expression tree of <code class=\"mm\">a * (b + c) &minus; d / (e &minus; f)</code> have, where every internal node is one operator and every leaf is one operand?</p>", "options": ["10", "12", "11", "9"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In an expression tree every operator becomes one internal node and every operand one leaf, and the tree is full, so the total is <code class=\"mm\">2k + 1</code> for <code class=\"mm\">k</code> operators.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Operators: <code class=\"mm\">*</code>, <code class=\"mm\">+</code>, <code class=\"mm\">&minus;</code>, <code class=\"mm\">/</code>, <code class=\"mm\">&minus;</code> &rarr; 5 operators.</li><li>Operands: <code class=\"mm\">a</code>, <code class=\"mm\">b</code>, <code class=\"mm\">c</code>, <code class=\"mm\">d</code>, <code class=\"mm\">e</code>, <code class=\"mm\">f</code> &rarr; 6 operands.</li><li>Total nodes: <code class=\"mm\">5 + 6 = 11 = 2 &times; 5 + 1</code>.</li><li>Shape check: root <code class=\"mm\">&minus;</code>, left <code class=\"mm\">*(a, +(b, c))</code>, right <code class=\"mm\">/(d, &minus;(e, f))</code>, which uses every token exactly once.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 11 nodes</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting 10 by taking only the operands and the four binary operators of the top level, or 12 by counting a parenthesis as a node. Parentheses are not nodes; they only fix the shape.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For any fully parenthesised binary expression: nodes = <code class=\"mm\">2k + 1</code> where <code class=\"mm\">k</code> is the operator count.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S2-Q25", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "expression-trees", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">An expression tree is built from the postfix expression <code class=\"mm\">a b + c d e + * * f &minus;</code>, with one operator per internal node and one operand per leaf. What is the height of the tree in edges?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Postfix is a postorder listing of the tree, so building it with a stack recovers the shape exactly. The height is then computed bottom-up as <code class=\"mm\">1 + max(height of the two children)</code>, with leaves at height 0.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">a b +</code> gives node <code class=\"mm\">P = +(a, b)</code>, height 1.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">d e +</code> gives node <code class=\"mm\">Q = +(d, e)</code>, height 1. Then <code class=\"mm\">c</code> and <code class=\"mm\">Q</code> with <code class=\"mm\">*</code> give <code class=\"mm\">R = *(c, Q)</code>, height <code class=\"mm\">1 + max(0, 1) = 2</code>.</li><li>The next <code class=\"mm\">*</code> joins <code class=\"mm\">P</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R</code>: <code class=\"mm\">S = *(P, R)</code>, height <code class=\"mm\">1 + max(1, 2) = 3</code>.</li><li>Finally <code class=\"mm\">f</code> with <code class=\"mm\">&minus;</code>: root <code class=\"mm\">&minus;(S, f)</code>, height <code class=\"mm\">1 + max(3, 0) = 4</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 3 by taking the height of the deepest subexpression only, or 5 by counting nodes on the longest path instead of edges.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Build the tree with a stack, then evaluate height bottom-up; the outermost operator always adds one level to the taller child.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q01", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "bst-insertion-deletion", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The keys 50, 30, 70, 20, 40, 60, 80 are inserted into an empty binary search tree in that order. The key 50 is then deleted by replacing it with its inorder successor. Which key is at the root of the resulting tree?</p>", "options": ["30", "40", "70", "60"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Deleting a node with two children in a BST means finding a key that can legally take its place: either the inorder predecessor (largest key in the left subtree) or the inorder successor (smallest key in the right subtree).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Inorder traversal of the tree: <code class=\"mm\">20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70, 80</code>.</li><li>The right subtree of <code class=\"mm\">50</code> is rooted at <code class=\"mm\">70</code>, and its leftmost key is <code class=\"mm\">60</code>, so the successor of 50 is <b>60</b>.</li><li>Copy 60 into the root position and delete 60 from its old place; 60 had no children, so nothing takes its place.</li><li>Result: root 60, left subtree 30 with children 20 and 40, right subtree 70 with right child 80. Inorder is still <code class=\"mm\">20, 30, 40, 60, 70, 80</code>, so the BST property holds.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D), 60.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 30, which is the inorder <i>predecessor</i> and also a legal choice — but the question fixes the successor rule. Answering 70 assumes the right child simply moves up, which breaks nothing here but is not the successor rule either.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Both predecessor and successor preserve the BST property; the rule must be read from the question, and only the successor rule gives 60.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q02", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "bst-insertion-deletion", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The keys 20, 10, 30, 5, 15, 25, 35, 3, 7, 12, 17 are inserted in that order into an empty binary search tree. Taking the root to be at depth 0, what is the sum of the depths of all eleven keys?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "22", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Insertion order fixes the shape of a BST completely: each new key descends from the root along the unique path its comparisons allow, so the depth of a key is the number of edges on that path.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>20 is the root, depth 0.</li><li>10 and 30 attach to 20, depth 1 each.</li><li>5, 15, 25, 35 attach one level lower, depth 2 each.</li><li>3, 7, 12, 17 attach one level lower again, depth 3 each.</li><li>Sum = <code class=\"mm\">0 + 2(1) + 4(2) + 4(3) = 0 + 2 + 8 + 12 = 22</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 22.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting depth in nodes rather than edges, which adds 1 per key and gives 33. Also easy to miscount the four depth-2 nodes as five by counting 10 and 30 twice.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For an insertion-built BST the level sizes are determined by the comparison path, so list the keys level by level before summing anything.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q03", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "bst-insertion-deletion", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The keys 45, 25, 65, 15, 35, 55, 75 are inserted in that order into an empty binary search tree. The key 25 is then deleted by replacing it with its inorder successor. Which key becomes the left child of 45?</p>", "options": ["35", "15", "40", "30"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> When the successor of the deleted node is a key inside the deleted node's right subtree, the successor is lifted into the vacated position and inherits the deleted node's left subtree unchanged.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The subtree rooted at 25 is: 25 with left child 15 and right child 35.</li><li>The leftmost key of the right subtree of 25 is 35 itself, so the successor is <b>35</b>.</li><li>35 has no children, so it moves up with no further repair: the position of 25 now holds 35, whose left child is 15 and whose right child is empty.</li><li>The parent of that position is 45, so the left child of 45 becomes 35.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A), 35.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 15, which keeps the original left subtree as the direct child of 45 and so loses the promotion step; or answering 30, a key that is not in the tree at all.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Draw the two-node subtree of the deleted key before and after the lift; the left subtree travels with the successor, it is not re-parented to the grandparent.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q04", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "bst-insertion-deletion", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The keys 4, 2, 6, 1, 3, 5, 7 are inserted in that order into an empty binary search tree. The key 2 is then deleted by replacing it with its inorder successor. What is the postorder traversal of the resulting tree?</p>", "options": ["1, 3, 5, 7, 6, 4", "1, 3, 7, 5, 6, 4", "3, 1, 5, 7, 6, 4", "1, 3, 5, 7, 4, 6"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Deletion changes the tree first and only then does the traversal read it; the two operations must be kept in order, and postorder visits left subtree, then right subtree, then the node itself.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The inserted tree is complete: root 4, children 2 and 6, grandchildren 1, 3, 5, 7.</li><li>The successor of 2 is the leftmost key of its right subtree, namely <b>3</b>; 3 is a leaf, so it is lifted into the position of 2.</li><li>Resulting tree: root 4, left child 3 with left child 1 and no right child, right child 6 with children 5 and 7.</li><li>Postorder: left subtree of 4 gives <code class=\"mm\">1, 3</code>; right subtree gives <code class=\"mm\">5, 7, 6</code>; finally the root 4. Total <code class=\"mm\">1, 3, 5, 7, 6, 4</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A), 1, 3, 5, 7, 6, 4.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Traversing the tree as it was <i>before</i> the deletion, which gives 1, 2, 3, 5, 7, 6, 4 and matches no option — so a candidate then guesses. Option (C) reverses the order inside the left subtree, confusing postorder with a mirrored inorder.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> After any structural edit, redraw the affected subtree and re-derive the traversal from the drawing instead of editing the old traversal list.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q05", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "bst-insertion-deletion", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about deletion in a binary search tree with distinct keys are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["Both the inorder predecessor and the inorder successor of a node with two children must themselves be leaves.", "Copying the inorder successor into a two-child node and then deleting the successor from the right subtree preserves the BST ordering.", "Two different insertion orders of the same set of distinct keys always produce the same tree shape.", "Deleting a node with two children can cost &Theta;(h) key comparisons in the worst case, where h is the height of the tree."], "answer_display": "Options (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Deletion in a BST rests on one fact: the inorder predecessor is the largest key below the deleted key and the inorder successor is the smallest key above it, so either can occupy the vacated position without breaking any ordering constraint.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Statement (A): the predecessor is the rightmost node of the left subtree, so it has no right child — but it may have a left child. Counterexample: insert 4, 2, 6, 1, 3; the predecessor of 4 is 3, a leaf, but insert 4, 2, 6, 1, 3, 2.5 and the predecessor of 4 is a node with a left child. False.</li><li>Statement (B): the successor is larger than every key in the deleted node's left subtree and smaller than every key in its right subtree, which is exactly the interval the position requires. True.</li><li>Statement (C): inserting 1, 2, 3 gives a right chain; inserting 2, 1, 3 gives a balanced tree from the same three keys. False.</li><li>Statement (D): finding the successor means walking down the left spine of the right subtree, at most h steps, and the recursive descent to the node itself is also at most h. True.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (D) are FALSE, so the correct set is (B), (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Accepting (A) because the standard picture of a successor is a leaf; the left-spine walk can end at a node that carries a left subtree.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Prove each claim against the interval argument, not against one picture; the predecessor and successor are the two keys whose values straddle the deleted key.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q06", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "bst-insertion-deletion", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The keys 20, 10, 30, 5, 15, 25, 35, 2, 7, 12, 17, 22, 27, 32, 37 are inserted in that order into an empty binary search tree. The key 20 is then deleted by replacing it with its inorder successor. Taking the root of the resulting tree to be at depth 0, what is the sum of the depths of all remaining fourteen keys?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "31", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A root deletion by successor moves the smallest key of the right subtree to the top, and every key on the spine it was taken from shifts up by one level — so the depths must be recomputed, not patched.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The inserted tree is perfectly balanced: root 20, children 10 and 30, grandchildren 5, 15, 25, 35, and great-grandchildren 2, 7, 12, 17, 22, 27, 32, 37.</li><li>The successor of 20 is the leftmost key of the right subtree: 30 &rarr; 25 &rarr; <b>22</b>. So 22 becomes the root and is removed from its old position.</li><li>Resulting tree: root 22; left child 10 with 5 (2, 7) and 15 (12, 17); right child 30 with 25 (27) and 35 (32, 37).</li><li>Depths: 22 at 0; 10 and 30 at 1; 5, 15, 25, 35 at 2; 2, 7, 12, 17, 27, 32, 37 at 3.</li><li>Sum = <code class=\"mm\">0 + 2(1) + 4(2) + 7(3) = 0 + 2 + 8 + 21 = 31</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 31.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Adding the deleted key 20 back into the depth sum, or keeping 22 at its old depth 3 and getting 32; both come from patching the old list instead of rebuilding the tree.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> After a root deletion, list the keys level by level from the new root. Fourteen keys in a near-complete shape means one key at depth 0, two at depth 1, four at depth 2 and seven at depth 3.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q07", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "bst-insertion-deletion", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A binary search tree holds distinct keys and has height h, measured in edges, so a tree with a single node has h = 0. What is the maximum number of key comparisons that a successful search can perform?</p>", "options": ["h", "h + 1", "h &minus; 1", "2h"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each comparison in a BST search either finds the key or moves one level down, so the number of comparisons equals the number of nodes on the search path — one more than the number of edges on it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A successful search for a key ends at the node holding it.</li><li>That node sits at depth d for some <code class=\"mm\">0 &le; d &le; h</code>, and the search compared the key once at every node from the root down to it.</li><li>The path holds <code class=\"mm\">d + 1</code> nodes, so the search made <code class=\"mm\">d + 1</code> comparisons.</li><li>The worst case is the deepest node, <code class=\"mm\">d = h</code>, giving <code class=\"mm\">h + 1</code> comparisons.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B), h + 1.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering h, which counts edges on the path instead of nodes — an off-by-one that also appears in the claim that a one-node tree needs zero comparisons.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Comparisons = nodes on the path = edges + 1. The same off-by-one decides the worst-case time of BST search, O(h) comparisons and O(h + 1) node visits.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q08", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "avl-trees-rotations", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The keys 10, 20, 30 are inserted in that order into an empty AVL tree. Which key ends up at the root after the rebalancing?</p>", "options": ["10", "20", "30", "15"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An insertion into an AVL tree can unbalance only the nodes on the insertion path, and the deepest unbalanced node is repaired by a rotation that lifts the middle key of the three involved to the top.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>After 10 and 20 the tree is 10 with right child 20; balance factors are 0 and 1 at worst, so no rotation.</li><li>Inserting 30 gives 10 &rarr; 20 &rarr; 30, a right-right chain. At node 10 the right subtree has height 1 while the left is empty, so the balance factor is 2.</li><li>Right-right means a single left rotation about 10: 20 rises, 10 becomes its left child and 30 stays its right child.</li><li>The root is therefore <b>20</b>, with children 10 and 30.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B), 20.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 10 by assuming the first key inserted stays at the root, or answering 30 by rotating the wrong way and producing 30 with a left child 20.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> In a right-right (or left-left) case the middle key of the three becomes the root; a single rotation is enough whenever the two imbalance directions agree.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q09", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "avl-trees-rotations", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The keys 30, 20, 10, 25, 28 are inserted in that order into an empty AVL tree. What is the preorder traversal of the final tree?</p>", "options": ["20, 10, 28, 25, 30", "20, 10, 25, 28, 30", "28, 20, 10, 25, 30", "20, 28, 10, 25, 30"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An AVL insertion performs at most one repair, but that repair may be a double rotation, and a later insertion can trigger a second one; both must be traced in order.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>30, 20, 10: a left-left chain at 30, so a single right rotation gives root 20 with children 10 and 30.</li><li>25: descends 20 &rarr; 30 &rarr; left, becoming the left child of 30. Balance factors stay within &plusmn;1.</li><li>28: descends 20 &rarr; 30 &rarr; 25 &rarr; right, becoming the right child of 25. Now node 30 has a left subtree of height 1 and no right subtree, and node 20 has a right subtree of height 2 against a left subtree of height 0 — a balance factor of 2 at 20.</li><li>The right child 30 leans left, so this is the right-left case: first a right rotation about 30 lifts 25, then a left rotation about 20 lifts 25 again to the root of that side.</li><li>Final tree: root 20, left child 10, right child 28 with children 25 and 30. Preorder is <code class=\"mm\">20, 10, 28, 25, 30</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A), 20, 10, 28, 25, 30.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Stopping after the first rotation at 30 and leaving 25 as the right child of 20, which gives option (B) and an unbalanced tree.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A right-left case needs the inner key (25 here) to rise twice; skipping the first rotation is the standard error, and the resulting tree visibly violates the balance condition.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q10", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "avl-trees-rotations", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Each of the following sequences is inserted, in the order shown, into an empty AVL tree. For which sequence does the whole insertion perform exactly one double rotation (LR or RL) and no single rotation?</p>", "options": ["10, 20, 30", "20, 10, 30", "30, 10, 20", "30, 20, 10"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The rotation a repair needs is decided by the direction of the imbalance and the direction of the taller child: agreeing directions need one single rotation, disagreeing directions need a double rotation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>10, 20, 30: a right-right chain at 10, so one single left rotation gives root 20. Not a double rotation.</li><li>20, 10, 30: a balanced tree from the start, no rotation at all.</li><li>30, 10, 20: 30 gets left child 10, then 20 becomes the right child of 10. At 30 the left subtree has height 1 and the right is empty, and the left child 10 leans right — a left-right case. A left rotation about 10 followed by a right rotation about 30 gives root 20 with children 10 and 30. Exactly one double rotation, no single rotation.</li><li>30, 20, 10: a left-left chain at 30, so one single right rotation gives root 20.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C), 30, 10, 20.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Choosing 10, 20, 30 because it is the textbook first example of rebalancing — but it is the single-rotation case. The double rotation needs the two imbalance directions to disagree, which only a zig-zag insertion produces.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Three keys always produce root = the median, but the cost differs: sorted triples cost one single rotation, a zig-zag triple costs one double rotation, and only the zig-zag shows LR or RL.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q11", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "avl-trees-rotations", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about AVL trees are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["Every AVL tree is a binary search tree.", "For every node, the heights of its left and right subtrees differ by at most 1.", "An AVL tree with n nodes has height O(log n).", "A single insertion into an AVL tree may require more than two rotations."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An AVL tree is a BST plus a balance invariant, and the invariant is what converts the O(h) bound of a plain BST into an O(log n) bound.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Statement (A): the AVL condition is imposed on top of the BST ordering, which the rotations are designed to preserve, so every AVL tree is a BST. True.</li><li>Statement (B): this is the defining balance condition. True.</li><li>Statement (C): the minimum number of nodes in an AVL tree of height h grows like &phi;<sup>h</sup>, so h = O(log n) with base &phi; &asymp; 1.618. True.</li><li>Statement (D): an insertion unbalances only nodes on the insertion path, and the deepest unbalanced node is repaired by one single rotation or one double rotation; after that repair every node on the path is balanced again, so at most two elementary rotations occur. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C); (D) is FALSE.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Believing that every node on the insertion path needs its own rotation. Only the deepest unbalanced node is repaired, and the repair fixes the ancestors as well.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> One insertion costs at most one single or one double rotation; it is deletion that can need O(log n) rotations, which is the usual place this pair of facts gets mixed up.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q12", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "avl-trees-rotations", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What is the minimum number of nodes in an AVL tree of height 4, where the height of a tree is the number of edges on the longest root-to-leaf path?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "12", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The sparsest AVL tree of a given height is built by making the two subtrees as unbalanced as the rule allows: heights h &minus; 1 and h &minus; 2. That recurrence gives the minimum node count N(h) = N(h&minus;1) + N(h&minus;2) + 1.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Base cases: <code class=\"mm\">N(0) = 1</code> (a single node) and <code class=\"mm\">N(1) = 2</code> (a root with one child).</li><li><code class=\"mm\">N(2) = N(1) + N(0) + 1 = 2 + 1 + 1 = 4</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">N(3) = N(2) + N(1) + 1 = 4 + 2 + 1 = 7</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">N(4) = N(3) + N(2) + 1 = 7 + 4 + 1 = 12</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 12.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Using the complete-tree count 2<sup>h+1</sup> &minus; 1 = 31, which is the maximum, not the minimum; or counting height in levels, which would answer 7.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The sequence 1, 2, 4, 7, 12, 20, 33 is the AVL minimum-node sequence; it is what makes the height logarithmic and it is worth memorising to height 5.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q13", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "avl-trees-rotations", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The keys 50, 40, 60, 30, 45, 55, 70, 20 are inserted in that order into an empty AVL tree. How many rotations, single or double, does the whole insertion sequence perform?</p>", "options": ["1", "2", "3", "0"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A rotation is performed only when a node's balance factor reaches &plusmn;2. A tree can be lopsided and still perfectly legal, so the balance factor must actually be computed at every step rather than assumed from the shape.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>50, 40, 60, 30, 45, 55, 70: a complete tree of height 2, every balance factor within &plusmn;1, no rotation.</li><li>Inserting 20 descends 50 &rarr; 40 &rarr; 30 and becomes the left child of 30.</li><li>Check the path bottom-up. Node 30: left subtree height 0, right subtree empty, factor &minus;1. Node 40: left subtree (30 with child 20) has height 1, right subtree (45) has height 0, factor &minus;1. Node 50: left subtree height 2, right subtree (60 with 55 and 70) height 1, factor &minus;1.</li><li>Every factor is within &plusmn;1, so no rotation is triggered anywhere on the path.</li><li>Final tree: root 50, left 40 with 30 (20) and 45, right 60 with 55 and 70. Preorder <code class=\"mm\">50, 40, 30, 20, 45, 60, 55, 70</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D), 0 rotations.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming that eight keys cannot possibly stay balanced, or counting 20 as a left-left case at 40 — 40's right subtree 45 is not empty, so the two sides differ by only one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Compute balance factors bottom-up along the insertion path; an imbalance needs a difference of 2, and one short right subtree is often enough to keep a chain legal.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q14", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "b-trees-b-plus-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">In a B-tree of order 5, where the order is the maximum number of children a node may have, what is the maximum number of keys a single node can hold?</p>", "options": ["5", "3", "4", "2"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A B-tree node with c children separates them with c &minus; 1 keys, so the child bound and the key bound differ by exactly one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The order fixes the maximum number of children: 5.</li><li>Between and around those 5 children sit 5 &minus; 1 = 4 separator keys.</li><li>So a node holds at most <b>4</b> keys, and the same node, when it holds 4 keys, is full.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C), 4.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 5, which is the child bound rather than the key bound. The minimum side is just as easily confused: at least &lceil;5/2&rceil; &minus; 1 = 2 keys for a non-root node.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Memorise the pair together: order m means at most m children, at most m &minus; 1 keys, and at least &lceil;m/2&rceil; &minus; 1 keys for every node except the root.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q15", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "b-trees-b-plus-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A B+ tree is stored on 4096-byte disk blocks. Each key occupies 8 bytes and each pointer 6 bytes, and an internal node stores n pointers together with n &minus; 1 keys. What is the largest n for which an internal node still fits in one block?</p>", "options": ["256", "292", "294", "293"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The fan-out of an internal node is a packing question: pointers and keys share the block, so the constraint is 6n + 8(n &minus; 1) &le; block size, and n must be an integer.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Write the space used by an internal node with n pointers: <code class=\"mm\">6n + 8(n &minus; 1) = 14n &minus; 8</code> bytes.</li><li>Require <code class=\"mm\">14n &minus; 8 &le; 4096</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">14n &le; 4104</code> and <code class=\"mm\">n &le; 293.14</code>.</li><li>Check n = 293: <code class=\"mm\">6(293) + 8(292) = 1758 + 2336 = 4094</code> bytes, which fits.</li><li>Check n = 294: <code class=\"mm\">6(294) + 8(293) = 1764 + 2344 = 4108</code> bytes, which overflows the block by 12 bytes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D), 293.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Dividing 4096 by the 14 bytes per pointer-key pair and rounding to the nearest integer, giving 294; the &minus;8 byte term makes the exact bound one lower, and the rounded-up answer overflows the block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Always check the candidate and its successor numerically; with a subtraction term in the inequality, rounding to nearest is wrong and the floor of the exact quotient is right.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q16", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "b-trees-b-plus-trees", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about B+ trees are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["Every record pointer is stored at the leaf level.", "The leaf nodes are linked in key order, so a range query need not return to the root for each key.", "A key that appears in an internal node is stored again in a leaf.", "Internal nodes store record pointers as well as keys."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A B+ tree separates the index from the data: internal nodes hold only separator keys and child pointers, and every record lives in a leaf, which is what makes the height uniform and range scans cheap.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Statement (A): the leaves are the only level that carries record pointers. True.</li><li>Statement (B): the leaves form a linked list in key order, so a scan follows the links instead of re-descending. True.</li><li>Statement (C): a separator key is a copy of a key that also exists in a leaf, which is why a search must always descend to the leaf. True.</li><li>Statement (D): internal nodes hold keys and child pointers only; the record pointers are in the leaves. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C); (D) is FALSE.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming that finding a key in an internal node ends the search, as it does in a B-tree. In a B+ tree the internal copy is only a signpost, so the search continues to a leaf.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The B-tree answers point queries in fewer levels; the B+ tree trades that for a uniform leaf level and a linked leaf chain, which is why file systems and database indexes use it.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q17", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "b-trees-b-plus-trees", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Consider a B-tree of order m, where m is the maximum number of children a node may have and every node except the root holds at least &lceil;m/2&rceil; &minus; 1 keys. Which of the following statements are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["Every internal node other than the root has at least &lceil;m/2&rceil; children.", "All leaves lie at the same level.", "For a fixed number of keys n, the height of the tree grows linearly in n.", "When the tree has more than one node, the root may hold as few as one key."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The B-tree invariants are an occupancy floor, an occupancy ceiling and a level condition; together they bound the height by a logarithm of the key count.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Statement (A): a node with at least &lceil;m/2&rceil; &minus; 1 keys has at least one more child than keys, so at least &lceil;m/2&rceil; children. True.</li><li>Statement (B): every leaf is at the same depth by the definition of a B-tree, which is what keeps every search the same length. True.</li><li>Statement (C): the minimum fan-out is 2 at the root and &lceil;m/2&rceil; below it, so the number of keys is at least exponential in the height and the height is O(log<sub>&lceil;m/2&rceil;</sub> n), not linear. False.</li><li>Statement (D): the root is exempt from the occupancy floor and may hold a single key with two children. True.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D); (C) is FALSE.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Applying the occupancy floor to the root and concluding that the root needs &lceil;m/2&rceil; &minus; 1 keys, which would forbid the one-key root that every growing tree passes through.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The floor applies to every node except the root, the ceiling applies to all nodes, and the equal-leaf-depth rule is what makes the height bound O(log n) instead of O(n).</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q18", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "b-trees-b-plus-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">In a B-tree of order 5, that is with at most 5 children per node, what is the minimum number of children the root can have when the tree contains more than one node?</p>", "options": ["2", "1", "3", "4"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The root is the one node exempt from the occupancy floor, but a root with a single child would be a redundant level: the tree is only allowed to grow a level when the root splits into two.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A non-root internal node of order 5 needs at least &lceil;5/2&rceil; &minus; 1 = 2 keys, hence at least 3 children.</li><li>The root needs at least 1 key, hence at least 2 children, whenever it is not a leaf.</li><li>A root with exactly 1 child would mean every search passes through it without a comparison that could discriminate, so it is not permitted as a stable state.</li><li>The minimum is therefore <b>2</b>, and this is exactly what a root split produces.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A), 2.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 3 by applying the non-root floor to the root, or 1 by taking \"at least one key\" literally and forgetting that a one-child root is collapsed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The root holds between 1 and m &minus; 1 keys and between 2 and m children; the non-root floor &lceil;m/2&rceil; &minus; 1 keys applies only below the root.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q19", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "b-trees-b-plus-trees", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A B-tree of order 5 holds at most 5 children and at most 4 keys per node. What is the maximum total number of keys in such a tree of height 2, where the height counts edges from the root and all leaves are at the same level?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "124", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The maximum key count of a B-tree of a given height is obtained by filling every node to the ceiling: 4 keys per node and the full 5-way branching at every internal level.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Height 2 means three levels: the root at level 1, its children at level 2, and the leaves at level 3.</li><li>Level 1: the root holds at most 4 keys.</li><li>Level 2: the root has at most 5 children, each holding at most 4 keys, so <code class=\"mm\">5 &times; 4 = 20</code> keys.</li><li>Level 3: each of those 5 nodes has at most 5 children, giving <code class=\"mm\">5 &times; 5 = 25</code> nodes, each with at most 4 keys, so <code class=\"mm\">25 &times; 4 = 100</code> keys.</li><li>Total <code class=\"mm\">4 + 20 + 100 = 124</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 124.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reading height 2 as two levels and answering 24; or using 5 keys per node instead of 4, which gives 155.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Keys at level i of a full B-tree of order m number m<sup>i&minus;1</sup>(m &minus; 1) counting from the root, so the total is (m &minus; 1)(1 + m + &hellip; + m<sup>h</sup>) = m<sup>h+1</sup> &minus; 1, here 5<sup>3</sup> &minus; 1 = 124.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q20", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "trie-basics", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The strings cat, car and dog are stored in a standard trie with one node per distinct prefix character and one root node. Counting the root, how many nodes does the trie have?</p>", "options": ["7", "9", "8", "10"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A trie merges the prefixes of its keys: one node per distinct prefix, so shared prefixes cost nothing extra and the node count is the number of distinct prefixes including the empty one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The root represents the empty prefix: 1 node.</li><li>cat contributes c, a, t. car reuses c and a and adds only r. So the c-branch holds 4 nodes after the root.</li><li>dog contributes d, o, g, none of them shared: 3 more nodes.</li><li>Total <code class=\"mm\">1 + 4 + 3 = 8</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C), 8.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting one node per character of every key, which gives 9 and ignores that car shares c and a with cat.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The node count is the number of distinct prefixes, not the total length of the keys; a trie saves exactly the length of every shared prefix.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q21", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "trie-basics", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A trie stores n keys whose maximum length is L characters, and each child lookup takes O(1) time. What is the worst-case time to search for a key of length L?</p>", "options": ["O(1)", "O(log n)", "O(L)", "O(n)"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A trie search is a walk down one root-to-node path with one step per character, so its cost is set by the length of the key, not by how many keys are stored.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The search starts at the root and reads the key character by character.</li><li>At each step it performs one child lookup, which is O(1) under the stated assumption.</li><li>A key of length L uses exactly L steps, whatever n is.</li><li>So the cost is <code class=\"mm\">O(L)</code>, which is O(1) only when the alphabet and key length are bounded.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C), O(L).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering O(log n) by analogy with balanced search trees, or O(1) by analogy with hashing. A trie is neither: it pays per character, and a long key is slow even in a small trie.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> This is the trie's headline property and also its cost: comparisons per key drop to zero, but the work becomes proportional to key length.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q22", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "trie-basics", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The eight strings bear, bell, bid, bull, buy, sell, stock and stop are stored in a standard trie with one node per distinct prefix character and one root node. Counting the root, how many nodes does the trie have?</p>", "options": ["21", "22", "24", "19"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each key adds exactly the characters that extend the longest prefix it shares with the keys already inserted, so the node count is one plus the sum over keys of that extension length.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>bear: b, e, a, r are all new &rarr; 4 nodes.</li><li>bell: b and e exist; l, l are new &rarr; 2 nodes.</li><li>bid: b exists; i, d are new &rarr; 2 nodes.</li><li>bull: b exists; u, l, l are new &rarr; 3 nodes.</li><li>buy: b and u exist; y is new &rarr; 1 node.</li><li>sell: s, e, l, l are all new &rarr; 4 nodes. Note e here is a different node from the e under b.</li><li>stock: s exists; t, o, c, k are new &rarr; 4 nodes.</li><li>stop: s, t, o exist; p is new &rarr; 1 node.</li><li>Total <code class=\"mm\">1 + 4 + 2 + 2 + 3 + 1 + 4 + 4 + 1 = 22</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B), 22.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Sharing the e of bell with the e of sell, which gives 21; a trie branches at the root by first character, so those two e's are distinct nodes. Counting only the 21 characters added and forgetting the root gives 21 as well.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Add the characters key by key and only merge a prefix with a key already inserted; count the root separately and last.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q23", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "trie-basics", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about standard tries are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["The time to look up a key of length L does not depend on how many keys the trie holds.", "A trie holding n keys always uses O(n) nodes, whatever the lengths of the keys.", "Keys that share a prefix share the nodes on the path for that prefix.", "Storing a key that is a proper prefix of another key requires an explicit end-of-key marker."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A trie indexes characters, not keys: the path encodes the key and the node count is driven by the total number of distinct prefixes, which is why its cost model differs from every comparison-based structure.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Statement (A): a lookup walks L nodes, one per character, and never inspects the other keys. True.</li><li>Statement (B): the node count is bounded by the total number of characters over all keys, which is unbounded in n alone. Counterexample: two keys of length 1000 need about 2000 nodes. False.</li><li>Statement (C): that sharing is the definition of the structure. True.</li><li>Statement (D): without a marker, the node ending \"cat\" cannot be distinguished from an internal node on the way to \"cater\". True.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D); (B) is FALSE.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming the node count is linear in the number of keys. It is linear in the total length of the keys, which is the real space cost of a trie and the reason compressed tries exist.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A trie trades space for time: O(L) lookups at O(total characters) nodes. A compressed (radix) trie merges unary chains to bring the space back down.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q24", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "trie-basics", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The children of every node of a trie are visited in alphabetical order. Which traversal then lists all stored keys in lexicographic order?</p>", "options": ["Postorder depth-first", "Preorder depth-first", "Breadth-first", "Reverse inorder"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Lexicographic order is the order in which a depth-first walk that visits children alphabetically reaches the nodes, so the keys are emitted as soon as their node is entered, before any sibling branch is explored.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>In a preorder walk a node is reported before its children, so a key is reported the moment its last character is reached.</li><li>Visiting the alphabetically smaller child first means every key with a smaller next character is reported before any key with a larger one.</li><li>That is exactly the lexicographic rule, and it also places a prefix key before all keys that extend it.</li><li>A postorder walk would report a key only after all longer keys that extend it, reversing the required order for prefixes; breadth-first mixes lengths.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B), preorder depth-first.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Choosing postorder, which is the traversal that would order \"cater\" before \"cat\" — the opposite of lexicographic order. Breadth-first is tempting because it looks ordered, but it sorts by length.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Lexicographic order on a trie is a preorder DFS with sorted children; this is the mechanism behind autocomplete and the reason tries are used for ordered dictionaries.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q25", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "trie-basics", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The five strings do, dog, done, cat and car are stored in a standard trie with one node per distinct prefix character and one root node. Counting the root, how many nodes does the trie have?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "10", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The node count of a trie is one plus the number of distinct non-empty prefixes of its keys; keys that are prefixes of other keys add no nodes of their own.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Start at the root: 1 node.</li><li>do contributes d, o &rarr; 2 nodes.</li><li>dog extends do by g &rarr; 1 node.</li><li>done shares do and extends by n, e &rarr; 2 nodes. Note that do, dog and done all branch from the same o.</li><li>cat contributes c, a, t &rarr; 3 nodes, since c and a are new.</li><li>car shares c and a and adds only r &rarr; 1 node.</li><li>Total <code class=\"mm\">1 + 2 + 1 + 2 + 3 + 1 = 10</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 10.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting one node per character of every key, which gives 12 by double-counting the shared d, o and the shared c, a; or forgetting that do, dog and done share the node for o.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> List the distinct prefixes rather than the characters: root, d, do, dog, don, done, c, ca, cat, car gives ten nodes, which is also the safest way to check the count by hand.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q26", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "bst-insertion-deletion", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The keys 40, 20, 60, 10, 30, 50, 70 are inserted in that order into an empty binary search tree. How many nodes of the resulting tree have exactly one child?</p>", "options": ["2", "3", "4", "0"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The number of children a node ends up with is fixed by how many later keys fall on each side of it while it is the active search position. A node gets two children exactly when at least one later key is smaller and at least one is larger than it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>40 becomes the root.</li><li>20 &lt; 40 goes left; 60 &gt; 40 goes right.</li><li>10 &lt; 40 then &lt; 20 becomes the left child of 20; 30 &lt; 40 then &gt; 20 becomes the right child of 20.</li><li>50 &lt; 40? No, 50 &gt; 40 then &lt; 60 becomes the left child of 60; 70 &gt; 40 then &gt; 60 becomes the right child of 60.</li><li>The tree is a perfect tree of height 2: root 40, children 20 and 60, and each of those has two children. The three internal nodes each have two children and the four leaves have none, so no node has exactly one child.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D), 0.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting 20 or 60 as one-child nodes by looking only at one level of the drawing, or confusing &ldquo;one child&rdquo; with &ldquo;one subtree that contains keys&rdquo;. Every internal node here has both children present.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Draw the final tree, then count children per node. A balanced insertion order fills both sides of every internal node, so the one-child count is zero.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q27", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "bst-insertion-deletion", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The keys 15, 10, 25, 5, 20, 30 are inserted in that order into an empty binary search tree. What is the preorder traversal of the resulting tree?</p>", "options": ["15, 10, 25, 5, 20, 30", "15, 10, 5, 25, 20, 30", "15, 10, 5, 20, 25, 30", "15, 25, 20, 30, 10, 5"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Preorder is root, then the whole left subtree, then the whole right subtree. In a binary search tree the shape is decided by the insertion order, and the traversal must be read off that shape, not off the sorted key order.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>15 is the root. 10 &lt; 15 becomes its left child; 25 &gt; 15 becomes its right child.</li><li>5 &lt; 15, &lt; 10 becomes the left child of 10. 20 &lt; 25 and &gt; 15 becomes the left child of 25. 30 &gt; 25 becomes the right child of 25.</li><li>Left subtree of the root is 10 with left child 5, so its preorder is <code class=\"mm\">10, 5</code>.</li><li>Right subtree of the root is 25 with children 20 and 30, so its preorder is <code class=\"mm\">25, 20, 30</code>.</li><li>Preorder: 15, then 10, 5, then 25, 20, 30.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B), 15, 10, 5, 25, 20, 30.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Writing the sorted order 5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30, which is the inorder traversal and is the same for every insertion order of these keys. Option (A) is the level-order reading of the first three keys only.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Inorder is always sorted in a binary search tree and therefore never distinguishes one insertion order from another; preorder and postorder do.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q28", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "bst-insertion-deletion", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The keys 8, 4, 12, 2, 6, 10, 14 are inserted in that order into an empty binary search tree. What is the height of the resulting tree, measured in edges, so that a tree with a single node has height 0?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Height measured in edges is the number of edges on the longest root-to-leaf path; a one-node tree has height 0 and each extra level adds one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>8 is the root.</li><li>4 &lt; 8 goes left of 8; 12 &gt; 8 goes right of 8.</li><li>2 &lt; 4 goes left of 4; 6 &gt; 4 goes right of 4.</li><li>10 &lt; 12 goes left of 12; 14 &gt; 12 goes right of 12.</li><li>The tree is perfect with three levels: level 0 holds 8, level 1 holds 4 and 12, level 2 holds 2, 6, 10, 14.</li><li>The longest root-to-leaf path has two edges, for example 8 &rarr; 4 &rarr; 2.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reporting 3 by counting the number of levels instead of the number of edges on the path, which is the usual source of an off-by-one in height questions.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Fix the convention before counting: levels equal edges plus one for a root-to-leaf path, so height in edges is always one less than the level count.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q29", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "bst-insertion-deletion", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The keys 50, 30, 70, 20, 40, 60, 80, 35 are inserted in that order into an empty binary search tree. The key 40, which has exactly one child, is then deleted by splicing that child into its place. Which key becomes the right child of 30?</p>", "options": ["35", "20", "45", "60"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Deletion in a binary search tree has three cases. A node with no children is simply removed, a node with one child is replaced by that child, and only a node with two children needs a predecessor or successor substitution.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Build the tree: 50 is the root, 30 its left child, 70 its right child.</li><li>20 goes left of 30, 40 goes right of 30. 60 goes left of 70, 80 goes right of 70.</li><li>35 &lt; 50, &lt; 30? No: 35 &gt; 30, so it continues to 40, and 35 &lt; 40 makes it the left child of 40.</li><li>Node 40 therefore has exactly one child, 35, and no right child.</li><li>Splicing replaces 40 by 35: the right pointer of 30 now points at 35, and 35 keeps its own subtree, which is empty.</li><li>Inorder after deletion: 20, 30, 35, 50, 60, 70, 80, still sorted, so the tree is a valid binary search tree.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A), 35.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Applying the two-child rule anyway and looking for a successor. Here the successor of 40 is 50, not 35, so that route gives the wrong key; and 45 is not even in the tree.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Count the children of the node being deleted before choosing a rule. One-child deletion is a pointer splice and never needs a successor or predecessor.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q30", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "bst-insertion-deletion", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about searching in a binary search tree with n distinct keys are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["In the worst case, a search in a binary search tree with n nodes performs &Theta;(n) comparisons.", "A successful search for a key stored at depth d, with the root at depth 0, performs exactly d + 1 comparisons.", "Inserting n distinct keys in increasing sorted order builds a tree of height &lceil;log<sub>2</sub> n&rceil;.", "Every binary search tree with n nodes has height O(log n)."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A binary search tree gives no balance guarantee on its own. Its search cost is the height of the tree, and the height is a property of the insertion order, not of n.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Statement (A): a tree that degenerates into a chain of n nodes has height n &minus; 1, and a search from the root to the deepest node touches every node, so the worst case is &Theta;(n). True.</li><li>Statement (B): the search compares the root once, then one key per edge descended. A node at depth d is reached after d edges, so d + 1 comparisons. True.</li><li>Statement (C): increasing order makes every new key larger than all earlier ones, so each insertion hangs off the right of the previous node. The result is a right chain of height n &minus; 1, not &lceil;log<sub>2</sub> n&rceil;. False.</li><li>Statement (D): the same right-chain example has height &Theta;(n), so O(log n) fails for general binary search trees. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (B); (C) and (D) are FALSE.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming the average-case O(log n) of a randomly built tree is a guarantee. Sorted or nearly sorted input is the standard way to force the linear case, and it is exactly what balanced trees such as AVL trees are designed to prevent.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Separate the cost model from the guarantee: cost equals height plus one comparisons, and the height is O(log n) only when the tree is balanced.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q31", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "bst-insertion-deletion", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Each of the following sequences is inserted, in the order shown, into an empty binary search tree. For which sequence is the preorder traversal of the resulting tree NOT 50, 30, 20, 40, 70, 60, 80?</p>", "options": ["50, 30, 70, 20, 40, 60, 80", "50, 30, 20, 70, 40, 60, 80", "50, 70, 30, 60, 20, 40, 80", "50, 30, 20, 40, 60, 70, 80"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The preorder traversal of a binary search tree determines the tree uniquely, because the first key is the root and the remaining keys split at the first value greater than the root. Once the tree is fixed, any insertion order that places every node before its own descendants rebuilds exactly that tree.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The target preorder gives root 50; 30 &lt; 50 starts the left block, so the left subtree is 30 with children 20 and 40; the right block is 70 with children 60 and 80.</li><li>The constraint on an insertion order is therefore: 50 first, 30 before 20 and 40, and 70 before 60 and 80. Anything else changes the shape.</li><li>(A) 50, 30, 70, 20, 40, 60, 80: every ancestor precedes its descendants. The tree is the target one.</li><li>(B) 50, 30, 20, 70, 40, 60, 80: 20 is inserted before 40 but 30 precedes both, so the shape is unchanged.</li><li>(C) 50, 70, 30, 60, 20, 40, 80: 60 comes after 70, and 30 comes before 20 and 40. Again the target tree.</li><li>(D) 50, 30, 20, 40, 60, 70, 80: 60 is inserted before 70. Since 60 &gt; 50, it first lands as the right child of 50; then 70 &gt; 60 becomes the right child of 60, and 80 the right child of 70. The right side is the chain 60&ndash;70&ndash;80, whose preorder is 50, 30, 20, 40, 60, 70, 80.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D), 50, 30, 20, 40, 60, 70, 80.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Believing the insertion order must be the preorder itself. It need not be: any linear extension of the ancestor relation works, which is why three different orders give the same tree here.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> To test an insertion order, check only one thing: is every node inserted before the keys that end up in its subtree? If yes the tree is unchanged; the first violation reshapes the tree.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q32", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "bst-insertion-deletion", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The keys 50, 30, 70, 20, 40, 60, 80, 35, 45, 65 are inserted in that order into an empty binary search tree. The key 30 is then deleted by replacing it with its inorder successor. How many leaf nodes does the resulting tree have?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Deleting a two-child node by successor replacement copies the smallest key of the right subtree into the deleted position and then removes that successor from where it sat, which is always a node with no left child.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Build the tree: 50 is the root; 30 is its left child with children 20 and 40; 70 is its right child with children 60 and 80.</li><li>35 &gt; 30 goes to the right of 30 and then left of 40; 45 &gt; 40 becomes the right child of 40; 65 &lt; 70 and &gt; 60 becomes the right child of 60.</li><li>Node 30 has two children. Its right subtree is rooted at 40, whose leftmost key is 35, so the successor of 30 is 35.</li><li>Copy 35 into the position of 30 and delete 35 from under 40. Node 35 was a leaf, so nothing takes its place.</li><li>The resulting tree: root 50; left subtree 35 with children 20 and 40, where 40 has only the right child 45; right subtree 70 with children 60 and 80, where 60 has the right child 65.</li><li>Leaves are the nodes with no children: 20, 45, 65 and 80, so there are four.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting 35 as a leaf after the move, or leaving 35 in place as well and so reporting five leaves. The successor is relocated, not duplicated, so 40 loses its left child and 35 stops being a leaf.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Successor replacement moves one key and removes one node from the right subtree. Recount the leaves from scratch after the move instead of adjusting the old count.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q33", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "avl-trees-rotations", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">In an AVL tree the balance factor of a node is defined as the height of its left subtree minus the height of its right subtree. A node has balance factor &minus;1. Which statement is TRUE?</p>", "options": ["Its right subtree is exactly one level taller than its left subtree.", "Its left subtree is exactly one level taller than its right subtree.", "Its two subtrees have equal height.", "The node breaks the AVL condition and must be rotated."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The balance factor encodes the difference of the two subtree heights, and its sign carries the direction. A negative value means the right side is heavier, a positive value means the left side is heavier, and zero means the two sides are level.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Balance factor = height(left) &minus; height(right) = &minus;1.</li><li>Rearranging, height(left) = height(right) &minus; 1, so the left subtree is one level shorter, that is the right subtree is one level taller.</li><li>The AVL condition allows balance factors of &minus;1, 0 and +1, so &minus;1 is legal and no rotation is triggered.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reversing the sign convention and calling &minus;1 a left-heavy node. Always read the definition given in the question: left minus right makes &minus;1 a right-heavy node.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Write the balance-factor definition at the top of any AVL question. Every rotation choice then follows mechanically from the sign at the unbalanced node and the sign at its taller child.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q34", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "avl-trees-rotations", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The keys 15, 10, 20, 5, 12, 25, 30 are inserted in that order into an empty AVL tree. What is the preorder traversal of the final tree?</p>", "options": ["15, 10, 5, 12, 25, 20, 30", "15, 10, 5, 12, 20, 25, 30", "25, 15, 10, 5, 12, 20, 30", "15, 10, 12, 5, 25, 20, 30"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An AVL insertion repairs only the deepest unbalanced node on the insertion path, using one single or one double rotation, and that repair restores the balance of every ancestor as well.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>15, 10, 20, 5, 12: the tree is 15 with left child 10 (children 5 and 12) and right child 20. Every balance factor is within &plusmn;1.</li><li>Insert 25: it becomes the right child of 20. Heights at 20 are left 0 and right 1, factor &minus;1; at 15 they are left 2 and right 2, factor 0. Still balanced.</li><li>Insert 30: it descends 15 &rarr; 20 &rarr; 25 and becomes the right child of 25.</li><li>At node 20 the left subtree is empty and the right subtree has height 2, so the factor is &minus;2. Its taller child 25 has factor &minus;1, the same direction, so a single left rotation at 20 is enough.</li><li>After the rotation 25 takes the place of 20, with left child 20 and right child 30. Node 15 now has left height 2 and right height 2, factor 0.</li><li>Final tree: 15 with left child 10 (children 5, 12) and right child 25 (children 20, 30). Preorder: 15, 10, 5, 12, 25, 20, 30.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A), 15, 10, 5, 12, 25, 20, 30.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reporting the plain binary search tree order 15, 10, 5, 12, 20, 25, 30 by forgetting the rotation entirely, or rotating at 15 instead of at the deepest unbalanced node 20.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Rotate at the lowest node whose factor reaches &plusmn;2. Rotating higher up would not fix the actual violation, and the repair would have to be undone later.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q35", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "avl-trees-rotations", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What is the maximum number of nodes in an AVL tree of height 3, where the height is the number of edges on the longest root-to-leaf path?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "15", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> To pack as many nodes as possible into a fixed height, fill every level completely. A complete binary tree of height h satisfies the AVL condition, because at every node the two subtree heights differ by at most one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Height 0 holds at most 1 node.</li><li>Height 1 holds at most 1 + 2 = 3 nodes.</li><li>Height 2 holds at most 1 + 2 + 4 = 7 nodes.</li><li>Height 3 holds at most 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 = 15 nodes.</li><li>Equivalently, 2<sup>h+1</sup> &minus; 1 with h = 3 gives 16 &minus; 1 = 15.</li><li>The complete tree of height 3 is itself AVL, so the bound is attained and not merely an upper limit.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 15.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 7 by treating the height as the number of levels, or answering 12 by quoting the minimum-node sequence instead of the maximum.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Keep the two AVL bounds apart: at most 2<sup>h+1</sup> &minus; 1 nodes, at least N(h) = N(h&minus;1) + N(h&minus;2) + 1 nodes, where the minimum sequence is 1, 2, 4, 7, 12, 20.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q36", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "avl-trees-rotations", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about AVL trees are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["The balance factor of every node is &minus;1, 0 or 1.", "Every complete binary tree satisfies the AVL balance condition.", "A rotation preserves the inorder sequence of the keys in the tree.", "An AVL tree with 20 nodes cannot have height 5, where height is measured in edges."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An AVL tree is a binary search tree plus a height invariant, and the rotations used to restore that invariant are chosen so that the ordering of keys never changes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) The invariant is exactly |height(left) &minus; height(right)| &le; 1 at every node, so the factor lies in {&minus;1, 0, 1}. True.</li><li>(B) In a complete binary tree every level except the last is full, so at any node the two subtree heights differ by at most one. True.</li><li>(C) A rotation only rewires parent and child pointers inside a subtree whose keys keep their relative order, so the inorder listing is unchanged. True.</li><li>(D) The sparsest AVL tree of height h satisfies N(h) = N(h&minus;1) + N(h&minus;2) + 1 with N(0) = 1, N(1) = 2, giving 4, 7, 12, 20 for h = 2, 3, 4, 5. Exactly 20 nodes is enough for height 5, so such a tree exists. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C); (D) is FALSE.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Treating the minimum-node sequence 1, 2, 4, 7, 12, 20 as an exclusive bound and concluding that height 5 needs more than 20 nodes. The value 20 is attainable, not exceeded.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For any &ldquo;can an AVL tree of n nodes have height h&rdquo; question, compare n with N(h): the height is possible exactly when N(h) &le; n &le; 2<sup>h+1</sup> &minus; 1.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q37", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "avl-trees-rotations", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The keys 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7 are inserted in that order into an empty AVL tree. Which key is at the root of the final tree?</p>", "options": ["3", "5", "4", "7"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Inserting keys in increasing order into an AVL tree triggers repeated single left rotations, and the tree that results is the complete tree of the same size, not a chain.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Insert 1, 2, 3: 3 creates a right-right chain at 1, so one left rotation makes 2 the root with children 1 and 3.</li><li>Insert 4: it becomes the right child of 3. Balance factors stay within &plusmn;1.</li><li>Insert 5: it makes 3&rsquo;s right subtree a chain 4&ndash;5, so a left rotation at 3 lifts 4 above 3 and 5. The root 2 now has left height 1 and right height 2.</li><li>Insert 6: the right subtree becomes 4 with children 3 and 5, and 5 gains the right child 6. The factor at 2 reaches &minus;2, so a left rotation at 2 lifts 4 to the root: 4 with left child 2 (children 1 and 3) and right child 5 (right child 6).</li><li>Insert 7: it makes 6&rsquo;s right subtree a chain, so a left rotation at 5 lifts 6 above 5 and 7.</li><li>The root stays 4, and the tree is 4 with left child 2 (children 1, 3) and right child 6 (children 5, 7), the complete tree on seven keys.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C), 4.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Guessing the middle key 4 because it is the median. That happens to be right here, but the median argument is not the reason and it fails for other sizes; also easy to answer 5 by forgetting the final rotation after inserting 6.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Sorted insertion into an AVL tree builds a complete tree level by level. For seven keys the complete tree has root 4, and four elementary rotations are performed in total.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q38", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "avl-trees-rotations", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The keys 100, 50, 150, 25, 75, 60, 90, 55, 58 are inserted in that order into an empty AVL tree. Counting a single rotation as one elementary rotation and a double rotation as two, how many elementary rotations does the whole insertion sequence perform?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A rotation is performed only when some node reaches a balance factor of &plusmn;2. A single rotation repairs an outside case (LL or RR), a double rotation repairs an inside case (LR or RL).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>100, 50, 150, 25, 75: the tree is 100 with left child 50 (children 25 and 75) and right child 150. All factors are within &plusmn;1.</li><li>60 becomes the left child of 75, and 90 the right child of 75. At 50 the left height is 1 and the right height is 2, factor &minus;1; no rotation.</li><li>55 becomes the left child of 60. At 60 the left height is 1 and the right 0, factor +1; at 50 the left height is 1 and the right height is 3, factor &minus;2.</li><li>At node 50 the taller child 75 has factor +1, the opposite direction, so this is an RL case: a right rotation at 75 followed by a left rotation at 50, two elementary rotations. Node 60 now sits above 50 and 75.</li><li>58 descends 100 &rarr; 50 &rarr; 60 &rarr; 55 and becomes the right child of 55. At 55 the factor is &minus;1, at 60 the left height is 2 against 0 on the right, factor +2.</li><li>At node 60 the taller child 55 has factor &minus;1, the opposite direction, so this is an LR case: a left rotation at 55 then a right rotation at 60, two more elementary rotations. Node 58 sits above 55 and 60.</li><li>Total: 2 + 2 = 4 elementary rotations. The final root is 75, with preorder 75, 50, 25, 58, 55, 60, 100, 90, 150.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting each double rotation as one event and answering 2, or counting the whole insertion as needing a rotation at every ancestor and answering far too many. Also easy to rotate at 100 rather than at the deepest unbalanced node 50 or 60.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Walk the insertion path bottom-up and stop at the first node with factor &plusmn;2. Compare its sign with the sign of its taller child: same sign means one rotation, opposite sign means two.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q39", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "b-trees-b-plus-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">In a B+ tree of order 3, where the order is the maximum number of children an internal node may have, what is the maximum number of keys an internal node can hold?</p>", "options": ["3", "2", "4", "1"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An internal node with k children separates them with k &minus; 1 keys, because each key is a boundary between two neighbouring child pointers. The maximum key count is therefore one less than the maximum child count.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Order m = 3 means at most 3 children per node.</li><li>Children and keys are paired as pointer, key, pointer, key, pointer, so keys = children &minus; 1.</li><li>Maximum keys = 3 &minus; 1 = 2.</li><li>Minimum keys for a non-root node is &lceil;m/2&rceil; &minus; 1 = 2 &minus; 1 = 1, so a legal internal node holds 1 or 2 keys.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B), 2.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reading the order as the key capacity and answering 3. The order is always the child capacity, so the key capacity is m &minus; 1.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Convert the order to a key range once and reuse it: a node of order m holds between &lceil;m/2&rceil; &minus; 1 and m &minus; 1 keys, the root being allowed as few as one.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q40", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "b-trees-b-plus-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A B-tree of order 4 has height 3, measured in edges from the root, and every node other than the root holds at least &lceil;m/2&rceil; &minus; 1 keys. What is the minimum number of keys such a tree can contain?</p>", "options": ["7", "12", "15", "8"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The minimum key count is obtained by making every node as empty as the rules allow, which minimises both the number of nodes per level and the keys inside each node.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Order m = 4 gives a minimum of &lceil;4/2&rceil; = 2 children and therefore &lceil;4/2&rceil; &minus; 1 = 1 key for every non-root node.</li><li>The root must have at least 2 children when the tree has more than one node, so it holds 1 key.</li><li>Level 1: 2 nodes, 1 key each, 2 keys.</li><li>Level 2: each level-1 node has 2 children, so 4 nodes, 1 key each, 4 keys.</li><li>Level 3: 8 nodes, 1 key each, 8 keys.</li><li>Total = 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 = 15 keys.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C), 15.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Using 2 keys per node because the maximum is 3, which gives 45, or forgetting the root&rsquo;s single key and answering 14.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Minimum keys at height h in a B-tree of order m is 1 + 2&middot;(&lceil;m/2&rceil;) + 2&middot;(&lceil;m/2&rceil;)<sup>2</sup> + &hellip;, with each term weighted by the minimum keys per node; here every weight is 1, so the sum is just the node count.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q41", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "b-trees-b-plus-trees", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about B-trees are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["All leaves of a B-tree lie at the same level.", "A B-tree of order m stores at most m &minus; 1 keys in a node.", "When a node overflows, its median key moves up into the parent node.", "A B-tree may store the same key in more than one node."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A B-tree is a balanced multiway search tree: every root-to-leaf path has the same length, every node holds a bounded number of sorted keys, and overflow is handled by splitting rather than by growing one branch.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) Equal leaf depth is part of the definition, which is what keeps every search the same length. True.</li><li>(B) Order m bounds the children at m and the keys at m &minus; 1, since k children need k &minus; 1 separators. True.</li><li>(C) A split promotes the median of the overflowing node to the parent and divides the rest into two nodes. True.</li><li>(D) A key appears exactly once in a B-tree; duplication of separator keys is a B+ tree property, not a B-tree one. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C); (D) is FALSE.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Mixing the B-tree and B+ tree definitions. In a B-tree each key is stored once and may carry a record pointer at any node; in a B+ tree keys are repeated, with all records at the leaves.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Three facts cover most B-tree theory questions: equal leaf depth, keys between &lceil;m/2&rceil; &minus; 1 and m &minus; 1 per non-root node, and split by promoting the median.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q42", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "b-trees-b-plus-trees", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about B+ trees are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["In a B+ tree, a successful search for a key always ends at a leaf.", "A root-to-leaf path in a B+ tree of height h, measured in edges, visits at most h + 1 nodes.", "Deleting a key from a B+ tree may leave every internal node unchanged.", "A B+ tree stores each key exactly once in the whole tree."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a B+ tree the internal nodes form an index that only routes the search; every record, and therefore every key that carries data, lives in the leaf level, and the leaves are chained in key order.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) Internal nodes hold separator keys that merely direct the descent; the matching record is always found in a leaf. True.</li><li>(B) A path of h edges passes through h + 1 nodes counting the root and the leaf. True.</li><li>(C) If the leaf still has enough keys after the removal and the deleted key is not a separator in an ancestor, no internal node changes at all. True.</li><li>(D) A key that acts as a separator in an internal node is stored again in the leaf that holds its record, so keys can appear more than once. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C); (D) is FALSE.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming a deletion must ripple up to the root. Only an underfull leaf, or the removal of a separator key, forces a change above the leaf level.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Remember the division of labour: internal nodes route, leaves store and are linked. Point queries cost the height, range queries cost the height plus the leaves scanned.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q43", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "b-trees-b-plus-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A B-tree of order 5 holds more than one node and has height 1, that is a root plus one level of leaves. Every node other than the root holds at least &lceil;m/2&rceil; &minus; 1 keys. What is the minimum number of keys the tree can contain?</p>", "options": ["3", "5", "4", "6"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The occupancy floor is what makes a B-tree dense: a node may not be arbitrarily empty, so a given height forces a minimum number of keys.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Order m = 5, so every non-root node holds at least &lceil;5/2&rceil; &minus; 1 = 3 &minus; 1 = 2 keys and has at least 3 children.</li><li>A root with more than one node in the tree must have at least 2 children, and 2 children need 1 key.</li><li>Height 1 means exactly two levels, so there are two leaves, each holding its minimum of 2 keys.</li><li>Total = 1 + 2 + 2 = 5 keys.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B), 5.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Giving each leaf only one key, which would answer 3. A leaf of order 5 must hold at least 2 keys, so one-key leaves are not legal here.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Always compute the floor &lceil;m/2&rceil; &minus; 1 first. The floor applies to every non-root node, leaves included, and it is what makes the minimum-height calculation meaningful.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q44", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "b-trees-b-plus-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The keys 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 are inserted in that order into an empty B-tree of order 3, that is at most 3 children and at most 2 keys per node. Which keys are stored in the root of the final tree?</p>", "options": ["30 only", "20 only", "30 and 40", "20 and 40"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A node that would exceed m &minus; 1 keys splits: its median key rises into the parent and the remaining keys form two nodes. When the root splits, the tree grows one level.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Insert 10 and 20: the root holds [10, 20], which is the maximum for order 3.</li><li>Insert 30: the root would hold three keys, so it splits. The median 20 goes up into a new root, and [10] and [30] become its two children.</li><li>Insert 40: it descends past 20 to the right leaf, which becomes [30, 40].</li><li>Insert 50: that leaf would hold [30, 40, 50], so it splits. Its median 40 rises to the root, which becomes [20, 40], and the leaf becomes [30] and [50].</li><li>Final tree: root [20, 40] with the three leaves [10], [30] and [50].</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D), 20 and 40.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Promoting the largest key 50 instead of the median, or stopping after the first split and reporting 20 alone.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Splitting always promotes the median, never the maximum or the minimum, and a split can cascade upwards one level at a time.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q45", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "trie-basics", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The single string banana is stored in a standard trie with one node per distinct prefix character and one root node. Counting the root, how many nodes does the trie have?</p>", "options": ["6", "8", "12", "7"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A standard trie stores one node per distinct prefix of the stored keys, plus a root that represents the empty prefix. A single key of length L therefore contributes exactly L nodes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Prefixes of banana: the empty prefix, b, ba, ban, bana, banan, banana.</li><li>That is 6 non-empty prefixes, so 6 nodes below the root.</li><li>Adding the root gives 6 + 1 = 7 nodes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D), 7.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting the repeated letters as one node because the alphabet is small, which gives 4 or 5, or forgetting the root and answering 6.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Nodes correspond to prefixes, not to distinct characters. Repetition inside one key still costs one node per position.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q46", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "trie-basics", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A standard trie is implemented with one array of 26 child pointers per node, each pointer taking 4 bytes, plus a 1-byte end-of-key flag. How many bytes does a single trie node occupy?</p>", "options": ["104", "105", "130", "108"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The array-of-children representation trades memory for O(1) child lookup: every node reserves a slot for every letter of the alphabet whether or not that letter occurs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Children: 26 slots &times; 4 bytes = 104 bytes.</li><li>End-of-key flag: 1 byte.</li><li>Total per node: 104 + 1 = 105 bytes.</li><li>Compare this with the n&middot;L + 1 node bound: a trie holding 500 keys of length 8 can reach 4001 nodes, which at 105 bytes each is about 420 KB, most of it empty child slots.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B), 105 bytes.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 104 by forgetting the end-of-key flag, which is what lets a key end at an internal node; or assuming each node stores only the characters actually present, which is the compressed, not the array-based, representation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The array representation makes lookups O(1) per character at the cost of &Theta;(&sigma;) space per node. Terminating a key needs an explicit flag because keys can end at internal nodes.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q47", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "trie-basics", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The seven strings ant, ante, anti, bat, batch, ban and band are stored in a standard trie with one node per distinct prefix character and one root node. Counting the root, how many nodes does the trie have?</p>", "options": ["12", "14", "13", "16"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The node count equals the number of distinct prefixes of the stored keys, counting the empty prefix as the root. Shared prefixes are counted once, which is exactly where the saving over 7 &times; 5 comes from.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Under the root, the first characters are a and b, so two nodes at level 1.</li><li>Branch a: a &rarr; n &rarr; t gives ant. From that t the children e and i give ante and anti. So a contributes a, n, t, e, i = 5 nodes.</li><li>Branch b: b &rarr; a. From that a, one child t and one child n. The t branch adds c and h for batch, so b, a, t, c, h = 5 nodes; the n branch adds d for band, so n and d = 2 more.</li><li>Total below the root: 5 + 5 + 2 = 12 nodes.</li><li>Adding the root: 13 nodes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C), 13.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting 7 &times; 4 characters and forgetting that ant, ante and anti share a, n, t; or forgetting that ban is a proper prefix of band, which is why the node for ban must be marked as an end of key rather than being a leaf.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Build the trie character by character and count nodes as you create them. A key that is a proper prefix of another ends at an internal node, which needs an explicit end marker.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q48", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "trie-basics", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about standard tries are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["Inserting a key of length L takes O(L) time when each child lookup is O(1).", "A trie holding n keys of length at most L has at most n&middot;L + 1 nodes, counting the root.", "Two keys that differ in their first character share no node below the root.", "In a standard trie, keys are stored only at leaves and never at internal nodes."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A trie moves the work from key comparisons to character steps: the path spelling a key is the key, so cost is measured per character and sharing happens along common prefixes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) The insertion follows one child pointer per character and creates a node only where the path is missing, so it costs O(L). True.</li><li>(B) Each of the n keys creates at most L new nodes, and the root is one more, so n&middot;L + 1 is a valid upper bound. True.</li><li>(C) The root&rsquo;s children are indexed by first character, so keys with different first characters leave through different children and never meet again. True.</li><li>(D) A key that is a proper prefix of another ends at an internal node, which is marked as an end of key; keys are therefore not confined to leaves. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C); (D) is FALSE.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Carrying over the B+ tree rule that all records live at the leaves. A trie has no such rule, which is why it needs an end-of-key flag.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Trie cost is O(L) per operation, independent of n, and its size is bounded by the total number of characters stored plus the root.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q49", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "trie-basics", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The three strings in, inn and int are stored in a standard trie with one node per distinct prefix character and one root node. Counting the root, how many nodes does the trie have?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "5", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each distinct prefix of the stored keys gets one node, and the empty prefix is the root.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The first character is i for all three keys, so one node for i.</li><li>The second character is n for all three keys, so one node for in.</li><li>From in, the keys continue with n for inn and t for int, so two more nodes.</li><li>Nodes below the root: i, in, inn, int, which is 4.</li><li>Adding the root gives 5.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 5.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting 2 + 3 + 3 = 8 characters without merging the shared prefixes, or forgetting that in is itself a stored key and so needs an end marker at the node for in.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Count distinct prefixes, not characters. Overlapping keys shrink the trie quickly, which is why tries suit dictionaries with long shared stems.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q50", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "trie-basics", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which structure answers a prefix query, listing every stored key that begins with a given string, in time proportional to the length of the prefix plus the number of keys reported, in the worst case?</p>", "options": ["A hash table with chaining", "A balanced binary search tree", "A trie", "A sorted array with binary search"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A prefix query needs the structure to group keys by their leading characters. Only a structure that is organised along characters can walk to the prefix once and then enumerate the subtree beneath it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A hash table scatters keys by their full value, so keys sharing a prefix land in unrelated buckets and every key must be examined. It gives no bound of the required form.</li><li>A balanced binary search tree can find one key starting with the prefix in O(log n) and then scan successors, but the cost includes the log n descent for the first key and it stores characters only implicitly through key comparisons.</li><li>A trie walks the prefix in O(L) steps and then traverses the subtree whose nodes are exactly the matching keys, so the cost is O(L + number of keys reported).</li><li>A sorted array needs a binary search plus a contiguous scan, and insertion into it is expensive, so it is not the intended answer for the query cost alone.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C), a trie.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Choosing the hash table because lookups are O(1). That bound is for an exact key match and collapses for prefix queries, which need ordering by character.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Match the structure to the query: exact match favours hashing, ordered and range queries favour balanced trees, and prefix queries favour tries.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q01", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "min-max-heaps", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A max-heap of n distinct keys is stored in an array <code class=\"mm\">A[1..n]</code> with 1-based indexing, the children of <code class=\"mm\">A[i]</code> being <code class=\"mm\">A[2i]</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A[2i+1]</code>. Which one of the following is guaranteed for every valid index?</p>", "options": ["A[i] ≥ A[j] for every pair of indices with i &lt; j", "The array is sorted in non-increasing order, so A[i] ≥ A[i+1] for every 1 ≤ i &lt; n", "A[i] ≥ A[2i] and A[i] ≥ A[2i+1] whenever those children exist", "A[i] ≥ A[i+1] for every 1 ≤ i &lt; n"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A max-heap is a complete binary tree in which every parent is at least as large as each of its children. Written in level order that is exactly the local constraint <code class=\"mm\">A[i] ≥ A[2i]</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A[i] ≥ A[2i+1]</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The heap property is local: it relates a node only to its two children.</li><li>It says nothing about siblings. For <code class=\"mm\">A = [9, 7, 8, 5, 6, 4, 3]</code> the property holds, yet <code class=\"mm\">A[2] = 7 &lt; A[3] = 8</code>.</li><li>It also says nothing about the array as a whole, so the array need not be sorted: in that same example <code class=\"mm\">A[2] = 7 &lt; A[3] = 8</code> while the maximum 9 sits at <code class=\"mm\">A[1]</code>.</li><li>Only the parent–child inequality survives for every index.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — a node is at least as large as each of its children.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) The example above breaks it: 2 &lt; 3 but <code class=\"mm\">A[2] = 7 &lt; A[3] = 8</code>.</li><li>(B) That is the sorted-array property, which is far stronger than heap order.</li><li>(D) The same example breaks it as well, since <code class=\"mm\">A[2] &lt; A[3]</code>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating a heap as a sorted array. A heap is only partially ordered, and that is precisely what makes it cheap to maintain.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A heap guarantees parent ≥ child and nothing else; the root is the maximum, the rest of the array is arbitrary.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q02", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "min-max-heaps", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A max-heap of 100 distinct keys is stored in <code class=\"mm\">A[1..100]</code>. Which statement about the position of the smallest key is correct?</p>", "options": ["It is always stored at <code class=\"mm\">A[100]</code>.", "It is stored at some index in the range 51–100, and each index in that range is possible.", "It must be stored at <code class=\"mm\">A[2]</code> or <code class=\"mm\">A[3]</code>.", "It may be stored at any index from 1 to 100."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a 1-indexed array of n nodes, node i is a leaf exactly when <code class=\"mm\">2i &gt; n</code>, that is when <code class=\"mm\">i &gt; ⌊n/2⌋</code>. Nodes <code class=\"mm\">1..⌊n/2⌋</code> are internal.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>For n = 100, <code class=\"mm\">⌊100/2⌋ = 50</code>, so nodes 51–100 are exactly the leaves.</li><li>An internal node has at least one child, and the heap property forces that child to be no larger than the parent. An internal node therefore always has a key smaller than itself somewhere below it, so it can never be the global minimum.</li><li>The minimum must therefore be a leaf, that is at some index in 51–100.</li><li>Every leaf position is attainable: place the smallest key at the leaf you want and fill the rest so that each parent dominates its subtree.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — the smallest key is a leaf, at some index in 51–100.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) <code class=\"mm\">A[100]</code> is a leaf, but the minimum may sit at any of the 50 leaves.</li><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">A[2]</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A[3]</code> are internal for n = 100, so neither can hold the minimum; they are where the second largest key may sit.</li><li>(D) No internal node can hold the minimum.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Mirroring the maximum and expecting the minimum at a fixed cell. The maximum is pinned to the root by the heap property; the minimum is only known to be somewhere on the bottom level.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> In a max-heap the maximum is <code class=\"mm\">A[1]</code> and the minimum lies among the leaves ⌊n/2⌋+1 to n.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q03", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "min-max-heaps", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which one of the following arrays of seven distinct keys, read as <code class=\"mm\">A[1..7]</code>, is a valid max-heap?</p>", "options": ["9, 8, 7, 6, 9, 4, 3", "8, 9, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3", "9, 6, 8, 7, 5, 4, 3", "9, 7, 8, 5, 6, 4, 3"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> For <code class=\"mm\">A[1..7]</code> the internal nodes are 1, 2 and 3, with children <code class=\"mm\">(2,3), (4,5), (6,7)</code>. Three inequalities decide everything: <code class=\"mm\">A[1] ≥ A[2], A[3]</code>; <code class=\"mm\">A[2] ≥ A[4], A[5]</code>; <code class=\"mm\">A[3] ≥ A[6], A[7]</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Check <code class=\"mm\">9, 8, 7, 6, 9, 4, 3</code>: <code class=\"mm\">A[2] = 8</code> but its child <code class=\"mm\">A[5] = 9</code>, so the parent is smaller than a child. Invalid.</li><li>Check <code class=\"mm\">8, 9, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3</code>: the root 8 is smaller than <code class=\"mm\">A[2] = 9</code>. Invalid.</li><li>Check <code class=\"mm\">9, 6, 8, 7, 5, 4, 3</code>: <code class=\"mm\">A[2] = 6</code> but its child <code class=\"mm\">A[4] = 7</code>. Invalid.</li><li>Check <code class=\"mm\">9, 7, 8, 5, 6, 4, 3</code>: 9 ≥ 7 and 9 ≥ 8; 7 ≥ 5 and 7 ≥ 6; 8 ≥ 4 and 8 ≥ 3. All six inequalities hold, so this is a max-heap.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) — <code class=\"mm\">9, 7, 8, 5, 6, 4, 3</code> is the only valid max-heap.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) has a child larger than its parent: 9 sits below 8.</li><li>(B) has a root smaller than its left child.</li><li>(C) has 7 below 6 at index 4 below index 2.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Scanning the array from left to right and checking only consecutive pairs. Heap order is between a node and the nodes at double and double-plus-one, never between neighbours.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Test <code class=\"mm\">A[i] ≥ A[2i]</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A[i] ≥ A[2i+1]</code> for the internal nodes only; that is the whole definition.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q04", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "min-max-heaps", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A min-heap stores n = 1000 keys in <code class=\"mm\">A[1..1000]</code>. How many of its nodes have exactly one child?<br/>(Enter an integer.)</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "1", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Node i has exactly one child when <code class=\"mm\">2i ≤ n</code> but <code class=\"mm\">2i + 1 &gt; n</code>, that is when <code class=\"mm\">2i = n</code> exactly.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Node i has two children when <code class=\"mm\">2i + 1 ≤ n</code>, one child when <code class=\"mm\">2i = n</code>, and none when <code class=\"mm\">2i &gt; n</code>.</li><li>So a node with exactly one child exists only when n is even, and then only the single index <code class=\"mm\">i = n/2</code> qualifies.</li><li>Here n = 1000 is even, so <code class=\"mm\">i = 500</code> has the left child <code class=\"mm\">A[1000]</code> and no right child.</li><li>Every other internal node (1 to 499) has two children, and nodes 501 to 1000 are leaves.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1 node — the node at index 500.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a complete tree must have either 0 or 2 children everywhere. Only the last internal node can be short of a right child, and only when n is even.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> In an n-node heap exactly one node has one child when n is even (index n/2), and none when n is odd.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q05", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "min-max-heaps", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Let H be a binary min-heap holding n ≥ 2 distinct keys in <code class=\"mm\">A[1..n]</code>. Which of the following statements are <b>TRUE</b>? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["The second smallest key is stored at <code class=\"mm\">A[2]</code> or <code class=\"mm\">A[3]</code>.", "Deleting the minimum and restoring the heap property takes O(log n) time.", "Deciding whether an arbitrary key k occurs in H takes O(log n) time in the worst case.", "Along every path from the root to a leaf the keys are non-decreasing."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Everything below the root is at least as large as the root, and the heap property applies along every parent–child edge, so it holds along every root-to-leaf path. There is no ordering between siblings, which is what limits search.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) Every key other than the root is in one of the two subtrees of the root, so the smallest of the remaining keys is the smaller of <code class=\"mm\">A[2]</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A[3]</code>. True.</li><li>(B) DELETE-MIN moves the last key to the root, shrinks the heap and sifts down one root-to-leaf path, which has length at most the height ⌊log₂ n⌋. True.</li><li>(C) A heap offers no way to choose a subtree: both children may contain k or neither, so in the worst case every node is examined, which is Θ(n). False.</li><li>(D) Each edge satisfies parent ≤ child, so the keys along any root-to-leaf path are non-decreasing. True.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D) are correct.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why option (C) is not selected.</b></p><ul><li>The heap order does not constrain the relative order of the two subtrees of a node, so a search for k cannot prune either side. Searching a heap is Θ(n) in the worst case, which is why a heap is not a search structure.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing “logarithmic depth” with “logarithmic search”. A binary search tree prunes one subtree; a heap cannot.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A heap gives O(1) access to the extremum, O(log n) insertion and deletion, and Θ(n) arbitrary search.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q06", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "min-max-heaps", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A min-max heap alternates min levels and max levels, the root being on level 0, a min level. Which one of the following is its defining invariant?</p>", "options": ["Every node on a min level is ≤ every key in its own subtree, and every node on a max level is ≥ every key in its own subtree.", "Every node on a min level is ≤ its two children only, and every node on a max level is ≥ its two children only.", "The keys obey the ordinary heap order along every root-to-leaf path, exactly as in a binary min-heap.", "Insertion compares a new key only with its parent, exactly as in an ordinary binary heap."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A min-max heap keeps two orders at once. On a min level the node is the smallest key of its subtree; on a max level it is the largest key of its subtree. The guarantee is against the whole subtree, not merely against the immediate children.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Levels alternate: level 0 min, level 1 max, level 2 min, and so on.</li><li>The min-level guarantee is what makes the root the global minimum, and the max-level guarantee is what makes the maximum one of the two children of the root.</li><li>Verifying against the grandchildren is enough in practice, because a child of a min-level node is on a max level and is already ≥ its own children.</li><li>That is why insertion and deletion compare a new key with its parent and its grandparent, and the cost stays O(log n).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — a min-level node dominates its whole subtree from below, a max-level node from above.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) Weakening the invariant to the two children only would allow a grandchild smaller than a min-level node, and the root would no longer be the minimum.</li><li>(C) That is the ordinary binary min-heap, which cannot give O(1) access to the maximum.</li><li>(D) A min-max heap must compare with the grandparent as well as the parent, which is exactly what the two-level order requires.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a min-max heap is just a min-heap that also happens to know the maximum. The subtree guarantee is the whole point: it bounds the maximum without a full scan.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Min-max heaps give O(1) access to both the minimum and the maximum and O(log n) insert, delete-min and delete-max.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q07", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "heapify-and-build-heap", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">BUILD-MAX-HEAP is the bottom-up procedure that calls MAX-HEAPIFY on i = <code class=\"mm\">⌊n/2⌋, ⌊n/2⌋ − 1, …, 1</code> for an array of n keys. Its tight running time on an arbitrary array is:</p>", "options": ["Θ(n log n)", "Θ(log n)", "Θ(n²)", "Θ(n)"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Bottom-up heap construction is linear, not n log n. Most nodes sit near the bottom of the tree and therefore sift down only a short distance.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A node of height h can descend at most h levels, so its MAX-HEAPIFY costs O(h).</li><li>There are at most <code class=\"mm\">⌈n/2^(h+1)⌉</code> nodes of height h.</li><li>Total work is <code class=\"mm\">Σₕ ⌈n/2^(h+1)⌉·O(h) = O(n·Σₕ h/2^h)</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">Σₕ h/2^h = 2</code>, a constant.</li><li>So the total is O(n), and since every key must be read at least once it is Θ(n).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) — Θ(n).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Θ(n log n) is the cost of inserting the keys one at a time, not of the bottom-up build.</li><li>(B) Θ(log n) is the cost of a single MAX-HEAPIFY call.</li><li>(C) No comparison-based heap build is quadratic.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Multiplying “about n/2 calls” by “O(log n) per call” and concluding n log n. The calls are not all deep: the deepest are the fewest.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Bottom-up build-heap is Θ(n); repeated insertion is Θ(n log n) in the worst case.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q08", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "heapify-and-build-heap", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">BUILD-MAX-HEAP is run on <code class=\"mm\">A[1..7] = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70]</code>, calling MAX-HEAPIFY on i = 3, 2, 1 in that order. A call exchanges <code class=\"mm\">A[i]</code> with its larger child whenever that child is larger, and then continues from that child. How many exchanges does the complete build perform?<br/>(Enter an integer.)</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> MAX-HEAPIFY on a node either stops immediately or performs exactly one exchange per level it descends. Counting exchanges therefore means counting the total number of levels descended by all three calls.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>MAX-HEAPIFY(3): <code class=\"mm\">A[3] = 30</code>, children <code class=\"mm\">A[6] = 60</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A[7] = 70</code>. The larger child is 70, so 30 and 70 are exchanged (1). Index 7 is a leaf, so the call ends. Array: <code class=\"mm\">10, 20, 70, 40, 50, 60, 30</code>.</li><li>MAX-HEAPIFY(2): <code class=\"mm\">A[2] = 20</code>, children <code class=\"mm\">A[4] = 40</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A[5] = 50</code>. Exchange 20 with 50 (2). Index 5 is a leaf. Array: <code class=\"mm\">10, 50, 70, 40, 20, 60, 30</code>.</li><li>MAX-HEAPIFY(1): <code class=\"mm\">A[1] = 10</code>, children <code class=\"mm\">A[2] = 50</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A[3] = 70</code>. Exchange 10 with 70 (3). At index 3 the children are <code class=\"mm\">A[6] = 60</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A[7] = 30</code>; exchange 10 with 60 (4). Index 6 is a leaf.</li><li>Total exchanges 1 + 1 + 2 = 4, and the final array is <code class=\"mm\">[70, 50, 60, 40, 20, 10, 30]</code>, which is a valid max-heap.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4 exchanges.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Stopping after the first exchange at a node instead of continuing down the path, or forgetting that the key sifted to index 3 must be sifted again from there.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> One exchange per level descended; the count is the sum of the descent depths, not the number of calls.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q09", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "heapify-and-build-heap", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">MAX-HEAPIFY(A, 2) is called on <code class=\"mm\">A = [16, 4, 10, 14, 7, 9, 3, 2, 8, 1]</code>. Which array results?</p>", "options": ["[16, 14, 10, 8, 7, 9, 3, 2, 4, 1]", "[16, 14, 10, 4, 7, 9, 3, 2, 8, 1]", "[16, 8, 10, 14, 7, 9, 3, 2, 4, 1]", "[16, 4, 10, 14, 8, 9, 3, 2, 7, 1]"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> MAX-HEAPIFY lets a key sink: at each step it is exchanged with the larger of its two children, and the process continues from that child until the key dominates both children or reaches a leaf.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>At i = 2 the key is 4, with children <code class=\"mm\">A[4] = 14</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A[5] = 7</code>. The larger child is 14, so exchange: <code class=\"mm\">[16, 14, 10, 4, 7, 9, 3, 2, 8, 1]</code>.</li><li>The key 4 is now at i = 4, with children <code class=\"mm\">A[8] = 2</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A[9] = 8</code>. The larger child is 8, so exchange: <code class=\"mm\">[16, 14, 10, 8, 7, 9, 3, 2, 4, 1]</code>.</li><li>The key 4 is now at i = 9, whose children <code class=\"mm\">A[18]</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A[19]</code> do not exist, so the call ends.</li><li>Two exchanges were performed, and the subtree rooted at 2 is now a valid max-heap.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — <code class=\"mm\">[16, 14, 10, 8, 7, 9, 3, 2, 4, 1]</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) stops after the first exchange and leaves 4 above 8, so the heap property still fails at index 4.</li><li>(C) exchanges with 8 first, but 8 is a grandchild and is never compared at index 2.</li><li>(D) moves 8 into the array while leaving 4 in place at index 2, which is not what sifting does.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Choosing the smaller child, or stopping as soon as the first exchange is made. MAX-HEAPIFY always picks the larger child, and it recurses.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A sift-down performs one exchange per level and must run to a leaf or to a point where the key already dominates its children.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q10", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "heapify-and-build-heap", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements are <b>TRUE</b>? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["BUILD-MAX-HEAP runs in Θ(n) time, whereas inserting the n keys one at a time costs Ω(n log n) in the worst case.", "BUILD-MAX-HEAP makes exactly <code class=\"mm\">⌊n/2⌋</code> calls to MAX-HEAPIFY.", "After BUILD-MAX-HEAP the largest key is at <code class=\"mm\">A[1]</code> and the second largest is at <code class=\"mm\">A[2]</code> or <code class=\"mm\">A[3]</code>.", "Building by successive insertion and building bottom-up always leave exactly the same array."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The bottom-up build calls MAX-HEAPIFY once on every internal node, in decreasing index order, so that each subtree is already a heap when its parent is processed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) The bottom-up build is Θ(n) by the height-sum argument. Successive insertion can force every new key to bubble up to the root, which is Ω(log n) per key and Ω(n log n) overall — attained, for instance, by inserting keys in increasing order into a max-heap. True.</li><li>(B) Internal nodes are exactly <code class=\"mm\">1..⌊n/2⌋</code>, and each is passed to MAX-HEAPIFY once. True.</li><li>(C) The heap property forces the root to be the maximum. Every other key lies in one of the two subtrees of the root, so the largest of them is the larger of <code class=\"mm\">A[2]</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A[3]</code>. True.</li><li>(D) The two procedures are both correct but not equivalent: on <code class=\"mm\">[1, 2, 3, 4]</code> bottom-up gives <code class=\"mm\">[4, 2, 3, 1]</code> while inserting 1, 2, 3, 4 in turn gives <code class=\"mm\">[4, 3, 2, 1]</code>. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C) are correct.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why option (D) is not selected.</b></p><ul><li>A heap is not unique for a given multiset of keys. The two construction orders can leave different valid heaps, so neither can be claimed to reproduce the other.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing the O(n log n) cost of insertion forces the build to be n log n, or assuming that a correct build must be unique.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Bottom-up build is linear and calls MAX-HEAPIFY once per internal node; the resulting heap is valid but not unique.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q11", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "heapify-and-build-heap", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">BUILD-MIN-HEAP is run on <code class=\"mm\">A[1..8] = [8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1]</code>, calling MIN-HEAPIFY on i = 4, 3, 2, 1 in that order. A call exchanges <code class=\"mm\">A[i]</code> with its smaller child whenever that child is smaller, and then continues from that child. How many exchanges does the complete build perform?<br/>(Enter an integer.)</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "6", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The descending array is the worst shape for a bottom-up build: nearly every internal node has to sift its key all the way to a leaf. Count one exchange per level descended.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>MIN-HEAPIFY(4): <code class=\"mm\">A[4] = 5</code>, single child <code class=\"mm\">A[8] = 1</code>. Exchange (1). Array <code class=\"mm\">[8, 7, 6, 1, 4, 3, 2, 5]</code>.</li><li>MIN-HEAPIFY(3): <code class=\"mm\">A[3] = 6</code>, children 3 and 2; smaller is 2 at index 7. Exchange (2). Index 7 is a leaf. Array <code class=\"mm\">[8, 7, 2, 1, 4, 3, 6, 5]</code>.</li><li>MIN-HEAPIFY(2): <code class=\"mm\">A[2] = 7</code>, children 1 and 4; smaller is 1 at index 4. Exchange (3). At index 4 the single child is <code class=\"mm\">A[8] = 5</code>, smaller than 7, so exchange again (4). Array <code class=\"mm\">[8, 1, 2, 5, 4, 3, 6, 7]</code>.</li><li>MIN-HEAPIFY(1): <code class=\"mm\">A[1] = 8</code>, children 1 and 2; smaller is 1 at index 2. Exchange (5). At index 2 the children are 5 and 4; smaller is 4 at index 5, so exchange (6). Index 5 has no children. Final array <code class=\"mm\">[1, 4, 2, 5, 8, 3, 6, 7]</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 6 exchanges.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a descending array needs no work because it already looks “reverse ordered”. In level order it is a heap with every parent larger than its children, which for a min-heap is the maximum amount of sifting.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Total exchanges = sum of descent depths. A reverse-sorted array maximises it; the total stays O(n) even so.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q12", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "heapify-and-build-heap", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">In the linear-time analysis of BUILD-MAX-HEAP the work is summed over node heights as <code class=\"mm\">Σₕ₊₀ ⌈n/2^(h+1)⌉·O(h) = O(n·Σₕ h/2^h) = O(n)</code>. Which statement explains why this bound is linear rather than Θ(n log n)?</p>", "options": ["Only O(log n) nodes are internal, so only O(log n) calls to MAX-HEAPIFY are ever made.", "Half of the nodes are leaves and do no work, a quarter descend at most one level, an eighth at most two, and so on, so <code class=\"mm\">Σₕ h/2^h</code> converges to the constant 2.", "Each call to MAX-HEAPIFY costs O(1) because the array is a complete binary tree.", "MAX-HEAPIFY recurses at most twice at every node."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The cost of a build is dominated by where the nodes sit, not by how many there are. Nodes are numerous exactly where their height is small.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A complete binary tree on n nodes has ⌈n/2⌋ leaves, each of height 0 and cost 0.</li><li>It has ⌈n/4⌉ nodes of height 1 costing 1 each, ⌈n/8⌉ of height 2 costing 2 each, and in general ⌈n/2^(h+1)⌉ nodes of height h costing h each.</li><li>Total work is <code class=\"mm\">n·Σₕ h/2^(h+1)</code>, and the series <code class=\"mm\">Σₕ h/2^h</code> converges to 2, so the total is at most 2n.</li><li>The few deep nodes cost log n each, but there are only a constant number of them, so their contribution is O(log n) — negligible against Θ(n).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — the height distribution makes the sum converge to a constant.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) There are ⌊n/2⌋ internal nodes, not O(log n).</li><li>(C) A single MAX-HEAPIFY can cost O(log n); the bound is not O(1) per call.</li><li>(D) MAX-HEAPIFY makes one recursive call, not two, and its depth is bounded by the height.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Summing the maximum cost of each call instead of weighting by how many calls have that cost. Multiplying “n/2 calls” by “log n each” ignores that only a handful of calls are deep.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Cost is bounded by the number of nodes at each height times that height, and that weighted sum is linear.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q13", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "heapify-and-build-heap", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A max-heap holds 500 keys. DELETE-MAX replaces the root by the last key, shrinks the heap by one and calls MAX-HEAPIFY on the root. What is the largest number of exchanges a single DELETE-MAX can perform on this heap?</p>", "options": ["7", "9", "8", "499"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The key moved to the root sifts down one root-to-leaf path, so the number of exchanges is bounded by the height of the tree, which is <code class=\"mm\">⌊log₂ n⌋</code> for n nodes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>For n = 500, <code class=\"mm\">2⁸ = 256 ≤ 500 &lt; 512 = 2⁹</code>, so the height is 8: levels 0 to 8.</li><li>After the deletion the heap holds 499 keys, and <code class=\"mm\">2⁸ = 256 ≤ 499 &lt; 512</code>, so the height is still 8.</li><li>The moved key can therefore descend at most 8 edges, performing 8 exchanges.</li><li>Eight exchanges are attainable: take distinct keys and make the moved key smaller than every key on one root-to-leaf path of length 8.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — 8 exchanges.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 7 is the height of a heap with at most 255 keys.</li><li>(B) 9 would need at least 512 keys.</li><li>(D) 499 is the number of keys, not the number of levels on a path.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading “n = 500” as a height of 500, or forgetting that the heap shrinks by one before the sift-down starts.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> DELETE-MAX costs Θ(log n): one move plus at most <code class=\"mm\">⌊log₂ n⌋</code> exchanges.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q14", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "heap-sort", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">HEAPSORT is implemented in place: it builds a max-heap and then repeatedly swaps <code class=\"mm\">A[1]</code> with the last unsorted slot. Its worst-case running time and its auxiliary space are:</p>", "options": ["Θ(n log n) time and Θ(n) extra space", "Θ(n²) time and Θ(1) extra space", "Θ(n log n) time and Θ(1) extra space", "Θ(n) time and Θ(n) extra space"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> HEAPSORT is the only common sorting algorithm that is simultaneously Θ(n log n) in the worst case and Θ(1) in extra space.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The build costs Θ(n).</li><li>There are n − 1 extraction steps; each swaps the root into place and sifts the new root down, costing O(log n).</li><li>Total Θ(n) + (n − 1)·O(log n) = Θ(n log n) in the best, average and worst case alike.</li><li>Nothing beyond a few index variables is allocated, so the extra space is Θ(1).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — Θ(n log n) time, Θ(1) extra space.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Θ(n) extra space describes merge sort, not heapsort.</li><li>(B) Θ(n²) describes quicksort's worst case or insertion sort, not heapsort.</li><li>(D) No comparison sort of n arbitrary keys can run in Θ(n) time.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming an in-place algorithm must be quadratic, as quicksort's worst case is. Heapsort gets both guarantees at once, which is why it is used when memory is tight and the input may be adversarial.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Heapsort: Θ(n log n) worst case, Θ(1) space, not stable.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q15", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "heap-sort", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">HEAPSORT begins by running BUILD-MAX-HEAP on <code class=\"mm\">A[1..9] = [5, 13, 2, 25, 7, 17, 20, 8, 4]</code>. Immediately after the build and before any extraction, what key is stored at <code class=\"mm\">A[3]</code>?<br/>(Enter an integer.)</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "20", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The build calls MAX-HEAPIFY on i = 4, 3, 2, 1 in that order. Only after all four calls is the array a max-heap, and the question asks about that moment.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>MAX-HEAPIFY(4): <code class=\"mm\">A[4] = 25</code> with child <code class=\"mm\">A[8] = 8</code>. 25 already dominates, so no exchange.</li><li>MAX-HEAPIFY(3): <code class=\"mm\">A[3] = 2</code> with children <code class=\"mm\">A[6] = 17</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A[7] = 20</code>. Exchange 2 with 20: array becomes <code class=\"mm\">[5, 13, 20, 25, 7, 17, 2, 8, 4]</code>.</li><li>MAX-HEAPIFY(2): <code class=\"mm\">A[2] = 13</code> with children 25 and 7. Exchange with 25: <code class=\"mm\">[5, 25, 20, 13, 7, 17, 2, 8, 4]</code>.</li><li>MAX-HEAPIFY(1): <code class=\"mm\">A[1] = 5</code> with children 25 and 20. Exchange with 25 (index 2). At index 2 the children are 13 and 7, so exchange 5 with 13. Final array <code class=\"mm\">[25, 13, 20, 8, 7, 17, 2, 5, 4]</code>, in which <code class=\"mm\">A[3] = 20</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 20.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading <code class=\"mm\">A[3]</code> from the input array (where it is 2), or stopping the build early and assuming the largest key at the root is all that matters.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The build must be traced to the end; the value at any interior index depends on every sift-down that passed through it.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q16", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "heap-sort", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about HEAPSORT are <b>TRUE</b>? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["It is not a stable sorting algorithm.", "It sorts in place, using Θ(1) auxiliary space beyond the array itself.", "Building a max-heap and swapping the root into the last unsorted slot produces ascending order.", "Its worst-case running time is asymptotically worse than merge sort's worst case."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A max-heap keeps the largest remaining key at the root. Moving that root to the end of the unsorted prefix, then restoring the heap on the prefix, fills the array from the back with ever smaller keys.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) Sift-down moves keys over long distances, so equal keys are freely reordered. False for stability, so the statement that it is unstable is true.</li><li>(B) The only extra storage is a constant number of index variables. True.</li><li>(C) The largest key goes to position n, the next largest to n − 1, and so on, so the final array is non-decreasing. True.</li><li>(D) Merge sort's worst case is Θ(n log n) and so is heapsort's; neither is asymptotically worse. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C) are correct.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why option (D) is not selected.</b></p><ul><li>Both algorithms are Θ(n log n) in the worst case. Heapsort is slower in practice because of cache behaviour and a larger constant factor, but that is not an asymptotic difference.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing “in place” with “stable”, or thinking heapsort has a quadratic worst case like quicksort.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Heapsort: Θ(n log n) worst case, Θ(1) space, unstable, and ascending output from a max-heap.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q17", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "heap-sort", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">HEAPSORT is rewritten to build a min-heap and, at every step, to swap <code class=\"mm\">A[1]</code> with the last unsorted slot of the same array. The array it finally produces is:</p>", "options": ["ascending", "identical to the input array", "ascending only if all keys are distinct", "descending"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In place, the extracted extremum is parked at the back of the shrinking unsorted prefix. A min-heap extracts the smallest key first, so the smallest keys are written into the last slots first.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Step 1 puts the minimum at position n.</li><li>Step 2 puts the second smallest at position n − 1.</li><li>Continuing, the smallest key ends at the last index and the largest at index 1, which is descending order.</li><li>To obtain ascending order in place, the heap must be a max-heap; a min-heap gives ascending order only when it is extracted into a separate output array.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) — descending.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Ascending order needs a max-heap when the extraction target is the array's tail.</li><li>(B) The array is a heap at every stage and is sorted at the end, so it cannot equal an arbitrary input.</li><li>(C) Distinctness is irrelevant; the direction is set by which extremum the heap exposes.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the heap type does not matter because both heaps “sort”. What matters is where the extracted key is written: into the array's tail, or into a separate output.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> In place, a max-heap yields ascending order and a min-heap yields descending order; out of place, the reverse.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q18", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "heap-sort", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">HEAPSORT and merge sort are both Θ(n log n) in the worst case, yet heapsort is usually the slower of the two on real hardware. The main reason is:</p>", "options": ["Sift-down jumps between positions far apart in the array, so heapsort suffers many more cache misses.", "Heapsort is not in place and must allocate an auxiliary array.", "Heapsort's worst case is Θ(n²).", "Heapsort cannot handle arrays that contain duplicate keys."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Asymptotic bounds count comparisons and exchanges, not memory accesses. On a real machine the constant factor is dominated by whether those accesses hit the cache.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The children of index i are at 2i and 2i + 1, so a sift-down from the root touches positions n/2 apart in a single step.</li><li>Each step of the descent therefore lands in a different cache line, and the first pass over a large array is essentially a random-access walk.</li><li>Merge sort, by contrast, reads and writes contiguous runs, which the prefetcher handles well; quicksort's partitioning is also mostly sequential.</li><li>That locality penalty, not the comparison count, is why heapsort loses in practice while remaining the better guarantee.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — poor locality of the sift-down accesses.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) Heapsort is the classic in-place Θ(n log n) sort; merge sort is the one that needs auxiliary space.</li><li>(C) Heapsort's worst case is Θ(n log n).</li><li>(D) Duplicate keys are handled correctly; heapsort is merely unstable.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading “same asymptotic bound” as “same speed”. Constants and memory behaviour decide the practical ordering.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Heapsort's advantage is the worst-case guarantee and Θ(1) space; its disadvantage is cache behaviour.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q19", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "heap-sort", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">In the standard in-place HEAPSORT, MAX-HEAPIFY is called once for each internal node during the build, and once after each of the n − 1 extraction swaps. What is the total number of MAX-HEAPIFY calls made by one complete run on n keys?</p>", "options": ["n − 1", "n log n", "<code class=\"mm\">⌊n/2⌋ + n</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">⌊n/2⌋ + n − 1</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Count the two phases separately. The build touches every internal node once; the extraction phase touches the root once per extraction.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Phase 1, the build: MAX-HEAPIFY is invoked on indices <code class=\"mm\">⌊n/2⌋, …, 1</code>, which is <code class=\"mm\">⌊n/2⌋</code> calls.</li><li>Phase 2, extraction: for k = n down to 2, the root is swapped with position k and MAX-HEAPIFY is called on the root of the shrunken heap. That is n − 1 calls.</li><li>The final extraction leaves a single element, which needs no call.</li><li>Total <code class=\"mm\">⌊n/2⌋ + (n − 1)</code> calls.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) — <code class=\"mm\">⌊n/2⌋ + n − 1</code> calls.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) n − 1 counts only the extraction phase.</li><li>(B) n log n is a bound on the work, not on the number of calls.</li><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">⌊n/2⌋ + n</code> counts one extraction too many: the last one leaves a single key and needs no sift-down.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting the calls as if the build called MAX-HEAPIFY on all n nodes. Leaves are already valid one-element heaps and are never passed to MAX-HEAPIFY.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Build: <code class=\"mm\">⌊n/2⌋</code> calls. Extraction: n − 1 calls. The total is <code class=\"mm\">⌊n/2⌋ + n − 1</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q20", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "priority-queue-applications", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A priority queue on n items must support INSERT and EXTRACT-MIN. Which representation gives the best worst-case bound for both operations together?</p>", "options": ["An unsorted array", "A sorted array", "A binary heap", "A singly linked list kept in insertion order"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The right structure is the one that keeps both operations logarithmic. Insertion into a heap costs O(log n) and extraction costs O(log n).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Unsorted array: INSERT is O(1) at the end, but EXTRACT-MIN needs a full scan, Θ(n).</li><li>Sorted array: EXTRACT-MIN is O(1) from one end, but INSERT must shift elements, Θ(n).</li><li>Binary heap: INSERT bubbles a key up one root-to-leaf path, O(log n); EXTRACT-MIN moves the last key to the root and sifts down, O(log n).</li><li>Linked list in insertion order: EXTRACT-MIN is Θ(n).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — a binary heap.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) and (D) are linear in extraction.</li><li>(B) is linear in insertion.</li><li>Each of the three loses one of the two operations; only the heap keeps both logarithmic.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Optimising one operation in isolation. A priority queue is judged on the pair, and the workload usually mixes both.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Binary heap: O(log n) insert and O(log n) extract-min, with O(1) peek.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q21", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "priority-queue-applications", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following are standard uses of a priority queue? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["Huffman's algorithm, repeatedly merging the two least frequent symbols.", "Prim's algorithm, repeatedly taking the cheapest edge leaving the current tree.", "Binary search for a key in a sorted array.", "Dijkstra's algorithm on a graph with non-negative edge weights."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A priority queue is the right tool whenever the algorithm repeatedly needs the current extremum of a changing set.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) Huffman's algorithm extracts the two smallest weights, merges them and inserts the sum: two extract-mins and one insert per merge. True.</li><li>(B) Prim's algorithm needs the minimum-weight crossing edge, which is exactly a decrease-key and extract-min on the frontier. True.</li><li>(C) Binary search indexes the middle of a sorted array directly; it needs no ordering structure at all. False.</li><li>(D) Dijkstra's algorithm repeatedly extracts the unsettled vertex of least tentative distance, which is a priority-queue operation. True.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D) are correct.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why option (C) is not selected.</b></p><ul><li>Binary search relies on the array already being sorted and on random access by index. It never asks for a minimum of a dynamic set, so a priority queue would only add overhead.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Equating “needs ordering” with “needs a priority queue”. A priority queue is for a set whose minimum changes as elements arrive and leave.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Greedy algorithms that repeatedly take the best available item — Huffman, Prim, Dijkstra, A* — are the natural home of the priority queue.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q22", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "priority-queue-applications", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Dijkstra's algorithm is implemented with a binary heap and without a decrease-key operation: when a distance improves, a duplicate entry is pushed instead. Its running time is:</p>", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">O((V + E) log V)</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">O(V²)</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">O(V·E)</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">O(V log E)</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With a binary heap the algorithm performs at most one insertion per edge relaxation and one extraction per heap entry, and each costs O(log V).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Each of the E edges can cause at most one successful relaxation, so at most E entries are pushed in addition to the V initial ones.</li><li>The heap therefore holds O(V + E) entries, and each push or pop costs O(log(V + E)) = O(log V) because E ≤ V² and <code class=\"mm\">log V² = 2 log V</code>.</li><li>Total <code class=\"mm\">O((V + E) log V)</code>.</li><li>With decrease-key the same bound holds with fewer entries; lazy deletion only changes the constant.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — <code class=\"mm\">O((V + E) log V)</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) <code class=\"mm\">O(V²)</code> is the bound for the unsorted-array or adjacency-matrix implementation.</li><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">O(V·E)</code> is the bound for Bellman-Ford, which tolerates negative edges.</li><li>(D) <code class=\"mm\">O(V log E)</code> does not account for the E relaxations.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking that skipping decrease-key makes the algorithm asymptotically worse. It only adds duplicate entries, and each is charged to the edge that created it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Dijkstra with a binary heap is <code class=\"mm\">O((V + E) log V)</code>, with or without decrease-key.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q23", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "priority-queue-applications", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Huffman coding is applied to six symbols whose frequencies are 45, 13, 12, 16, 9 and 5. What is the total number of bits in the encoded file, that is the sum over symbols of frequency × code length?<br/>(Enter an integer.)</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "224", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The total length of a Huffman-coded file equals the weighted external path length of the Huffman tree: the sum, over every merge, of the weight of the merged node.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Start with the six leaves 45, 16, 13, 12, 9, 5.</li><li>Merge 9 and 5 into 14 (weight 14). Remaining: 45, 16, 14, 13, 12.</li><li>Merge 13 and 12 into 25. Remaining: 45, 25, 16, 14.</li><li>Merge 16 and 14 into 30. Remaining: 45, 30, 25.</li><li>Merge 30 and 25 into 55. Remaining: 55, 45.</li><li>Merge 55 and 45 into 100. The total is 14 + 25 + 30 + 55 + 100 = 224 bits, an average of 2.24 bits per symbol against 3 bits for a fixed-length code.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 224 bits.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Summing the code lengths of the six symbols instead of weighting each by its frequency, or forgetting that the total equals the sum of the merge weights.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Total Huffman cost = sum of all merge weights = weighted external path length.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q24", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "priority-queue-applications", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A stream of n numbers must be processed so that the 100 largest can be reported, using a heap of fixed capacity. Which of the following statements are <b>TRUE</b>? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["A min-heap of capacity 100 is the right structure: its root is the smallest of the current 100 largest and is the key to evict.", "A max-heap of capacity 100 works equally well.", "Each arriving element costs O(log 100) time.", "The total cost is <code class=\"mm\">O(n log 100)</code>, which is O(n) when the capacity is fixed."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> To keep the k largest elements you must be able to identify, and discard, the smallest of the k you are holding. That requires the root to be the minimum, hence a min-heap.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) With a min-heap of capacity 100 the root is the weakest of the current top 100, so comparing each new element with the root and replacing it when the new element is larger keeps exactly the top 100. True.</li><li>(B) A max-heap of capacity 100 exposes the largest of the stored elements, which is the last thing you would ever evict. False.</li><li>(C) One comparison against the root, and at most one replacement followed by a sift-down of depth log₂ 100 ≈ 7. True.</li><li>(D) n elements at O(log k) each gives O(n log k), and with k fixed at 100 this is O(n). True.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D) are correct.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why option (B) is not selected.</b></p><ul><li>The heap must expose the element you want to throw away, not the one you want to keep. Keeping the k largest therefore needs a min-heap of size k, just as keeping the k smallest needs a max-heap.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reaching for a max-heap because the task says “largest”. The heap is chosen by which element must be evictable.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Top-k largest: min-heap of size k, O(n log k) time and O(k) space.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q25", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "priority-queue-applications", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">M sorted lists holding n elements in total are merged into one sorted list using a heap that holds one current element from each list. The running time is:</p>", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">O(n·M)</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">O(n log M)</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">O(n log n)</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">O(M log n)</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> At any moment the next output element is the smallest of the M current heads, so a heap of size M answers each step in O(log M).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Initialise the heap with the first element of each non-empty list: O(M) time.</li><li>Repeat n times: extract the minimum, append it to the output, and insert the next element of the list it came from.</li><li>Each of the n steps costs one extract-min and at most one insert, each O(log M).</li><li>Total O(n log M), which for M = 2 collapses to the linear merge of merge sort.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — <code class=\"mm\">O(n log M)</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) <code class=\"mm\">O(n·M)</code> is the cost of scanning all M heads at every step.</li><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">O(n log n)</code> would be right only if every element had to be compared with every other, which the heap avoids; it is also worse than <code class=\"mm\">O(n log M)</code> whenever M ≪ n.</li><li>(D) <code class=\"mm\">O(M log n)</code> has the roles of n and M interchanged.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Sorting the concatenation instead of merging. Re-sorting costs <code class=\"mm\">O(n log n)</code>, which ignores that the M lists are already sorted.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> k-way merge with a heap is <code class=\"mm\">O(n log k)</code>, the engine behind external sorting and multiway merge.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q26", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "min-max-heaps", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A max-heap of <span class=\"mm\">12</span> distinct keys is stored in <span class=\"mm\">A[1..12]</span>, the children of <span class=\"mm\">A[i]</span> being <span class=\"mm\">A[2i]</span> and <span class=\"mm\">A[2i+1]</span>. Which one of the following indices is guaranteed to hold a leaf?</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">A[3]</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">A[5]</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">A[6]</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">A[9]</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a complete binary tree stored in a 1-based array, the nodes <span class=\"mm\">1 .. &lfloor;n/2&rfloor;</span> are exactly the internal nodes and the nodes <span class=\"mm\">&lfloor;n/2&rfloor;+1 .. n</span> are exactly the leaves.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Here <span class=\"mm\">n = 12</span>, so the internal nodes are <span class=\"mm\">1 .. &lfloor;12/2&rfloor; = 1 .. 6</span>.</li><li>The leaves are therefore <span class=\"mm\">7 .. 12</span>.</li><li>Indices 3, 5 and 6 are internal: node 3 owns 6 and 7, node 5 owns 10 and 11, and node 6 owns 12 (and would own 13, which does not exist).</li><li>Only <span class=\"mm\">A[9]</span> lies in <span class=\"mm\">7 .. 12</span>, so it must be a leaf.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D), A[9].</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Node 6 is the trap: it has only a left child, so a student who checks for a right child declares it a leaf. A node with exactly one child is still internal, and with <span class=\"mm\">n</span> even there is exactly one such node, at index <span class=\"mm\">n/2</span>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q27", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "min-max-heaps", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A max-heap holds <span class=\"mm\">n &ge; 3</span> distinct keys in <span class=\"mm\">A[1..n]</span>. Where can the second-largest key be?</p>", "options": ["At <span class=\"mm\">A[2]</span> only", "At <span class=\"mm\">A[2]</span> or <span class=\"mm\">A[3]</span>", "At <span class=\"mm\">A[2]</span>, <span class=\"mm\">A[3]</span> or <span class=\"mm\">A[4]</span>", "At any leaf of the heap"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The heap order only constrains a parent against its own children, so the largest key is at the root and the second largest must be a child of the root: no deeper node can be larger than its own ancestors.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Every node other than the root is a descendant of the root and of some child of the root, and a max-heap forces each parent to be at least its children.</li><li>So every key below level 1 is <span class=\"mm\">&le;</span> one of the two level-1 keys <span class=\"mm\">A[2], A[3]</span>.</li><li>The second-largest key is therefore <span class=\"mm\">max(A[2], A[3])</span>.</li><li>It is <span class=\"mm\">A[2]</span> when that child is larger and <span class=\"mm\">A[3]</span> otherwise; neither position is fixed.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B), at A[2] or A[3].</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Two opposite mistakes: assuming the heap is sorted and answering <span class=\"mm\">A[2]</span> alone, or assuming the second largest can drift to a leaf. <span class=\"mm\">A[4]</span> is the third-largest candidate, not the second.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q28", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "min-max-heaps", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A max-heap stores <span class=\"mm\">n = 45</span> keys in <span class=\"mm\">A[1..45]</span>. What is the largest index <span class=\"mm\">i</span> such that <span class=\"mm\">A[i]</span> can have two children?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "22", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Node <span class=\"mm\">i</span> has two children exactly when both <span class=\"mm\">2i</span> and <span class=\"mm\">2i+1</span> exist, that is when <span class=\"mm\">2i+1 &le; n</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The condition is <span class=\"mm\">2i+1 &le; 45</span>, i.e. <span class=\"mm\">2i &le; 44</span>, i.e. <span class=\"mm\">i &le; 22</span>.</li><li>At <span class=\"mm\">i = 22</span> the children are 44 and 45, both present.</li><li>At <span class=\"mm\">i = 23</span> the children would be 46 and 47, both absent, so node 23 is a leaf.</li><li>Hence the largest such index is 22.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 22.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering <span class=\"mm\">&lfloor;45/2&rfloor; = 22</span> for the wrong reason — the last internal node — and then giving 23 for the last two-child node, or answering 45 by reading the last index. Node <span class=\"mm\">&lfloor;n/2&rfloor;</span> has two children only when <span class=\"mm\">n</span> is odd; here 45 is odd, so 22 is right, but the general test is <span class=\"mm\">2i+1 &le; n</span>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q29", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "min-max-heaps", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">In a min-max heap the root sits on level 0, which is a min level, and levels alternate. Where can the second-smallest key lie?</p>", "options": ["It must be a child of the root", "It must be a grandchild of the root", "It is either a child or a grandchild of the root", "It can lie at any level below the root"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> On a min level a node is no greater than every key in its own subtree; on a max level a node is no smaller. That gives both an upper and a lower constraint two levels apart.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Level 1 is a max level, so a level-1 node is at least its own subtree but nothing bounds it from below except the root.</li><li>Level 2 is a min level, so a level-2 node is at most its own subtree; it can be as small as the second smallest key.</li><li>A node on level 3 or deeper has a parent on level 2, a min level, and a min-level parent is no greater than its children.</li><li>So any node below level 2 is larger than its own level-2 parent, and the second-smallest key is confined to levels 1 and 2 — the children and grandchildren of the root.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C), a child or a grandchild of the root.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Transplanting the max-heap fact that the second largest is a child of the root. In a min-max heap the alternation pushes the second smallest one level deeper, to a grandchild, so option (A) is the trap. Option (D) fails because every deeper node is bounded below by its own min-level ancestor.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q30", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "min-max-heaps", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about min-max heaps are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["The root stores the minimum key of the heap.", "Every node on a min level is no greater than every key in its own subtree.", "Inserting a key into a min-max heap takes <span class=\"mm\">O(1)</span> time in the worst case.", "A min-max heap can be built from an arbitrary array of <span class=\"mm\">n</span> keys in <span class=\"mm\">O(n)</span> time."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A min-max heap is a complete binary tree in which even levels are min levels and odd levels are max levels; the invariant is stated per subtree, and the same bottom-up trickle-down that builds a binary heap builds this one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: the root lies on level 0, a min level, so it is no greater than every key in the tree.</li><li>(B) is true: that subtree inequality is the defining invariant of a min level, just as the reverse holds on a max level.</li><li>(C) is false: a new key is placed at the end and trickled up, comparing with the parent and the grandparent and swapping as needed, so an insert costs <span class=\"mm\">O(log n)</span>.</li><li>(D) is true: the bottom-up build calls trickle-down on the internal nodes from <span class=\"mm\">&lfloor;n/2&rfloor;</span> down to 1, and the same height-counting argument gives <span class=\"mm\">O(n)</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (C) is the usual slip: constant-time access to both extremes is mistaken for constant-time insertion. Finding the minimum is <span class=\"mm\">O(1)</span>; changing the heap is logarithmic.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q31", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "min-max-heaps", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">How many distinct max-heaps can be built from <span class=\"mm\">6</span> distinct keys?</p>", "options": ["6", "12", "15", "20"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Choosing which key goes at the root fixes nothing else, so the count obeys <span class=\"mm\">H(n) = C(n&minus;1, L) &middot; H(L) &middot; H(R)</span>, where <span class=\"mm\">L</span> and <span class=\"mm\">R</span> are the sizes of the left and right subtrees of a complete tree. Equivalently <span class=\"mm\">H(n) = n! / &prod; size(v)</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>For <span class=\"mm\">n = 6</span> the complete tree has a left subtree of 3 nodes (indices 2, 4, 5) and a right subtree of 2 nodes (indices 3, 6).</li><li>The largest key must occupy the root, and any 3 of the remaining 5 keys may go to the left subtree: <span class=\"mm\">C(5,3) = 10</span> ways.</li><li>A 3-node heap has <span class=\"mm\">H(3) = C(2,1) = 2</span> shapes and a 2-node heap has <span class=\"mm\">H(2) = 1</span>.</li><li>Total <span class=\"mm\">10 &times; 2 &times; 1 = 20</span>. The closed form agrees: <span class=\"mm\">6! / (6 &middot; 3 &middot; 2 &middot; 1 &middot; 1 &middot; 1) = 720 / 36 = 20</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D), 20.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering <span class=\"mm\">6! = 720</span> counts every arrangement, not the heap-ordered ones; <span class=\"mm\">15 = C(6,2)</span> comes from a half-remembered binomial. The recursive split is the only reliable route.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q32", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "min-max-heaps", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about min-max heaps are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["In a min-max heap of <span class=\"mm\">15</span> keys the largest key must be stored at <span class=\"mm\">A[2]</span> or <span class=\"mm\">A[3]</span>.", "In a min-max heap of <span class=\"mm\">n &ge; 1</span> keys the smallest key is stored at <span class=\"mm\">A[1]</span>.", "A node on a max level is no smaller than every key in its own subtree.", "Inserting a key into a min-max heap may require comparing it with its grandparent as well as with its parent."], "answer_display": "Options (B), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Levels alternate min and max, and the guarantee attached to a node is always about its own subtree. A max-level node is therefore bounded below by nothing except its min-level ancestors, and it may sit far from the root.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is false: with 15 keys the tree has levels 0 to 3, and level 3 is a max level, so a leaf such as <span class=\"mm\">A[15]</span> may hold the largest key.</li><li>(B) is true: the root is on a min level, so it is no greater than every key.</li><li>(C) is true: that is the invariant of a max level.</li><li>(D) is true: on insertion the key is trickled up comparing with the parent when the key lands on a max level and with the grandparent when it lands on a min level, because the guarantee for a min-level node is against its subtree, which is two levels below its max-level parent.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (B), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (A) is the classic error, imported from binary heaps: in a min-max heap the maximum is not confined to the children of the root, because a max-level node deeper in the tree is only required to dominate its own subtree.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q33", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "heapify-and-build-heap", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A max-heap is stored in <span class=\"mm\">A[1..23]</span>. At which index does the last node that is not a leaf lie?</p>", "options": ["11", "12", "22", "23"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a 1-based array of <span class=\"mm\">n</span> keys the internal nodes are exactly <span class=\"mm\">1 .. &lfloor;n/2&rfloor;</span>, which is why BUILD-MAX-HEAP starts its loop at <span class=\"mm\">&lfloor;n/2&rfloor;</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Node <span class=\"mm\">i</span> is internal exactly when it has at least one child, i.e. <span class=\"mm\">2i &le; 23</span>.</li><li>The largest such <span class=\"mm\">i</span> is <span class=\"mm\">&lfloor;23/2&rfloor; = 11</span>, whose left child is 22.</li><li>Node 12 has children 24 and 25, neither of which exists, so it is a leaf.</li><li>The last non-leaf is therefore index 11.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A), index 11.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 12 by rounding <span class=\"mm\">23/2</span> upwards, or 23 by taking the last occupied slot. The loop bound is a floor, and the leaves are <span class=\"mm\">12 .. 23</span>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q34", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "heapify-and-build-heap", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">MAX-HEAPIFY<span class=\"mm\">(A, i)</span> is called on a node whose two subtrees are already max-heaps. After exchanging <span class=\"mm\">A[i]</span> with its larger child, on which subtree does the procedure recurse?</p>", "options": ["Always the left child", "Always the right child", "The child subtree that received the old <span class=\"mm\">A[i]</span>", "Both child subtrees, one after the other"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Only the subtree that lost its root can now violate the heap order, because the other subtree was already a max-heap and its root did not move. MAX-HEAPIFY therefore follows a single root-to-leaf path.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The exchange puts the larger child at <span class=\"mm\">i</span>, so the heap order at <span class=\"mm\">i</span> is restored.</li><li>The other child subtree is untouched and still a max-heap.</li><li>The old <span class=\"mm\">A[i]</span> has moved down into one subtree and may be smaller than that subtree's children, so only there can a violation remain.</li><li>Recursing on that one subtree makes the whole procedure take <span class=\"mm\">O(log n)</span> time — a single path, not a traversal.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (D) turns MAX-HEAPIFY into a linear-time traversal and destroys the <span class=\"mm\">O(log n)</span> bound that makes BUILD-MAX-HEAP linear. Option (B) ignores that the larger child may be the left one.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q35", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "heapify-and-build-heap", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">BUILD-MIN-HEAP is run on <span class=\"mm\">A[1..9] = [9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1]</span>, calling MIN-HEAPIFY on <span class=\"mm\">i = 4, 3, 2, 1</span> in that order. A call exchanges <span class=\"mm\">A[i]</span> with its smaller child while that child is smaller, then continues from that child. What key ends up at <span class=\"mm\">A[4]</span>?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "6", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Bottom-up build repairs the tree from the deepest internal node upwards, so when a node is processed both of its subtrees are already valid min-heaps and one sift-down per node suffices.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Start <span class=\"mm\">[9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1]</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">i = 4</span>: children 2 and 1; 1 is smaller, so exchange <span class=\"mm\">A[4]</span> and <span class=\"mm\">A[9]</span> &rarr; <span class=\"mm\">[9, 8, 7, 1, 5, 4, 3, 2, 6]</span>. Node 9 is a leaf, stop.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">i = 3</span>: children 4 and 3; 3 is smaller, exchange with <span class=\"mm\">A[7]</span> &rarr; <span class=\"mm\">[9, 8, 3, 1, 5, 4, 7, 2, 6]</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">i = 2</span>: children 1 and 5; 1 is smaller, exchange with <span class=\"mm\">A[4]</span> &rarr; <span class=\"mm\">[9, 1, 3, 8, 5, 4, 7, 2, 6]</span>; continue at 4: children 2 and 6, exchange with <span class=\"mm\">A[8]</span> &rarr; <span class=\"mm\">[9, 1, 3, 2, 5, 4, 7, 8, 6]</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">i = 1</span>: children 1 and 3; 1 is smaller, exchange with <span class=\"mm\">A[2]</span> &rarr; <span class=\"mm\">[1, 9, 3, 2, 5, 4, 7, 8, 6]</span>; continue at 2: children 2 and 5, exchange with <span class=\"mm\">A[4]</span> &rarr; <span class=\"mm\">[1, 2, 3, 9, 5, 4, 7, 8, 6]</span>; continue at 4: children 8 and 6, exchange with <span class=\"mm\">A[9]</span> &rarr; <span class=\"mm\">[1, 2, 3, 6, 5, 4, 7, 8, 9]</span>.</li><li>The final array is <span class=\"mm\">[1, 2, 3, 6, 5, 4, 7, 8, 9]</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">A[4] = 6</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 6.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Processing the indices top-down (1, 2, 3, 4) instead of bottom-up leaves violations behind and gives a different value at <span class=\"mm\">A[4]</span>. Also note that a sift-down continues past the first exchange: at <span class=\"mm\">i = 1</span> the value 9 travels two levels.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q36", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "heapify-and-build-heap", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">MAX-HEAPIFY<span class=\"mm\">(A, 2)</span> is called on <span class=\"mm\">A = [20, 5, 12, 11, 9, 8, 7]</span>, both subtrees of node 2 already being max-heaps. Which array results?</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">[20, 5, 12, 11, 9, 8, 7]</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">[20, 11, 12, 5, 9, 8, 7]</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">[20, 9, 12, 11, 5, 8, 7]</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">[20, 12, 11, 5, 9, 8, 7]</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> MAX-HEAPIFY compares a node with both children and exchanges with the larger of the two, not with the left child by default.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Node 2 holds 5; its children are <span class=\"mm\">A[4] = 11</span> and <span class=\"mm\">A[5] = 9</span>.</li><li>The larger child is 11, and <span class=\"mm\">11 &gt; 5</span>, so exchange <span class=\"mm\">A[2]</span> with <span class=\"mm\">A[4]</span> &rarr; <span class=\"mm\">[20, 11, 12, 5, 9, 8, 7]</span>.</li><li>Continue from node 4, which now holds 5 and has no children (its children would be 8 and 9, beyond <span class=\"mm\">n = 7</span>).</li><li>The result is <span class=\"mm\">[20, 11, 12, 5, 9, 8, 7]</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (C) exchanges with the left child 9 without checking the right child 11 — the most common heapify error. Option (A) assumes no exchange is needed because 5 is not the smallest key in the array.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q37", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "heapify-and-build-heap", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about the bottom-up construction of a max-heap are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["BUILD-MAX-HEAP calls MAX-HEAPIFY on <span class=\"mm\">i = &lfloor;n/2&rfloor;, &lfloor;n/2&rfloor;&minus;1, &hellip;, 1</span> in that order.", "Calling MAX-HEAPIFY on <span class=\"mm\">i = 1, 2, &hellip;, &lfloor;n/2&rfloor;</span> in increasing order also produces a max-heap.", "In the linear-time analysis at most <span class=\"mm\">&lceil;n/2^(h+1)&rceil;</span> nodes have height <span class=\"mm\">h</span>.", "The total number of exchanges performed by BUILD-MAX-HEAP in the worst case is <span class=\"mm\">&Theta;(n)</span>."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The build works because a node is repaired only after both of its subtrees are heaps, and because most nodes are near the bottom, where sift-down is cheap. Summing work by height rather than by node is what yields the linear bound.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: that descending loop order is exactly what guarantees both subtrees are heaps when a node is processed.</li><li>(B) is false: a top-down sweep repairs a node before its subtrees are heaps, and a later exchange in a subtree can break the order at an ancestor. The correct top-down alternative is repeated insertion, which costs <span class=\"mm\">O(n log n)</span>.</li><li>(C) is true: a node of height <span class=\"mm\">h</span> sits at depth at least <span class=\"mm\">h</span>, so it lies among the last <span class=\"mm\">2^(h+1)</span> positions; there are at most <span class=\"mm\">&lceil;n/2^(h+1)&rceil;</span> of them.</li><li>(D) is true: the total is <span class=\"mm\">&sum;_h O(h) &middot; n/2^(h+1) = O(n)</span>, and the worst case really does reach <span class=\"mm\">&Theta;(n)</span> exchanges, so the bound is tight.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (B) is the trap: the loop order looks like a harmless reversal, but repairing a parent before its children cannot work, because the child's repair may push a large key up past the parent again.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q38", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "heapify-and-build-heap", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">BUILD-MAX-HEAP is run on <span class=\"mm\">A[1..7] = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]</span>. Each MAX-HEAPIFY exchanges <span class=\"mm\">A[i]</span> with its larger child while that child is larger and then continues from that child. What is the total number of exchanges performed?</p>", "options": ["2", "3", "5", "4"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The number of exchanges equals the total distance travelled by the sifted keys. An ascending array is the worst shape for a max-heap build: every internal node is smaller than its children, so every sift-down starts.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Start <span class=\"mm\">[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">i = 3</span>: children 6 and 7; exchange 3 with 7 &rarr; <span class=\"mm\">[1, 2, 7, 4, 5, 6, 3]</span>. 1 exchange.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">i = 2</span>: children 4 and 5; exchange 2 with 5 &rarr; <span class=\"mm\">[1, 5, 7, 4, 2, 6, 3]</span>. 2 exchanges.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">i = 1</span>: children 5 and 7; exchange 1 with 7 &rarr; <span class=\"mm\">[7, 5, 1, 4, 2, 6, 3]</span>; at node 3, children 6 and 7, exchange 1 with 6 &rarr; <span class=\"mm\">[7, 5, 6, 4, 2, 1, 3]</span>. 4 exchanges in total.</li><li>The final heap is <span class=\"mm\">[7, 5, 6, 4, 2, 1, 3]</span>, reached in 4 exchanges.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D), 4.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting one exchange per internal node gives 3 and ignores the second step of the sift-down at the root. Counting every comparison instead of every exchange gives 5 or 6.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q39", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "heap-sort", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which statement about the stability of HEAPSORT is TRUE?</p>", "options": ["It is stable, because equal keys are never compared with each other.", "It is not stable: exchanging the root with the last slot can move an equal key past its twin.", "It is stable in the worst case but not on average.", "It is stable whenever all keys are distinct and unstable otherwise."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A sort is stable when equal keys keep their relative order. HEAPSORT moves keys by long-range exchanges between the root and the end of the heap, which can leapfrog an equal key.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The build phase and each sift-down exchange a key with a child or with the last slot, never with a neighbour.</li><li>A key can therefore travel from one end of the array to the other in a single exchange, crossing any equal key in between.</li><li>Consequently equal keys can be reversed, so the order among them is not preserved.</li><li>HEAPSORT is thus not stable; merge sort and insertion sort are the standard stable alternatives with the same or better guarantees.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (D) is a category error: stability is a property of the algorithm on inputs that contain equal keys, not something the algorithm acquires when keys are distinct. Option (A) confuses 'no comparison between equal keys' with 'equal keys keep their order'.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q40", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "heap-sort", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">HEAPSORT builds a max-heap of <span class=\"mm\">n</span> keys and then repeatedly exchanges the root with the last slot of the shrinking heap. How many such root-and-last exchanges does it perform?</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">n &minus; 1</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">n</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">&lfloor;n/2&rfloor;</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">n log&#8322; n</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each exchange fixes one more key in its final position at the end of the array, and the last remaining key is already in place.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>After the build, the largest key is at <span class=\"mm\">A[1]</span> and the heap size is <span class=\"mm\">n</span>.</li><li>Exchange 1 puts the largest key at <span class=\"mm\">A[n]</span> and shrinks the heap to <span class=\"mm\">n&minus;1</span>.</li><li>After <span class=\"mm\">k</span> exchanges the heap holds <span class=\"mm\">n&minus;k</span> keys and the last <span class=\"mm\">k</span> slots are final.</li><li>The process stops when one key remains, that is after <span class=\"mm\">n&minus;1</span> exchanges.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A), n − 1.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering <span class=\"mm\">n</span> forgets that the final single-element heap needs no exchange. Answering <span class=\"mm\">&lfloor;n/2&rfloor;</span> confuses the extraction phase with the build phase, which starts at the last internal node.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q41", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "heap-sort", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">HEAPSORT is run on <span class=\"mm\">A[1..8] = [3, 9, 2, 7, 5, 8, 1, 6]</span>. After the build, the first extraction exchanges <span class=\"mm\">A[1]</span> with <span class=\"mm\">A[8]</span>, shrinks the heap to 7 keys and calls MAX-HEAPIFY at the root. What key is then stored at <span class=\"mm\">A[1]</span>?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "8", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Extraction is one swap plus one sift-down. The new root value is the largest of the keys still in the heap, which is the larger child of the old root once the swap has brought the old last key up.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>BUILD-MAX-HEAP on <span class=\"mm\">[3, 9, 2, 7, 5, 8, 1, 6]</span>: <span class=\"mm\">i = 4</span> leaves 7 and 6 alone; <span class=\"mm\">i = 3</span> exchanges 2 with 8; <span class=\"mm\">i = 2</span> leaves 9 and 7 alone; <span class=\"mm\">i = 1</span> exchanges 3 with 9, then 3 with 7, then 3 with 6.</li><li>The heap is <span class=\"mm\">[9, 7, 8, 6, 5, 2, 1, 3]</span>.</li><li>Extraction: exchange <span class=\"mm\">A[1] = 9</span> with <span class=\"mm\">A[8] = 3</span> &rarr; <span class=\"mm\">[3, 7, 8, 6, 5, 2, 1, 9]</span>, heap size 7.</li><li>MAX-HEAPIFY at the root: children 7 and 8; exchange 3 with 8 &rarr; <span class=\"mm\">[8, 7, 3, 6, 5, 2, 1, 9]</span>; at node 3 the children are 2 and 1, both smaller than 3, so the sift stops.</li><li>So <span class=\"mm\">A[1] = 8</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 8.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting the largest remaining key 7 by ignoring that the build already moved 8 to <span class=\"mm\">A[3]</span>, or reporting 9 by forgetting that it has been parked at <span class=\"mm\">A[8]</span> outside the heap. The heap boundary, not the array boundary, decides.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q42", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "heap-sort", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">HEAPSORT runs on <span class=\"mm\">A = [16, 14, 10, 8, 7, 9, 3, 2, 4, 1]</span>, which is already a max-heap. After the first extraction — exchange <span class=\"mm\">A[1]</span> with <span class=\"mm\">A[10]</span>, shrink the heap to 9 keys, MAX-HEAPIFY at the root — which array results?</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">[14, 8, 10, 2, 7, 9, 3, 4, 1, 16]</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">[1, 14, 10, 8, 7, 9, 3, 2, 4, 16]</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">[14, 8, 10, 4, 7, 9, 3, 2, 1, 16]</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">[14, 8, 10, 4, 7, 9, 3, 1, 2, 16]</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The sift-down path is determined at every step by the larger child. Here the old last key 1 travels down three levels, and each exchange must pick the larger of the two children.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Exchange <span class=\"mm\">A[1] = 16</span> with <span class=\"mm\">A[10] = 1</span> &rarr; <span class=\"mm\">[1, 14, 10, 8, 7, 9, 3, 2, 4, 16]</span>, heap size 9.</li><li>Node 1: children 14 and 10; exchange 1 with 14 &rarr; <span class=\"mm\">[14, 1, 10, 8, 7, 9, 3, 2, 4, 16]</span>.</li><li>Node 2: children 8 and 7; exchange 1 with 8 &rarr; <span class=\"mm\">[14, 8, 10, 1, 7, 9, 3, 2, 4, 16]</span>.</li><li>Node 4: children 2 and 4; exchange 1 with 4 &rarr; <span class=\"mm\">[14, 8, 10, 4, 7, 9, 3, 2, 1, 16]</span>. Node 9 is a leaf.</li><li>Final array <span class=\"mm\">[14, 8, 10, 4, 7, 9, 3, 2, 1, 16]</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (B) stops after the first exchange and forgets the rest of the sift-down. Option (D) exchanges with the smaller child 2 at the last level instead of the larger child 4 — the classic heapify error.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q43", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "heap-sort", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about HEAPSORT are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["HEAPSORT is a comparison sort whose worst-case running time is <span class=\"mm\">&Theta;(n log n)</span>.", "HEAPSORT sorts in place, using <span class=\"mm\">O(1)</span> auxiliary space beyond the array.", "HEAPSORT is stable.", "The build phase costs <span class=\"mm\">O(n)</span> and the extraction phase <span class=\"mm\">&Theta;(n log n)</span>, so the total is <span class=\"mm\">&Theta;(n log n)</span>."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> HEAPSORT combines a linear-time build with <span class=\"mm\">n&minus;1</span> logarithmic sift-downs, and it needs no second array: every exchange happens inside the input.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: the worst case of the extraction loop is <span class=\"mm\">(n&minus;1) &middot; &Theta;(log n)</span>, and no comparison sort beats <span class=\"mm\">&Omega;(n log n)</span> in the worst case.</li><li>(B) is true: only a constant number of index and temporary variables are used beyond the array itself.</li><li>(C) is false: long-range exchanges can reverse equal keys, so HEAPSORT is not stable.</li><li>(D) is true: <span class=\"mm\">O(n) + &Theta;(n log n) = &Theta;(n log n)</span>, and the build term never dominates.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (C) is the standard slip. A second trap is to think the <span class=\"mm\">O(n)</span> build makes the total <span class=\"mm\">O(n)</span>: the extraction loop alone is <span class=\"mm\">&Theta;(n log n)</span>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q44", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "heap-sort", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"mm\">5</span> distinct keys, how many of the <span class=\"mm\">5!</span> input permutations cause BUILD-MAX-HEAP to perform no exchange at all?</p>", "options": ["5", "15", "24", "8"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An input that needs no exchange is already a max-heap, so the count is the number of distinct max-heaps on <span class=\"mm\">n</span> keys, <span class=\"mm\">n! / &prod; size(v)</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>For <span class=\"mm\">n = 5</span> the complete tree has a left subtree of 3 nodes and a right subtree of 1 node.</li><li>The subtree sizes are 5, 3, 1, 1, 1, whose product is 15.</li><li>So the number of heaps is <span class=\"mm\">120 / 15 = 8</span>.</li><li>Check by recursion: <span class=\"mm\">H(5) = C(4,3) &middot; H(3) &middot; H(1) = 4 &middot; 2 &middot; 1 = 8</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D), 8.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering <span class=\"mm\">5! = 120</span> or <span class=\"mm\">24</span> treats every permutation as heap-shaped. The tempting <span class=\"mm\">15 = C(6,2)</span> is the subtree-size product, not the count.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q45", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "priority-queue-applications", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">On a binary min-heap of <span class=\"mm\">n</span> keys, which of the following operations costs <span class=\"mm\">&Theta;(n)</span> in the worst case?</p>", "options": ["INSERT", "SEARCH for an element with a given key value", "EXTRACT-MIN", "DECREASE-KEY, when the position of the element in the array is known"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A heap orders keys only along root-to-leaf paths, so it supports navigation by position, not lookup by value. Anything that must find an arbitrary key degenerates to a linear scan.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>INSERT places the key at the end and trickles it up one path: <span class=\"mm\">O(log n)</span>.</li><li>EXTRACT-MIN swaps the root with the last key and sifts down one path: <span class=\"mm\">O(log n)</span>.</li><li>DECREASE-KEY with the position known sifts up from that position: <span class=\"mm\">O(log n)</span>.</li><li>SEARCH has no position to start from and no ordering to exploit between siblings, so in the worst case every array slot must be examined: <span class=\"mm\">&Theta;(n)</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering DECREASE-KEY because 'changing a key sounds expensive'. With the position in hand it is logarithmic; without it, it is a search first. The heap is not a search tree.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q46", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "priority-queue-applications", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A scheduler keeps runnable jobs in a binary min-heap keyed by remaining time. What is the cost of identifying the job with the least remaining time, without removing it?</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">O(1)</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">O(log n)</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">O(n)</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">O(n log n)</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A min-heap keeps its minimum at the root, so reading it is a single array access. Only removing it disturbs the structure.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The heap property guarantees <span class=\"mm\">A[1] &le; A[i]</span> for every <span class=\"mm\">i</span>, so the minimum is at index 1.</li><li>Reading <span class=\"mm\">A[1]</span> needs no comparisons and no restructuring.</li><li>The logarithmic cost belongs to EXTRACT-MIN, which replaces the root and sifts down.</li><li>Hence the query is <span class=\"mm\">O(1)</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A), O(1).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering <span class=\"mm\">O(log n)</span> by conflating peek with extract. A priority queue is chosen precisely because peeking is constant time while extraction is logarithmic.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q47", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "priority-queue-applications", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Huffman coding is applied to six symbols with frequencies <span class=\"mm\">5, 9, 12, 13, 16</span> and <span class=\"mm\">45</span>. What is the length of the codeword assigned to the symbol with frequency <span class=\"mm\">45</span>?</p>", "options": ["1 bit", "2 bits", "3 bits", "4 bits"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Huffman's algorithm repeatedly merges the two smallest weights. The depth of a symbol in the final tree is its codeword length, and the largest weight is merged last of all.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Merge <span class=\"mm\">5 + 9 = 14</span>: weights <span class=\"mm\">{12, 13, 14, 16, 45}</span>.</li><li>Merge <span class=\"mm\">12 + 13 = 25</span>: weights <span class=\"mm\">{14, 16, 25, 45}</span>.</li><li>Merge <span class=\"mm\">14 + 16 = 30</span>: weights <span class=\"mm\">{25, 30, 45}</span>.</li><li>Merge <span class=\"mm\">25 + 30 = 55</span>: weights <span class=\"mm\">{45, 55}</span>, then merge <span class=\"mm\">45 + 55 = 100</span>.</li><li>The symbol of weight 45 is merged at the very last step, so it is a child of the root: its codeword has length 1.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A), 1 bit.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the codeword must be longer than one bit, or that the answer depends on the number of symbols. With total weight 100, the heaviest symbol alone accounts for 45 % of the traffic, and one bit suffices for it. Note the tree is not a balanced tree.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q48", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "priority-queue-applications", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Huffman coding is applied to six symbols with frequencies <span class=\"mm\">4, 5, 6, 9, 11, 15</span>. What is the total number of bits in the encoded file, that is <span class=\"mm\">&sum;</span> over symbols of frequency <span class=\"mm\">&times;</span> codeword length?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "124", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Every merge in Huffman's algorithm adds the two subtree weights, and the sum of all merge weights equals the total encoded length <span class=\"mm\">&sum; f_i l_i</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Merge <span class=\"mm\">4 + 5 = 9</span>: <span class=\"mm\">{6, 9, 9, 11, 15}</span>.</li><li>Merge <span class=\"mm\">6 + 9 = 15</span>: <span class=\"mm\">{9, 11, 15, 15}</span>.</li><li>Merge <span class=\"mm\">9 + 11 = 20</span>: <span class=\"mm\">{15, 15, 20}</span>.</li><li>Merge <span class=\"mm\">15 + 15 = 30</span>: <span class=\"mm\">{20, 30}</span>, then merge <span class=\"mm\">20 + 30 = 50</span>.</li><li>Codeword lengths: 4 and 5 get 4 bits; 6 gets 3 bits; 9 and 11 get 2 bits; 15 gets 2 bits.</li><li>Total <span class=\"mm\">4&middot;4 + 5&middot;4 + 6&middot;3 + 9&middot;2 + 11&middot;2 + 15&middot;2 = 16 + 20 + 18 + 18 + 22 + 30 = 124</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 124.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting the merge weights instead of the symbol contributions, or forgetting that two symbols of equal weight may be merged in either order — the total is the same either way, but only if every merge is counted once. Summing the merge weights, <span class=\"mm\">9 + 15 + 20 + 30 + 50 = 124</span>, is a useful check.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q49", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "priority-queue-applications", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about implementations of a priority queue are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["A binary heap supports INSERT, EXTRACT-MIN and DECREASE-KEY in <span class=\"mm\">O(log n)</span> worst case when the position of the element is known.", "A sorted array supports INSERT in <span class=\"mm\">O(log n)</span> and EXTRACT-MIN in <span class=\"mm\">O(1)</span>.", "A Fibonacci heap supports DECREASE-KEY in <span class=\"mm\">O(1)</span> amortized and EXTRACT-MIN in <span class=\"mm\">O(log n)</span> amortized.", "A balanced binary search tree supports INSERT, DELETE and FIND-MIN in <span class=\"mm\">O(log n)</span> worst case."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A priority queue trades off three operations: insert, extract-the-extreme and decrease-key. Arrays, heaps, Fibonacci heaps and balanced trees sit at different points of that trade-off.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: with the index in hand, sift-up and sift-down each touch one root-to-leaf path.</li><li>(B) is false: finding the insertion point in a sorted array is <span class=\"mm\">O(log n)</span>, but shifting the tail to make room is <span class=\"mm\">O(n)</span>, so INSERT is linear.</li><li>(C) is true: a Fibonacci heap cuts a node and defers consolidation, giving constant amortized decrease-key, while extract-min pays <span class=\"mm\">O(log n)</span> amortized for the consolidation.</li><li>(D) is true: a balanced BST keeps height <span class=\"mm\">O(log n)</span>, and the minimum is the leftmost node, reached in <span class=\"mm\">O(log n)</span> worst case.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (B) is the classic half-truth: the search is logarithmic but the shift is not. A second trap is believing the Fibonacci heap beats a binary heap everywhere — its extract-min constant is far worse in practice, which is why binary heaps are the default.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U6-Q50", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 6, "unit_slug": "heaps-priority-queues", "subtopic": "priority-queue-applications", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A stream of <span class=\"mm\">n = 1000</span> numbers is processed with a min-heap of capacity <span class=\"mm\">k = 10</span> to keep the ten largest seen so far: the first ten numbers are inserted, and each of the remaining numbers is compared with the root and, if it is larger, replaces the root and is sifted down. In the worst case, how many sift-down operations are performed?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "990", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The top-k pattern keeps the k best in a heap whose root is the weakest survivor. Each new element costs one comparison with the root, and a sift-down only when it beats the root.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The first <span class=\"mm\">k = 10</span> numbers build the initial heap; the build is not a sift-down per element.</li><li>The remaining <span class=\"mm\">1000 &minus; 10 = 990</span> numbers are each compared with the root once.</li><li>A number larger than the root replaces it and sifts down, which is one sift-down operation.</li><li>In the worst case — an increasing stream — every one of the 990 numbers is larger, so 990 sift-downs are performed.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 990.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 1000 by counting every element, or 100 by confusing the capacity with the number of sift-downs. The first k elements build the heap, and only the <span class=\"mm\">n &minus; k</span> later ones can trigger a replacement.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q01", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "hash-functions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A hash table stores decimal keys with the division method <span class=\"mm\">h(k) = k mod m</span>. Which choice of <span class=\"mm\">m</span> spreads the keys best?</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">m = 1000</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">m = 1024</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">m = 997</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">m = 1 000 000</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In the division method the slot is the remainder modulo <span class=\"mm\">m</span>, so the arithmetic of <span class=\"mm\">m</span> decides which digit patterns survive. A prime that is not close to a power of two mixes the whole key; a composite that shares a factor with the structure of the keys throws information away.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">1000 = 2³·5³</span> and <span class=\"mm\">1 000 000 = 10⁶</span> are both highly composite, so keys that share their last three decimal digits, or that differ by a multiple of 1000, pile into the same slots.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">1024 = 2¹⁰</span> is worse still for this method: <span class=\"mm\">k mod 1024</span> is exactly the low ten bits of <span class=\"mm\">k</span>, so every key that agrees in its last ten binary digits collides.</li><li>997 is prime and is not near a power of two, so it carries none of that structural bias.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C), <span class=\"mm\">m = 997</span>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> A power-of-two table size is the right reflex for the multiplication method, where the residue is read off by a shift. Reusing that reflex here gives the worst of the four choices.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q02", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "hash-functions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">In the multiplication method <span class=\"mm\">h(k) = ⌊m(kA mod 1)⌋</span>, the table size <span class=\"mm\">m</span> is normally chosen as a power of two because</p>", "options": ["it forces the multiplier <span class=\"mm\">A</span> to be an integer", "the product <span class=\"mm\">m(kA mod 1)</span> is then a shift of the binary representation, so no division is needed", "power-of-two table sizes make collisions impossible", "it makes the hash value independent of the key"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The multiplication method computes the fractional part of <span class=\"mm\">kA</span> and stretches it over the table. With <span class=\"mm\">m = 2^p</span> the stretch is a shift of the binary point, so the whole computation is a multiply and a bit extraction — no division, which is the expensive operation on most machines.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">kA mod 1</span> is a fraction in <span class=\"mm\">[0, 1)</span>; multiplying it by <span class=\"mm\">2^p</span> moves its binary point <span class=\"mm\">p</span> places to the right.</li><li>Taking the floor then keeps exactly the top <span class=\"mm\">p</span> bits of that fraction, which a compiler turns into a shift and a mask.</li><li>Nothing in the argument constrains <span class=\"mm\">A</span>, and no table size can prevent collisions — there are always more keys than slots.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B), the shift that replaces a division.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the speed of the multiplication method as a guarantee about collisions. A power of two buys arithmetic speed only; the spread comes from the choice of <span class=\"mm\">A</span>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q03", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "hash-functions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A table of 7 slots uses <span class=\"mm\">h(k) = k mod 7</span>. The keys 5, 12, 19, 26 and 33 are inserted. How many of these five keys hash to the same slot?</p>", "options": ["2", "3", "4", "5"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two keys collide under the division method exactly when their difference is a multiple of <span class=\"mm\">m</span>. Here the five keys form one arithmetic progression with common difference 7.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">12 = 7 + 5</span>, <span class=\"mm\">19 = 14 + 5</span>, <span class=\"mm\">26 = 21 + 5</span>, <span class=\"mm\">33 = 28 + 5</span>.</li><li>Each is a multiple of 7 plus 5, so each leaves remainder 5: <span class=\"mm\">h(k) = 5</span> for all five keys.</li><li>All five land in slot 5, so all five share one slot.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D), all five.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading <span class=\"mm\">12 mod 7</span> as 2 by halving, or counting only the pairs that happen to be adjacent. The reliable test is whether the difference is a multiple of <span class=\"mm\">m</span>: here every difference is 7, 14, 21 or 28.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q04", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "hash-functions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The multiplication method uses <span class=\"mm\">h(k) = ⌊m(kA mod 1)⌋</span> with <span class=\"mm\">A = 0.618</span> and <span class=\"mm\">m = 10</span>. What is <span class=\"mm\">h(30)</span>?</p>", "options": ["4", "5", "6", "8"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The method keeps the fractional part of <span class=\"mm\">kA</span>, scales it by <span class=\"mm\">m</span> and floors it. The integer part of <span class=\"mm\">kA</span> is discarded before the scaling, not after.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">kA = 30 × 0.618 = 18.54</span>.</li><li>The fractional part is <span class=\"mm\">0.54</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">m × 0.54 = 10 × 0.54 = 5.4</span>, and <span class=\"mm\">⌊5.4⌋ = 5</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B), 5.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reducing after the multiply instead of taking the fraction first: <span class=\"mm\">18.54 mod 10 = 8.54</span> gives 8, option (D). The fractional part must be isolated before the table size is applied.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q05", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "hash-functions", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">For the division method <span class=\"mm\">h(k) = k mod m</span>, which of the following statements are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["If <span class=\"mm\">m</span> is a power of two, the hash value depends only on the low-order bits of <span class=\"mm\">k</span>.", "A prime <span class=\"mm\">m</span> that is not close to a power of two generally spreads keys better than a composite <span class=\"mm\">m</span>.", "For every <span class=\"mm\">m</span>, the keys <span class=\"mm\">k</span> and <span class=\"mm\">k + m</span> hash to the same slot.", "The division method requires <span class=\"mm\">m</span> to be larger than the largest key stored."], "answer_display": "Options ((A), (B)) and ((C))", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The division method is entirely a statement about remainders, so every property it has follows from the divisibility structure of <span class=\"mm\">m</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: <span class=\"mm\">k mod 2^p</span> keeps the low <span class=\"mm\">p</span> bits and drops every higher bit, so two keys that agree below bit <span class=\"mm\">p</span> cannot be told apart.</li><li>(B) is true: a prime has no proper divisors to resonate with digit patterns in the keys, and being away from a power of two avoids the low-bit truncation of (A).</li><li>(C) is true: <span class=\"mm\">(k + m) mod m = k mod m</span> for every <span class=\"mm\">m</span>, since the added term is an exact multiple of the modulus.</li><li>(D) is false: <span class=\"mm\">k mod m</span> is defined for every <span class=\"mm\">k</span>; keys larger than <span class=\"mm\">m</span> simply wrap, which is the normal case.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (D) is the usual slip — believing the table must be at least as large as the key space. The division method exists precisely to fold a large key space into a small table.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q06", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "hash-functions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A universal family is built as <span class=\"mm\">h_{a,b}(k) = ((ak + b) mod p) mod m</span>, where <span class=\"mm\">p = 17</span> is prime and larger than every key, <span class=\"mm\">a ∈ {1, …, 16}</span> and <span class=\"mm\">b ∈ {0, …, 16}</span>. How many distinct hash functions does this family contain?</p>", "options": ["256", "272", "289", "306"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The family is parameterised by the pair <span class=\"mm\">(a, b)</span>, and each pair gives a different function, so the size of the family is the number of admissible pairs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">a</span> ranges over <span class=\"mm\">1 … p − 1</span>, which is 16 values; <span class=\"mm\">a = 0</span> is excluded because <span class=\"mm\">h_{0,b}</span> would ignore the key.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">b</span> ranges over <span class=\"mm\">0 … p − 1</span>, which is 17 values.</li><li>The count is <span class=\"mm\">p(p − 1) = 17 × 16 = 272</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B), 272.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering <span class=\"mm\">17 × 17 = 289</span> by letting <span class=\"mm\">a = 0</span> in, which would add 17 constant functions that are useless as hashes.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q07", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "open-addressing-linear-quadratic-double", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A table of 11 slots, numbered 0 to 10, starts empty. Insertion uses <span class=\"mm\">h(k) = k mod 11</span> with linear probing. The keys 22, 33, 44, 15 and 26 are inserted in that order. In which slot does 26 end up?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "5", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Linear probing follows the probe sequence <span class=\"mm\">(h(k) + i) mod m</span> until it meets a free slot, so the slot a key occupies is decided by every key inserted before it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">22 mod 11 = 0</span>; slot 0 is free, so 22 goes to slot 0.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">33 mod 11 = 0</span>; slot 0 is taken, so 33 probes slot 1, which is free.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">44 mod 11 = 0</span>; slots 0 and 1 are taken, so 44 goes to slot 2.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">15 mod 11 = 4</span>; slot 4 is free, so 15 goes to slot 4.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">26 mod 11 = 4</span>; slot 4 is taken, so the probe moves to slot 5, which is free.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 5.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Stopping at the first occupied slot and calling the insertion a failure, or applying the displacement to the key rather than to the probe index. The probe index advances; the home slot does not move.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q08", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "open-addressing-linear-quadratic-double", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which collision-resolution scheme is the one that suffers from primary clustering?</p>", "options": ["separate chaining", "linear probing", "quadratic probing", "double hashing"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Primary clustering is the growth of runs of occupied slots that any key hashing into the run must join. It appears when the probe step is a fixed constant, because then all keys with nearby home slots share the same run.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>In linear probing the step is 1, so once a run of occupied slots forms, every key that hashes into any part of it appends itself to the end of the run and lengthens it.</li><li>A run therefore attracts more keys the longer it gets: the expected search cost grows like <span class=\"mm\">1/(1 − α)²</span>, far worse than the <span class=\"mm\">1/(1 − α)</span> of uniform hashing.</li><li>Quadratic probing removes runs of adjacent slots, though it keeps a weaker secondary clustering: keys with the same home slot follow the same probe sequence. Double hashing makes that sequence key-dependent, so it removes secondary clustering as well.</li><li>Chaining has no probe sequence at all, so clustering does not arise.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B), linear probing.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing primary clustering with the mere existence of collisions. Every scheme has collisions; primary clustering is the specific pathology of a constant probe step, where collisions reinforce each other.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q09", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "open-addressing-linear-quadratic-double", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A table of 10 slots uses quadratic probing with probe sequence <span class=\"mm\">(h(k) + i²) mod 10</span> for <span class=\"mm\">i = 0, 1, 2, …</span>, where <span class=\"mm\">h(k) = k mod 10</span>. The keys 12, 22, 32 and 42 are inserted in that order. In which slot does 42 land?</p>", "options": ["1", "5", "7", "9"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Quadratic probing adds the square of the probe index rather than the index itself, so the offsets are <span class=\"mm\">0, 1, 4, 9, 16, …</span> reduced modulo the table size. Every key here has home slot 2.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">12 mod 10 = 2</span>: slot 2 is free, so 12 goes to slot 2.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">22 mod 10 = 2</span>: slot 2 is taken; <span class=\"mm\">i = 1</span> gives <span class=\"mm\">2 + 1 = 3</span>, free, so 22 goes to slot 3.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">32 mod 10 = 2</span>: slots 2 and 3 are taken; <span class=\"mm\">i = 2</span> gives <span class=\"mm\">2 + 4 = 6</span>, free, so 32 goes to slot 6.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">42 mod 10 = 2</span>: slots 2, 3 and 6 are taken; <span class=\"mm\">i = 3</span> gives <span class=\"mm\">(2 + 9) mod 10 = 1</span>, which is free.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A), slot 1.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the offsets run <span class=\"mm\">2, 3, 4, 5</span> as in linear probing and answering 5. The offset is <span class=\"mm\">i²</span>, and it must be reduced modulo the table size, which is why <span class=\"mm\">2 + 9</span> wraps to 1 rather than to 11.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q10", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "open-addressing-linear-quadratic-double", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about open addressing are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["The load factor can never exceed 1.", "A deleted key cannot simply leave an empty slot, because that would break later probe sequences; a tombstone marker is needed.", "Linear probing needs at most two probes per insertion as long as the load factor stays below 1/2.", "Double hashing clusters less than linear probing, but its second hash function must never return 0."], "answer_display": "Options ((A), (B)) and ((D))", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In open addressing every key lives in the table array itself, so the number of keys is bounded by the number of slots, and every operation has to walk a probe sequence that later operations will also follow.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: with <span class=\"mm\">n</span> keys in <span class=\"mm\">m</span> slots, <span class=\"mm\">α = n/m ≤ 1</span>, and at <span class=\"mm\">α = 1</span> the table is full and no insertion can succeed.</li><li>(B) is true: emptying a slot in the middle of a run would make a later search stop early at that hole and wrongly conclude that the key is absent, so the slot must be marked deleted rather than free.</li><li>(C) is false: the number of probes is unbounded for a single insertion even at moderate load. A run of occupied slots is joined at its end, so one insertion can walk a long way — the bound is probabilistic, not two.</li><li>(D) is true: a step of 0 leaves the key stuck on its home slot, and with a prime table size any step in <span class=\"mm\">1 … m − 1</span> reaches every slot.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (C) treats an average as a worst case. The expected probe count is small below <span class=\"mm\">α = 1/2</span>, but a single insertion into a long run can examine far more than two slots.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q11", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "open-addressing-linear-quadratic-double", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A table of 11 slots uses double hashing with <span class=\"mm\">h₁(k) = k mod 11</span> and <span class=\"mm\">h₂(k) = 7 − (k mod 7)</span>, probing the slots <span class=\"mm\">(h₁(k) + i·h₂(k)) mod 11</span>. The keys 4, 15, 26 and 37 are inserted in that order. In which slot does 37 land?</p>", "options": ["2", "5", "8", "9"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Double hashing uses a key-dependent step, so two keys with the same home slot follow different probe sequences. Here all four keys hash to slot 4, which makes the second hash the only thing separating them.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">h₁(4) = 4</span>: slot 4 is free, so 4 goes to slot 4.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">h₁(15) = 4</span>; <span class=\"mm\">h₂(15) = 7 − 1 = 6</span>; the probe reaches <span class=\"mm\">(4 + 6) mod 11 = 10</span>, free, so 15 goes to slot 10.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">h₁(26) = 4</span>; <span class=\"mm\">h₂(26) = 7 − 5 = 2</span>; the probe reaches <span class=\"mm\">(4 + 2) mod 11 = 6</span>, free, so 26 goes to slot 6.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">h₁(37) = 4</span>; <span class=\"mm\">h₂(37) = 7 − 2 = 5</span>; slot <span class=\"mm\">(4 + 5) mod 11 = 9</span> is free, so 37 goes to slot 9.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D), slot 9.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using <span class=\"mm\">k mod 7</span> as the step instead of <span class=\"mm\">7 − (k mod 7)</span>, which sends 37 to slot 6 — already taken by 26 — and then round the wrong loop. Read the second function as written.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q12", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "collision-resolution-chaining", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A chained hash table has 20 slots and holds 60 keys. What is its load factor?</p>", "options": ["1/3", "3", "20", "0.6"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The load factor is the average number of keys per slot, <span class=\"mm\">α = n/m</span>. With chaining it is exactly the average chain length, so it is not bounded by 1.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">n = 60</span> keys and <span class=\"mm\">m = 20</span> slots.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">α = 60/20 = 3</span>, so the average chain holds three keys.</li><li>That is why the expected search cost in a chained table is written <span class=\"mm\">Θ(1 + α)</span> — the 1 accounts for reaching the head of the chain.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B), 3.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering <span class=\"mm\">1/3</span> by inverting the ratio. Load factor is keys per slot, not slots per key, and in a chained table it is allowed to exceed 1.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q13", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "collision-resolution-chaining", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">In a chained hash table with load factor <span class=\"mm\">α</span>, under simple uniform hashing the expected number of keys examined by an unsuccessful search is</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">α</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">1 + α</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">1 + α/2</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">ln(1/(1 − α))</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An unsuccessful search must walk the whole chain of the slot it lands in, because the key is nowhere. Under simple uniform hashing the expected chain length is <span class=\"mm\">α</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Every key hashes independently and uniformly, so the expected number of keys in any one slot is <span class=\"mm\">n/m = α</span>.</li><li>An unsuccessful search visits exactly those keys: there is no early exit and no successful match.</li><li>So the expected count is <span class=\"mm\">α</span> — not <span class=\"mm\">1 + α</span>, because the head of the chain is itself one of the <span class=\"mm\">α</span> keys.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A), <span class=\"mm\">α</span>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Choosing <span class=\"mm\">1 + α</span> by reflex. The extra 1 belongs to a successful search, where the counted keys include the match; for an unsuccessful search the chain is simply empty on average <span class=\"mm\">α</span> times. The fourth option is the open-addressing formula, which does not apply to chaining.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q14", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "collision-resolution-chaining", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about separate chaining are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["The load factor may exceed 1.", "Deleting a key is easy once its node is found, because a chain is an ordinary linked list.", "Even when every key collides in one slot, a search costs <span class=\"mm\">Θ(1 + α)</span>.", "Chaining needs extra space for pointers and has poorer cache locality than open addressing."], "answer_display": "Options ((A), (B)) and ((D))", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Separate chaining keeps the table as an array of list heads, so collisions cost list nodes rather than table slots. That single design decision explains all of its advantages and its one real cost.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: nothing in the structure limits how many keys a chain may hold, so <span class=\"mm\">α = n/m</span> can be far above 1.</li><li>(B) is true: a deletion is an ordinary list deletion. In open addressing the same operation needs tombstones because the slot is part of every probe sequence.</li><li>(C) is false: if all keys collide, one chain holds all <span class=\"mm\">n</span> keys and a search walks it, so the cost is <span class=\"mm\">Θ(n)</span>. The <span class=\"mm\">Θ(1 + α)</span> bound is an average under simple uniform hashing.</li><li>(D) is true: each node carries a pointer, and the nodes are scattered in memory, so a search misses the cache repeatedly where open addressing would stay inside one array.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (C) quotes the average as a guarantee. Every hash table has a worst case of <span class=\"mm\">Θ(n)</span> when the hash function fails to spread; chaining is not exempt.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q15", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "collision-resolution-chaining", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A chained table of 10 slots receives the keys 5, 15, 25, 2, 12 and 3 in that order, with <span class=\"mm\">h(k) = k mod 10</span>. Which slot holds the longest chain, and how long is it?</p>", "options": ["slot 2, 2 keys", "slot 5, 2 keys", "slot 5, 3 keys", "slot 3, 3 keys"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With chaining, every key with the same home slot joins the same list, so the longest chain is the largest number of keys sharing one remainder.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">5 mod 10 = 5</span>, <span class=\"mm\">15 mod 10 = 5</span> and <span class=\"mm\">25 mod 10 = 5</span> — three keys in slot 5.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">2 mod 10 = 2</span> and <span class=\"mm\">12 mod 10 = 2</span> — two keys in slot 2.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">3 mod 10 = 3</span> — one key in slot 3.</li><li>The longest chain is therefore slot 5 with three keys.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C), slot 5 with 3 keys.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the last digit of 15 and 25 as 5 but the last digit of 5 as itself and then losing count, or assuming a chain cannot grow past two because the table has ten slots.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q16", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "load-factor-and-rehashing", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A chained table of 10 slots holds 45 keys. Under simple uniform hashing the expected number of comparisons in a successful search is <span class=\"mm\">1 + α/2</span>, where <span class=\"mm\">α</span> is the load factor. Give that value to two decimal places.</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "3.25", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A successful search walks its chain until it meets the key. Under simple uniform hashing the key sits on average half way along a chain of mean length <span class=\"mm\">α</span>, and the extra 1 counts the head of the chain being examined before the walk starts.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">α = n/m = 45/10 = 4.5</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">α/2 = 2.25</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">1 + α/2 = 1 + 2.25 = 3.25</span>, already exact to two places.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3.25.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using <span class=\"mm\">α</span> alone and answering 4.50 — that is the unsuccessful-search figure. A successful search stops early, which is why its expected cost is roughly half the chain plus the head.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q17", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "load-factor-and-rehashing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A hash table has 13 slots and currently holds 9 keys. What is its load factor?</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">4/13</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">9/13</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">13/9</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">9/4</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The load factor measures how full the table is: keys stored divided by slots available, <span class=\"mm\">α = n/m</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">n = 9</span> keys and <span class=\"mm\">m = 13</span> slots.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">α = 9/13 ≈ 0.69</span>.</li><li>Below 1, as it must be for an open-addressing table that still has room to insert.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B), <span class=\"mm\">9/13</span>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Inverting the ratio to <span class=\"mm\">13/9</span>, or subtracting to get <span class=\"mm\">4/13</span> — the number of free slots. Load factor counts what is stored, not what is left.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q18", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "load-factor-and-rehashing", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about rehashing are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["A single rehash costs <span class=\"mm\">Θ(n)</span>, because every key is reinserted into the new table.", "If the table doubles on every rehash, the amortized cost of one insertion stays <span class=\"mm\">Θ(1)</span>.", "Rehashing keeps every key on its original probe sequence.", "A load factor close to 1 makes open addressing degenerate towards a linear scan."], "answer_display": "Options ((A), (B)) and ((D))", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Rehashing rebuilds the table at a new size and reinserts every key, because the slot of a key depends on the table size. Its cost is therefore proportional to the number of keys, and the amortised argument comes from the doubling.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: each of the <span class=\"mm\">n</span> keys is inserted once more, so one rehash costs <span class=\"mm\">Θ(n)</span>.</li><li>(B) is true: the rehashes after <span class=\"mm\">1, 2, 4, …, n</span> insertions cost <span class=\"mm\">1 + 2 + 4 + … + n &lt; 2n</span> in total, which is <span class=\"mm\">O(1)</span> amortised per insertion.</li><li>(C) is false: the home slot is recomputed modulo the new table size, so probe sequences change. That is the point of the operation.</li><li>(D) is true: the expected probe count of open addressing grows like <span class=\"mm\">1/(1 − α)</span> or worse, which diverges as <span class=\"mm\">α → 1</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (C) is the natural but wrong guess: if the probe sequences survived, a rehash would be pointless. The rebuild exists precisely because every home slot is recomputed against the new modulus.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q19", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "load-factor-and-rehashing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">With open addressing and uniform hashing, an unsuccessful search examines <span class=\"mm\">1/(1 − α)</span> slots in the expected case. For <span class=\"mm\">α = 0.75</span> that number is</p>", "options": ["2", "3", "4", "5"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Under uniform hashing each probe lands on an occupied slot with probability <span class=\"mm\">α</span> independently of the others, so the expected number of probes before the first free slot is the mean of a geometric distribution: <span class=\"mm\">1/(1 − α)</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">1 − α = 1 − 0.75 = 0.25</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">1/0.25 = 4</span>.</li><li>So four probes are expected before a free slot is found.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C), 4.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading <span class=\"mm\">α = 0.75</span> as three quarters full and answering 3, or inverting to <span class=\"mm\">1/α</span>. The formula divides by the free fraction, not by the occupied one.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q20", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "open-addressing-linear-quadratic-double", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A table of 13 slots uses <span class=\"mm\">h(k) = k mod 13</span> with linear probing. The keys 1, 14, 27, 40 and 53 are inserted in that order into an empty table. Counting every slot examined during probing, the slot that finally holds the key included, how many slot examinations do the five insertions take in total?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "15", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> All five keys share one home slot, so they form a single run that grows by one slot per insertion. The number of examinations for the <span class=\"mm\">i</span>-th insertion into that run is <span class=\"mm\">i</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">1 mod 13 = 1</span>, <span class=\"mm\">14 mod 13 = 1</span>, <span class=\"mm\">27 mod 13 = 1</span>, <span class=\"mm\">40 mod 13 = 1</span>, <span class=\"mm\">53 mod 13 = 1</span>: the home slot is 1 for every key.</li><li>Insertion 1 examines slot 1 and takes it: 1 examination. Insertion 2 examines slots 1 and 2: 2. Insertion 3 examines 1, 2, 3: 3. Insertion 4 examines 1, 2, 3, 4: 4. Insertion 5 examines 1, 2, 3, 4, 5: 5.</li><li>The total is <span class=\"mm\">1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 = 15</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 15.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting only the occupied slots each key skipped and forgetting the free slot it finally took, which gives 10. The slot that accepts the key is examined too, and must be counted.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q21", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "hash-functions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A table of <span class=\"mm\">2^10 = 1024</span> slots uses <span class=\"mm\">h(k) = k mod 1024</span> on 32-bit keys. Two distinct keys differ only in bits 20 to 31. Which statement is correct?</p>", "options": ["They always collide, whatever bits 0 to 19 hold.", "They collide only if they also agree on bits 0 to 9.", "They never collide, because the hash uses only the low-order bits.", "They collide exactly when they differ in bits 20 to 31."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The modulus is a power of two, so the remainder keeps exactly the low ten bits of the key. Two keys are indistinguishable to the hash function exactly when their low ten bits agree.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">k mod 2^10</span> is the integer formed by bits 0 to 9 of <span class=\"mm\">k</span>; every higher bit is discarded.</li><li>Keys that differ only in bits 20 to 31 agree in bits 0 to 19, and therefore in bits 0 to 9.</li><li>So their low ten bits are equal and the two keys collide, no matter what bits 0 to 19 happen to be.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A), they always collide.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (C) reverses the consequence: because the hash ignores the high bits, keys that differ only there cannot be separated. Option (B) demands agreement on bits 0 to 9 as if it were extra luck, when it is already forced by agreement on bits 0 to 19.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q22", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "open-addressing-linear-quadratic-double", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A table of 12 slots uses quadratic probing with probe sequence <span class=\"mm\">(h(k) + i²) mod 12</span> for <span class=\"mm\">i = 0, 1, 2, …</span>. For any key, how many distinct slots can its probe sequence reach?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Quadratic probing does not sweep the table: the offsets <span class=\"mm\">i²</span> repeat once <span class=\"mm\">i</span> grows, so only some residues modulo <span class=\"mm\">m</span> are ever produced. Whether the whole table is reachable depends on <span class=\"mm\">m</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Compute the offsets <span class=\"mm\">i² mod 12</span> for <span class=\"mm\">i = 0 … 11</span>: <span class=\"mm\">0, 1, 4, 9, 4, 1, 0, 1, 4, 9, 4, 1</span>.</li><li>The distinct offsets are <span class=\"mm\">{0, 1, 4, 9}</span> — only four values.</li><li>The sequence has period 6 because <span class=\"mm\">(i + 6)² − i² = 12i + 36</span> is a multiple of 12, so probing beyond <span class=\"mm\">i = 5</span> repeats slots already visited.</li><li>Hence any key can reach only four of the twelve slots, and an insertion can fail with eight slots still free.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming quadratic probing always reaches the whole table, which is only guaranteed when <span class=\"mm\">m</span> is prime and <span class=\"mm\">α &lt; 1/2</span>. Here <span class=\"mm\">m = 12</span> is not prime and the sequence stalls after four slots.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q23", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "collision-resolution-chaining", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A chained hash table has <span class=\"mm\">m</span> slots and <span class=\"mm\">n</span> keys, with <span class=\"mm\">α = n/m</span> and simple uniform hashing. Which of the following statements are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["The expected length of any one chain is <span class=\"mm\">α</span>.", "If all <span class=\"mm\">n</span> keys collide in a single slot, an unsuccessful search costs <span class=\"mm\">Θ(n)</span>.", "The expected number of comparisons in an unsuccessful search depends on <span class=\"mm\">α</span> but not on <span class=\"mm\">n</span> and <span class=\"mm\">m</span> separately.", "Chaining cannot be used when the number of keys is not known in advance."], "answer_display": "Options ((A), (B)) and ((C))", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Simple uniform hashing gives every key an independent uniform home slot, so each chain is a binomial sample: its mean is <span class=\"mm\">n/m</span> whatever the individual values of <span class=\"mm\">n</span> and <span class=\"mm\">m</span> happen to be.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: each of the <span class=\"mm\">n</span> keys lands in a given slot with probability <span class=\"mm\">1/m</span>, so the expected chain length is <span class=\"mm\">n/m = α</span>.</li><li>(B) is true: an unsuccessful search walks the whole chain, which here holds all <span class=\"mm\">n</span> keys, so the cost is <span class=\"mm\">Θ(n)</span>.</li><li>(C) is true: the expected cost is <span class=\"mm\">α</span>, a function of the ratio alone. Twenty keys in ten slots and two hundred in a hundred behave identically in expectation.</li><li>(D) is false: the table starts small and is rehashed as keys arrive, so the final number of keys never has to be known in advance.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (B) and option (C) look contradictory — one says the cost depends on <span class=\"mm\">n</span>, the other says it does not. Both are right: (C) is the expectation under simple uniform hashing, (B) is the realised cost when the hash function degenerates and every key lands in one chain.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q24", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "load-factor-and-rehashing", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A table of 11 slots uses open addressing with uniform hashing and currently holds 8 keys. Using <span class=\"mm\">1/(1 − α)</span> for the expected number of probes in an unsuccessful search, give that number to two decimal places.</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "3.67", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Under uniform hashing the probes are independent, so the expected number of slots examined before the first free one is the mean of a geometric distribution, <span class=\"mm\">1/(1 − α)</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">α = n/m = 8/11 ≈ 0.7273</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">1 − α = 3/11</span>, so the expected count is <span class=\"mm\">11/3 ≈ 3.6667</span>.</li><li>To two decimal places that is <span class=\"mm\">3.67</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3.67.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Rounding down to 3.66, or answering <span class=\"mm\">1/α ≈ 1.38</span> by dividing by the occupied fraction. The divisor is the fraction of free slots, and the exact value <span class=\"mm\">11/3</span> rounds up to 3.67.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q25", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "load-factor-and-rehashing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A table of 8 slots is rehashed to double its size as soon as an insertion would push the load factor above 0.75. The seventh key is the one that triggers the rehash. What is the load factor immediately after that rehash?</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">3/8</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">7/16</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">7/8</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">0.75</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The load factor after a rehash is the number of keys carried over divided by the new table size, so both the trigger rule and the growth factor matter.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>With <span class=\"mm\">m = 8</span> the threshold <span class=\"mm\">α ≤ 0.75</span> allows <span class=\"mm\">n = 6</span> keys (<span class=\"mm\">6/8 = 0.75</span>).</li><li>The seventh insertion would give <span class=\"mm\">7/8 = 0.875 &gt; 0.75</span>, so it triggers the rehash; the table doubles to <span class=\"mm\">m = 16</span>.</li><li>All 7 keys are reinserted, so <span class=\"mm\">α = 7/16 = 0.4375</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B), <span class=\"mm\">7/16</span>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting <span class=\"mm\">7/8</span>, the load factor that triggered the rehash, or <span class=\"mm\">3/8</span> by forgetting the key that caused it. The new key is carried into the new table like every other one.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q26", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "hash-functions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A hash function distributes 1000 distinct keys over a table of 100 slots. Whatever hash function is used, at least one slot must hold at least how many keys?</p>", "options": ["1", "10", "100", "1000"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The pigeonhole principle bounds the busiest slot from below: if <span class=\"mm\">n</span> keys occupy <span class=\"mm\">m</span> slots, some slot holds at least <span class=\"mm\">⌈n/m⌉</span> keys.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Here <span class=\"mm\">n = 1000</span> and <span class=\"mm\">m = 100</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">⌈1000/100⌉ = 10</span>.</li><li>So some slot holds 10 or more keys, whatever the hash function. A perfectly balanced function would put exactly 10 in every slot, which is the best case.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B), 10.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that a good hash function makes collisions impossible. With ten times as many keys as slots, collisions are forced; uniform hashing only makes them evenly spread, so the average chain stays short while the longest one cannot be made shorter than 10.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q27", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "hash-functions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which one of the following is <b>not</b> a property that a hash function is expected to have?</p>", "options": ["it distributes the keys roughly uniformly over the slots", "it is deterministic: the same key always maps to the same slot", "it maps distinct keys to distinct slots in every case", "it is cheap to compute, in time proportional to the length of the key"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A hash function compresses a large universe of keys into a small table, so by definition it cannot be injective. What is required is uniformity, determinism and cheap evaluation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Uniformity keeps the average chain and the average probe count small.</li><li>Determinism is what makes a stored key findable again.</li><li>Cheap evaluation keeps the <span class=\"mm\">Θ(1)</span> promise of a hash table.</li><li>Injectivity is impossible: with <span class=\"mm\">n > m</span> keys and <span class=\"mm\">m</span> slots two keys must share a slot, and even with <span class=\"mm\">n ≤ m</span> collisions are allowed and expected.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the name \"hash function\" as a one-to-one mapping. It is a many-to-one mapping, and the whole machinery of collision resolution exists because of that.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q28", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "hash-functions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A table of 13 slots uses the division method <span class=\"mm\">h(k) = k mod 13</span> with separate chaining. The keys 26, 39, 52, 65, 14 and 27 are inserted in that order. How many slots of the table are non-empty?</p>", "options": ["2", "3", "4", "6"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The division method sends a key to the slot equal to its remainder modulo <span class=\"mm\">m</span>. Keys that are multiples of <span class=\"mm\">m</span>, or that differ by a multiple of <span class=\"mm\">m</span>, all share one slot.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">26 = 13·2, 39 = 13·3, 52 = 13·4, 65 = 13·5</span>, so all four keys give remainder 0.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">14 mod 13 = 1</span> and <span class=\"mm\">27 mod 13 = 1</span>.</li><li>Only slots 0 and 1 are used, so two slots are non-empty.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A), 2 slots.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that six keys must occupy six slots, or that chaining \"spreads\" colliding keys over neighbouring slots. Chaining keeps the colliding keys in the one slot they hash to; it is linear probing that would spread them.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q29", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "hash-functions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The multiplication method uses <span class=\"mm\">h(k) = ⌊m(kA mod 1)⌋</span> with <span class=\"mm\">A = (√5 − 1)/2 ≈ 0.618034</span> and <span class=\"mm\">m = 1000</span>. What is <span class=\"mm\">h(1234)</span>?</p>", "options": ["653", "654", "762", "234"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The multiplication method multiplies the key by a constant in <span class=\"mm\">(0,1)</span>, keeps only the fractional part of the product, and scales that fractional part up to the table size.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">kA = 1234 × 0.618034 = 762.6539…</span>.</li><li>The fractional part is <span class=\"mm\">0.6539…</span>, that is <span class=\"mm\">kA mod 1</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">1000 × 0.6539… = 653.9…</span>.</li><li>Taking the floor gives <span class=\"mm\">h(1234) = 653</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A), 653.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 762, which is <span class=\"mm\">⌊kA⌋</span>, the integer part. The method deliberately discards the integer part: the whole point of multiplying by a constant is that the fractional part depends on every digit of the key.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q30", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "hash-functions", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about universal hashing are <b>true</b>? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["for a hash function drawn uniformly at random from a universal family, <span class=\"mm\">Pr[h(x) = h(y)] ≤ 1/m</span> for any two distinct keys <span class=\"mm\">x, y</span>", "a universal family guarantees that no two distinct keys ever collide", "the family <span class=\"mm\">h_{a,b}(k) = ((ak + b) mod p) mod m</span>, where <span class=\"mm\">p</span> is a prime larger than every key and <span class=\"mm\">a ≠ 0</span>, is universal", "the expected-cost guarantee is lost if an adversary may choose the keys <i>after</i> seeing the chosen hash function"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A family is universal when a randomly chosen member collides any fixed pair of distinct keys with probability at most <span class=\"mm\">1/m</span>. Randomness is what makes the <span class=\"mm\">Θ(1)</span> bound hold for <i>every</i> key set, not just for an average one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is the definition, so it is true.</li><li>(B) is false: the bound is a probability, not a prohibition. Two keys still collide with probability up to <span class=\"mm\">1/m</span>.</li><li>(C) is the standard construction: <span class=\"mm\">ak + b</span> with random <span class=\"mm\">a ≠ 0</span> and <span class=\"mm\">b</span> modulo a prime <span class=\"mm\">p > </span> every key, then reduced modulo <span class=\"mm\">m</span>, is universal.</li><li>(D) is true. The bound is taken over the random choice of <span class=\"mm\">h</span> <i>before</i> the keys are fixed; an adversary who sees <span class=\"mm\">h</span> first can pick a colliding pair and defeat any fixed function.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating universality as a guarantee of collision-freeness, or as a property of one function. It is a property of a <i>family</i>, and it is the random choice from the family that supplies the guarantee.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q31", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "hash-functions", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A hash function is drawn uniformly at random from a universal family into a table of <span class=\"mm\">m = 50</span> slots, and 101 distinct keys are hashed. What is the expected number of unordered pairs of keys that collide?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "101", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Linearity of expectation turns a count of colliding pairs into a sum of indicators, one per pair, each with probability at most <span class=\"mm\">1/m</span> under universality.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>There are <span class=\"mm\">C(101,2) = 101·100/2 = 5050</span> unordered pairs of keys.</li><li>Each pair collides with probability <span class=\"mm\">1/m = 1/50</span> for a randomly drawn universal function.</li><li>The expected number of colliding pairs is <span class=\"mm\">5050/50 = 101</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 101</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding <span class=\"mm\">1/m</span> once per key, which gives <span class=\"mm\">101/50 ≈ 2</span> and answers a different question. The unit here is the pair, and there are <span class=\"mm\">C(101,2)</span> of them; also note the answer is exactly <span class=\"mm\">n(n−1)/(2m) = 101</span> when <span class=\"mm\">n − 1 = 2m</span>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q32", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "collision-resolution-chaining", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A hash table with separate chaining holds <span class=\"mm\">n</span> keys. The worst-case time to search for a key that is <i>not</i> in the table is:</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">Θ(1)</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">Θ(1 + α)</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">Θ(n)</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">Θ(log n)</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With chaining the search cost is the length of one list. In the worst case every key hashes to the same slot, so one list holds all <span class=\"mm\">n</span> keys.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>An unsuccessful search scans the whole list of the slot that <span class=\"mm\">h(k)</span> names.</li><li>That list can hold up to <span class=\"mm\">n</span> keys.</li><li>Hence the worst case is <span class=\"mm\">Θ(n)</span>, independent of the load factor.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C), <span class=\"mm\">Θ(n)</span>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering <span class=\"mm\">Θ(1 + α)</span>, which is the <i>expected</i> cost under simple uniform hashing. The question asks for the worst case, where the hash function is as unhelpful as possible.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q33", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "collision-resolution-chaining", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which one of the following is <b>true</b> of separate chaining?</p>", "options": ["the load factor may exceed 1, because each slot holds a list rather than a single key", "the number of keys can never exceed the number of slots", "a key can be found only if every slot of the table is non-empty", "deleting a key requires a tombstone marker in its slot"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A chained slot stores a pointer to a list, so a slot can absorb any number of keys. The table never \"fills up\"; only the average chain length grows.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The load factor is <span class=\"mm\">α = n/m</span>, the average number of keys per slot.</li><li>Nothing in the structure caps a list, so <span class=\"mm\">α > 1</span> is perfectly ordinary.</li><li>Empty slots are normal: they simply mean no key hashes there.</li><li>Deletion unlinks a node from a list; tombstones belong to open addressing.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Carrying the open-addressing rule <span class=\"mm\">α < 1</span> across to chaining. Only a scheme that stores the key inside the table itself needs a free slot for every key.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q34", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "collision-resolution-chaining", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A chained table has <span class=\"mm\">m = 20</span> slots and <span class=\"mm\">n = 60</span> keys, with simple uniform hashing and load factor <span class=\"mm\">α = n/m</span>. Which of the following are <b>true</b>? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["an unsuccessful search examines about <span class=\"mm\">α = 3</span> keys on average, in addition to the slot access", "a successful search examines about <span class=\"mm\">1 + α/2 = 2.5</span> keys on average", "the expected time of either search is <span class=\"mm\">Θ(1 + α)</span>", "a search always examines at least one key in every slot of the table"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Under simple uniform hashing each key is equally likely to hash to any slot, so a list has <span class=\"mm\">α</span> keys on average. A successful search walks on average half of its list, because the target is equally likely to sit anywhere in it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">α = 60/20 = 3</span>.</li><li>Unsuccessful search: the whole list is scanned, about <span class=\"mm\">α = 3</span> keys.</li><li>Successful search: about <span class=\"mm\">1 + α/2 = 2.5</span> keys.</li><li>Both are <span class=\"mm\">Θ(1 + α)</span>, which is <span class=\"mm\">Θ(1)</span> whenever <span class=\"mm\">α</span> is held constant.</li><li>(D) is false: a search touches one slot and its list, not the whole table.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using <span class=\"mm\">α</span> for both searches. A successful search is cheaper by roughly a factor of two, and the difference matters when the formulas are compared across resolutions schemes.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q35", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "collision-resolution-chaining", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Deleting a key from a chained table is a local operation on one list. Which statement explains why the same deletion is awkward with open addressing?</p>", "options": ["open addressing stores the keys inside the table itself, so emptying a slot can cut the probe sequence of other keys short and make them unfindable, so a tombstone is used instead", "open addressing does not store the keys inside the table at all", "open addressing keeps the keys in sorted order, so a deletion has to shift many keys", "open addressing cannot hold duplicate keys, so the position of the deleted key is lost"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In open addressing a search stops at the first empty slot it meets. Every key is placed by following that same probe sequence, so an empty slot is a terminator that other keys rely on.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Key <span class=\"mm\">x</span> was placed at slot <span class=\"mm\">p</span> after probing past its home slot, because that home slot was occupied.</li><li>If the home slot is emptied, a later search for <span class=\"mm\">x</span> stops there and never reaches <span class=\"mm\">p</span>.</li><li>Marking the slot DELETED keeps the sequence alive while still allowing the slot to be reused.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a tombstone is a tidiness convention. It is a correctness requirement: without it, keys become unreachable, and the table silently loses entries.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q36", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "collision-resolution-chaining", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about separate chaining are <b>true</b>? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["if all <span class=\"mm\">n</span> keys hash to the same slot, an unsuccessful search takes <span class=\"mm\">Θ(n)</span> time", "increasing the number of slots always lowers the worst-case search time", "under simple uniform hashing with <span class=\"mm\">α</span> held constant, the expected cost of an unsuccessful search is <span class=\"mm\">Θ(1)</span>", "chaining spends extra memory on links but never needs tombstones"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Chaining separates the two things that decide search cost: the expected cost, which the load factor controls, and the worst case, which the hash function alone controls.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: one list of <span class=\"mm\">n</span> keys is scanned in full.</li><li>(B) is false: a hash function that sends every key to one slot defeats any table size, so the worst case stays <span class=\"mm\">Θ(n)</span>.</li><li>(C) is true: the expected cost is <span class=\"mm\">Θ(1 + α)</span>, and <span class=\"mm\">α</span> is a constant.</li><li>(D) is true: deletion unlinks a node, so no marker is ever needed.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that more slots cure a bad hash function. More slots lower the <i>expected</i> cost but leave the worst case untouched, because all keys can still land in one slot.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q37", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "collision-resolution-chaining", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A chained table of 5 slots uses <span class=\"mm\">h(k) = k mod 5</span>. The keys 3, 13, 23, 4, 14, 24 and 5 are inserted in that order, each at the tail of its chain. What is the length of the longest chain?</p>", "options": ["2", "3", "4", "7"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With chaining, colliding keys form one list per slot; the longest chain is the largest number of keys sharing a slot.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">3, 13, 23 mod 5 = 3</span>, so slot 3 receives three keys.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">4, 14, 24 mod 5 = 4</span>, so slot 4 receives three keys.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">5 mod 5 = 0</span>, so slot 0 receives one key.</li><li>The longest chain therefore has three keys.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B), 3.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 7, the total number of keys. The question asks for one chain, not the whole table, and insertion position inside a chain does not change its length.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q38", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "open-addressing-linear-quadratic-double", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A table of 10 slots uses <span class=\"mm\">h(k) = k mod 10</span> with linear probing. Slots 3 and 4 already hold the keys 13 and 24. The key 43 is inserted. How many slots are examined, counting the first examination of <span class=\"mm\">h(43)</span> as one probe?</p>", "options": ["1", "2", "3", "4"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Linear probing tries <span class=\"mm\">(h(k) + i) mod m</span> for <span class=\"mm\">i = 0, 1, 2, …</span> and stops at the first free slot; every slot it looks at is one probe.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">h(43) = 43 mod 10 = 3</span>: slot 3 is occupied by 13, one probe.</li><li>Slot 4 is occupied by 24, second probe.</li><li>Slot 5 is empty, third probe; 43 is placed in slot 5.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C), 3 probes.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting only the collisions (2) or only the slot finally used (1). The probe count includes the successful examination of the empty slot.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q39", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "open-addressing-linear-quadratic-double", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A table of 7 slots, numbered 0 to 6, starts empty and uses <span class=\"mm\">h(k) = k mod 7</span> with linear probing. The keys 8, 15, 1 and 22 are inserted in that order. In which slot does the key 22 end up?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Linear probing wraps around modulo <span class=\"mm\">m</span>, so the table behaves as a ring of slots.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">h(8) = 1</span>, slot 1 is free, so 8 goes to slot 1.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">h(15) = 1</span> is taken, slot 2 is free, so 15 goes to slot 2.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">h(1) = 1</span> is taken, slot 2 is taken, slot 3 is free, so 1 goes to slot 3.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">h(22) = 1</span> is taken, then 2 and 3 are taken, and slot 4 is free, so 22 goes to slot 4.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting that an earlier key may itself have been displaced, and reporting the home slot of the previous key instead of following the run of occupied slots to its end.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q40", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "open-addressing-linear-quadratic-double", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A table of 8 slots uses quadratic probing with the sequence <span class=\"mm\">(h(k) + i²) mod 8</span> for <span class=\"mm\">i = 0, 1, 2, …</span>. As <span class=\"mm\">i</span> runs from 0 to 7, how many distinct slots does the sequence visit?</p>", "options": ["2", "3", "4", "8"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The set of offsets <span class=\"mm\">i² mod m</span> is much smaller than <span class=\"mm\">m</span> when <span class=\"mm\">m</span> is not prime. If the offsets repeat, the probe sequence revisits slots and can cycle without ever finding a free one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">i²</span> for <span class=\"mm\">i = 0…7</span> is <span class=\"mm\">0, 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49</span>.</li><li>Modulo 8 these are <span class=\"mm\">0, 1, 4, 1, 0, 1, 4, 1</span>.</li><li>The distinct offsets are <span class=\"mm\">{0, 1, 4}</span>, three of them.</li><li>So the sequence can reach only three slots, whatever the home slot; with <span class=\"mm\">m = 8</span> a probe can cycle among them.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B), 3 distinct slots.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing quadratic probing always covers the whole table. That needs <span class=\"mm\">m</span> prime, and even then only about half the slots are guaranteed to be reachable.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q41", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "open-addressing-linear-quadratic-double", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A table of 11 slots starts empty and uses linear probing with <span class=\"mm\">h(k) = k mod 11</span>. The keys 11, 22, 33 and 0 are inserted in that order. What is the total number of probes made by the four insertions, counting the first examination of <span class=\"mm\">h(k)</span> as one probe?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "10", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The <span class=\"mm\">i</span>-th key of a run of colliding keys needs <span class=\"mm\">i + 1</span> probes, because every earlier key of the run already occupies one slot ahead of the home slot.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">11 mod 11 = 0</span>, slot 0 free: 1 probe.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">22 mod 11 = 0</span>, slot 0 taken, slot 1 free: 2 probes.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">33 mod 11 = 0</span>, slots 0 and 1 taken, slot 2 free: 3 probes.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">0 mod 11 = 0</span>, slots 0, 1, 2 taken, slot 3 free: 4 probes.</li><li>Total <span class=\"mm\">1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 10</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting only the successful placements (4) or only the collisions (0 + 1 + 2 + 3 = 6). The probe count is the number of slots examined, which is the collision count plus one per insertion.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q42", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "open-addressing-linear-quadratic-double", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">When a key is deleted from a table that uses open addressing with linear probing, the slot is normally marked DELETED (a tombstone) rather than left empty. Why?</p>", "options": ["an empty slot would end the probe sequence of keys inserted after it, so those keys could no longer be found", "a tombstone makes later insertions cheaper", "an empty slot would break the load-factor invariant", "a tombstone keeps the keys of a slot in sorted order"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A search under open addressing stops at the first empty slot. An empty slot therefore acts as a terminator for every probe sequence that passes through it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A key <span class=\"mm\">y</span> may sit several slots past its home slot because of earlier collisions.</li><li>Emptying an intermediate slot makes a search for <span class=\"mm\">y</span> stop before reaching it.</li><li>A DELETED marker lets the search continue while still allowing the slot to be reused.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking a tombstone speeds up insertion. It is a correctness device; in fact many tombstones slow searches down, which is why the table is rehashed when they accumulate.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q43", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "open-addressing-linear-quadratic-double", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about open addressing are <b>true</b>? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["with uniform hashing the expected number of probes in an unsuccessful search is at most <span class=\"mm\">1/(1 − α)</span>", "with linear probing the expected number of probes in an unsuccessful search is about <span class=\"mm\">½(1 + 1/(1 − α)²)</span>", "a table with open addressing can hold more keys than it has slots, provided <span class=\"mm\">α</span> stays small", "under linear probing, keys whose home slots coincide form a cluster, and the cluster slows down later insertions and searches"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Open addressing keeps every key inside the table, so it can hold at most <span class=\"mm\">m</span> keys. Uniform hashing gives the idealised probe bound; linear probing pays an extra quadratic penalty because of clustering.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is the classical bound for uniform hashing: at most <span class=\"mm\">1/(1 − α)</span> probes.</li><li>(B) is the linear-probing bound from Knuth: about <span class=\"mm\">½(1 + 1/(1 − α)²)</span> probes, which is much worse as <span class=\"mm\">α</span> approaches 1.</li><li>(C) is false: the table has <span class=\"mm\">m</span> slots and holds at most <span class=\"mm\">m</span> keys, so <span class=\"mm\">α ≤ 1</span> always.</li><li>(D) is true: a run of occupied slots grows at its ends, and every key hashing into the run must probe past all of it.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reusing the chaining intuition that <span class=\"mm\">α</span> may exceed 1. Only a scheme that stores keys outside the table can carry more keys than slots.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q44", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "open-addressing-linear-quadratic-double", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A table of size <span class=\"mm\">m</span>, where <span class=\"mm\">m</span> is prime, uses quadratic probing with the sequence <span class=\"mm\">(h(k) + i²) mod m</span>. Which one of the following is <b>true</b>?</p>", "options": ["the sequence visits every slot exactly once, so a key can be inserted even when <span class=\"mm\">α</span> is close to 1", "the sequence visits distinct slots for <span class=\"mm\">i = 0, 1, …, ⌊m/2⌋</span>, so an insertion is guaranteed to succeed while <span class=\"mm\">α ≤ ½</span>", "quadratic probing removes clustering completely", "quadratic probing needs a second hash function to compute the step size"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> For prime <span class=\"mm\">m</span> the offsets <span class=\"mm\">i² mod m</span> are distinct for <span class=\"mm\">i = 0, …, ⌊m/2⌋</span>, so half the table is reachable from any home slot. That is why the table is kept at most half full.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>If <span class=\"mm\">i² ≡ j² (mod m)</span> with <span class=\"mm\">i ≠ j</span> then <span class=\"mm\">(i − j)(i + j) ≡ 0 (mod m)</span>; since <span class=\"mm\">m</span> is prime and <span class=\"mm\">i, j ≤ ⌊m/2⌋</span>, this is impossible unless <span class=\"mm\">i = j</span>.</li><li>So the first <span class=\"mm\">⌊m/2⌋ + 1</span> probes are distinct, and one of them is free while at most half the table is occupied.</li><li>Beyond half full the sequence repeats and an insertion may fail although free slots exist.</li><li>(C) and (D) are wrong: quadratic probing still suffers secondary clustering, and the step is <span class=\"mm\">i²</span>, not a second hash function.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the probe sequence always covers the table, as linear probing does. Quadratic probing can loop, and even with prime <span class=\"mm\">m</span> it only guarantees half the table.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q45", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "load-factor-and-rehashing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A table of 17 slots holds 12 keys. What is its load factor, to two decimal places?</p>", "options": ["0.71", "1.42", "0.59", "12.00"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The load factor is the number of stored keys divided by the number of slots, <span class=\"mm\">α = n/m</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">n = 12</span>, <span class=\"mm\">m = 17</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">α = 12/17 = 0.70588…</span>.</li><li>Rounded to two decimals, <span class=\"mm\">α = 0.71</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A), 0.71.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Inverting the ratio and reporting <span class=\"mm\">17/12 = 1.42</span>, which is slots per key rather than keys per slot.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q46", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "load-factor-and-rehashing", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">An open-addressing table has 16 slots. How many keys may it hold before its load factor reaches 0.75?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "12", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The load factor is <span class=\"mm\">α = n/m</span>, so the key count that produces a given <span class=\"mm\">α</span> is <span class=\"mm\">n = αm</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">m = 16</span> and the target <span class=\"mm\">α = 0.75</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">n = 0.75 × 16 = 12</span>.</li><li>At 12 keys the load factor is exactly <span class=\"mm\">12/16 = 0.75</span>; the thirteenth key would exceed it.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 12</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 13, on the reading that the table must stay <i>below</i> 0.75. The table reaches 0.75 at 12 keys; the threshold is breached only by the next insertion.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q47", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "load-factor-and-rehashing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A table doubles its size and rehashes every key whenever an insertion would push the load factor past a fixed threshold. Over a long sequence of insertions, the amortised cost of one insertion is:</p>", "options": ["<span class=\"mm\">Θ(1)</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">Θ(log n)</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">Θ(n)</span>", "<span class=\"mm\">Θ(n log n)</span>"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Doubling makes the expensive rehashes rare: the total work of all rehashes up to <span class=\"mm\">n</span> insertions is proportional to <span class=\"mm\">n</span>, so it spreads to a constant per insertion.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Rehashing <span class=\"mm\">k</span> keys costs <span class=\"mm\">Θ(k)</span>.</li><li>The rehashes move <span class=\"mm\">1 + 2 + 4 + … + n/2 < n</span> keys in total.</li><li>Dividing that total by <span class=\"mm\">n</span> insertions gives a constant.</li><li>So the amortised cost is <span class=\"mm\">Θ(1)</span>, even though one individual insertion can cost <span class=\"mm\">Θ(n)</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A), <span class=\"mm\">Θ(1)</span>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering <span class=\"mm\">Θ(n)</span> from the cost of the single worst insertion. Amortised analysis averages the total work over the sequence, and geometric growth is what keeps that average constant.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q48", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "load-factor-and-rehashing", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">An open-addressing table starts with 1 slot and doubles its size, rehashing every key, whenever an insertion would push the load factor above 1. How many rehashes take place while 100 keys are inserted one at a time into the initially empty table?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "7", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With a doubling policy the table size follows the powers of two, and a rehash happens exactly when the next key would not fit, that is when the table already holds <span class=\"mm\">m</span> keys.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The table grows <span class=\"mm\">1 → 2 → 4 → 8 → 16 → 32 → 64 → 128</span>.</li><li>A rehash is triggered by the insertions that find the table full: keys 2, 3, 5, 9, 17, 33 and 65.</li><li>That is seven rehashes, and the table reaches 128 slots, which holds all 100 keys.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 7</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting the final size 128 as the number of rehashes, or assuming one rehash per doubling of the key count. The rehash count is <span class=\"mm\">⌈log₂ n⌉</span> for this policy, not <span class=\"mm\">n</span> and not <span class=\"mm\">n/2</span>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q49", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "load-factor-and-rehashing", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about rehashing are <b>true</b>? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["rehashing computes a fresh slot for every key with the new table size, and the old table is then discarded", "when the table doubles, the load factor is halved before the next insertion", "rehashing is needed only with chaining, because an open-addressing table never fills up", "with doubling, the total work done by all the rehashes over <span class=\"mm\">n</span> insertions is <span class=\"mm\">Θ(n)</span>, so the amortised cost of an insertion stays <span class=\"mm\">Θ(1)</span>"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Rehashing rebuilds the table at a new size. Because the slot of a key depends on the table size, every key must be re-inserted; the cost is repaid by geometric growth.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: with <span class=\"mm\">h(k) = k mod m</span> changing <span class=\"mm\">m</span> changes the slot of almost every key.</li><li>(B) is true: <span class=\"mm\">α = n/m</span> becomes <span class=\"mm\">n/2m = α/2</span> when <span class=\"mm\">n</span> is unchanged and <span class=\"mm\">m</span> doubles.</li><li>(C) is false: an open-addressing table fills up at <span class=\"mm\">α = 1</span> and is exactly the structure that must be rehashed.</li><li>(D) is true: the moved keys sum to less than <span class=\"mm\">2n</span>, so the amortised cost is constant.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Supposing that rehashing only copies the keys into free slots of the old layout. The slot function itself changes with <span class=\"mm\">m</span>, so the keys must be rehashed from scratch.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U7-Q50", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 7, "unit_slug": "hashing", "subtopic": "load-factor-and-rehashing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A table of size <span class=\"mm\">m</span> uses linear probing and has load factor <span class=\"mm\">α = 0.8</span>. The expected number of probes in an unsuccessful search is about:</p>", "options": ["2.5", "5", "13", "25"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> For linear probing Knuth's bound for an unsuccessful search is <span class=\"mm\">½(1 + 1/(1 − α)²)</span> probes, against <span class=\"mm\">1/(1 − α)</span> for the idealised uniform hashing.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">1 − α = 0.2</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">1/(1 − α)² = 1/0.04 = 25</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">½(1 + 25) = 13</span> probes.</li><li>Uniform hashing would give <span class=\"mm\">1/0.2 = 5</span> probes, so clustering costs more than a factor of two here.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C), about 13 probes.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using the uniform-hashing formula <span class=\"mm\">1/(1 − α) = 5</span>. That bound assumes every probe is independent, which linear probing violates precisely because of clustering.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q51", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "data-types-operators", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    char c = &#x27;A&#x27;;\n    printf(&quot;%d %c\\n&quot;, c + 1, c + 1);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["66 B", "65 B", "66 A", "66 66"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A <code class=\"mm\">char</code> operand of an arithmetic expression is promoted to <code class=\"mm\">int</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">c + 1</code> is the integer 66. The conversion specification decides how that integer is printed: <code class=\"mm\">%d</code> prints its decimal value, <code class=\"mm\">%c</code> prints the character whose code is that value.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">'A'</code> has code 65 on every ASCII or UTF-8 machine, so <code class=\"mm\">c</code> holds 65.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">c + 1</code> is evaluated twice, each time as the integer 66.</li><li>The first <code class=\"mm\">%d</code> prints <code class=\"mm\">66</code>; the second, <code class=\"mm\">%c</code>, prints the character with code 66, which is <code class=\"mm\">'B'</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A), 66 B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading <code class=\"mm\">c + 1</code> as a character increment, so that both fields print <code class=\"mm\">B</code>. The <i>value</i> is the integer 66; only the format string turns it into a character, and <code class=\"mm\">%d</code> asks for the number.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q52", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "data-types-operators", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">On a machine where <code class=\"mm\">int</code> is 32 bits, what does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int i = -1;\n    unsigned int u = 1;\n    if (i &lt; u) printf(&quot;less\\n&quot;);\n    else       printf(&quot;not less\\n&quot;);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["less", "not less", "it prints nothing", "the behaviour is undefined"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> When the operands of a comparison have the same rank but different signedness, the usual arithmetic conversions convert the <i>signed</i> operand to unsigned. The result is well defined — it is not undefined behaviour — but it is almost never what the programmer expects.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">i</code> and <code class=\"mm\">u</code> are both 32-bit, so they have equal rank and the <code class=\"mm\">int</code> operand is converted to <code class=\"mm\">unsigned int</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">-1</code> converts modulo 2³² to <code class=\"mm\">4294967295</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">4294967295 &lt; 1</code> is false, so the <code class=\"mm\">else</code> branch runs and the program prints <code class=\"mm\">not less</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B), not less.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting <code class=\"mm\">less</code> because −1 is arithmetically smaller than 1. Signedness is converted first and arithmetic comes second; casting the comparison to <code class=\"mm\">if (i &lt; (int)u)</code> restores the mathematical reading.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q53", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "data-types-operators", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What value does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int x = 0x2A;\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, x ^ 0xFF);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "213", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Bitwise exclusive-or sets a bit exactly when the two operands differ, so XOR with <code class=\"mm\">0xFF</code> complements the low eight bits of the operand and leaves every higher bit untouched.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">0x2A = 0010 1010₂ = 42</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">0xFF = 1111 1111₂ = 255</code>.</li><li>XOR bit by bit gives <code class=\"mm\">1101 0101₂ = 0xD5 = 213</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 213.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating <code class=\"mm\">0xFF</code> as a decimal literal and computing <code class=\"mm\">42 ^ 255</code> as a subtraction, giving <code class=\"mm\">-213</code>. XOR is not subtraction: 42 and 255 have no bit in common where both are 1, so every bit of the low byte flips.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q54", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "data-types-operators", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about the usual arithmetic conversions in C are <b>true</b>? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["when an int and a long meet in an arithmetic expression, the int operand is converted to long before the operation is performed", "a char operand of an arithmetic expression is first promoted to int", "assigning a double value to an int variable rounds the value to the nearest integer", "when an int and an unsigned int of the same rank are compared, the int operand is converted to unsigned"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The conversions follow a rank ladder: a narrower operand is widened to the type of the wider one, and when the ranks are equal the signed operand gives way to the unsigned one. Conversion <i>to</i> a narrower type at an assignment is a separate rule with its own truncation behaviour.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: <code class=\"mm\">long</code> outranks <code class=\"mm\">int</code>, so the <code class=\"mm\">int</code> operand is converted to <code class=\"mm\">long</code> and the whole operation is done in <code class=\"mm\">long</code>.</li><li>(B) is true: the integer promotions lift every type narrower than <code class=\"mm\">int</code> — <code class=\"mm\">char</code>, <code class=\"mm\">short</code>, bit-fields — to <code class=\"mm\">int</code> (or to <code class=\"mm\">unsigned int</code> if <code class=\"mm\">int</code> cannot hold all its values).</li><li>(C) is false: conversion of a floating value to an integer type <i>truncates towards zero</i>. <code class=\"mm\">int x = 3.9;</code> stores 3, and <code class=\"mm\">int y = -3.9;</code> stores −3.</li><li>(D) is true: with equal ranks the unsigned type wins, which is exactly the trap that makes <code class=\"mm\">-1 &lt; 1u</code> false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing the assignment rounds. Rounding is what <code class=\"mm\">round()</code> or <code class=\"mm\">lround()</code> do; the language conversion discards the fraction, so a loop like <code class=\"mm\">for (double d = 0; d &lt; 3; d += 0.9)</code> that stores <code class=\"mm\">(int)d</code> repeats values instead of advancing smoothly.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q55", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "data-types-operators", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print, and why?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    float  f = 0.1f;\n    double d = 0.1;\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, f == d);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["0 — 0.1f is stored as the nearest float, 0.100000001490116…, and promoting it to double cannot recover 0.1", "1 — both constants denote the same decimal number 0.1", "0 — the double operand is converted to float and its extra bits are lost", "the program does not compile, because a float and a double cannot be compared"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A binary floating-point type can represent only a finite set of values, and 0.1 is not one of them in either base-2 format. The two constants are rounded to their own formats, and the comparison then follows the usual arithmetic conversions, which promote the <code class=\"mm\">float</code> operand <i>up</i> to <code class=\"mm\">double</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">0.1f</code> is rounded to the nearest 24-bit significand: <code class=\"mm\">0.100000001490116119384765625</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">0.1</code> is rounded to the nearest 53-bit significand: <code class=\"mm\">0.1000000000000000055511151231257827</code>.</li><li>The conversion promotes <code class=\"mm\">f</code> to <code class=\"mm\">double</code> <i>exactly</i>, keeping its already-rounded value, so the two doubles differ by about <code class=\"mm\">1.5·10⁻⁹</code>.</li><li>The comparison is therefore false and <code class=\"mm\">0</code> is printed.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A), it prints 0.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (C) states the right verdict with the wrong mechanism: nothing is converted down to <code class=\"mm\">float</code>. In the usual arithmetic conversions the operand of higher rank is the target, so <code class=\"mm\">float</code> widens to <code class=\"mm\">double</code> and the double value is untouched. Equality between floating-point values should be tested with a tolerance, not with <code class=\"mm\">==</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q56", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "data-types-operators", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(&quot;%d %d\\n&quot;, &#x27;5&#x27; - &#x27;0&#x27;, (int)&#x27;5&#x27;);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["5 53", "5 5", "53 5", "5 0"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A character constant has type <code class=\"mm\">int</code> in C and its value is the character's code, so <code class=\"mm\">'5'</code> is the number 53, not the number 5. Subtracting <code class=\"mm\">'0'</code> (48) is the standard way to recover the digit's value.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">'5'</code> has code 53 and <code class=\"mm\">'0'</code> has code 48.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">53 - 48 = 5</code>, printed by the first <code class=\"mm\">%d</code>.</li><li>The cast <code class=\"mm\">(int)'5'</code> changes nothing, since the constant already has type <code class=\"mm\">int</code>; the second <code class=\"mm\">%d</code> prints 53.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A), 5 53.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming <code class=\"mm\">'5'</code> has the value 5 because it looks like the digit. Only the digits' <i>differences</i> are guaranteed to be contiguous in the execution character set; the individual codes are not portable, which is exactly why the <code class=\"mm\">- '0'</code> idiom is used.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q57", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "data-types-operators", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about C operators are <b>true</b>? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["in a = b = 5 the assignment operator associates from right to left, so 5 is stored in b first", "the expression i++ + ++i has a well-defined value in C", "the % operator cannot be applied to float or double operands", "sizeof is a library function that is called at run time"], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Three separate facts are being tested: the associativity of assignment, the sequencing rules that make an expression with two unsequenced modifications undefined, the operand types <code class=\"mm\">%</code> accepts, and the fact that <code class=\"mm\">sizeof</code> is an operator whose result is a compile-time constant.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: assignment is right-associative, so <code class=\"mm\">a = b = 5</code> parses as <code class=\"mm\">a = (b = 5)</code>. The value of <code class=\"mm\">b = 5</code> is 5, which is then assigned to <code class=\"mm\">a</code>.</li><li>(B) is false: <code class=\"mm\">i</code> is modified twice with no intervening sequence point, so the behaviour is undefined — the compiler may produce any value.</li><li>(C) is true: <code class=\"mm\">%</code> requires integer operands; for floating-point remainders the library function <code class=\"mm\">fmod()</code> is used instead.</li><li>(D) is false: <code class=\"mm\">sizeof</code> is an operator, not a function, and its result does not require the operand to be evaluated.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading (B) as \"the compiler evaluates left to right, so it is fine\". Order of evaluation is unspecified and, for two unsequenced modifications of the same object, the behaviour is undefined rather than merely unpredictable. The same rule forbids <code class=\"mm\">a[i] = i++;</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q58", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "control-structures", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int i = 0, s = 0;\n    while (i &lt; 10) {\n        i++;\n        if (i % 3 == 0) continue;\n        s += i;\n    }\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, s);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["55", "37", "18", "45"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">continue</code> abandons the rest of the current iteration and jumps to the loop condition. It does not end the loop, and it does not undo anything already done — here the increment has already happened, so the skipped values are exactly the multiples of three.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The loop runs for <code class=\"mm\">i = 1 … 10</code>, so the unreduced total is <code class=\"mm\">55</code>.</li><li>The multiples of 3 in that range are 3, 6 and 9, whose sum is <code class=\"mm\">18</code>.</li><li>Those three iterations reach the <code class=\"mm\">continue</code> before <code class=\"mm\">s += i</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">s = 55 − 18 = 37</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B), 37.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Putting the increment <i>after</i> the <code class=\"mm\">continue</code> in the loop body. Then <code class=\"mm\">i</code> would never advance on the multiples of three and the loop would spin forever — the reason the increment is placed before the test in this program.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q59", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "control-structures", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int i, j, c = 0;\n    for (i = 1; i &lt;= 3; i++)\n        for (j = i; j &lt;= 3; j++)\n            if ((i + j) % 2 == 0) c++;\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, c);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["4", "3", "6", "5"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The inner loop starts at <code class=\"mm\">j = i</code>, not at 1, so the program visits only the pairs on and above the diagonal of a 3×3 grid — six pairs in all. <code class=\"mm\">(i + j)</code> is even exactly when <code class=\"mm\">i</code> and <code class=\"mm\">j</code> have the same parity.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">i = 1</code>: <code class=\"mm\">j = 1, 2, 3</code> gives sums 2, 3, 4 — even for <code class=\"mm\">j = 1</code> and <code class=\"mm\">j = 3</code>, so 2 hits.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">i = 2</code>: <code class=\"mm\">j = 2, 3</code> gives sums 4, 5 — one hit.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">i = 3</code>: <code class=\"mm\">j = 3</code> gives sum 6 — one hit.</li><li>Total <code class=\"mm\">2 + 1 + 1 = 4</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A), 4.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting all nine pairs and halving, which gives 4 by accident on a 3×3 grid but is wrong in general: the diagonal pairs are counted once and the off-diagonal ones are not visited at all. Trace the bounds of the inner loop before reasoning about parity.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q60", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "control-structures", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">How many times is the body of this loop executed?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int i, c = 0;\n    for (i = 0; i &lt; 100; i += 3) c++;\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, c);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "34", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A loop whose counter advances by a fixed step <code class=\"mm\">s</code> from <code class=\"mm\">a</code> while staying below <code class=\"mm\">n</code> runs <code class=\"mm\">⌈(n − a)/s⌉</code> times — equivalently, the number of multiples of <code class=\"mm\">s</code> in <code class=\"mm\">[a, n − 1]</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The values taken are <code class=\"mm\">0, 3, 6, …, 99</code>, since 102 is not below 100.</li><li>Write <code class=\"mm\">3k = 99</code>, so the largest <code class=\"mm\">k</code> is 33.</li><li>The count is <code class=\"mm\">k + 1 = 34</code>, one for each of <code class=\"mm\">k = 0 … 33</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 34.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Dividing 100 by 3 and rounding to the nearest integer, which gives 33. The last multiple strictly below the bound is 99, and 0 counts as an iteration, so the off-by-one is two-sided: the correct value is <code class=\"mm\">⌊99/3⌋ + 1 = 34</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q61", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "control-structures", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about C control statements are <b>true</b>? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["the case labels of a switch must be integer constant expressions", "a switch statement must contain a default label", "continue inside a for loop executes the loop's update expression before the next test", "a break inside a switch that is nested in a loop leaves the loop"], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">switch</code> is a computed jump into a block of labelled statements, so its labels must be known when the program is translated. <code class=\"mm\">continue</code> belongs to the loop, and in a <code class=\"mm\">for</code> loop it performs the update step. <code class=\"mm\">break</code> belongs to the innermost enclosing <code class=\"mm\">switch</code> or loop, whichever comes first.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: each <code class=\"mm\">case</code> is followed by an integer constant expression, and no two labels in one <code class=\"mm\">switch</code> may have the same value.</li><li>(B) is false: <code class=\"mm\">default</code> is optional. With no matching label and no <code class=\"mm\">default</code>, the whole <code class=\"mm\">switch</code> body is skipped.</li><li>(C) is true: <code class=\"mm\">continue</code> in a <code class=\"mm\">for</code> loop jumps to the update expression, then the condition — unlike a <code class=\"mm\">while</code> loop, where it jumps straight to the condition.</li><li>(D) is false: <code class=\"mm\">break</code> terminates only the innermost enclosing <code class=\"mm\">switch</code>; the surrounding loop continues. Leaving the loop needs a flag, a <code class=\"mm\">goto</code>, or a <code class=\"mm\">return</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing <code class=\"mm\">break</code> exits \"the block\", so a <code class=\"mm\">break</code> inside a <code class=\"mm\">switch</code> inside a <code class=\"mm\">for</code> would leave the loop. That is the classic cause of a search loop that keeps running after the match has been found.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q62", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "control-structures", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int i = 0;\n    if (i++ || i++)\n        printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, i);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["0", "1", "2", "3"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">||</code> short-circuits: the right operand is evaluated only when the left one is false. Each <code class=\"mm\">i++</code> yields the old value and then increments, so the two postfix increments produce 0 and 1 and leave <code class=\"mm\">i = 2</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The left <code class=\"mm\">i++</code> yields 0 — false — and <code class=\"mm\">i</code> becomes 1. Because the left operand is false, the right operand <i>is</i> evaluated.</li><li>The right <code class=\"mm\">i++</code> yields 1 — true — and <code class=\"mm\">i</code> becomes 2.</li><li>The condition is true, so <code class=\"mm\">printf</code> runs and prints the current <code class=\"mm\">i</code>, which is 2.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C), 2.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 1, from the value the second operand produced. The operand <i>value</i> is 1, but the program prints the variable after both increments. Note also that this program is well defined: <code class=\"mm\">||</code> sequences its operands, so the two modifications of <code class=\"mm\">i</code> do not overlap.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q63", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "control-structures", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int i, j, s = 0;\n    for (i = 1; i &lt;= 4; i++) {\n        for (j = 1; j &lt;= 4; j++) {\n            if (i == j)     continue;\n            if (i + j &gt; 5)  break;\n            s += i * j;\n        }\n    }\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, s);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["30", "36", "26", "24"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">continue</code> skips one iteration of the inner loop, while <code class=\"mm\">break</code> abandons the whole inner loop and returns to the outer one. Because <code class=\"mm\">j</code> increases, once <code class=\"mm\">i + j</code> exceeds 5 every later <code class=\"mm\">j</code> would too, so <code class=\"mm\">break</code> ends that row.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">i = 1</code>: <code class=\"mm\">j = 1</code> is skipped (diagonal); <code class=\"mm\">j = 2 → 2</code>, <code class=\"mm\">j = 3 → 3</code>, <code class=\"mm\">j = 4</code> breaks (5 is not &gt; 5, so 4 is added). Sum so far 2 + 3 + 4 = 9.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">i = 2</code>: <code class=\"mm\">j = 1 → 2</code>, <code class=\"mm\">j = 2</code> skipped, <code class=\"mm\">j = 3 → 6</code>, <code class=\"mm\">j = 4</code> breaks. Sum so far 9 + 8 = 17.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">i = 3</code>: <code class=\"mm\">j = 1 → 3</code>, <code class=\"mm\">j = 2 → 6</code>, <code class=\"mm\">j = 3</code> skipped, <code class=\"mm\">j = 4</code> breaks. Sum so far 17 + 9 = 26.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">i = 4</code>: <code class=\"mm\">j = 1 → 4</code>, <code class=\"mm\">j = 2</code> breaks. Total <code class=\"mm\">26 + 4 = 30</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A), 30.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating <code class=\"mm\">break</code> as if it left both loops, which would stop after the first row and give 9. Only the inner loop is abandoned; the outer loop continues with the next <code class=\"mm\">i</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q64", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "functions-and-parameter-passing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nvoid f(int a[]) { a[0] = 99; }\n\nint main(void) {\n    int x[3] = {1, 2, 3};\n    f(x);\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, x[0]);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["1", "99", "0", "the program does not compile"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> C has only call by value, but an array argument is not passed as a whole: the array name decays to a pointer to its first element, so the <i>value</i> copied into the callee is that address. Writing through the pointer therefore changes the caller's array.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The parameter <code class=\"mm\">int a[]</code> is adjusted by the compiler to <code class=\"mm\">int *a</code>.</li><li>The call <code class=\"mm\">f(x)</code> passes the address of <code class=\"mm\">x[0]</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">a[0] = 99</code> stores 99 at that address, which is <code class=\"mm\">x[0]</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B), 99.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Concluding that C must have call by reference because the array changed. Nothing was passed by reference: a pointer was passed by value, and the pointee is shared. The distinction matters — reassigning the parameter itself, <code class=\"mm\">a = other;</code>, is invisible to the caller.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q65", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "functions-and-parameter-passing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">On a machine where a pointer occupies 8 bytes and an <code class=\"mm\">int</code> occupies 4 bytes, what does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nvoid f(int a[10]) {\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, (int)(sizeof(a) / sizeof(a[0])));\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int x[10];\n    f(x);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["10", "2", "8", "40"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Inside the callee the parameter has already been adjusted to a pointer, so <code class=\"mm\">sizeof</code> measures the pointer, not the caller's array. The array size is simply not available to the callee.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The declaration <code class=\"mm\">int a[10]</code> as a parameter means <code class=\"mm\">int *a</code>; the 10 is ignored.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">sizeof(a)</code> is the size of a pointer: 8 bytes.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">sizeof(a[0])</code> is the size of an <code class=\"mm\">int</code>: 4 bytes.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">8 / 4 = 2</code>, so the program prints 2.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B), 2.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting 10, because <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(x)</code> in <code class=\"mm\">main</code> would indeed be 40 and <code class=\"mm\">40 / 4</code> is 10. The correct idiom is to pass the length as a second argument — which is exactly why the C library functions take a count alongside the pointer.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q66", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "functions-and-parameter-passing", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What numeric value does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\ndouble half(int n) { return n / 2; }\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(&quot;%.1f\\n&quot;, half(7));\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The type of the <i>operands</i> decides how a division is performed; the type of the <i>result</i> only decides what happens afterwards. Both operands of <code class=\"mm\">n / 2</code> are <code class=\"mm\">int</code>, so integer division is performed and the conversion to <code class=\"mm\">double</code> happens when the value is returned.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">7 / 2</code> with two <code class=\"mm\">int</code> operands truncates to 3.</li><li>The <code class=\"mm\">return</code> converts 3 to <code class=\"mm\">3.0</code> for the <code class=\"mm\">double</code> return type.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">%.1f</code> prints <code class=\"mm\">3.0</code>, so the numeric value is 3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3 (printed as 3.0).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the <code class=\"mm\">double</code> return type makes the division floating-point, so the answer would be 3.5. The return type is not an operand; writing <code class=\"mm\">return n / 2.0;</code> or <code class=\"mm\">return (double)n / 2;</code> is what changes the arithmetic.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q67", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "functions-and-parameter-passing", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about passing arguments in C are <b>true</b>? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["every argument in C is passed by value, so a callee can change a caller's object only through a pointer the caller supplies", "an array name used as an argument is converted to a pointer to its first element", "a function may return an array by value", "a function defined as int f(void) may be called with two int arguments"], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> C has one argument-passing mechanism, call by value. Sharing is achieved by passing an address, which is itself a value. The language has no array values as arguments or return values — an array name in an expression is converted to a pointer.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: the callee receives a copy. Mutating the copy is invisible to the caller; mutating through a copy of an address is not.</li><li>(B) is true: this conversion, called array-to-pointer decay, is why <code class=\"mm\">sizeof</code> inside the callee measures a pointer.</li><li>(C) is false: a function cannot return an array. It can return a pointer to a static or heap-allocated array, or wrap the array in a <code class=\"mm\">struct</code> and return that.</li><li>(D) is false: in a prototype the <code class=\"mm\">void</code> parameter list means \"no parameters\", so a call with two arguments is a constraint violation that a conforming compiler must diagnose.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Calling C \"call by reference\" for pointers. The pointer is copied; only the pointee is shared. That is why <code class=\"mm\">void swap(int *a, int *b)</code> works while <code class=\"mm\">void swap(int a, int b)</code> does not — and why an old-style <code class=\"mm\">int f()</code> with an empty list, which means \"unspecified parameters\", behaves differently from <code class=\"mm\">int f(void)</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q68", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "functions-and-parameter-passing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, add(add(1, 2), add(3, 4)));\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["10", "7", "6", "12"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each argument expression is fully evaluated to a value before it is passed, so nested calls are reduced innermost-first. The order in which the two sibling calls run is unspecified, but here neither has side effects, so the result is the same either way.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">add(1, 2)</code> returns 3.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">add(3, 4)</code> returns 7.</li><li>The outer call is <code class=\"mm\">add(3, 7)</code>, which returns 10.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A), 10.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Supposing the two inner calls share state and give 12 or 6. <code class=\"mm\">add</code> is pure: its result depends only on its arguments, so the nesting is plain substitution.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q69", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "functions-and-parameter-passing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint g = 5;\n\nvoid f(int g) {\n    g = g + 1;\n    printf(&quot;%d &quot;, g);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    f(g);\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, g);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["6 5", "6 6", "5 5", "5 6"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two independent ideas meet here: a parameter shadows a file-scope variable of the same name inside the function body, and the argument is a <i>copy</i> of the caller's value, so the file-scope <code class=\"mm\">g</code> is never touched.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">main</code> calls <code class=\"mm\">f(g)</code>, passing the value 5.</li><li>Inside <code class=\"mm\">f</code>, the parameter <code class=\"mm\">g</code> hides the global; <code class=\"mm\">g = g + 1</code> changes only the parameter, and 6 is printed.</li><li>On return the global <code class=\"mm\">g</code> is still 5, and <code class=\"mm\">main</code> prints 5.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A), 6 5.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking the parameter <i>is</i> the global because the names match, which predicts \"6 6\". Shadowing is purely lexical: the parameter is a distinct object in the callee's activation record.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q70", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "recursion-stack-execution", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint f(int n) {\n    if (n &lt;= 1) return 1;\n    return f(n - 1) + f(n - 2);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, f(5));\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["8", "5", "13", "3"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The function satisfies <code class=\"mm\">f(n) = f(n−1) + f(n−2)</code> with <code class=\"mm\">f(0) = f(1) = 1</code>, so its values are the Fibonacci numbers shifted by one index. Evaluate bottom-up to avoid re-expanding the recursion tree by hand.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">f(0) = 1</code>, <code class=\"mm\">f(1) = 1</code> from the base case.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">f(2) = 1 + 1 = 2</code>; <code class=\"mm\">f(3) = 2 + 1 = 3</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">f(4) = 3 + 2 = 5</code>; <code class=\"mm\">f(5) = 5 + 3 = 8</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A), 8.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 5 by stopping at <code class=\"mm\">f(4)</code>, or 13 by using the base case <code class=\"mm\">f(0) = 0</code>. Read the base case off the code: <code class=\"mm\">n &lt;= 1</code> returns 1, so both <code class=\"mm\">f(0)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">f(1)</code> are 1 and the sequence is 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q71", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "recursion-stack-execution", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint f(int n) { return n == 0 ? 0 : 1 + f(n / 2); }\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, f(100));\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "7", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each call adds 1 and halves the argument with integer division, so the function counts how many times 100 must be halved before it reaches 0 — that is, <code class=\"mm\">⌊log₂ 100⌋ + 1</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The arguments are 100, 50, 25, 12, 6, 3, 1, 0.</li><li>That is seven halvings before the base case: <code class=\"mm\">100 → 50 → 25 → 12 → 6 → 3 → 1 → 0</code>.</li><li>So <code class=\"mm\">f(100) = 7</code>; note <code class=\"mm\">2⁶ = 64 ≤ 100 &lt; 128 = 2⁷</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 7.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 6 from <code class=\"mm\">log₂ 100 ≈ 6.64</code> rounded down. The count includes the step that produces the 0 argument, which is why the formula carries the extra 1.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q72", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "recursion-stack-execution", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint calls = 0;\n\nint f(int n) {\n    calls++;\n    if (n &lt;= 1) return n;\n    return f(n - 1) + f(n - 2);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int r = f(6);\n    printf(&quot;%d %d\\n&quot;, r, calls);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["8 25", "8 15", "13 25", "8 13"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two different quantities are being reported: the returned value and the number of invocations. The invocations form the recursion tree, whose size <code class=\"mm\">T(n) = T(n−1) + T(n−2) + 1</code> grows like the Fibonacci numbers, not like <code class=\"mm\">n</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Value: <code class=\"mm\">f(0)=0</code>, <code class=\"mm\">f(1)=1</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">f(2)=1, f(3)=2, f(4)=3, f(5)=5, f(6)=8</code>.</li><li>Calls: <code class=\"mm\">T(0)=T(1)=1</code> and <code class=\"mm\">T(n)=T(n−1)+T(n−2)+1</code>, giving <code class=\"mm\">T(2)=3, T(3)=5, T(4)=9, T(5)=15, T(6)=25</code>.</li><li>So the program prints <code class=\"mm\">8 25</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A), 8 25.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading 8 and assuming the call count equals the number of distinct subproblems, which is 7. The naive recursion recomputes overlapping subproblems, so <code class=\"mm\">f(2)</code> alone is invoked five times — this redundancy is precisely what memoisation removes.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q73", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "recursion-stack-execution", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about recursion and the run-time stack in C are <b>true</b>? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["any recursive function can be rewritten iteratively using an explicit stack", "a tail-recursive call can in principle reuse the caller's activation record instead of pushing a new one", "unbounded recursion can exhaust the call stack and abort the program", "every recursive function must have exactly one base case"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A recursive call is implemented by pushing an activation record, so recursion and an explicit stack are interchangeable in expressive power. Whether the stack can be reused depends on whether any work remains after the call returns.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: simulate the push/pop discipline with a stack of frames or of pending states.</li><li>(B) is true: in a tail call the caller has nothing left to do, so its frame may be replaced rather than stacked — tail-call optimisation. Note that standard C does not <i>require</i> it, so a deep tail recursion may still overflow in practice.</li><li>(C) is true: each frame consumes stack space, and a program that recurses far enough without a base case reaching will exhaust it, typically terminating with a segmentation fault.</li><li>(D) is false: a function may have several base cases, and a mutual recursion may have its base cases spread over two functions.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing tail-call optimisation is guaranteed in C, so that <code class=\"mm\">int f(int n) { return n ? f(n - 1) : 0; }</code> is safe for huge <code class=\"mm\">n</code>. It is a compiler courtesy, commonly performed at <code class=\"mm\">-O2</code> and absent at <code class=\"mm\">-O0</code>; portable code keeps the depth bounded.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q74", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "recursion-stack-execution", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nvoid f(int n) {\n    if (n == 0) return;\n    printf(&quot;%d&quot;, n % 2);\n    f(n / 2);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    f(6);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["011", "110", "101", "6"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The digit is printed <i>before</i> the recursive call, so the output runs from the least significant bit upwards — the binary expansion of the number written backwards. Moving the <code class=\"mm\">printf</code> after the call would print it in the usual order.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">f(6)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">6 % 2 = 0</code> is printed, then <code class=\"mm\">f(3)</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">f(3)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">3 % 2 = 1</code> is printed, then <code class=\"mm\">f(1)</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">f(1)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">1 % 2 = 1</code> is printed, then <code class=\"mm\">f(0)</code> returns.</li><li>The output is <code class=\"mm\">011</code>, the reverse of <code class=\"mm\">110₂ = 6</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A), 011.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 110 because that is the binary form of 6. The order of the <code class=\"mm\">printf</code> relative to the recursive call decides the direction; only a post-order print produces the familiar expansion.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U1-Q75", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "c-programming-core", "subtopic": "recursion-stack-execution", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint f(int n) {\n    if (n &lt;= 2) return n;\n    return f(n - 1) + f(n - 3);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, f(9));\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "22", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The recurrence <code class=\"mm\">f(n) = f(n−1) + f(n−3)</code> with three fixed initial values is a linear recurrence. Evaluating it bottom-up from the base cases is far safer than expanding the recursion tree.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Base cases: <code class=\"mm\">f(0)=0, f(1)=1, f(2)=2</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">f(3)=f(2)+f(0)=2</code>; <code class=\"mm\">f(4)=f(3)+f(1)=3</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">f(5)=f(4)+f(2)=5</code>; <code class=\"mm\">f(6)=f(5)+f(3)=7</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">f(7)=f(6)+f(4)=10</code>; <code class=\"mm\">f(8)=f(7)+f(5)=15</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">f(9)=f(8)+f(6)=15+7=22</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 22.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using <code class=\"mm\">f(n−2)</code> out of habit and computing a Fibonacci-like sequence, which gives 34. The second term is <code class=\"mm\">n − 3</code> and the base case covers three values, so read both from the code before starting the table.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2C-Q01", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointer-arithmetic", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">On a machine where <code class=\"mm\">int</code> is 32 bits, what does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">int a[4] = {3, 6, 9, 12};\nint *p = a + 1;\n*p = *p * 2;\nprintf(\"%d\", a[1]);</div>", "options": ["6", "9", "12", "18"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Dereferencing a pointer names the object it points at. An assignment through that name stores into the array element itself, not into a copy of the pointer's value.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">p = a + 1</code> makes <code class=\"mm\">p</code> point at <code class=\"mm\">a[1]</code>, whose value is 6.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">*p</code> is therefore 6, and <code class=\"mm\">*p * 2</code> is 12.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">*p = 12</code> writes 12 into <code class=\"mm\">a[1]</code>; <code class=\"mm\">p</code> itself is unchanged.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">a[1]</code> now holds 12, which is what is printed.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">12</code> (option C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Believing that <code class=\"mm\">*p = *p * 2</code> changes only a temporary copy. The left side of the assignment is an lvalue that denotes <code class=\"mm\">a[1]</code>, so the array is modified in place.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <code class=\"mm\">*p</code> on the left of <code class=\"mm\">=</code> is a store to the pointee; the pointer value never moves.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2C-Q02", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointer-arithmetic", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">On a machine where <code class=\"mm\">int</code> is 4 bytes, what integer does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">int a[10];\nint *p = &amp;a[7], *q = &amp;a[2];\nprintf(\"%d\", (int)(p - q));</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "5", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Subtracting two pointers into the same array yields a count of <i>elements</i>, not of bytes. The compiler divides the byte difference by the size of the pointed-to type.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">p</code> addresses <code class=\"mm\">a[7]</code> and <code class=\"mm\">q</code> addresses <code class=\"mm\">a[2]</code>.</li><li>The byte difference is <code class=\"mm\">(7 - 2) &times; 4 = 20</code> bytes.</li><li>The result of <code class=\"mm\">p - q</code> is <code class=\"mm\">20 / 4 = 5</code> elements.</li><li>The printed integer is 5.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 5</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reporting 20 by thinking in bytes. Pointer difference is defined in units of the pointed-to type; only a cast to <code class=\"mm\">char *</code> turns it into a byte count.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <code class=\"mm\">p - q = (address(p) - address(q)) / sizeof(*p)</code>, and the sign tells you which pointer is further along.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2C-Q03", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointer-arithmetic", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">On a strictly conforming C implementation, what happens to the fragment below?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">int a[4] = {1, 2, 3, 4};\nvoid *v = a;\nv = v + 1;</div>", "options": ["v advances by exactly 1 byte, because the pointed-to type is void.", "The compiler must issue a diagnostic: arithmetic on a void * is not defined by the C standard.", "v advances by sizeof(int) bytes, because the array decays to int *.", "v advances by 4 elements, because a is an array of four ints."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Pointer arithmetic is defined only for pointers to complete object types, because the step size must be <code class=\"mm\">sizeof</code> of the pointed-to type. <code class=\"mm\">void</code> is an incomplete type with no size, so the standard defines no arithmetic on <code class=\"mm\">void *</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">v = a</code> stores the address of <code class=\"mm\">a[0]</code> in a <code class=\"mm\">void *</code>; that conversion is perfectly legal.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">v + 1</code> requires a step size, but <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(void)</code> does not exist in standard C, so the expression violates a constraint.</li><li>A conforming implementation must therefore produce a diagnostic message.</li><li>The widely available byte-wise behaviour is a GCC extension, not ISO C.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(void) == 1</code> and that <code class=\"mm\">v + 1</code> moves one byte. That is the GNU extension; it is not what the language guarantees.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Cast to <code class=\"mm\">char *</code> when a byte-wise step is really wanted; keep <code class=\"mm\">void *</code> for storage and passing, not for arithmetic.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2C-Q04", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointer-arithmetic", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">For <code class=\"mm\">int a[3] = {1, 2, 3};</code> and the declarations below, which statements are true? (Select all that apply.)</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">const int *p = a;\nint * const q = a;</div>", "options": ["p may be reassigned to point at a different object.", "q may be reassigned to point at a different object.", "*p = 5; is rejected at compile time.", "*q = 5; is accepted and stores 5 into a[0]."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">const</code> qualifies whatever stands to its left; if nothing stands to its left, it qualifies the thing on its right. So <code class=\"mm\">const int *p</code> is a pointer to a constant int (the pointee is protected) and <code class=\"mm\">int * const q</code> is a constant pointer to int (the pointer is protected).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) TRUE.</b> Only the pointee is const in <code class=\"mm\">p</code>; the pointer itself is an ordinary modifiable variable, so <code class=\"mm\">p = a + 1;</code> compiles.</li><li><b>(B) FALSE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">q</code> is itself const, so <code class=\"mm\">q = a + 1;</code> is a constraint violation.</li><li><b>(C) TRUE.</b> Writing through <code class=\"mm\">p</code> would modify a const-qualified object, which the compiler must reject.</li><li><b>(D) TRUE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">q</code> points at a plain <code class=\"mm\">int</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">*q = 5</code> stores 5 into <code class=\"mm\">a[0]</code>; only the pointer value is frozen.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reading both declarations as \"the pointer is constant\" because the word <code class=\"mm\">const</code> appears in both. Position decides which of the two objects is protected.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Read declarations from the identifier outwards: <code class=\"mm\">q</code> is const, then a pointer, then to int; <code class=\"mm\">p</code> is a pointer to const int.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2C-Q05", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointer-arithmetic", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">int a[5] = {2, 4, 6, 8, 10};\nint *p = a, *q = a + 4;\nint s = 0;\nwhile (p &lt;= q) { s += *p++; p++; }\nprintf(\"%d\", s);</div>", "options": ["12", "18", "20", "30"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">*p++</code> reads the current element and then advances <code class=\"mm\">p</code> by one; a second <code class=\"mm\">p++</code> in the same iteration advances it again. The loop therefore walks the array two elements at a time.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">p</code> is <code class=\"mm\">a[0]</code>: <code class=\"mm\">s = 0 + 2 = 2</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">p</code> moves to <code class=\"mm\">a[2]</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">a + 2 &le; a + 4</code> holds: <code class=\"mm\">s = 2 + 6 = 8</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">p</code> moves to <code class=\"mm\">a[4]</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">a + 4 &le; a + 4</code> holds: <code class=\"mm\">s = 8 + 10 = 18</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">p</code> moves to <code class=\"mm\">a[6]</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">a + 6 &le; a + 4</code> fails, so the loop ends with <code class=\"mm\">s = 18</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">18</code> (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Adding all five elements (30) by missing the second increment, or 20 by stopping one iteration early. The two increments per pass are the whole point of the fragment.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <code class=\"mm\">*p++</code> is <code class=\"mm\">*(p++)</code>: dereference first, increment after. Count the increments per iteration before tracing.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2C-Q06", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointer-arithmetic", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">On a target where <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(int) == 4</code> and <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(struct rec) == 16</code>, what integer does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">struct rec { int k; char tag; double w; };\nstruct rec r[5];\nstruct rec *p = &amp;r[1];\nprintf(\"%d\", (int)((char *)(p + 3) - (char *)&amp;r[0]));</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "64", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Pointer arithmetic steps by the size of the pointed-to type. Here the type is a whole <code class=\"mm\">struct rec</code>, so one step covers one array element. Casting both addresses to <code class=\"mm\">char *</code> converts the difference into bytes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">p = &r[1]</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">p + 3 = &r[4]</code>.</li><li>The element index difference from <code class=\"mm\">r[0]</code> is 4.</li><li>Each element is <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(struct rec) = 16</code> bytes.</li><li>Byte offset <code class=\"mm\">= 4 &times; 16 = 64</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 64</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Using <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(int) = 4</code> because the first member is an <code class=\"mm\">int</code>, or answering 3 because <code class=\"mm\">p</code> starts at index 1. The step size is the whole struct and the offset is measured from <code class=\"mm\">r[0]</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <code class=\"mm\">(char *)a - (char *)b</code> is the only portable byte count; everything else is scaled by the pointee's size.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2C-Q07", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointer-arithmetic", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">int a[4] = {1, 2, 3, 4};\nint *p = a, *q = a + 3;\nwhile (p &lt; q) { int t = *p; *p++ = *q; *q-- = t; }\nprintf(\"%d %d\", a[0], a[1]);</div>", "options": ["4 3", "1 2", "4 2", "3 4"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The loop is an in-place reversal driven by two pointers walking towards each other. <code class=\"mm\">*p++ = *q</code> copies the right value into the left slot and then advances <code class=\"mm\">p</code>; the order inside the statement is what makes it correct.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Pass 1: <code class=\"mm\">t = a[0] = 1</code>; <code class=\"mm\">a[0] = a[3] = 4</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">a[3] = t = 1</code>. Array is <code class=\"mm\">{4, 2, 3, 1}</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">p</code> now points at <code class=\"mm\">a[1]</code>, <code class=\"mm\">q</code> at <code class=\"mm\">a[2]</code>.</li><li>Pass 2: <code class=\"mm\">t = a[1] = 2</code>; <code class=\"mm\">a[1] = a[2] = 3</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">a[2] = t = 2</code>. Array is <code class=\"mm\">{4, 3, 2, 1}</code>, and the pointers cross.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">p &lt; q</code> is now false, so the loop ends.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">a[0] = 4</code> and <code class=\"mm\">a[1] = 3</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">4 3</code> (option A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Swapping with a single assignment <code class=\"mm\">*p = *q; *q = t;</code> without the temporary, or advancing <code class=\"mm\">p</code> before reading <code class=\"mm\">*p</code>. Either slip corrupts one element and produces a plausible-looking wrong answer such as <code class=\"mm\">4 2</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <code class=\"mm\">*p++ = *q</code> is <code class=\"mm\">*(p++) = *q</code>: the store happens at the old <code class=\"mm\">p</code>. Trace both pointers explicitly on small arrays.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2C-Q08", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointers-to-functions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The C library function <code class=\"mm\">qsort</code> takes a pointer to a comparison function. When the array being sorted holds <code class=\"mm\">int</code> values, which declaration is correct for that comparison function?</p>", "options": ["int cmp(const void *a, const void *b);", "int cmp(void *a, void *b);", "void cmp(const void *a, const void *b);", "int cmp(const void *a, const void *b, size_t n);"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">qsort</code> is type-agnostic: it declares the comparator as <code class=\"mm\">int (*)(const void *, const void *)</code>. Any function assigned to it must have exactly that type, so the parameters are <code class=\"mm\">const void *</code> and the return type is <code class=\"mm\">int</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A)</b> matches the declared parameter type exactly, so it can be passed without a cast.</li><li><b>(B)</b> drops the <code class=\"mm\">const</code>; assigning it to the parameter is a constraint violation.</li><li><b>(C)</b> returns <code class=\"mm\">void</code>, but the comparator's return value carries the ordering, so the type does not match.</li><li><b>(D)</b> has three parameters, which also fails the type match.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Writing the comparator with plain <code class=\"mm\">int *</code> parameters because the array holds ints. The function is called through a <code class=\"mm\">void *</code> type, so the conversion must happen inside the function.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A function pointer argument is matched on its whole type: return type and every parameter type, including qualifiers.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2C-Q09", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointers-to-functions", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What integer does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">int pos(int x) { return x &gt; 0 ? 1 : 0; }\n\nint count(int (*f)(int), int *a, int n) {\n    int s = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) s += f(a[i]);\n    return s;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a[5] = {3, -1, 4, -1, 5};\n    printf(\"%d\", count(pos, a, 5));\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A function name used as an argument decays to a pointer to that function, so <code class=\"mm\">count</code> receives <code class=\"mm\">pos</code> and calls it once per element. The callback decides what is being counted.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">count(pos, a, 5)</code> binds <code class=\"mm\">f = pos</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">f(3) = 1</code>, <code class=\"mm\">f(-1) = 0</code>, <code class=\"mm\">f(4) = 1</code>, <code class=\"mm\">f(-1) = 0</code>, <code class=\"mm\">f(5) = 1</code>.</li><li>The running sum is <code class=\"mm\">1 + 0 + 1 + 0 + 1 = 3</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Summing the array elements themselves (10) or counting all five calls. The callback's return value, not the argument, is what is accumulated.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <code class=\"mm\">f(a[i])</code> is a call through the pointer; only what <code class=\"mm\">f</code> returns enters the sum.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2C-Q10", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointers-to-functions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Consider the declaration below. What is the type of the value that <code class=\"mm\">get</code> returns?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">int (*get(int))(char);</div>", "options": ["a pointer to a function that takes a char and returns int", "an int", "a pointer to a function that takes an int and returns char", "an array of pointers to functions taking a char"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Read a C declarator from the identifier outwards, applying the postfix operators first. Inside <code class=\"mm\">(*get(int))</code> the <code class=\"mm\">(int)</code> binds to <code class=\"mm\">get</code> immediately, so <code class=\"mm\">get</code> is a function taking an <code class=\"mm\">int</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">get</code> is a function taking <code class=\"mm\">(int)</code>.</li><li>The outer <code class=\"mm\">*</code> says its return value is a pointer.</li><li>That pointer is followed by <code class=\"mm\">(char)</code>, so it points at a function taking a <code class=\"mm\">char</code>.</li><li>The leading <code class=\"mm\">int</code> is that pointed-to function's return type.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Swapping the two parameter lists and saying <code class=\"mm\">get</code> returns a pointer to a function taking an <code class=\"mm\">int</code> and returning <code class=\"mm\">char</code>. The parameter that binds directly to the name belongs to <code class=\"mm\">get</code> itself.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Peel the declarator: name, then the parentheses that touch it, then the prefix <code class=\"mm\">*</code>, then the rest.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2C-Q11", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointers-to-functions", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">For the standard library's <code class=\"mm\">qsort</code>, which statements are true? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["The comparison function must return a negative value, zero, or a positive value according to the required ordering of the two elements.", "Each call passes the comparison function pointers to the two elements being compared, so it must cast them to the element type before reading them.", "qsort knows the element type, so the comparison function may read the elements without casting.", "Passing NULL as the comparison function is allowed and leaves the array in its original order."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">qsort</code> is a generic sort: it knows only an element size in bytes, never an element type. All type knowledge must therefore live in the comparator, which receives <code class=\"mm\">const void *</code> arguments.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) TRUE.</b> The sign of the result is the entire interface: negative means the first element sorts before the second, zero means they are equivalent, positive means after.</li><li><b>(B) TRUE.</b> The pointers are to the stored elements, and a <code class=\"mm\">void *</code> cannot be dereferenced, so the comparator casts to <code class=\"mm\">const int *</code> (or whatever the element type is) before reading.</li><li><b>(C) FALSE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">qsort</code> receives only <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(element)</code>, which is a number of bytes, not a type.</li><li><b>(D) FALSE.</b> A null comparator would have to be called to order any pair, so the requirement is a valid function pointer; passing <code class=\"mm\">NULL</code> is undefined behaviour.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A) and (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming the library can infer types from <code class=\"mm\">sizeof</code>. A byte count cannot tell an <code class=\"mm\">int</code> from a <code class=\"mm\">float</code>, so the cast in the comparator is mandatory, not decorative.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> In a generic callback the type information travels with the callback; the library carries only sizes and addresses.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2C-Q12", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointers-to-functions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">With <code class=\"mm\">int f(int);</code> and <code class=\"mm\">double g(int);</code> both in scope, what must the compiler do with the declaration below?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">int (*p)(int) = g;</div>", "options": ["It compiles cleanly; p points at g.", "It must issue a diagnostic: assigning a double (*)(int) to an int (*)(int) is a constraint violation.", "It compiles with a warning, and calling p returns a double.", "It is accepted because both functions take exactly one int."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two function types are compatible only when their return types are compatible and their parameter lists match. A differing return type is enough to make the assignment a constraint violation, which a conforming compiler must diagnose.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">g</code> has type <code class=\"mm\">double (*)(int)</code> after decay.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">p</code> has type <code class=\"mm\">int (*)(int)</code>.</li><li>The parameter lists agree but the return types differ, so no implicit conversion exists between the two pointer types.</li><li>The implementation must therefore produce a diagnostic; a cast would be required to silence it, and even then calling <code class=\"mm\">g</code> through <code class=\"mm\">p</code> would be undefined behaviour.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Checking only the parameter list. The return type is part of the function type, and mismatching it is exactly as serious as mismatching a parameter.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Two function pointers are interchangeable only when the whole types match; a cast hides the message but not the undefined behaviour.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2C-Q13", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "pointers-to-functions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }\nint sub(int a, int b) { return a - b; }\n\nint (*op(char c))(int, int) { return c == '+' ? add : sub; }\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"%d\", op('-') (7, 2) * op('+') (1, 1));\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["9", "10", "12", "14"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">op</code> returns a pointer to a function taking two ints. A call written as <code class=\"mm\">op(c) (x, y)</code> first evaluates <code class=\"mm\">op(c)</code> to obtain the pointer, then calls through it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">op('-')</code> returns <code class=\"mm\">sub</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">sub(7, 2) = 5</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">op('+')</code> returns <code class=\"mm\">add</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">add(1, 1) = 2</code>.</li><li>The expression is <code class=\"mm\">5 * 2 = 10</code>.</li><li>The output is 10.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">10</code> (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reading <code class=\"mm\">op('-') (7, 2)</code> as a single call to <code class=\"mm\">op</code> with three arguments, or answering 9 by computing <code class=\"mm\">7 - 2 + 1 + 1</code> as one left-to-right expression. The two calls are separate factors of a product.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <code class=\"mm\">f(c)(x, y)</code> is a call whose callee is the result of <code class=\"mm\">f(c)</code>; the outer parentheses are the real call.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2C-Q14", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "dynamic-memory-allocation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which statement about <code class=\"mm\">void *p = malloc(0);</code> is true?</p>", "options": ["It always returns NULL, because zero bytes cannot be allocated.", "It may return NULL or a unique pointer that can safely be passed to free; both are conforming.", "It is a compile-time error.", "It is undefined behaviour in every case."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A request for zero bytes is a valid request whose outcome the standard deliberately leaves open: the implementation may return a null pointer, or it may return a unique pointer that is not null and that must later be freed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">malloc(0)</code> is well defined; it is not an error and not a constraint violation, so it compiles and runs.</li><li>Returning <code class=\"mm\">NULL</code> is permitted.</li><li>Returning a unique non-null pointer that can be passed to <code class=\"mm\">free</code> is equally permitted.</li><li>Portable code therefore treats the result like any other allocation: check for <code class=\"mm\">NULL</code> before use, and free it if it is non-null.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Asserting that <code class=\"mm\">malloc(0)</code> must return <code class=\"mm\">NULL</code>. Treating the return value as a failure indication leads to a leak on implementations that return a real pointer.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> \"Implementation-defined\" is not the same as \"undefined\": both outcomes are legal, so write code that survives either.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2C-Q15", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "dynamic-memory-allocation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A program executes the statement below. Which statement is true?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">p = realloc(p, 0);</div>", "options": ["It is always equivalent to free(p) and returns NULL.", "The behaviour is implementation-defined: the block may be freed and NULL returned, or a unique pointer may be returned that must later be freed.", "It always returns p unchanged.", "It always returns a valid pointer to a zero-byte block that need not be freed."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">realloc</code> with a new size of zero sits on the boundary between reallocation and deallocation, and the standard leaves the choice to the implementation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>One permitted behaviour is to free the block and return <code class=\"mm\">NULL</code>, which makes the statement look exactly like <code class=\"mm\">free(p)</code>.</li><li>The other permitted behaviour is to return a unique pointer to a zero-size object, which the program must later free.</li><li>Because both are allowed, the statement is not portable as a substitute for <code class=\"mm\">free</code>: on the second kind of implementation the returned pointer leaks.</li><li>The safe idiom is to call <code class=\"mm\">free(p)</code> explicitly when the block is no longer wanted.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Writing <code class=\"mm\">realloc(p, 0)</code> as a shorthand for <code class=\"mm\">free(p)</code>. It happens to work on many implementations, which is exactly what makes the bug survive testing.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Use <code class=\"mm\">free</code> to release memory and <code class=\"mm\">realloc</code> only to resize to a non-zero size.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2C-Q16", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "dynamic-memory-allocation", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following are undefined behaviour? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["Calling free twice on the same non-null pointer returned by malloc.", "Calling free(NULL).", "Calling free on the address of an automatic variable.", "Reading through a pointer to a block after a successful realloc has moved that block."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">free</code> accepts only a null pointer or a pointer returned by an allocation function that is still live. Everything else is undefined behaviour; the single explicit exception is the null pointer.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) UB.</b> The second <code class=\"mm\">free</code> releases an object whose lifetime already ended: a double free.</li><li><b>(B) Defined.</b> The standard says that if the argument is a null pointer, no action occurs. This is the one case that is explicitly safe.</li><li><b>(C) UB.</b> An automatic variable was never returned by an allocation function, so passing its address to <code class=\"mm\">free</code> is a constraint on the argument that the program violates.</li><li><b>(D) UB.</b> When <code class=\"mm\">realloc</code> moves the block it deallocates the old one, so every pointer into the old block becomes dangling.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Marking <code class=\"mm\">free(NULL)</code> as an error. It is defined and idiomatic precisely so that cleanup code needs no null checks; the dangerous case is the double free, not the null one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> After every <code class=\"mm\">free</code> or moving <code class=\"mm\">realloc</code>, no pointer into that block is usable any more — set them to <code class=\"mm\">NULL</code> if they may be reused.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2C-Q17", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "dynamic-memory-allocation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which declaration allocates a 5 &times; 5 <code class=\"mm\">int</code> matrix as one contiguous block with a single allocation call?</p>", "options": ["int **m = malloc(5 * sizeof *m); followed by a loop of malloc(5 * sizeof **m)", "int (*m)[5] = malloc(5 * sizeof *m);", "int m[5][5] = malloc(25);", "int *m[5] = malloc(5 * sizeof *m);"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A pointer to an array of 5 ints describes a row, so <code class=\"mm\">sizeof *m</code> is the size of a whole row and <code class=\"mm\">m[i][j]</code> is valid. One call then yields a single contiguous block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A)</b> is the pointer-array layout: six allocations, and the rows need not be adjacent.</li><li><b>(B)</b> allocates <code class=\"mm\">5 &times; 20 = 100</code> bytes in one call, and the row-pointer arithmetic addresses the rows correctly.</li><li><b>(C)</b> is invalid: <code class=\"mm\">m</code> is an array, not a pointer, so it cannot be assigned, and 25 bytes would be far too small anyway.</li><li><b>(D)</b> allocates storage for only five row pointers, not for any ints, and the type of <code class=\"mm\">m</code> is an array of pointers, which cannot be assigned to.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Believing that a two-dimensional array must be built from an array of pointers. The row-pointer type is the compact alternative, and it also makes <code class=\"mm\">free(m)</code> sufficient.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <code class=\"mm\">int (*m)[5]</code> is one pointer to a 5-int row; <code class=\"mm\">int *m[5]</code> is an array of five pointers. One character moves the whole meaning.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2C-Q18", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "dynamic-memory-allocation", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">On a machine where <code class=\"mm\">int</code> is 4 bytes, how many bytes does the call below request in total?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">int (*m)[5] = malloc(5 * sizeof *m);</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "100", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">sizeof</code> applied to <code class=\"mm\">*m</code> measures the pointed-to type, which is an array of 5 ints, not a single int and not a pointer.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">*m</code> has type <code class=\"mm\">int[5]</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">sizeof(int[5]) = 5 &times; 4 = 20</code> bytes.</li><li>The call requests <code class=\"mm\">5 &times; 20 = 100</code> bytes.</li><li>That block holds 25 ints, laid out row after row.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 100</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Taking <code class=\"mm\">sizeof *m</code> as <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(int *)</code> and answering 40. The dereference strips one level of pointer, leaving the array type.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <code class=\"mm\">sizeof *p</code> is the size of what <code class=\"mm\">p</code> points at; for a row pointer that is a whole row.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2C-Q19", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "dynamic-memory-allocation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Consider the function below. Which statement about the pointer it returns is correct?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">int *buf(void) {\n    static int t[3] = {1, 2, 3};\n    return t;\n}</div>", "options": ["The pointer is dangling as soon as buf returns.", "The pointer stays valid: t has static storage duration and lives until the program ends.", "The caller must pass the pointer to free.", "The behaviour is undefined, because an array name decays to a pointer."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Returning a pointer is safe exactly when the object it points at outlives the function. <code class=\"mm\">static</code> gives an object static storage duration, so it is created before the program starts and destroyed after it ends.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">t</code> is declared <code class=\"mm\">static</code> inside <code class=\"mm\">buf</code>, so its lifetime is the whole program, not the call.</li><li>In <code class=\"mm\">return t;</code> the array decays to <code class=\"mm\">int *</code>, which is the declared return type.</li><li>The pointer therefore refers to a live object after the call returns.</li><li>The object was not obtained from an allocation function, so the caller must not free it; and because it is shared, a second call overwrites the same storage.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Applying the \"never return a pointer to a local\" rule without checking the storage class. The rule is about automatic locals; a <code class=\"mm\">static</code> local survives.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Ask what the storage duration is, not where the declaration sits. Static locals are safe to return and unsafe to free.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2C-Q20", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "macro-expansion-scope-lifetime", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Assuming <code class=\"mm\">FOO</code> is never defined anywhere in the program, what does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#if FOO\nint x = 1;\n#else\nint x = 2;\n#endif\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"%d\", x);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["1", "2", "a compile-time error", "nothing"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Inside <code class=\"mm\">#if</code> the preprocessor evaluates a constant integer expression, and every identifier that survives macro replacement is replaced by 0. An undefined name is therefore false, not an error.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">FOO</code> is not a macro, so it is not replaced by anything.</li><li>The remaining identifier is treated as 0, so the controlling expression is <code class=\"mm\">0</code>.</li><li>The <code class=\"mm\">#else</code> branch is selected and <code class=\"mm\">x</code> is defined as 2.</li><li>The program prints 2.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">2</code> (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Expecting a diagnostic for an undefined name in <code class=\"mm\">#if</code>, or expecting <code class=\"mm\">#ifdef</code> semantics. <code class=\"mm\">#if</code> tests a value; <code class=\"mm\">#ifdef</code> tests whether a name is defined at all.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> In <code class=\"mm\">#if</code>, an unknown identifier is 0, so <code class=\"mm\">#if FOO</code> and <code class=\"mm\">#if 0</code> are the same when <code class=\"mm\">FOO</code> is undefined.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2C-Q21", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "macro-expansion-scope-lifetime", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What integer does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#define P 3\n#define Q (P + 1)\n#define R Q * Q\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"%d\", R);\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "16", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Object-like macros are pure text substitution, applied repeatedly until no macro name remains. Parentheses written in a macro body are carried into every expansion, which is what protects the result here.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">R</code> expands to <code class=\"mm\">Q * Q</code>.</li><li>Each <code class=\"mm\">Q</code> expands to <code class=\"mm\">(P + 1)</code>, giving <code class=\"mm\">(P + 1) * (P + 1)</code>.</li><li>Each <code class=\"mm\">P</code> expands to 3, giving <code class=\"mm\">(3 + 1) * (3 + 1)</code>.</li><li>That is <code class=\"mm\">4 * 4 = 16</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 16</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Without the parentheses in <code class=\"mm\">Q</code>, <code class=\"mm\">R</code> would expand to <code class=\"mm\">P + 1 * P + 1</code> and print 7 — the classic reminder that the body needs its own parentheses, not just the parameters.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Parenthesise the entire body of every macro; a body without parentheses inherits the precedence of whatever surrounds the expansion.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2C-Q22", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "macro-expansion-scope-lifetime", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What is the effect of the macro call below?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#define MAX(a, b) ((a) &gt; (b) ? (a) : (b))\n\nint i = 1, j = 0;\nint m = MAX(i++, j);</div>", "options": ["i is incremented exactly once and m is 1.", "i is incremented twice, because the body mentions a twice: m is 1 and i becomes 2.", "i is never incremented, because the argument is not an lvalue.", "MAX is a function call, so i is incremented once and m is 2."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A function-like macro substitutes its argument text at every place the parameter appears in the body. The argument <code class=\"mm\">i++</code> appears twice in <code class=\"mm\">MAX</code>'s body, so the increment is evaluated twice.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The call expands to <code class=\"mm\">((i++) > (j) ? (i++) : (j))</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">i++</code> yields 1 and sets <code class=\"mm\">i</code> to 2; <code class=\"mm\">1 &gt; 0</code> is true.</li><li>The true branch evaluates <code class=\"mm\">i++</code> again: it yields 2 and sets <code class=\"mm\">i</code> to 3.</li><li>So <code class=\"mm\">m</code> is 2 and <code class=\"mm\">i</code> is 3 — and because the same object is modified twice between sequence points, the behaviour is undefined.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: option (B): the argument is evaluated twice.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reading the parentheses in the body as protection. Parentheses fix precedence; they do nothing about how many times an argument is evaluated.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Never pass an expression with side effects to a macro that mentions the parameter more than once. A function or an <code class=\"mm\">inline</code> function evaluates each argument exactly once.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2C-Q23", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "macro-expansion-scope-lifetime", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What does this program print?</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">#define STR(x) #x\n#define XSTR(x) STR(x)\n#define N 42\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"%s\", XSTR(N));\n    return 0;\n}</div>", "options": ["N", "42", "&quot;N&quot;", "a compile-time error"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The <code class=\"mm\">#</code> operator stringises its argument <i>without</i> expanding it first. The standard two-level idiom <code class=\"mm\">STR(XSTR(x))</code> exists to force one expansion before stringising.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">XSTR(N)</code> is a macro call, so its argument is macro-expanded: <code class=\"mm\">N</code> becomes 42.</li><li>The expansion is then substituted into the body, giving <code class=\"mm\">STR(42)</code>.</li><li>Now <code class=\"mm\">#</code> stringises the already-expanded argument, producing the string literal <code class=\"mm\">&quot;42&quot;</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">printf(&quot;%s&quot;, ...)</code> prints 42 without the quotes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">42</code> (option B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Calling <code class=\"mm\">STR(N)</code> directly, which stringises the unexpanded token and prints <code class=\"mm\">N</code>. The extra indirection through <code class=\"mm\">XSTR</code> is the whole point.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <code class=\"mm\">#</code> suppresses expansion of its operand; wrap the macro in a second macro when the value, not the name, must appear.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2C-Q24", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "macro-expansion-scope-lifetime", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">For <code class=\"mm\">#define SQ(x) ((x) * (x))</code>, which statements are true? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["SQ(i++) evaluates i++ twice, so the behaviour is undefined.", "The fully parenthesised body removes the precedence hazard but not the multiple-evaluation hazard.", "Wrapping the body in parentheses guarantees that each argument is evaluated exactly once.", "An inline function is generally preferable when the argument has side effects."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two independent hazards attach to macros: precedence, which parentheses fix, and multiple evaluation, which only a real function fixes. <code class=\"mm\">SQ</code> mentions <code class=\"mm\">x</code> twice, so any argument with a side effect runs twice.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) TRUE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">SQ(i++)</code> becomes <code class=\"mm\">((i++) * (i++))</code>, which modifies <code class=\"mm\">i</code> twice without an intervening sequence point: undefined behaviour.</li><li><b>(B) TRUE.</b> The parentheses make <code class=\"mm\">SQ(2 + 3)</code> give 25 instead of 11, but they do not reduce the number of evaluations.</li><li><b>(C) FALSE.</b> Parentheses are a purely syntactic device and cannot control evaluation count.</li><li><b>(D) TRUE.</b> An <code class=\"mm\">inline</code> function has typed parameters, evaluates each argument once, and keeps the body in one scope.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Believing a well-parenthesised macro is safe. Parenthesising is necessary but not sufficient; the parameter count in the body is the other half of the story.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A macro that mentions a parameter more than once is safe only for arguments that are side-effect free.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U2C-Q25", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "pointers-memory", "subtopic": "macro-expansion-scope-lifetime", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which statements about the C preprocessor are true? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["A function-like macro's name is not replaced where it is not followed by (.", "A self-referential macro such as #define F(x) F(x) is not expanded again inside its own expansion, so it terminates.", "The preprocessor can evaluate sizeof(int) inside an #if.", "#include &quot;x.h&quot; searches only the system include directories."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The preprocessor works on preprocessing tokens before the compiler exists, so it has no type information at all. Its expansion rules are defined so that replacement always terminates.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) TRUE.</b> A function-like macro is recognised as a call only when the next preprocessing token is <code class=\"mm\">(</code>; otherwise the identifier is left alone, which is why a function pointer can share the macro's name.</li><li><b>(B) TRUE.</b> A macro name is not replaced inside its own expansion, so <code class=\"mm\">F(1)</code> becomes <code class=\"mm\">F(1)</code> and stops.</li><li><b>(C) FALSE.</b> <code class=\"mm\">sizeof</code> is a compile-time operator of the language, not an expression the preprocessor can evaluate; using it in <code class=\"mm\">#if</code> is a constraint violation.</li><li><b>(D) FALSE.</b> The quoted form is searched for first in the directory of the including file and then in the implementation's search path; the angle-bracket form is the one aimed at system directories.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A) and (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Treating <code class=\"mm\">#if</code> as a C expression evaluator. It handles integer constants and macro names only; anything needing a type, such as <code class=\"mm\">sizeof</code> or a cast, belongs to the compiler.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The preprocessor sees tokens, not types. Every question that needs a type, a value or a size is answered after preprocessing, by the compiler.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q51", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "arrays-multidimensional", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A two-dimensional array is declared as <code class=\"mm\">int A[3][4];</code> and stored in row-major order. If its storage is viewed as a one-dimensional array <code class=\"mm\">B</code> with <code class=\"mm\">B[0]</code> holding <code class=\"mm\">A[0][0]</code>, which element of <code class=\"mm\">B</code> holds <code class=\"mm\">A[2][3]</code>?</p>", "options": ["B[7]", "B[10]", "B[11]", "B[12]"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In row-major order the whole of row 0 is stored before row 1 begins, so the flat offset of <span class=\"mm\">A[i][j]</span> in an array of <span class=\"mm\">C</span> columns is <span class=\"mm\">i · C + j</span>, counted from 0.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The array has <span class=\"mm\">C = 4</span> columns, so each row occupies 4 consecutive slots.</li><li>Row 2 therefore begins at flat index <span class=\"mm\">2 · 4 = 8</span>.</li><li>Within row 2, column 3 adds another 3 slots: <span class=\"mm\">8 + 3 = 11</span>.</li><li>The element <span class=\"mm\">A[2][3]</span> is the 12th element, so its 0-based index is 11.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">B[11]</code> — option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting the element's position as 12 and forgetting that <code class=\"mm\">B</code> is 0-based. The 12th element sits at index 11.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Row-major flat index = <span class=\"mm\">i · C + j</span>; column-major flat index = <span class=\"mm\">j · R + i</span>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q52", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "arrays-multidimensional", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">How many elements does the three-dimensional array <code class=\"mm\">int A[4][6][5];</code> hold?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "120", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A multidimensional array declaration gives the size of each dimension, and the total number of elements is the product of those sizes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The three dimensions are 4, 6 and 5.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">4 · 6 = 24</span> elements per outermost slice.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">24 · 5 = 120</span> elements in all.</li><li>Each of the 120 elements is a separate <code class=\"mm\">int</code> object, laid out contiguously.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 120.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Adding the dimensions (4 + 6 + 5 = 15) instead of multiplying them, or reading the declaration as 4 elements of type <code class=\"mm\">int[6][5]</code> and reporting 4.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Total elements = product of all declared sizes.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q53", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "arrays-multidimensional", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">On a machine where <code class=\"mm\">sizeof(int)</code> is 4, how many bytes does the array <code class=\"mm\">int A[10][20];</code> occupy?</p>", "options": ["800", "200", "2000", "8000"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An array occupies the number of its elements multiplied by the size of one element; the shape of the array does not add any padding of its own.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The array holds <span class=\"mm\">10 · 20 = 200</span> elements.</li><li>Each element is an <code class=\"mm\">int</code> of 4 bytes.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">200 · 4 = 800</span> bytes in total.</li><li>All 800 bytes are contiguous.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 800 bytes — option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Multiplying only one dimension by the element size (10 · 4 = 40) or treating the two dimensions as two separate arrays and doubling the count.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Bytes = product of dimensions × element size.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q54", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "arrays-multidimensional", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\"><code class=\"mm\">int A[R][C];</code> is stored in column-major order, each <code class=\"mm\">int</code> occupies 4 bytes, and <code class=\"mm\">A[0][0]</code> begins at byte address <code class=\"mm\">base</code>. Which expression gives the byte address of <code class=\"mm\">A[i][j]</code>?</p>", "options": ["base + (i * C + j) * 4", "base + (i * R + j) * 4", "base + (j * C + i) * 4", "base + (j * R + i) * 4"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Column-major order stores all of column 0 first, then all of column 1, and so on. The stride along a column is therefore 1, and the stride between columns is the number of rows <span class=\"mm\">R</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Every complete column before column <span class=\"mm\">j</span> holds <span class=\"mm\">R</span> elements, so <span class=\"mm\">j · R</span> elements precede column <span class=\"mm\">j</span>.</li><li>Inside column <span class=\"mm\">j</span>, the element in row <span class=\"mm\">i</span> is <span class=\"mm\">i</span> places further along.</li><li>The flat index is <span class=\"mm\">j · R + i</span>, and the byte offset is that index times 4.</li><li>Adding <code class=\"mm\">base</code> gives the address.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">base + (j * R + i) * 4</code> — option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Option (C) keeps the correct shape but swaps the roles of <span class=\"mm\">R</span> and <span class=\"mm\">C</span>. In column-major order the number of rows, not the number of columns, is the stride between columns.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Row-major: <span class=\"mm\">i · C + j</span>. Column-major: <span class=\"mm\">j · R + i</span>. The stride always belongs to the dimension that is *not* varying fastest.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q55", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "arrays-multidimensional", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">For a two-dimensional array <code class=\"mm\">int A[100][100];</code> compiled by a standard C compiler, which of the following statements are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["C lays a two-dimensional array out in row-major order, so the last subscript varies fastest in memory.", "The loop nest for (i = 0; i < 100; i++) for (j = 0; j < 100; j++) sum += A[i][j]; reads memory in a friendlier order than the same nest with i and j exchanged.", "A conforming compiler may store A in column-major order if that happens to be faster on the target machine.", "The address of A[i][j] is computed with a constant number of arithmetic operations, whatever i and j are."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> C fixes the layout of an array: elements are stored in row-major order, so the rightmost subscript moves fastest. Locality, and therefore speed, follows from that layout.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) TRUE. The C standard requires array elements to be contiguous with the last subscript varying fastest, which is row-major order.</li><li>(B) TRUE. With <code class=\"mm\">j</code> innermost, consecutive iterations touch consecutive addresses; exchanging the loops walks one element per 100-element row and touches a new cache line every time.</li><li>(C) FALSE. The layout is fixed by the language, not chosen by the compiler for speed. A program that wants column-major storage must emulate it (for example <code class=\"mm\">A[j][i]</code>).</li><li>(D) TRUE. The address is <code class=\"mm\">base + (i * 100 + j) * sizeof(int)</code>, a fixed number of multiplications and additions.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Believing the compiler may reorder storage. It may reorder *accesses* and vectorise loops, but the address of every element is fixed by the declaration.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Row-major layout means the innermost loop should vary the last subscript.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q56", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "arrays-multidimensional", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A tridiagonal <span class=\"mm\">n × n</span> matrix has non-zero entries only on the main diagonal and on the two diagonals immediately beside it. All of its non-zero entries are packed, with no padding, into a one-dimensional array. For <span class=\"mm\">n = 100</span>, how many elements does that array hold?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "298", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A tridiagonal matrix has <span class=\"mm\">n</span> entries on the main diagonal, <span class=\"mm\">n − 1</span> on the diagonal above it and <span class=\"mm\">n − 1</span> below it, giving <span class=\"mm\">3n − 2</span> non-zero entries.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Main diagonal: <span class=\"mm\">n = 100</span> entries.</li><li>Super-diagonal: <span class=\"mm\">n − 1 = 99</span> entries.</li><li>Sub-diagonal: <span class=\"mm\">n − 1 = 99</span> entries.</li><li>Total <span class=\"mm\">100 + 99 + 99 = 298</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 298.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting <span class=\"mm\">3n = 300</span> by forgetting that each of the two off-diagonals is one entry short, or packing the full <span class=\"mm\">n² = 10 000</span> cells out of habit.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Banded matrices are counted band by band, not row by row.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q57", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "arrays-multidimensional", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\"><code class=\"mm\">int A[3][4][5];</code> begins at byte address 0 and is stored in column-major order, meaning the first subscript varies fastest, with each <code class=\"mm\">int</code> occupying 4 bytes. What is the byte address of <code class=\"mm\">A[2][1][3]</code>?</p>", "options": ["148", "152", "164", "192"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In column-major order the strides grow with the dimensions in declaration order: the first subscript has stride 1, the second has stride equal to the first dimension, and the third has stride equal to the product of the first two.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Stride of the first subscript: 1.</li><li>Stride of the second subscript: <span class=\"mm\">3</span> (the size of the first dimension).</li><li>Stride of the third subscript: <span class=\"mm\">3 · 4 = 12</span>.</li><li>Flat index of <span class=\"mm\">A[2][1][3]</span> is <span class=\"mm\">2 · 1 + 1 · 3 + 3 · 12 = 2 + 3 + 36 = 41</span>.</li><li>Byte address <span class=\"mm\">41 · 4 = 164</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 164 — option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Applying the row-major formula <span class=\"mm\">(i · 4 + j) · 5 + k = 48</span>, which gives 192 and is option (D). Column-major order reverses which subscript moves fastest.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Column-major strides are <span class=\"mm\">1, d₁, d₁d₂</span>; row-major strides are <span class=\"mm\">d₂d₃, d₃, 1</span>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q58", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "singly-doubly-circular-linked-lists", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">In a singly linked list, node <code class=\"mm\">p</code> is followed by node <code class=\"mm\">q</code>. A new node <code class=\"mm\">r</code> is to be inserted immediately after <code class=\"mm\">p</code>. How many <code class=\"mm\">next</code> fields must be written to complete the insertion?</p>", "options": ["2", "1", "3", "4"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Inserting after a known node touches exactly two links: the new node must point at the old successor, and the predecessor must point at the new node. Order matters, because the old successor's address is lost once <code class=\"mm\">p->next</code> is overwritten.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">r->next = p->next;</code> — the new node adopts the old successor <code class=\"mm\">q</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">p->next = r;</code> — the predecessor now points at the new node.</li><li>No other link changes: nothing else referred to the position between <code class=\"mm\">p</code> and <code class=\"mm\">q</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2 — option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Writing <code class=\"mm\">p->next = r;</code> first. That destroys the only pointer to <code class=\"mm\">q</code>, and the rest of the list is lost.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Always give the new node its successor before overwriting the predecessor's link.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q59", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "singly-doubly-circular-linked-lists", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\"><code class=\"mm\">p</code> points to a node of a singly linked list and no other pointer to the list is available. Which of the following operations can be completed in Θ(1) time using <code class=\"mm\">p</code> alone?</p>", "options": ["Delete the node that p points to, whatever node it happens to be.", "Delete the node that follows p .", "Find the node that precedes p .", "Reverse the entire list."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A singly linked list carries only forward links, so from a node you can reach everything after it and nothing before it. Work that needs the predecessor is expensive; work that needs only the successor is constant time.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is false: removing <code class=\"mm\">p</code> itself requires changing <code class=\"mm\">p->prev</code>, and no predecessor link exists.</li><li>(B) is true: <code class=\"mm\">q = p->next; p->next = q->next; free(q);</code> unlinks the successor with a fixed number of operations.</li><li>(C) is false: the predecessor can only be found by walking from the head, which costs Θ(n).</li><li>(D) is false: a full reversal must visit every node.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming <code class=\"mm\">p</code> is enough to delete its own node. That is true in a doubly linked list, where <code class=\"mm\">p->prev</code> is stored, and false in a singly linked list.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> In a singly linked list, \"delete the node after p\" is Θ(1); \"delete p\" is not.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q60", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "singly-doubly-circular-linked-lists", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The loop below runs on a singly linked list of 8 nodes whose data are 1, 2, …, 8 in that order. It starts with <code class=\"mm\">slow = fast = head</code>.</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">while (fast != NULL &amp;&amp; fast-&gt;next != NULL) {\n    slow = slow-&gt;next;\n    fast = fast-&gt;next-&gt;next;\n}</div><p class=\"qtext\">Which node does <code class=\"mm\">slow</code> point to when the loop ends?</p>", "options": ["the node holding 4", "the node holding 6", "the node holding 5", "the node holding 8"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The two-pointer technique advances one pointer twice as fast as the other, so when the fast pointer reaches the end the slow pointer has covered half the list. With an even number of nodes it lands on the second of the two middle nodes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Start: <code class=\"mm\">slow = 1</code>, <code class=\"mm\">fast = 1</code>.</li><li>Iteration 1: <code class=\"mm\">slow = 2</code>, <code class=\"mm\">fast = 3</code>.</li><li>Iteration 2: <code class=\"mm\">slow = 3</code>, <code class=\"mm\">fast = 5</code>.</li><li>Iteration 3: <code class=\"mm\">slow = 4</code>, <code class=\"mm\">fast = 7</code>.</li><li>Iteration 4: <code class=\"mm\">slow = 5</code>, <code class=\"mm\">fast = NULL</code> because <code class=\"mm\">fast->next</code> of node 7 is node 8 and node 8's successor is NULL.</li><li>The test <code class=\"mm\">fast != NULL</code> now fails, so the loop stops with <code class=\"mm\">slow</code> on node 5.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: the node holding 5 — option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Expecting node 4 by assuming the slow pointer stops on the first middle node. For an even length the second middle node is reached, because the loop makes one further half-step before the fast pointer runs off the end.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For n nodes, this loop leaves <code class=\"mm\">slow</code> at position <span class=\"mm\">⌊n/2⌋ + 1</span>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q61", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "singly-doubly-circular-linked-lists", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Two sorted singly linked lists hold 6 and 9 nodes. They are merged into a single sorted list by repeatedly comparing the two current front nodes and appending the smaller one. What is the largest number of comparisons the merge can perform?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "14", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each comparison consumes exactly one node from one of the two lists, and the merge stops as soon as one list is exhausted — the remaining tail is appended without further comparisons.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Every comparison appends one node, so after <span class=\"mm\">m + n − 1</span> comparisons at most one node is still unplaced.</li><li>That last node needs no comparison, which is why the bound is <span class=\"mm\">m + n − 1</span>.</li><li>Here <span class=\"mm\">6 + 9 − 1 = 14</span>.</li><li>The bound is attained, for example, when the lists interleave and the final element of one list is larger than every element of the other.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 14.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 15 by assuming every node is compared. The last remaining node is appended without a comparison, so the count is one short of <span class=\"mm\">m + n</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Merging two sorted lists costs at most <span class=\"mm\">m + n − 1</span> comparisons and Θ(1) extra space when the nodes are relinked rather than copied.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q62", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "singly-doubly-circular-linked-lists", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about singly linked lists and arrays are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["A singly linked list of n elements occupies one contiguous block of memory, just as an array does.", "Reaching the k-th element of a singly linked list from the head costs Θ(k) time.", "Inserting a new node immediately after a node whose address is already known costs Θ(1) time.", "For n integers, a singly linked list always needs less memory than an array of n integers."], "answer_display": "Options (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An array buys constant-time indexing by storing its elements contiguously; a linked list buys constant-time insertion and deletion at a known position by paying one pointer per node.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) FALSE. Each node is allocated separately and may sit anywhere in the heap; only the links impose order.</li><li>(B) TRUE. There is no index arithmetic, so the list must be walked node by node — Θ(k) steps.</li><li>(C) TRUE. Two link writes complete the insertion, as long as the position is already known.</li><li>(D) FALSE. Each node carries at least one pointer, so a list of n integers needs n integers plus n pointers; for small element types the array is the smaller structure.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Treating the linked list as a compact structure. It trades memory and locality for cheap insertion and deletion; it is not the smaller representation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Array: Θ(1) index, Θ(n) insert. List: Θ(n) index, Θ(1) insert at a known node.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q63", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "singly-doubly-circular-linked-lists", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The loop below reverses a singly linked list in place. It starts with <code class=\"mm\">p = NULL</code> and <code class=\"mm\">q = head</code> on the list 1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5.</p><div style=\"font-family:Consolas,'DejaVu Sans Mono',monospace;font-size:13.4px;line-height:1.55;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:10px 12px;margin:0 0 12px;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\">while (q != NULL) {\n    r = q-&gt;next;\n    q-&gt;next = p;\n    p = q;\n    q = r;\n}</div><p class=\"qtext\">After exactly three iterations of the loop body, which one of the following is TRUE?</p>", "options": ["p points to the node holding 3, q points to the node holding 4, and the node holding 1 has next equal to NULL.", "p points to the node holding 4, q points to the node holding 5, and the node holding 1 has next equal to NULL.", "p points to the node holding 3, q points to the node holding 4, and the node holding 1 still points to 2.", "p is NULL and q points to the node holding 1."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The loop maintains three invariants: <code class=\"mm\">p</code> is the head of the part already reversed, <code class=\"mm\">q</code> is the head of the part not yet touched, and the reversed part ends in a NULL link. One iteration moves one node from the second part to the first.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Iteration 1: <code class=\"mm\">r = 2</code>, node 1 points to NULL, <code class=\"mm\">p = 1</code>, <code class=\"mm\">q = 2</code>.</li><li>Iteration 2: <code class=\"mm\">r = 3</code>, node 2 points to node 1, <code class=\"mm\">p = 2</code>, <code class=\"mm\">q = 3</code>.</li><li>Iteration 3: <code class=\"mm\">r = 4</code>, node 3 points to node 2, <code class=\"mm\">p = 3</code>, <code class=\"mm\">q = 4</code>.</li><li>The reversed prefix is 3 → 2 → 1 → NULL and the untouched suffix is 4 → 5.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reading <code class=\"mm\">p</code> as the node just processed and expecting it to be 4 after three iterations. It is the node processed in the third iteration, which is 3, and <code class=\"mm\">q</code> has already moved on to 4.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Three pointers are needed because the only link to the rest of the list is overwritten during the reversal; <code class=\"mm\">r</code> preserves it.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q64", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "stacks-applications-infix-postfix", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What is the prefix form of the infix expression <code class=\"mm\">A + B * C</code>?</p>", "options": ["+ * A B C", "+ A * B C", "* + A B C", "A + * B C"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In prefix (Polish) notation every operator precedes its two operands. The operator of lowest precedence sits outermost, so the root of the expression tree is written first.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The expression tree has <code class=\"mm\">+</code> at the root, with <code class=\"mm\">A</code> on the left and <code class=\"mm\">B * C</code> on the right.</li><li>Writing the root first: <code class=\"mm\">+</code>.</li><li>Then the left subtree, which is the single operand <code class=\"mm\">A</code>.</li><li>Then the right subtree in prefix form: <code class=\"mm\">* B C</code>.</li><li>Concatenating: <code class=\"mm\">+ A * B C</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">+ A * B C</code> — option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Option (A) puts the multiplication outermost, which would mean <code class=\"mm\">(A + B) * C</code>. The operator written first must be the last one evaluated.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Prefix writes operators before operands, postfix after; the tree is the same.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q65", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "stacks-applications-infix-postfix", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The prefix expression <code class=\"mm\">- * 5 4 + 3 2</code> is evaluated with a stack, where <code class=\"mm\">-</code>, <code class=\"mm\">*</code> and <code class=\"mm\">+</code> are binary operators. What is the integer result?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "15", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A prefix expression is evaluated right to left: a digit is pushed, and an operator pops its two operands, applies itself, and pushes the result. The first operand popped is the left one, then the right.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The tokens, read right to left, are <code class=\"mm\">2, 3, +, 4, 5, *, -</code>.</li><li>Push 2, then push 3. Reach <code class=\"mm\">+</code>: the first value popped is the left operand, so <span class=\"mm\">3 + 2 = 5</span> is pushed. The stack holds <span class=\"mm\">[5]</span>.</li><li>Push 4, then push 5, so the stack holds <span class=\"mm\">[5, 4, 5]</span> with 5 on top.</li><li>Reach <code class=\"mm\">*</code>: pop 5 (left) and 4 (right), push <span class=\"mm\">5 · 4 = 20</span>. The stack holds <span class=\"mm\">[5, 20]</span>.</li><li>Reach <code class=\"mm\">-</code>: pop 20 (left) and 5 (right), push <span class=\"mm\">20 − 5 = 15</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 15.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reading the expression left to right and pairing the operands by eye. A prefix expression must be scanned right to left, and the operand popped first is the left one — getting that backwards turns <span class=\"mm\">20 − 5</span> into <span class=\"mm\">5 − 20 = −15</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The expression means <code class=\"mm\">(5 * 4) - (3 + 2)</code>; the scan order, not the operand order in the text, decides which value is subtracted from which.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q66", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "stacks-applications-infix-postfix", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">What is the postfix form of <code class=\"mm\">(A - B) * (C + D) / E</code>?</p>", "options": ["A B - C D + * E /", "A B - C D + E / *", "A B - C + D * E /", "A B C D - + * E /"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The operator-stack algorithm outputs operands as they arrive, pushes operators, pops on a closing bracket and at the end. <code class=\"mm\">*</code> and <code class=\"mm\">/</code> have equal precedence and associate left to right, so <code class=\"mm\">*</code> is popped before <code class=\"mm\">/</code> is pushed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Read <code class=\"mm\">(A - B)</code>: output <code class=\"mm\">A B</code>, push <code class=\"mm\">-</code>, pop it on the closing bracket → <code class=\"mm\">A B -</code>.</li><li>Push <code class=\"mm\">*</code>.</li><li>Read <code class=\"mm\">(C + D)</code>: output <code class=\"mm\">C D</code>, push and pop <code class=\"mm\">+</code> → <code class=\"mm\">A B - C D +</code>.</li><li>The closing bracket pops <code class=\"mm\">*</code> → <code class=\"mm\">A B - C D + *</code>.</li><li>Push <code class=\"mm\">/</code>, output <code class=\"mm\">E</code>, and at the end pop <code class=\"mm\">/</code> → <code class=\"mm\">A B - C D + * E /</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">A B - C D + * E /</code> — option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Option (B) applies the division before the multiplication, which would compute <code class=\"mm\">(A - B) * ((C + D) / E)</code>. Equal precedence with left associativity forbids that.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> When precedences tie, the operator already on the stack is popped first.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q67", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "stacks-applications-infix-postfix", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Each capital letter is an operand and each of <code class=\"mm\">+ - * /</code> is a binary operator. Which of the following are well-formed postfix expressions, in which every operator has exactly two operands and the whole expression reduces to a single value?</p>", "options": ["A B + C *", "A B + * C", "A B C * +", "A + B C *"], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A postfix expression is well formed exactly when, scanning left to right, the count of operands always stays at least one ahead of the operators and ends at exactly one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) <code class=\"mm\">A B +</code> leaves one value; <code class=\"mm\">C</code> makes two; <code class=\"mm\">*</code> reduces them to one. Valid, and equals <code class=\"mm\">(A + B) * C</code>.</li><li>(B) <code class=\"mm\">A B +</code> leaves one value, and <code class=\"mm\">*</code> then needs two. Invalid.</li><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">B C *</code> leaves one value; <code class=\"mm\">A</code> makes two; <code class=\"mm\">+</code> reduces them to one. Valid, and equals <code class=\"mm\">A + (B * C)</code>.</li><li>(D) contains an operator before two operands have been read, which no postfix evaluation can use. Invalid.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Judging validity by counting operators and operands overall. The counts can balance while the running value count dips below one in the middle, as in <code class=\"mm\">A B + * C</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A postfix string is valid iff the running value count never drops below 1 and ends at 1.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q68", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "stacks-applications-infix-postfix", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">In an expression language where <code class=\"mm\">^</code> is right-associative and binds more tightly than <code class=\"mm\">*</code>, what is the postfix form of <code class=\"mm\">2 ^ 3 ^ 2 * 4</code>?</p>", "options": ["2 3 ^ 2 ^ 4 *", "2 3 2 ^ ^ 4 *", "2 3 2 4 * ^ ^", "2 3 ^ 2 4 * ^"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Right associativity groups a chain of equal-precedence operators from the right: <span class=\"mm\">2 ^ 3 ^ 2</span> means <span class=\"mm\">2 ^ (3 ^ 2)</span>, not <span class=\"mm\">(2 ^ 3) ^ 2</span>. The whole chain is one operand of the multiplication.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The expression is <span class=\"mm\">(2 ^ (3 ^ 2)) * 4</span>.</li><li>The inner power <span class=\"mm\">3 ^ 2</span> becomes postfix <code class=\"mm\">3 2 ^</code>.</li><li>The outer power takes <code class=\"mm\">2</code> and that result: <code class=\"mm\">2 3 2 ^ ^</code>.</li><li>The multiplication then takes that value and 4: <code class=\"mm\">2 3 2 ^ ^ 4 *</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">2 3 2 ^ ^ 4 *</code> — option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Option (A) is the left-associative reading <span class=\"mm\">(2 ^ 3) ^ 2</span>, which is a different number (<span class=\"mm\">64</span> against <span class=\"mm\">512</span>). With equal-precedence right-associative operators the stack is not popped until the whole chain has been read.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Right associativity delays the pop; left associativity forces it as soon as the next equal operator arrives.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q69", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "stacks-applications-infix-postfix", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A stack must support <code class=\"mm\">push</code>, <code class=\"mm\">pop</code> and <code class=\"mm\">getMin</code>, which returns the smallest value currently stored, all in Θ(1) worst-case time. Which of the following statements are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["Keeping a second stack that stores the running minimum alongside every element gives Θ(1) getMin at the cost of O(n) extra space.", "With only the main stack and no extra storage, getMin can still be answered in Θ(1) worst-case time.", "In that two-stack design every push writes one entry to each stack and every pop removes one entry from each.", "After a pop , getMin can still be answered in Θ(1) because the previous minimum is still held in the auxiliary stack."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A single stack does not remember the minimum of the elements below its top, so that information must be stored as the stack grows. The standard trick is a parallel stack of running minima.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) TRUE. The auxiliary stack holds one entry per element, so the extra space is O(n) and the top entry answers <code class=\"mm\">getMin</code> in one step.</li><li>(B) FALSE. Finding the minimum of the current contents means examining every element, which is Θ(n), not Θ(1).</li><li>(C) TRUE. Because the two stacks are kept in step, the top of the auxiliary stack always describes the minimum of exactly the elements currently in the main stack.</li><li>(D) TRUE. Popping the main stack pops the auxiliary stack too, and the new top is the minimum of the remaining elements — the answer before the pop was pushed earlier and is still there.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Storing only the current minimum. After that element is popped the older minimum is gone, which is exactly why one entry per element is needed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A running-minimum stack makes <code class=\"mm\">getMin</code> a peek, and every operation stays Θ(1).</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q70", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "queues-circular-queues-deques", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A circular queue uses an array of 10 slots numbered 0 to 9, leaves one slot permanently unused, and treats <code class=\"mm\">front == rear</code> as empty. Right now <code class=\"mm\">front = 3</code> and <code class=\"mm\">rear = 7</code>. How many elements are stored?</p>", "options": ["3", "5", "6", "4"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a circular queue with one slot left unused, the number of stored elements is the forward distance from <code class=\"mm\">front</code> to <code class=\"mm\">rear</code>, taken modulo the array size.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The forward distance is <span class=\"mm\">7 − 3 = 4</span>.</li><li>Because the distance is positive, no wrap is needed: <span class=\"mm\">(7 − 3 + 10) mod 10 = 4</span>.</li><li>The four elements occupy slots 3, 4, 5 and 6; slot 7 is the next free position.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4 — option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reporting 4 as \"slots 3 to 7\", which counts slot 7 as occupied. <code class=\"mm\">rear</code> is the *next free* position in this convention, not the last occupied one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Count = <span class=\"mm\">(rear − front + n) mod n</span> when one slot is kept free.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q71", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "queues-circular-queues-deques", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A circular queue is held in an array of <code class=\"mm\">n</code> slots and keeps a separate <code class=\"mm\">count</code> field. What is the largest number of elements it can hold?</p>", "options": ["n − 1", "n / 2", "n", "n + 1"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The unused slot exists only to let <code class=\"mm\">front == rear</code> mean \"empty\" without ambiguity. A separate counter removes that ambiguity, so every slot can be used.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Without a counter, a full queue and an empty queue can both have <code class=\"mm\">front == rear</code>, so one slot must stay free.</li><li>With a <code class=\"mm\">count</code> field, full means <code class=\"mm\">count == n</code> and empty means <code class=\"mm\">count == 0</code> — two different tests.</li><li>Therefore all <code class=\"mm\">n</code> slots are usable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">n</code> — option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Carrying over the <code class=\"mm\">n − 1</code> capacity of the slot-free implementation. The counter is exactly what buys the extra slot back.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Capacity is <code class=\"mm\">n − 1</code> without a counter and <code class=\"mm\">n</code> with one.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q72", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "queues-circular-queues-deques", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A circular queue uses an array of 6 slots numbered 0 to 5 and keeps a <code class=\"mm\">count</code> field. <code class=\"mm\">front</code> is 4 and <code class=\"mm\">count</code> is 5. What is <code class=\"mm\">rear</code>, the index at which the most recent element was inserted?</p>", "options": ["1", "2", "3", "5"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The five stored elements occupy consecutive slots starting at <code class=\"mm\">front</code>, so the last one sits <span class=\"mm\">count − 1</span> steps further on, modulo the array size.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Elements are at <span class=\"mm\">(4 + 0) mod 6 = 4</span>, <span class=\"mm\">(4 + 1) mod 6 = 5</span>, <span class=\"mm\">(4 + 2) mod 6 = 0</span>, <span class=\"mm\">(4 + 3) mod 6 = 1</span> and <span class=\"mm\">(4 + 4) mod 6 = 2</span>.</li><li>The most recent element is the last of these, at index 2.</li><li>Equivalently <span class=\"mm\">(front + count − 1) mod 6 = 8 mod 6 = 2</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2 — option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Computing <span class=\"mm\">(front + count) mod 6 = 3</span>, which is the next *free* slot rather than the last inserted element.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> With a counter: last index = <span class=\"mm\">(front + count − 1) mod n</span>, next free = <span class=\"mm\">(front + count) mod n</span>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q73", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "queues-circular-queues-deques", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Ten people numbered 1 to 10 wait in a queue in that order. The following step is repeated until one person is left: the person at the front is removed and eliminated, and then the next two people at the front are moved, in order, to the back of the queue. Which numbered person remains?</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A queue performs this elimination in the order the process describes, because moving a person to the back is exactly the rotation a circular queue supports in Θ(1).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Eliminated in order: 1, 4, 7, 10, 5, 9, 6, 3, 8.</li><li>The queue is rotated after each elimination, so after eliminating 1 the order becomes 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 2, 3.</li><li>Continuing the same way removes nine people, and 2 is never reached at the front while more than one person remains.</li><li>The last person left is 2.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Eliminating the first person and then also skipping them, or rotating only one person per step. The step says: eliminate the front, then move exactly two people.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> This is Josephus elimination with step 3, done with a queue in Θ(n) rotations overall.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q74", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "queues-circular-queues-deques", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A deque is held in a circular array of <code class=\"mm\">n</code> slots with one slot left unused, using <code class=\"mm\">front</code> and <code class=\"mm\">rear</code> indices taken modulo <code class=\"mm\">n</code>. Which of the following statements are TRUE?</p>", "options": ["Every insertion forces the array to be reallocated, because elements can arrive at either end.", "pop_back moves rear one slot backwards modulo n , in Θ(1) time.", "push_front moves front one slot backwards modulo n and writes the new element there, in Θ(1) time.", "Used with push_back and pop_back only, the deque behaves exactly as a stack."], "answer_display": "Options (B), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A circular array supports growth at both ends because \"one slot backwards\" is just a decrement modulo <code class=\"mm\">n</code>; no element ever has to move.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) FALSE. Reallocation happens only when the deque is full, exactly as for a circular queue; individual insertions at either end are Θ(1).</li><li>(B) TRUE. <code class=\"mm\">rear</code> is the next free slot at the back, so removing from the back steps it backwards: <code class=\"mm\">rear = (rear - 1 + n) % n</code>.</li><li>(C) TRUE. <code class=\"mm\">front</code> steps backwards in the same way, and the new element is written at the new <code class=\"mm\">front</code>.</li><li>(D) TRUE. Restricting the operations to one end removes the deque's extra freedom, and the last-in first-out discipline of <code class=\"mm\">push_back</code>/<code class=\"mm\">pop_back</code> is that of a stack.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (B), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming a deque must shift elements when it grows at the front. The modular index does the work, so the cost is constant.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A deque in a circular array is a queue that can move <code class=\"mm\">front</code> in either direction.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U3-Q75", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "linear-data-structures", "subtopic": "queues-circular-queues-deques", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A queue is stored in a circular singly linked list, and the only pointer kept is <code class=\"mm\">rear</code>, which points to the last node, so that <code class=\"mm\">rear->next</code> is the front node. Which one of the following is TRUE?</p>", "options": ["Enqueue is Θ(1) but dequeue is Θ(n).", "Dequeue is Θ(1) but enqueue is Θ(n).", "Both enqueue and dequeue are Θ(n).", "Both enqueue and dequeue are Θ(1)."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A circular list makes the last node and the first node neighbours, so a single pointer to the last node gives immediate access to both ends.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Enqueue: create a node, link it after <code class=\"mm\">rear</code>, then set <code class=\"mm\">rear</code> to the new node — a fixed number of writes.</li><li>Dequeue: the front is <code class=\"mm\">rear->next</code>, so unlink that node by setting <code class=\"mm\">rear->next = front->next</code> — again a fixed number of writes.</li><li>The one special case is a single-element queue, where the front and the rear are the same node; the code then has to reset <code class=\"mm\">rear</code> to NULL, which is still Θ(1).</li><li>Neither operation walks the list, so both are Θ(1).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Expecting dequeue to be expensive because a plain singly linked list with only a head pointer cannot reach the tail cheaply. Here the pointer is to the *rear* of a *circular* list, which makes the front the next node along.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> One pointer suffices for both ends when the list is circular: keep it at the rear.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S3-Q01", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "binary-tree-properties", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "The height of a binary tree is the number of edges on the longest path from the root down to a leaf, and the root sits at level 0. What is the largest number of nodes a binary tree of height 6 can have?", "options": ["63", "64", "127", "128"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Level <i>i</i> of a binary tree holds at most 2<sup><i>i</i></sup> nodes, so a tree of height <i>h</i> holds at most 2<sup><i>h</i>+1</sup> &minus; 1 nodes, the count for a perfect tree in which every level is completely filled.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Level 0 holds 1 node, level 1 holds at most 2, level 2 at most 4, and so on up to level 6.</li><li>The sum is 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16 + 32 + 64, a geometric series with 7 terms.</li><li>1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16 + 32 + 64 = 2<sup>7</sup> &minus; 1 = 127.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">127 nodes, option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 64, the capacity of the last level alone, or 128, which is 2<sup>7</sup> and counts one level too many. The exponent is <i>h</i> + 1 because levels are numbered from 0.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S3-Q02", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "binary-tree-properties", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Every node of a binary tree has either 0 or 2 children. The tree has 32 leaves. How many nodes does the tree have?", "options": ["62", "63", "64", "65"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In any binary tree the number of leaves exceeds the number of two-child nodes by exactly one: <i>n</i><sub>0</sub> = <i>n</i><sub>2</sub> + 1. A tree in which no node has exactly one child has no <i>n</i><sub>1</sub> nodes at all, so its node count is <i>n</i><sub>0</sub> + <i>n</i><sub>2</sub>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><i>n</i><sub>0</sub> = 32, so <i>n</i><sub>2</sub> = 31.</li><li>There are no one-child nodes, so the total is 32 + 31 = 63.</li><li>Check with the general form for a full binary tree: <i>n</i> = 2<i>n</i><sub>0</sub> &minus; 1 = 64 &minus; 1 = 63.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">63 nodes, option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 64 by doubling the leaves. A full binary tree has 2<i>n</i><sub>0</sub> &minus; 1 nodes, not 2<i>n</i><sub>0</sub>, because the root is counted once in both halves of the doubling.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S3-Q03", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "binary-tree-properties", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A complete binary tree has 200 nodes. The root is at level 0, and every level except possibly the last is completely filled, with the last level filled from left to right. How many nodes lie on the last level?", "options": ["72", "73", "74", "100"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A complete binary tree fills its levels in order, so every level before the last is perfect. Level <i>i</i> holds 2<sup><i>i</i></sup> nodes, and the nodes on the last level are simply what is left over after the full levels above it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Levels 0 to 6 are complete: 2<sup>7</sup> &minus; 1 = 127 nodes.</li><li>127 &le; 200 &lt; 255 = 2<sup>8</sup> &minus; 1, so the last level is level 7.</li><li>Nodes on level 7 = 200 &minus; 127 = 73, and 73 &le; 128, so the level is partially filled as required.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">73 nodes, option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Subtracting 128 instead of 127, which answers 72 and treats the first seven levels as a perfect tree of height 6 in the other convention. With the root at level 0, levels 0&ndash;6 hold 127 nodes, not 128.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S3-Q04", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "binary-tree-properties", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A binary tree has 100 nodes. Its height is the number of edges on the longest root-to-leaf path, with the root at level 0. What is the minimum possible height?", "options": [], "answer_display": "6", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A tree of height <i>h</i> holds at most 2<sup><i>h</i>+1</sup> &minus; 1 nodes, so the shortest tree that can hold <i>n</i> nodes is the one whose capacity first reaches <i>n</i>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>At height 5 the capacity is 2<sup>6</sup> &minus; 1 = 63, which is less than 100.</li><li>At height 6 the capacity is 2<sup>7</sup> &minus; 1 = 127, which is at least 100.</li><li>So a complete tree with its 100 nodes packed into levels 0 to 6 works, and no tree of height 5 can hold them.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">6.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 7 by taking log<sub>2</sub> 100 &asymp; 6.64 and rounding up. That rounds up the height <i>plus one</i>; the level count is 7, but the height in edges is 6.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S3-Q05", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "binary-tree-properties", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A binary tree has 40 nodes, exactly 15 of which have exactly one child. How many leaves does the tree have?", "options": ["12", "13", "14", "15"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two facts fix the shape counts of a binary tree: the three counts add to the total, <i>n</i><sub>0</sub> + <i>n</i><sub>1</sub> + <i>n</i><sub>2</sub> = <i>n</i>, and the leaves exceed the two-child nodes by one, <i>n</i><sub>0</sub> = <i>n</i><sub>2</sub> + 1.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>With <i>n</i><sub>1</sub> = 15: <i>n</i><sub>0</sub> + <i>n</i><sub>2</sub> = 40 &minus; 15 = 25.</li><li>Substituting <i>n</i><sub>2</sub> = <i>n</i><sub>0</sub> &minus; 1 gives 2<i>n</i><sub>0</sub> &minus; 1 = 25, so <i>n</i><sub>0</sub> = 13 and <i>n</i><sub>2</sub> = 12.</li><li>Check: 13 + 15 + 12 = 40.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">13 leaves, option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Halving the 25 remaining nodes and answering 12 or 13 by eye. The leaf rule <i>n</i><sub>0</sub> = <i>n</i><sub>2</sub> + 1 makes the split uneven, and it is the only thing that decides which of the two neighbours is correct.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S3-Q06", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "binary-tree-properties", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A binary tree has 1000 nodes. What is the largest possible number of leaves it can have?", "options": ["499", "500", "501", "999"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Leaves are bought with internal nodes. If a tree has <i>L</i> leaves, it has exactly <i>L</i> &minus; 1 nodes with two children, and the remaining internal nodes must have exactly one child. Every one-child node costs a slot that could otherwise have been a leaf, so the leaf count is maximised by using as few of them as possible.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Write the total as <i>n</i> = <i>L</i> + (<i>L</i> &minus; 1) + <i>y</i>, where <i>y</i> is the number of one-child nodes.</li><li>So <i>L</i> = (<i>n</i> + 1 &minus; <i>y</i>)/2, which is largest when <i>y</i> is as small as possible.</li><li>With <i>n</i> = 1000, <i>y</i> = 0 would need <i>L</i> = 500.5, which is not an integer, so the smallest legal <i>y</i> is 1.</li><li>Then <i>L</i> = (1000 + 1 &minus; 1)/2 = 500, with 499 two-child nodes and 1 one-child node, and 500 + 499 + 1 = 1000.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">500 leaves, option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming a chain of 1000 nodes has the most leaves; it has exactly one. Leaves and internal nodes alternate, so roughly half the nodes can be leaves at best, and the parity of <i>n</i> decides whether the half rounds down.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S3-Q07", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "binary-tree-properties", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A complete binary tree with <i>n</i> nodes is stored in a 1-indexed array <code class=\"mm\">A[1..n]</code>, level by level and left to right within a level. Which of the following statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The children of <code class=\"mm\">A[i]</code> are <code class=\"mm\">A[2i]</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A[2i+1]</code>, whenever those indices do not exceed <i>n</i>.", "For <i>i</i> &gt; 1, the parent of <code class=\"mm\">A[i]</code> is <code class=\"mm\">A[&lfloor;i/2&rfloor;]</code>.", "The last internal node, that is the parent of <code class=\"mm\">A[n]</code>, is <code class=\"mm\">A[&lfloor;n/2&rfloor;]</code>.", "In a complete binary tree with 12 nodes, <code class=\"mm\">A[6]</code> is a leaf."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The array layout is a level-order numbering, and level-order numbering makes the index arithmetic exact: doubling an index moves to the next level, and halving it moves back to the parent.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is the definition of the layout: the two children of the <i>i</i>-th node occupy positions 2<i>i</i> and 2<i>i</i>+1. True.</li><li>(B) inverts the same rule, since integer division by 2 maps both 2<i>i</i> and 2<i>i</i>+1 back to <i>i</i>. True.</li><li>(C) follows: the parent of the last node is the deepest index that still has a child, and &lfloor;<i>n</i>/2&rfloor; is exactly that index. True.</li><li>(D) with <i>n</i> = 12, &lfloor;12/2&rfloor; = 6, so <code class=\"mm\">A[6]</code> is the last internal node; its left child is <code class=\"mm\">A[12]</code>. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Calling <code class=\"mm\">A[n/2]</code> the last leaf. It is the last node that still has a child, so it is internal; the leaves begin at &lfloor;<i>n</i>/2&rfloor; + 1 and run to <i>n</i>.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S3-Q08", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "tree-traversals-inorder-pre-post", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A complete binary tree with 7 nodes is stored as <code class=\"mm\">A[1..7]</code> with the root at <code class=\"mm\">A[1]</code>, and the nodes hold the keys A, B, C, D, E, F, G in that array order. Which traversal visits the nodes in the order D B E A F C G?", "options": ["Preorder", "Inorder", "Postorder", "Level order"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The array order is the level order, so the root is A with children B and C; B has children D and E, and C has children F and G. An inorder traversal visits the left subtree, then the node, then the right subtree.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Left subtree of A is rooted at B, whose left subtree is D and right subtree is E: inorder gives D B E.</li><li>Then the root A.</li><li>Right subtree of A is rooted at C, whose children are F and G: inorder gives F C G.</li><li>Concatenating: D B E A F C G.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Inorder, option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Choosing preorder because the sequence begins with a leaf. Preorder would start at the root and read A B D E C F G; postorder would end at the root and read D E B F G C A.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S3-Q09", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "tree-traversals-inorder-pre-post", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A binary tree with distinct keys has preorder K D B A C F E M L P and inorder A B C D E F K L M P. What is its postorder traversal?", "options": ["A C B E F D L P M K", "A B C D E F K L M P", "C A B F E D P L M K", "K D B A C F E M L P"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Preorder names the root first, so its first key is the root of the whole tree; that root splits the inorder sequence into a left part and a right part. Applying the same rule to each part rebuilds the tree, and postorder then reads the rebuilt tree as left, right, root.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Root K. Inorder splits into left {A B C D E F} and right {L M P}.</li><li>Left part, preorder D B A C F E: root D, its left {A B C} rooted at B with children A and C, its right {F E} rooted at F with left child E.</li><li>Right part, preorder M L P: root M with left child L and right child P.</li><li>Postorder of the left subtree is A C B E F D, of the right subtree L P M, then the root K.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">A C B E F D L P M K, option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reading the answer off the inorder sequence because it happens to be alphabetical. Inorder is a projection of the tree, not a substitute for rebuilding it; only the two sequences together fix the shape.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S3-Q10", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "tree-traversals-inorder-pre-post", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A binary tree with distinct keys has preorder K D B A C F E M L P and inorder A B C D E F K L M P. Its height, counted in edges with the root at level 0, is", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Rebuilding the tree from a preorder and inorder pair fixes the shape completely, and the height is then read off the rebuilt tree.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Root K, left subtree {A B C D E F} rooted at D, right subtree {L M P} rooted at M.</li><li>Under D, the left subtree {A B C} is rooted at B with leaves A and C, and the right subtree {F E} is rooted at F with left child E.</li><li>The longest root-to-leaf paths are K&ndash;D&ndash;B&ndash;A, K&ndash;D&ndash;B&ndash;C, K&ndash;D&ndash;F&ndash;E and K&ndash;M&ndash;L, all of length 3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">3.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting nodes instead of edges on the longest path, which gives 4. With the root at level 0 the height is the largest level number, and level 3 is the deepest one here.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S3-Q11", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "tree-traversals-inorder-pre-post", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A binary tree with distinct keys has preorder K D B A C F E M L P and inorder A B C D E F K L M P. How many of its nodes have exactly one child?", "options": [], "answer_display": "1", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A node has exactly one child when, in the rebuilt tree, one of its two positions is empty and the other is not. The traversals must be used to rebuild the shape first; the counts then follow directly.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Root K has children D and M: two children.</li><li>D has B and F; B has A and C; M has L and P: all two children.</li><li>F has only the left child E: exactly one child.</li><li>A, C, E, L and P are leaves. So exactly one node, F, has a single child.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">1.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting leaves or counting the internal nodes. The question asks for the third category, the nodes with a single child, which is what <i>n</i><sub>1</sub> measures.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S3-Q12", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "tree-traversals-inorder-pre-post", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A binary tree with distinct keys has preorder 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 and inorder 3 2 4 1 6 5 7. Its postorder traversal is", "options": ["3 4 2 6 7 5 1", "4 3 2 7 6 5 1", "3 2 4 6 5 7 1", "2 3 4 5 6 7 1"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The first key of the preorder is the root, and its position in the inorder splits the remaining keys into the left subtree and the right subtree. Recursing on each part rebuilds the tree, and postorder reads it as left, right, root.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Root 1. Inorder splits into left {3 2 4} and right {6 5 7}.</li><li>Left part, preorder 2 3 4: root 2 with left child 3 and right child 4.</li><li>Right part, preorder 5 6 7: root 5 with left child 6 and right child 7.</li><li>Postorder: 3, 4, 2 for the left; 6, 7, 5 for the right; then 1.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">3 4 2 6 7 5 1, option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reversing the preorder, which would give 7 6 5 4 3 2 1. Postorder is not the reverse of preorder; it is the reverse of the order in which the preorder finishes each subtree, which is a different thing.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S3-Q13", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "tree-traversals-inorder-pre-post", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Every node of a binary tree has either 0 or 2 children. Its preorder traversal is A B C D E F G and its postorder traversal is C D B F G E A. Its inorder traversal is", "options": ["C B D A F E G", "C D B A F G E", "B C D A E F G", "C B D A F G E"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a tree where every node has 0 or 2 children, the two subtrees of the root are each complete blocks in both sequences, so the split can be found without an inorder sequence: the second key of the preorder is the root of the left subtree, and its position in the postorder marks the end of that subtree.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Root A. The left subtree starts at B in the preorder, and B is the third key of the postorder, so the left subtree is {B C D} and the right is {E F G}.</li><li>Left subtree: preorder B C D, postorder C D B. Root B, and C is the left child, D the right child, so its inorder is C B D.</li><li>Right subtree: preorder E F G, postorder F G E. Root E, with F and G as its children, so its inorder is F E G.</li><li>Full inorder: C B D, then A, then F E G.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">C B D A F E G, option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming preorder plus postorder never determines a tree. It fails in general only because a node with a single child looks identical on both sides; the no-one-child condition removes exactly that ambiguity.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S3-Q14", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "tree-traversals-inorder-pre-post", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following statements about the memory a tree traversal needs are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A recursive inorder traversal uses recursion-stack space proportional to the height of the tree.", "An iterative inorder traversal with an explicit stack also needs O(h) space, where h is the height.", "Morris inorder traversal needs neither a stack nor parent pointers, at the cost of temporarily rewriting some right pointers.", "Level-order traversal needs no auxiliary memory, because every node is visited exactly once."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Every traversal that must remember where to come back to pays for that memory. Recursion hides the memory in the call stack, an explicit stack makes it visible, and Morris threading borrows the tree's own empty pointers instead.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) the recursion depth is the height, so the stack holds <i>h</i> frames. True.</li><li>(B) the explicit stack holds at most one frame per level, again O(<i>h</i>). True.</li><li>(C) Morris stores the successor in the empty right pointer of the predecessor and restores it on the way back, so its extra space is O(1). True.</li><li>(D) level order needs a queue that can hold a whole level, so it uses O(w) space where w is the width. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Confusing &ldquo;each node is visited once&rdquo; with &ldquo;nothing is stored&rdquo;. Visiting each node once is a statement about time; the queue still has to hold every node of a level before it is expanded.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S3-Q15", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "tree-traversals-inorder-pre-post", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following statements about level-order traversal are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["With a FIFO queue, the largest number of nodes the queue ever holds equals the maximum number of nodes on any single level.", "Level-order traversal of a binary tree needs O(w) auxiliary space, where w is the maximum width of the tree.", "For a tree with distinct keys, the level-order traversal together with the inorder traversal determines the tree uniquely.", "For a complete binary tree, level-order traversal and preorder traversal produce the same sequence."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Level order is a breadth-first sweep: a FIFO queue holds the frontier, so its size tracks the width of the tree rather than its height. Because it names the root first, it carries the same kind of information as a preorder traversal.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) the queue holds exactly the nodes of the current level plus the part of the next level already generated, and its peak is the widest level. True.</li><li>(B) that peak is w, so the space bound is O(w). True.</li><li>(C) the first key of the level order is the root; the inorder sequence then splits into the two subtrees, and the remaining level order keys split accordingly, so the recursion determines the tree. True.</li><li>(D) for the 7-node complete tree the level order is A B C D E F G while the preorder is A B D E C F G. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Believing that only a depth-first traversal can pin down a tree. Any traversal that reveals the root of every subtree together with the inorder sequence is enough; level order reveals the root of every subtree, level by level.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S3-Q16", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "threaded-binary-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "In a fully inorder-threaded binary tree, how is the inorder successor of a node that has a non-empty right child found?", "options": ["By following the node's right thread", "By taking the leftmost node of its right subtree, following left links", "By taking the rightmost node of its left subtree", "By moving to the parent and then to the parent's right child"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Threads replace only the pointers that would otherwise be empty. A node that really has a right child keeps an ordinary link there, so its successor lies inside its own right subtree, at the first node visited there.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>If the right pointer is a real link, the successor is the smallest key greater than the node, which is the leftmost node of the right subtree.</li><li>Follow left links from the right child until a node whose left pointer is a thread is reached; that node is the successor.</li><li>Only when the right pointer is a thread does the successor come straight from the stored thread.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">The leftmost node of its right subtree, option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Following the right pointer blindly, which jumps into the subtree rather than to the successor. In a threaded tree the right pointer means two different things depending on its tag bit, and only the tag says which.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S3-Q17", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "threaded-binary-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A fully inorder-threaded binary tree uses a dummy header node whose left link points to the root and whose right link points to itself. The right thread of the last node in the inorder sequence points to", "options": ["the root", "the header node", "NULL", "the first node in the inorder sequence"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The header node exists precisely so that no thread is ever NULL: every empty pointer is redirected, and the two ends of the inorder sequence wrap around to the header, which makes traversal terminate on a single test.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The last node in inorder has no right child, so its right pointer is a thread.</li><li>That thread must name the successor of the last node, and the successor is the header, which stands for &ldquo;past the end&rdquo;.</li><li>Symmetrically, the left thread of the first node points to the header, and the header's own left link is the root.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">The header node, option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering NULL, which is what a tree without a header does. The header is what buys the O(1) termination test and the ability to find the predecessor of the first node.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S3-Q18", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "threaded-binary-trees", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A binary tree has root 50. The left child of 50 is 30, whose children are 20 and 40; the right child of 50 is 70, whose children are 60 and 80. The tree is stored as a fully inorder-threaded binary tree. How many of its right pointers are threads?", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A right pointer becomes a thread exactly when the node has no right child, so counting right threads is counting nodes with an empty right link.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The leaves are 20, 40, 60 and 80, and none of them has a right child.</li><li>30, 50 and 70 each have a right child, so their right pointers stay ordinary links.</li><li>So 4 right pointers are threads. Cross-check: the tree has 7 nodes, a fully threaded tree has <i>n</i> + 1 = 8 threads in total, and the 4 left threads belong to the same four leaves.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">4.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting only the leaves that are right children, or answering 8 by counting both directions. The question fixes the direction, and only the four leaves are missing a right child.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S3-Q19", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "threaded-binary-trees", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following statements about threaded binary trees are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Each pointer field needs a one-bit tag, or an equivalent encoding, to distinguish a thread from a real child link.", "In a fully threaded binary tree with n nodes, exactly n + 1 pointers are threads.", "An inorder-threaded tree can be traversed in inorder without recursion and without an auxiliary stack.", "Inorder threading by itself also removes the need for a stack in a preorder traversal."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Threading replaces the <i>n</i> + 1 empty pointers of an <i>n</i>-node tree with links to predecessors and successors. Because a pointer can now mean two things, the node must record which meaning applies.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) without a tag the traversal cannot tell &ldquo;go down&rdquo; from &ldquo;jump to successor&rdquo;. True.</li><li>(B) a binary tree with <i>n</i> nodes has 2<i>n</i> pointers of which <i>n</i> &minus; 1 are real links, leaving <i>n</i> + 1 empty ones. True.</li><li>(C) that is the whole purpose of inorder threading. True.</li><li>(D) preorder needs to remember a right subtree while descending into a left one, and inorder threads do not encode that. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming threads serve every traversal equally. They encode the inorder neighbour only, so any traversal that needs a different kind of &ldquo;what comes next&rdquo; still needs its own stack.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S3-Q20", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "threaded-binary-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A binary tree is threaded only on the right: every empty right pointer is replaced by a thread to the inorder successor, and the left pointers are left as ordinary links. Which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["The tree can be traversed in inorder without a stack, but finding the inorder predecessor of an arbitrary node still needs a stack or a parent pointer.", "Neither inorder nor reverse-inorder traversal can be done without a stack.", "Both inorder and reverse-inorder traversal can be done without a stack, because the threads cover both directions.", "Right threading turns the tree into a doubly linked list, so both directions cost O(1)."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A thread stores one neighbour. Right threading stores the successor, so the inorder walk is a chain of threads and real links; nothing stores the predecessor, and the left links only point downwards.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Successor of a node: if its right pointer is a thread, follow it; otherwise descend to the leftmost node of the right subtree. Either way O(1) amortised per step, so the inorder walk needs no stack.</li><li>Predecessor of a node: the information is not stored anywhere. You must either keep a stack of the path or store parent pointers.</li><li>Hence one direction is stack-free and the other is not.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reading threads as if they were symmetric. A fully threaded tree stores both neighbours and costs two tag bits; a single-threaded tree buys half the memory and gives up the reverse direction.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S3-Q21", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "expression-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "In the expression tree of <code class=\"mm\">a + b * c</code>, where <code class=\"mm\">*</code> binds tighter than <code class=\"mm\">+</code>, the root node holds", "options": ["+", "*", "a", "b"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An expression tree is built from the outside in: the operator applied last sits at the root, and its operands become the two subtrees.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Precedence makes <code class=\"mm\">b * c</code> a single operand of the addition.</li><li>So the expression is <code class=\"mm\">a + (b * c)</code>, and the last operator applied is <code class=\"mm\">+</code>.</li><li>The root is therefore <code class=\"mm\">+</code>, with <code class=\"mm\">a</code> as the left subtree and the multiplication as the right subtree.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">+, option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Putting <code class=\"mm\">*</code> at the root because it is the first operator read from the left. The root is the operator of lowest precedence, not the first one encountered.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S3-Q22", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "expression-trees", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Evaluate the prefix expression <code class=\"mm\">* + 2 3 &minus; 8 4</code>. Every value is an exact integer.", "options": [], "answer_display": "20", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A prefix expression is read left to right with a stack: an operand is pushed, and an operator pops its two operands and pushes the result. Equivalently, the first operator after the leading operator owns the next two complete sub-expressions.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">+ 2 3</code> evaluates to 5.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">&minus; 8 4</code> evaluates to 4.</li><li>The leading <code class=\"mm\">*</code> multiplies them: 5 &times; 4 = 20.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">20.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Evaluating the subtraction as 4 &minus; 8. In prefix form the operator's first operand is the left one, so <code class=\"mm\">&minus; 8 4</code> is 8 &minus; 4; reading operands in the wrong order silently negates the whole expression.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S3-Q23", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "expression-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "The prefix expression <code class=\"mm\">* &minus; a b + c d</code> represents which infix expression?", "options": ["a &minus; b * c + d", "(a &minus; b) * (c + d)", "a &minus; (b * (c + d))", "((a &minus; b) * c) + d"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Prefix form fixes the grouping completely, so the infix form can be recovered by reading the tree that the prefix string encodes: the leading operator is the root and each of its two operands is itself a complete prefix expression.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The root is <code class=\"mm\">*</code>.</li><li>Its left operand is <code class=\"mm\">&minus; a b</code>, which is <code class=\"mm\">(a &minus; b)</code>.</li><li>Its right operand is <code class=\"mm\">+ c d</code>, which is <code class=\"mm\">(c + d)</code>.</li><li>So the expression is <code class=\"mm\">(a &minus; b) * (c + d)</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">(a &minus; b) * (c + d), option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Dropping the parentheses and relying on precedence, which changes the meaning to <code class=\"mm\">a &minus; (b * c) + d</code>. Prefix form is fully parenthesised by construction; the brackets are not decoration.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S3-Q24", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "expression-trees", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following statements about expression trees are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The postfix form of an expression is exactly the postorder traversal of its expression tree.", "The prefix form is the preorder traversal, and the inorder traversal gives the infix form without its parentheses.", "A postfix expression of length m can be evaluated with a single stack in O(m) time.", "An expression tree for a valid infix expression with n binary operators has exactly n leaves."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The three notations are the three traversals of one tree: prefix is preorder, infix is inorder and postfix is postorder. That is why a stack suffices to evaluate a postfix string, and why an expression tree for <i>n</i> operators has <i>n</i> + 1 operands.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) postorder visits both children before the operator, which is exactly postfix. True.</li><li>(B) preorder visits the operator first, and inorder puts it between the operands. True.</li><li>(C) each token is pushed or popped once, so the work is linear. True.</li><li>(D) a binary operator has two operands, so <i>n</i> operators need <i>n</i> + 1 leaves, not <i>n</i>. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting one leaf per operator. Every internal node of a full binary tree has two children, so leaves outnumber operators by exactly one.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U4-S3-Q25", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "trees-traversals", "subtopic": "expression-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Consider the expression tree of <code class=\"mm\">(a + b) * (c &minus; d)</code>, in which every internal node is a binary operator. Using the Sethi&ndash;Ullman labelling, what is the minimum number of registers needed to evaluate the tree without spilling?", "options": ["1", "2", "3", "4"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The Sethi&ndash;Ullman label of a leaf is 1. An internal node whose two children have equal labels <i>l</i> gets <i>l</i> + 1, because one child's result must be held in a register while the other is computed; if the labels differ, the node takes the larger of the two, because the more demanding subtree is evaluated first and its result kept. The root's label is the number of registers needed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Leaves a, b, c, d all carry label 1.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">+</code> has children labelled 1 and 1, so it takes label 2. Likewise <code class=\"mm\">&minus;</code> takes label 2.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">*</code> has children labelled 2 and 2, so it takes 2 + 1 = 3.</li><li>Three registers suffice: compute (a + b) into R1 using R2, then compute (c &minus; d) into R2 using R3 while R1 is held, then multiply into R3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">3, option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 2 because each subtree alone needs only 2. The two subtrees overlap in time: the left result must stay live while the right one is computed, and that extra live value is the third register.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q51", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "bst-insertion-deletion", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "The keys 50, 25, 75, 12, 37, 62 and 87 are inserted into an empty binary search tree in that order. How many key comparisons does a successful search for the key 37 perform?", "options": ["3", "2", "4", "5"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A search compares the target with one key per node on the path from the root down to the node that holds it, so the number of comparisons is the depth of that node plus one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The root holds 50. Compare 37 with 50: <code class=\"mm\">37 &lt; 50</code>, so go left. One comparison.</li><li>The left child is 25. Compare 37 with 25: <code class=\"mm\">37 &gt; 25</code>, so go right. Two comparisons.</li><li>The right child of 25 is 37. Compare 37 with 37: equal, found. Three comparisons.</li><li>37 sits at depth 2, so the comparison count is <code class=\"mm\">2 + 1 = 3</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">3 comparisons, option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 2 by counting the edges walked instead of the keys compared. The root itself is one comparison, and the node where the search succeeds is another.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For a tree of height <i>h</i>, a successful search needs between 1 and <i>h</i> + 1 comparisons.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q52", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "bst-insertion-deletion", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "The keys 10, 20, 30 and 40 are inserted into an empty binary search tree in that order. Taking the height as the number of edges on the longest root-to-leaf path, what is the height of the resulting tree?", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A binary search tree built from keys that arrive in increasing order degenerates into a chain: every new key is larger than everything already stored, so it always descends the right spine.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>10 becomes the root.</li><li>20 &gt; 10, so 20 becomes the right child of 10.</li><li>30 &gt; 10 and 30 &gt; 20, so 30 becomes the right child of 20.</li><li>40 becomes the right child of 30. The tree is the path 10 &rarr; 20 &rarr; 30 &rarr; 40.</li><li>That path has three edges, so the height is 3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">3.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 4 by counting the nodes on the path. Height counts edges, so a four-node chain has height 3; counting levels would give 4 as well, which is the same mistake in a different disguise.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Sorted input is the worst case for a plain BST: height <i>n</i> &minus; 1 and every operation &Theta;(<i>n</i>).</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q53", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "bst-insertion-deletion", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which one of the following insertion orders of the keys 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 produces a binary search tree of height 2, the height being the number of edges on the longest root-to-leaf path?", "options": ["1, 2, 3, 4, 5", "3, 2, 1, 5, 4", "5, 4, 3, 2, 1", "1, 3, 5, 2, 4"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The shape of a binary search tree is fixed entirely by the insertion order, and the height is minimised when the first key is a middle key, so that both sides of the root receive keys.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) <code class=\"mm\">1, 2, 3, 4, 5</code>: each key is larger than the last, giving the chain 1 &rarr; 2 &rarr; 3 &rarr; 4 &rarr; 5 and height 4.</li><li>(B) <code class=\"mm\">3, 2, 1, 5, 4</code>: root 3, left child 2 with left child 1, right child 5 with left child 4. The longest path 3 &rarr; 2 &rarr; 1 has two edges, so the height is 2.</li><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">5, 4, 3, 2, 1</code>: the mirror chain, height 4.</li><li>(D) <code class=\"mm\">1, 3, 5, 2, 4</code>: root 1, right child 3, whose children are 2 and 5, and 5 has left child 4. The path 1 &rarr; 3 &rarr; 5 &rarr; 4 has three edges, so the height is 3.</li><li>Only (B) reaches height 2, which is also the smallest possible for five nodes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">3, 2, 1, 5, 4 &mdash; option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Judging a sequence by how sorted it looks. Sequence (B) starts descending, which reads like a chain, yet the keys 5 and 4 branch off the right of the root and keep the tree shallow.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Five keys fit in height 2 exactly when the first key is the median, 3.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q54", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "bst-insertion-deletion", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which of the following statements about deleting a key from a binary search tree with distinct keys are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Deleting a leaf rewrites no link field other than the one that pointed to it.", "Deleting a node with exactly one child splices that child into the deleted node's place.", "Deleting a node with two children always reduces the height of the tree.", "If the node to be deleted has two children, replacing it by its inorder predecessor keeps the BST ordering valid."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Deletion has three cases, decided by how many children the node has. The ordering invariant is preserved in each case because the replacement key is either the largest key below the deleted one or the smallest key above it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) TRUE.</b> A leaf is referred to only by its parent, so setting that one link to <code class=\"mm\">NULL</code> removes it completely.</li><li><b>(B) TRUE.</b> With one child the subtree below the node is a valid BST, so lifting the child into the vacated position keeps every ordering constraint.</li><li><b>(C) FALSE.</b> The predecessor sits at the rightmost end of the left subtree, at the very depth the deleted node occupied, so the height usually stays the same. Deleting the root of a chain of three, 1 &rarr; 2 &rarr; 3, leaves a chain of two: the height drops, but it need not in general.</li><li><b>(D) TRUE.</b> The inorder predecessor is the largest key smaller than the deleted key, so it is greater than every key of the left subtree and smaller than every key of the right subtree.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Treating (C) as obviously true because the tree gets smaller. Size and height are not the same measure: a deletion that removes a deep leaf can leave the height untouched, and one that replaces a node by a key at the same depth certainly does.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Only a node with two children forces a search, and even then the cost is bounded by the height.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q55", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "bst-insertion-deletion", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "The postorder traversal of a binary search tree of distinct keys is 15, 30, 35, 25, 55, 75, 65, 45. Taking the height as the number of edges on the longest root-to-leaf path, what is the height of the tree?", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a postorder traversal the root is the last element, and in a BST every key smaller than the root lies in its left subtree while every larger key lies in its right subtree. That lets the sequence be split recursively.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The root is 45, the last element. The keys below 45 are 15, 30, 35, 25 and those above are 55, 75, 65.</li><li>Left subtree from 15, 30, 35, 25: its root is 25; the keys 15 lie left of it and 30, 35 lie right, so 35 is the right child of 25 with 30 as its left child.</li><li>Right subtree from 55, 75, 65: its root is 65, with 55 on the left and 75 on the right.</li><li>The longest root-to-leaf paths are 45 &rarr; 25 &rarr; 35 &rarr; 30 and 45 &rarr; 65 &rarr; 55, each three edges.</li><li>The height is 3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">3.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Splitting at the first key larger than the root instead of at the root itself. Postorder puts the root last, so the root is 45; reading the split off the front of the sequence builds the wrong tree.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A traversal that names the root in a known position plus the BST ordering is enough to rebuild the tree.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q56", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "bst-insertion-deletion", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which of the following statements about searching in and inserting into a binary search tree of <i>n</i> distinct keys are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Insertion always places the new key at a leaf position.", "The number of distinct binary search trees that can be built from n distinct keys is 2<sup>n</sup>.", "A successful search performs at most h + 1 key comparisons, where h is the height of the tree in edges.", "If the keys are inserted in sorted order, the resulting tree has height n &minus; 1."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Insertion follows exactly the path a failed search for the key would follow and then attaches the key where that search would have stopped, which is always at an empty child slot of a leaf.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) TRUE.</b> The new node has no children at the moment it is created, so it is a leaf. Later insertions may give it children.</li><li><b>(B) FALSE.</b> The count is the <i>n</i>-th Catalan number, <code class=\"mm\">C<sub>n</sub> = (2n)! / ((n+1)! n!)</code>, which is 5 for three keys, 14 for four and 42 for five &mdash; far below 2<sup>5</sup> = 32 at the fifth step and below 2<sup>n</sup> in general.</li><li><b>(C) TRUE.</b> The deepest node is at depth <i>h</i>, and reaching it takes one comparison per node on the path, that is <i>h</i> + 1.</li><li><b>(D) TRUE.</b> Sorted input builds a chain of <i>n</i> nodes, whose height is <i>n</i> &minus; 1.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Estimating the number of shapes as 2<sup>n</sup> by treating every key as free to go left or right. The keys are ordered, so the choices are not independent: only the Catalan trees are valid BSTs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Catalan numbers, not powers of two, count BST shapes.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q57", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "bst-insertion-deletion", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A binary search tree of distinct keys has the preorder traversal 8, 5, 2, 7, 6, 12, 10, 15. Which one of the following is its postorder traversal?", "options": ["2, 7, 6, 5, 10, 15, 12, 8", "2, 6, 5, 7, 10, 15, 12, 8", "2, 6, 7, 5, 10, 15, 12, 8", "2, 5, 6, 7, 10, 12, 15, 8"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In preorder the root comes first, and in a BST every key smaller than the root belongs to the left subtree and every larger key to the right subtree. Reading the preorder sequence and splitting it at the root rebuilds the tree, and a postorder of that tree is then mechanical.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Root 8. The keys below 8 are 5, 2, 7, 6 and those above are 12, 10, 15.</li><li>Left subtree from 5, 2, 7, 6: root 5, with 2 on the left and the pair 7, 6 on the right. In that pair 7 is the root and 6 &lt; 7 goes to its left.</li><li>Right subtree from 12, 10, 15: root 12 with left child 10 and right child 15.</li><li>Postorder is left, right, root. Left subtree: <code class=\"mm\">2, 6, 7, 5</code> (6 before 7, since 6 is 7's left child). Right subtree: <code class=\"mm\">10, 15, 12</code>.</li><li>Appending the root gives <code class=\"mm\">2, 6, 7, 5, 10, 15, 12, 8</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">2, 6, 7, 5, 10, 15, 12, 8 &mdash; option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Writing 7 before 6 in the left subtree. In postorder a node is printed after its children, and 6 is 7's left child, so 6 comes first. Option (A) is exactly that slip.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Preorder plus the BST ordering determines the tree; preorder plus postorder does not, unless no node has a single child.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q58", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "bst-insertion-deletion", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "The five keys 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 are inserted one at a time into an empty binary search tree in some order. How many of the 120 possible insertion orders build the tree whose preorder traversal is 3, 1, 2, 5, 4?", "options": ["4", "5", "8", "6"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The root must be the first key inserted, and after that the keys of the left and right subtrees may interleave in any way, as long as each subtree's own relative order is one that builds that subtree. This gives the count <code class=\"mm\">n! / &prod; size(v)</code> over all nodes <i>v</i>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The target tree has root 3, left subtree 1 with right child 2, and right subtree 5 with left child 4.</li><li>Subtree sizes: node 3 has size 5, node 1 has size 2, node 5 has size 2, and nodes 2 and 4 have size 1.</li><li>The count is <code class=\"mm\">5! / (5 &times; 2 &times; 2 &times; 1 &times; 1) = 120 / 20 = 6</code>.</li><li>Checking directly: 3 must come first, 1 must precede 2, and 5 must precede 4. Of the 4! = 24 orders of the remaining keys, exactly those in which 1 precedes 2 and 5 precedes 4 build this tree, and there are 24 / 2 / 2 = 6 of them, for example <code class=\"mm\">3, 1, 5, 2, 4</code> and <code class=\"mm\">3, 5, 1, 4, 2</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">6 &mdash; option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 120 / 5 = 24 by fixing only the root. The order inside each subtree matters too, and the two subtrees then interleave, which is what the product of subtree sizes measures.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The same formula counts the insertion orders that produce any given BST shape.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q59", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "avl-trees-rotations", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "In an AVL tree the balance factor of a node is the height of its left subtree minus the height of its right subtree, and every node must have balance factor &minus;1, 0 or 1. Which one of the following balance factors cannot occur at a node of an AVL tree?", "options": ["&minus;1", "0", "2", "1"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The AVL condition is stated as an absolute bound on the balance factor: the two subtree heights of every node differ by at most one, so the balance factor lies in the set {&minus;1, 0, 1}.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A balance factor of &minus;1 means the right subtree is one level taller: allowed.</li><li>A balance factor of 0 means the subtrees have equal height: allowed.</li><li>A balance factor of 1 means the left subtree is one level taller: allowed.</li><li>A balance factor of 2 means one subtree is two levels taller than the other, which is exactly the situation a rotation is performed to remove.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">2 &mdash; option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Reading the definition as a bound on the heights rather than on their difference. A subtree may be as tall as it likes; what is bounded is the gap between the two siblings.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The height bound log<sub>2</sub>(n + 1) &le; h + 1 &lt; 1.44 log<sub>2</sub>(n + 2) follows from this one local rule.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q60", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "avl-trees-rotations", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "What is the minimum number of nodes in an AVL tree of height 5, where the height is the number of edges on the longest root-to-leaf path?", "options": [], "answer_display": "20", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The sparsest AVL tree of a given height makes its two subtrees as unequal as the rule permits, with heights <i>h</i> &minus; 1 and <i>h</i> &minus; 2. That gives the recurrence <code class=\"mm\">N(h) = N(h&minus;1) + N(h&minus;2) + 1</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Base cases: <code class=\"mm\">N(0) = 1</code> and <code class=\"mm\">N(1) = 2</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">N(2) = 2 + 1 + 1 = 4</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">N(3) = 4 + 2 + 1 = 7</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">N(4) = 7 + 4 + 1 = 12</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">N(5) = 12 + 7 + 1 = 20</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">20.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Using the perfect-tree count 2<sup>6</sup> &minus; 1 = 63, which is the maximum for height 5, not the minimum; or answering 12 by reading the height as one level less.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The sequence 1, 2, 4, 7, 12, 20, 33 is the AVL minimum-node sequence; its growth rate is the golden ratio, which is what makes the height logarithmic.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q61", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "avl-trees-rotations", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "The keys 50, 30, 70, 20, 40 and 10 are inserted in that order into an empty AVL tree. Which rotation restores the AVL property when the last key is inserted?", "options": ["A single left rotation at 50", "A left-right double rotation at 30", "A right-left double rotation at 50", "A single right rotation at 50"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Inserting into the left subtree of the left child of a node is the LL case, and it is repaired by one right rotation at the unbalanced node. A double rotation is needed only when the insertion goes into the inner subtree.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>After 50, 30, 70, 20, 40 the tree is balanced: 50 has left 30 and right 70, and 30 has left 20 and right 40.</li><li>Insert 10 as the left child of 20. Node 30 now has left height 2 and right height 1, balance factor 1, still fine.</li><li>Node 50 now has left height 3 and right height 1, balance factor 2 &mdash; unbalanced.</li><li>The insertion went left of 50 and then left again (50 &rarr; 30 &rarr; 20 &rarr; 10), the LL case, so a single right rotation at 50 applies.</li><li>After the rotation 30 is the root, with 20 (left, holding 10), 50 (right, holding 40 and 70) beneath it, and every balance factor is within &plusmn;1.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">A single right rotation at 50 &mdash; option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Naming the rotation after the direction the key travelled rather than after the pivot. The key went left, and the repair is a right rotation; the two names are opposite.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> LL &rarr; right rotation, RR &rarr; left rotation, LR &rarr; left then right, RL &rarr; right then left.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q62", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "avl-trees-rotations", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Counting a double rotation as two elementary rotations, what is the maximum number of elementary rotations that a single insertion into an AVL tree can require?", "options": ["2", "1", "3", "&Theta;(log n)"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An insertion raises the height of one subtree by one, so the first unbalanced node met on the way back up is repaired by a single rotation (LL or RR) or by a double rotation (LR or RL). Either way the height of that subtree returns to what it was before the insertion.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Climbing from the new leaf to the root, the first node whose balance factor becomes &plusmn;2 is the lowest unbalanced node.</li><li>An LL or RR case is fixed by one elementary rotation.</li><li>An LR or RL case is fixed by two elementary rotations, applied at the child and then at the node.</li><li>Once fixed, the subtree has its original height, so no ancestor can have become unbalanced, and the climb stops.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">2 elementary rotations &mdash; option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming the repair must propagate to the root, as it does on deletion. Insertion restores the subtree height exactly, so at most one rebalancing site exists.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Deletion is the operation that can need O(log n) rotations, because it can lower a subtree's height.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q63", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "avl-trees-rotations", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "The keys 5, 3, 8, 2, 4 and 1 are inserted in that order into an empty AVL tree. Counting a double rotation as two elementary rotations, how many elementary rotations does the whole insertion sequence perform?", "options": ["0", "3", "1", "2"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An insertion triggers a rotation only when some node's balance factor reaches &plusmn;2 on the way back to the root. The cheapest way to count is to insert one key at a time, check the balance factors, and rotate only where the rule is broken.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Insert 5: root.</li><li>Insert 3: left child of 5, all balance factors fine.</li><li>Insert 8: right child of 5. Node 5 has left height 1 and right height 1, balance 0.</li><li>Insert 2: left child of 3. Node 3 has balance 1, node 5 has left height 2 and right height 1, balance 1.</li><li>Insert 4: right child of 3. Node 3 is balanced again, node 5 still balance 1.</li><li>Insert 1: left child of 2. Node 3 now has left height 2 and right height 1, balance 1, fine. Node 5 has left height 3 and right height 1, balance 2 &mdash; the LL case.</li><li>One right rotation at 5 makes 3 the root, with 2 (and 1) on its left and 5 (with 4 and 8) on its right. Total elementary rotations: 1.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">1 elementary rotation &mdash; option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting a rotation at node 3 as well. Node 3 never reaches balance factor 2, because the key 4 was inserted into its right subtree before 1 deepened its left.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Trace the balance factors, not the visual tilt of the tree.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q64", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "avl-trees-rotations", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which of the following statements about AVL trees are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Every AVL tree is also a complete binary tree.", "The height of an AVL tree with n nodes is O(log n).", "An insertion is repaired by at most one single or one double rotation, applied at the lowest unbalanced node.", "Recomputing the heights after an insertion touches every node of the tree."], "answer_display": "Options (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An AVL tree is a binary search tree with one extra local invariant: at every node the two subtree heights differ by at most one. Everything else about the structure follows from that invariant.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) FALSE.</b> Completeness requires every level but the last to be full. The sparsest AVL tree of height 3 has 7 nodes in the shape of a Fibonacci tree, not a complete one, and larger examples differ even more.</li><li><b>(B) TRUE.</b> The minimum node count grows like &phi;<sup>h</sup>, so <i>h</i> = O(log n), specifically <i>h</i> &lt; 1.44 log<sub>2</sub>(n + 2).</li><li><b>(C) TRUE.</b> The first unbalanced node on the climb from the new leaf is repaired, the subtree regains its old height, and no ancestor is disturbed.</li><li><b>(D) FALSE.</b> Only the nodes on the path from the inserted key to the root can change height, which is O(log n) nodes, not all <i>n</i>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">options (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming balance implies completeness. An AVL tree may be quite ragged as long as no node is two levels out of balance.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> AVL buys a logarithmic height bound with one integer per node; it does not buy the array layout that completeness would give.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q65", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "avl-trees-rotations", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "What is the maximum possible height of an AVL tree that has exactly 20 nodes, where the height is the number of edges on the longest root-to-leaf path?", "options": ["4", "5", "6", "7"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The tallest AVL tree with a given number of nodes is the sparsest one, so the question is inverted: find the smallest height whose minimum node count already exceeds 20, and the answer is the height before it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Minimum node counts: <code class=\"mm\">N(0) = 1</code>, <code class=\"mm\">N(1) = 2</code>, <code class=\"mm\">N(2) = 4</code>, <code class=\"mm\">N(3) = 7</code>, <code class=\"mm\">N(4) = 12</code>, <code class=\"mm\">N(5) = 20</code>, <code class=\"mm\">N(6) = 33</code>.</li><li>Height 5 needs at least 20 nodes, and exactly 20 nodes are available, so the sparsest height-5 AVL tree fits.</li><li>Height 6 needs at least 33 nodes, which is more than 20, so no AVL tree with 20 nodes can reach height 6.</li><li>The maximum height is therefore 5.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">5 &mdash; option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Using the complete-tree bound log<sub>2</sub>(20 + 1) &asymp; 4.39 and rounding down to 4. That bound gives the minimum height of a balanced tree, not the maximum height an AVL tree is allowed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For a fixed node count, the AVL height lies between &lfloor;log<sub>2</sub> n&rfloor; and about 1.44 log<sub>2</sub> n.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q66", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "b-trees-b-plus-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "In a B-tree of order 5, where the order is the maximum number of children a node may have, what is the minimum number of keys that a node other than the root must hold?", "options": ["1", "3", "4", "2"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A node with <i>k</i> keys has <i>k</i> + 1 children, so bounding the children bounds the keys. In a B-tree of order <i>m</i> every node except the root has between &lceil;<i>m</i>/2&rceil; and <i>m</i> children, hence between &lceil;<i>m</i>/2&rceil; &minus; 1 and <i>m</i> &minus; 1 keys.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Order <i>m</i> = 5, so a node has at most 5 children and at most 4 keys.</li><li>Minimum children: &lceil;5/2&rceil; = 3.</li><li>Minimum keys: 3 &minus; 1 = 2.</li><li>The root is exempt: it may hold a single key and have two children.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">2 keys &mdash; option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 1 by halving the maximum key count and rounding down. The half-full condition is on the children, and the &minus;1 for keys is applied after rounding up.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For order <i>m</i>: at most <i>m</i> &minus; 1 keys, at least &lceil;<i>m</i>/2&rceil; &minus; 1 keys in every non-root node.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q67", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "b-trees-b-plus-trees", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A B+ tree of order 4, where the order is the maximum number of children an internal node may have, has height 1: a root and one level of leaves. What is the maximum number of keys it can store?", "options": [], "answer_display": "12", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a B+ tree every key is stored in a leaf, and the leaves are all at the same depth. The root of a height-1 tree is the only internal node, so its child count fixes the number of leaves.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Order 4 means at most 4 children per internal node, so the root can point to at most 4 leaves.</li><li>A leaf of order 4 holds at most 4 &minus; 1 = 3 keys.</li><li>Maximum keys = 4 leaves &times; 3 keys = 12.</li><li>Reaching 12 needs 11 separators in the root, and 11 keys fit in a root of order 4, so 12 is attainable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">12.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 3, the capacity of one leaf, or 15 by giving the root 4 keys as well. The root of a B+ tree stores separator keys copied from the leaves, not extra records.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A B+ tree of order <i>m</i> and height <i>h</i> holds at most <i>m</i><sup><i>h</i></sup>(<i>m</i> &minus; 1) keys.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q68", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "b-trees-b-plus-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which one of the following statements about B+ trees, as distinct from B-trees, is TRUE?", "options": ["Every record pointer is stored in a leaf, and internal nodes hold only keys that direct the search.", "A B+ tree of a given order holds fewer keys per node than a B-tree of the same order.", "A B+ tree cannot answer a range query without scanning the whole tree.", "In a B+ tree every key appears exactly once in the whole tree."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The defining difference is where the records live. A B-tree stores a record with every key at every level; a B+ tree stores records only in the leaves and keeps copies of some keys in internal nodes purely as routing information.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) TRUE.</b> Internal nodes carry keys and child pointers only, which is what makes them narrow and the tree shallow.</li><li><b>(B) FALSE.</b> Because internal nodes carry no record pointers, a node of fixed size holds more keys, not fewer.</li><li><b>(C) FALSE.</b> The leaves are linked in key order, so a range query walks from the first matching leaf along the chain.</li><li><b>(D) FALSE.</b> A key that separates two leaves is copied into the parent, so it appears in the internal node and again in the leaf.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Concluding that the duplicated keys waste space. The duplication is exactly what keeps internal nodes narrow, so the tree is shallower and the duplicates pay for themselves.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> B+ tree: records at the leaves only, leaves chained. B-tree: records wherever the key is.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q69", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "b-trees-b-plus-trees", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which of the following statements about B-trees of order <i>m</i> are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["An internal node with k keys has exactly k children.", "Every leaf is at the same depth.", "A node other than the root holds between &lceil;m/2&rceil; &minus; 1 and m &minus; 1 keys.", "The root may hold a single key."], "answer_display": "Options (B), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A B-tree is a balanced multiway search tree: it keeps every leaf at one depth, keeps every node at least half full, and stores <i>k</i> keys and <i>k</i> + 1 children together so that the keys separate the child ranges.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) FALSE.</b> The <i>k</i> keys split the key space into <i>k</i> + 1 intervals, so the node needs <i>k</i> + 1 children, not <i>k</i>.</li><li><b>(B) TRUE.</b> This is the balance condition, and it is what bounds the height by log<sub>&lceil;m/2&rceil;</sub> n.</li><li><b>(C) TRUE.</b> Half-full means at least &lceil;<i>m</i>/2&rceil; children, and one fewer key than children.</li><li><b>(D) TRUE.</b> The root is exempt from the half-full rule; a one-key root with two children is a legal B-tree.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">options (B), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Pairing <i>k</i> keys with <i>k</i> children. Each key is a separator, so there is always one more child than key.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Keys and children always differ by one in a B-tree node.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q70", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "b-trees-b-plus-trees", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A B-tree of order 5, that is at most 5 children and at most 4 keys per node, starts empty. The keys 10, 20, 30, 40 and 50 are inserted in that order. The insertion of 50 overflows the root, which splits. Which key moves up into the new root?", "options": ["20", "30", "40", "50"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> When a node overflows it is split around its median key, which is promoted to the parent; the keys below the median form the left node and those above form the right node. For an odd number of keys the median is unique.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Insert 10, 20, 30, 40: the root holds 4 keys, exactly the maximum for order 5.</li><li>Insert 50: the root now holds 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 &mdash; five keys, one too many.</li><li>The median of the five is the third, 30.</li><li>Split: 30 moves up into a new root, with 10, 20 in the left child and 40, 50 in the right child.</li><li>Each child now holds 2 keys, which satisfies the minimum &lceil;5/2&rceil; &minus; 1 = 2 for a non-root node.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">30 &mdash; option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Promoting 20 or 40, the middle of the two halves. The median of the overflowing node is the key that leaves it, and with five keys there is exactly one middle key.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> After the split the tree has height 1 and both children are at the minimum occupancy.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q71", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "trie-basics", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "The strings <code class=\"mm\">top</code>, <code class=\"mm\">toy</code> and <code class=\"mm\">try</code> are stored in a standard trie with one node per distinct prefix character and one root node. Counting the root, how many nodes does the trie have?", "options": ["6", "8", "7", "9"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A trie shares a node whenever two keys share a prefix, so the node count is one for the root plus one for every distinct prefix of every key.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">top</code> contributes the prefixes t, to, top: three nodes.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">toy</code> reuses t and to, and adds toy: one new node.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">try</code> reuses t, and adds tr and try: two new nodes.</li><li>Total = 1 (root) + 3 + 1 + 2 = 7.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">7 nodes &mdash; option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting 3 &times; 3 = 9 by giving each string its own path, or 6 by forgetting the root. Sharing the prefix t is what makes the answer smaller than the sum of the key lengths.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The node count is 1 plus the number of distinct prefixes, never more than 1 + total key length.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q72", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "trie-basics", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which of the following statements about standard tries are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Every node other than the root corresponds to exactly one prefix of at least one stored key.", "A key that is a prefix of another stored key ends at a node that also has children.", "A successful search for a key of length L visits L + 1 nodes, counting the root.", "The number of nodes is at least the total length of all stored keys."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A trie is a tree of prefixes: the path from the root to a node spells that node's prefix, and a node is a key when a flag there marks the end of a stored string.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><b>(A) TRUE.</b> The path spelling gives the prefix, and the node exists only because some key passes through it.</li><li><b>(B) TRUE.</b> If <code class=\"mm\">do</code> and <code class=\"mm\">dog</code> are both stored, the node for <code class=\"mm\">do</code> is a key and also has the child that continues to <code class=\"mm\">dog</code>.</li><li><b>(C) TRUE.</b> One node per character plus the root: 1 + L visits.</li><li><b>(D) FALSE.</b> Shared prefixes collapse, so the node count can be far below the total length. The strings <code class=\"mm\">a</code>, <code class=\"mm\">ab</code>, <code class=\"mm\">abc</code> have total length 6 but need only 4 nodes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Assuming a trie stores every character of every key separately. The whole point of the structure is that a shared prefix is stored once.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Search cost is O(L) regardless of how many keys are stored, which is what a hash table cannot promise for prefix queries.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q73", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "trie-basics", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A trie stores <i>n</i> keys, the longest key has length <i>L</i>, and no prefix is shared between any two keys. What is the worst-case number of nodes in the trie, as a function of <i>n</i> and <i>L</i>?", "options": ["&Theta;(nL)", "&Theta;(n + L)", "&Theta;(n log L)", "&Theta;(L<sup>n</sup>)"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each key contributes at most one node per character, and with no shared prefixes every character of every key needs its own node. The root is a single extra node.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Key <i>i</i> has length <i>l<sub>i</sub></i> &le; <i>L</i>, so it needs <i>l<sub>i</sub></i> nodes below the root.</li><li>The total is <code class=\"mm\">1 + &Sigma; l<sub>i</sub> &le; 1 + nL</code>.</li><li>The bound is attained when every key has length <i>L</i> and no two share a first character, so the answer is &Theta;(<i>nL</i>).</li><li>Sharing prefixes is what pulls the count down; the question rules that out.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">&Theta;(nL) &mdash; option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering &Theta;(n + L) by analogy with a compact structure. Without sharing there is nothing to compact: the nodes are proportional to the total number of characters.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A trie trades space for time: O(L) search, but up to O(nL) nodes.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q74", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "trie-basics", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "The five 4-bit strings <code class=\"mm\">0000</code>, <code class=\"mm\">0001</code>, <code class=\"mm\">0100</code>, <code class=\"mm\">0111</code> and <code class=\"mm\">1000</code> are stored in a binary trie with one node per distinct prefix, one root, and one child slot per bit. How many nodes of the trie have exactly one child?", "options": ["3", "4", "5", "6"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A node has two children exactly when two stored strings share the prefix that node spells and then diverge on the next bit. Counting the single-child nodes therefore means listing every prefix that is shared but not yet branched.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Under the root: 0 and 1, so the root has two children.</li><li>Under 0: 00 and 01, two children.</li><li>Under 00: only 000, one child.</li><li>Under 000: 0000 and 0001, two children.</li><li>Under 01: 010 and 011, two children. Under 010: only 0100, one child. Under 011: only 0111, one child.</li><li>Under 1: only 10, one child. Under 10: only 100, one child. Under 100: only 1000, one child.</li><li>The single-child nodes are 00, 010, 011, 1, 10 and 100: six in total.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">6 &mdash; option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Counting only the obvious chains such as 1 &rarr; 10 &rarr; 100 and missing 00, 010 and 011, which are single-child nodes because their siblings were never needed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The trie has 15 nodes here: 1 root + 14 prefix nodes.</p>"}, {"id": "DS-U5-Q75", "subject": "programming-data-structures", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "search-trees", "subtopic": "trie-basics", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A trie stores keys over a 26-letter alphabet, with one node per distinct prefix character plus one root node. A search for a key of 10 characters that is present in the trie visits how many nodes, counting the root?", "options": [], "answer_display": "11", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A search walks one node per character of the key, starting from the root and following the child indexed by each character in turn. The node reached after the last character holds the key.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The search begins at the root, which is node 1.</li><li>Each of the 10 characters moves the search one node down, giving nodes 2 through 11.</li><li>After the tenth character the search is at the node that stores the key, so the total is 1 + 10 = 11.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> <span class=\"ansline\">11.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common trap.</b> Answering 10 by counting only the characters. The root is visited too, and it carries no character of its own.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Search cost in a trie is &Theta;(L) and is independent of the number of keys stored.</p>"}];